; expand.ax — macro expansion, as a pass of its own.
;
; Expansion used to live in `codegen.ax`, applied at EMIT time: a head
; that named a macro had its template instantiated and the result
; emitted in the call's place. That placement is the cause of most of
; what was wrong with macros, and none of it was visible to any gate.
;
; What it cost, each item measured on the compiler this file replaces:
;
;   - Nothing type-checked an expansion. `(macro (bad x) (takesInt
;     "not an int"))` compiled and ran, exit 0. So did an undefined
;     name in a template. `check` reported OK on both, because the
;     checker's rule for a macro call is "cannot be typed before
;     expansion" and expansion had not happened yet.
;   - A macro-generated `match` was never checked for exhaustiveness.
;     The identical match written by hand is AX3005; written by a
;     macro it compiled silently.
;   - `check` and `build` disagreed. A template using `while`, `set`
;     or a field access emitted LLVM referencing an undefined
;     register - `check` said OK, `build` died as AX4003 `opt failed`
;     against `<toolchain>`, a diagnostic that blames the toolchain
;     for the compiler's own bug and carries no span into the source.
;   - Substitution reached 8 of the tags a template can contain and
;     returned the other 8 verbatim, so a parameter reference inside
;     `letm`, `set`, `while`, field access, field store, struct
;     construction, `alloc` or `handle` survived into the IR as the
;     TEMPLATE's identifier. Every one of those was silent.
;   - Recursion was unbounded. `(macro (loopy x) (loopy x))` segfaulted
;     the compiler - exit 139, no output, no diagnostic - in about
;     10 ms of CPU time, so not even slowly enough to interrupt.
;   - `()` in a template segfaulted it too: the parser answers node
;     handle 0 for the empty form and the substituter dereferenced it.
;     `lowerConds`, the sibling walker in the same file, guards; the
;     substituter did not.
;
; This pass runs between `resolveImports` and `checkModule`, so what
; the checker sees is the expanded program and every one of those
; becomes an ordinary diagnostic about ordinary code. The rules it
; implements, and where each differs from the emit-time expander:
;
; HYGIENE. A binder introduced by a TEMPLATE is renamed at every
; expansion, so it cannot capture a name the caller passed in.
; `(macro (addTo x) (let ((tmp 100)) (+ tmp x)))` called under a
; caller's own `tmp` used to answer 200 and now answers 101. The fresh
; name is `<name>.<counter>`, which is deliberate on both sides: `.`
; is TK_DOT to the lexer, so no Axiom source can spell such an
; identifier and a collision is impossible by construction, and `.` is
; legal in an unquoted LLVM identifier, so the name survives codegen's
; register naming with nothing to escape. The counter is per-run and
; monotonic, so the IR stays byte-identical across runs and
; `check-reproducible.sh` keeps holding.
;
; Renaming is the FORWARD half of hygiene. The REVERSE half - a
; caller's binder capturing a free identifier of the template - is
; `expQualify` below: a free identifier resolves where the macro was
; written. It used to resolve where the macro was called, so one macro
; meant two things depending on the caller's module (50 against 6,
; `tests/selfhost/364-macro-definition-site.ax`).
;
; That covers a macro defined in a MODULE, because its mechanism is
; the mangled name `resolveImports` already assigns to an imported
; declaration, and an entry-file declaration keeps its bare one. An
; entry-file macro is covered by a SECOND mechanism since 2026-08-16:
; `substTpl` stamps a template's free identifiers, and both resolvers
; - `checkVar`, and `emitVar` through `dispatchCall` - skip the local
; scope for a stamped reference, because a macro is a top-level
; declaration and its template's free identifiers therefore had no
; enclosing local scope where they were written
; (`tests/selfhost/394-macro-entry-capture.ax`, 130). This paragraph
; called the entry-file residue "a loud refusal rather than a wrong
; answer - AX3004 `expected function type`" until then, and it was a
; SILENT wrong answer: the `AX3004` came from an arity mismatch in the
; probe, not from the capture. `docs/macro-system.md` section 3 carries both
; halves.
;
; SHADOWING. A macro name no longer outranks every binder in the
; program. `(macro (v) 9) (fn (f v) v)` used to answer 9, because
; emission consulted the macro table before the symbol table. This
; pass carries the set of names bound at each point and a locally
; bound name is a variable, not a macro invocation.
;
; ARITY. A macro is applied to exactly as many arguments as it has
; parameters. The emit-time expander dropped extra arguments WITHOUT
; evaluating them, packed several into a `begin` when the macro had
; exactly one parameter, and left a parameter with no argument as its
; own name in the output - which is how `%q` reached `opt` as an
; undefined value. All three are now AX3018.
;
; COVERAGE. Every tag a template can contain has a case below, node
; handle 0 is guarded, and the default arm REFUSES with AX3021 rather
; than passing the node through. That last part is the point: the
; eight silent miscompiles were all one bug - a tag added to the
; parser and not to the substituter - and a default that returns the
; node unchanged makes the ninth one silent too. A default that
; refuses makes it loud, in the commit that adds the tag.
(import Mem)
(import Str)
(import Fmt)
(import Vec)
(import Intern)
(import core)
(import lexer)
(import parser)
(import namespace)
(import diag)
(import raw)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Limits.
;
; The depth cap is what stands between a self-referential macro and
; the segfault it used to produce. 128 is far above any real macro -
; `stdlib/Pre.ax`'s deepest expands to depth 1 - and far below the
; stack this process has.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expDepthLimit Int)
(pub fn (expDepthLimit)
  128)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The expander's environment.
;   macros — Vec of TAG_D_MACRO nodes, in declaration order
;   out    — Vec of Diag, the same sink `checkModule` fills
;   fresh  — the gensym counter. NOT shared with the checker's
;            `tcCounter`: that one names type variables `_tN`, those
;            names are printed inside AX3004 messages, and the AXDL
;            goldens pin them byte for byte. A renamed binder must not
;            renumber a type variable.
;   depth  — expansion depth at the current point
;   decls  — the merged declaration list, for resolving a template's
;            free identifiers at its DEFINITION site (`expQualify`)
;   macmod — the module of the macro currently being instantiated, 0
;            for one defined in the entry file. Set and restored around
;            each instantiation rather than threaded as a parameter:
;            `substTpl` runs to completion before the expansion it
;            produces is walked, so no two macros are ever mid-
;            substitution at once.
;   scNext   — word 24, the fresh scope-id counter for the scope-set
;            hygiene (MAC-HYG-9). Every instantiation introduces one
;            fresh scope; every `syntax/for` loop one per element
;            walk. NEVER the gensym counter: `fresh` numbers the
;            renamed spellings the IR pins byte for byte (MAC-EXP-12),
;            and scope ids must not renumber them.
;   scChain  — word 25, the current scope chain, outermost-first: the
;            instantiation scope, then one id per enclosing for-loop.
;            Saved and restored around each instantiation; pushed and
;            truncated around each for-element walk, paired with the
;            for-stack's own marks.
;   scTpl    — word 26, one ScopeBind per template binder in scope,
;            pushed where the binder is renamed and truncated when its
;            body is done. Every template-binder reference resolves
;            here.
;   scSeq    — word 27, push-order stamps shared by template binders
;            and for-bindings, so a reference can order a template
;            hit against a for-binding. Monotonic per pass, never
;            reset: only co-active entries are ever compared.
;   word 28  — vestigial, written 0 and read by nothing. It held the
;            switch for the check that compared the scope records
;            against a second, renaming-only track; M4 deleted that
;            track and the check with it (MAC-HYG-9). Kept for the
;            same additive-numbering reason as word 19.
;   words 31-32 — struct-name interner and typed declaration buckets,
;            built after declaration macros finish, before bodies expand.
;   optNames — the functions whose signature result is `Option`,
;            collected once so `?` does not scan every declaration
;            again for each function.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct MEnv
  (macros : Nodes)
  (out : Diags)
  (mut fresh : Int)
  (mut depth : Int)
  (decls : (Vec ASTNode))
  (mut macmod : Int)
  (mut walk : Int)
  (mut nodes : Int)
  (mut blown : Int)
  (mut site : Int)
  (frames : Frames)
  (mut declmod : Int)
  (units : Units)
  (mut forstack : ForBinds)
  (bares : (Vec String))
  (fulls : (Vec String))
  (mods : ModuleImports)
  (mut qsite : Int)
  (gens : GeneratedOrigins)
  (callbs : Int)
  (mut seqbinds : SeqBinds)
  (mut tplbinds : Strings)
  (mut declnodes : Int)
  (midx : MangleIdx)
  (mut scNext : Int)
  (mut scChain : (Vec Int))
  (mut scTpl : ScopeBinds)
  (mut scSeq : Int)
  (retired28 : Int)
  (mut defer : Int)
  (mut deferHits : Int)
  (structNames : InternState)
  (structBuckets : NodesRows)
  (mut carrier : Int)
  (optNames : Strings))

; The two budgets on the OUTPUT, which the parser's
; own AX2005 guard cannot see: it measures the source,
; and expansion routinely produces a tree deeper and
; larger than any source that guard would have let
; through. `walk` is the current depth of the
; expansion walk, which IS the depth of the tree being
; built, since `expandExpr` recurses structurally.
; `nodes` counts every node it visits. `blown` is set
; once so the refusal is reported once rather than at
; every node after it.
; The module whose declaration is being expanded -
; the INVOCATION SITE, as against `macmod`, which is
; the definition site of the macro currently being
; instantiated. A private macro is reachable only
; from its own module, and this is the module the
; comparison is against.
; One ExpFrame per instantiation, in instantiation
; order - outermost first for any one invocation,
; since an outer macro instantiates before the
; template it produced is walked. `expAttachFrames`
; joins these to the CHECKER's diagnostics afterwards,
; by span HANDLE: every node a template produces is
; rebuilt carrying the invocation's span (MAC-EXP-14),
; the same record by reference, so the join is pointer
; equality and cannot collide across files.
; The module of the DECLARATION being expanded. Word 9
; is overwritten during each template walk (that is
; what makes nested private macros resolve); this one
; is not, and it is the module the checker will stamp
; on a diagnostic inside this declaration - the frame
; join needs exactly that.
; The CUnit2 table (file, src, name), 0 for callers
; that have none (repl, codegen's re-expansion). A
; diagnostic anchored at a MODULE's node must carry
; that module's unit index, or the renderer resolves
; its span against the entry file's bytes and the
; location lands mid-identifier in whatever text
; happens to share the offset - the derivability
; gate caught exactly that on the first module-side
; declaration-macro refusal.
; The syntax/for binding stack (MAC-CAP-5): one
; 4-word record per ACTIVE iteration - the binder's
; name, the current element, the sequence's identity
; key ("constructors Color"), and the element index.
; Innermost binding last, searched backward. Saved,
; RESET to empty, and restored around each template
; instantiation - a nested macro's template must not
; see the enclosing template's iteration variables,
; exactly as `macmod` is scoped - and restored BEFORE
; the output walk, because a binding is dead the
; moment `substTpl` returns.
; What a MODULE-SIDE generated declaration needs, and
; the reason MAC-EXP-16's v1 refused to make one: the
; two mangling records import resolution built
; (bare -> Mod$name, and the self-mapping a private
; declaration gets), and the (module, import filter)
; table it recorded on the way past. A declaration
; generated in a module has to land in the namespace
; its neighbours landed in, and the pass that puts
; them there has already run.
; The module a declaration-macro invocation was
; written in, held across the whole instantiation -
; word 9 cannot serve, because instantiation moves it
; to the MACRO's module so that a nested private
; macro resolves at its definition site. What needs
; the invocation's module is the query vocabulary: a
; module deriving over its own type asks about a name
; its neighbours can see, whatever an importer's name
; list says.
; Every declaration phase D generated, as (final name,
; macro name) pairs, for the one consumer that has to
; tell them apart from what the file declares:
; `axiom symbols` (MAC-TOOL-4). It is the pass's
; ANSWER rather than another parameter - the callers
; that do not care discard it, as they discarded the 0
; this returned before.
; The binders in scope AT THE INVOCATION, held only
; while a template is being instantiated. It exists
; for one question: whether a template's FREE
; identifier is shadowed at the call site, which is
; MAC-HYG-8's first hole and a silent wrong answer
; until something looks.
; Vestigial since 2026-08-16: this held the call
; site's binders for the capture check `AX3032`'s
; retirement deleted. Kept rather than removed because
; the numbering is additive - taking a field out of
; the middle moves every word after it - and written
; and read by nothing now.
; Word 20: the SEQUENCE bindings of the rule being
; instantiated (MAC-LANG-16) - `Vec SeqBind`, a name
; against the forms a repeating element absorbed.
; On the env rather than threaded through `substTpl`
; for the same reason `forstack` is: the walk that
; needs it is twenty signatures below the walk that
; knows it, and every one of those signatures already
; carries `env`.
; Word 21: the names the TEMPLATE itself binds around
; the substitution in flight - a generated function's
; parameters. They look exactly like free identifiers
; to `substTpl`, because a generated function's
; parameters are NOT gensym-renamed (there is nothing
; to protect them from: the function is fresh), and
; without this they would be stamped "resolve at top
; level" and resolve to nothing. Measured:
; `372-decl-macro.ax` was `AX3001 undefined variable
; x` for `(fn (nm x) (+ (target x) extra))`.
(pub :: expMacrosVec (-> MEnv Nodes))
(pub fn (expMacrosVec env)
  env.macros)

(pub :: expOutVec (-> MEnv Diags))
(pub fn (expOutVec env)
  env.out)

(pub :: expDepth (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expDepth env)
  env.depth)

(pub :: expDeclsVec (-> MEnv Nodes))
(pub fn (expDeclsVec env)
  env.decls)

; The macro's module, as a word: a String when there is one and 0 when
; there is not. `expMacModName` is the String view; see `nodeCName` in
; parser.ax for why the cast lives in the accessor.
(pub :: expMacMod (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expMacMod env)
  env.macmod)

(pub :: expMacModName (-> MEnv String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expMacModName env)
  (as (expMacMod env) String))

; The invocation site's module, as a String; 0 for the entry file.
(:: expSiteName (-> MEnv String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (expSiteName env)
  (as env.site String))

; The quoting site, as a word: a String when there is one and 0 when
; there is not.
(pub :: expQSite (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expQSite env)
  env.qsite)

(pub :: expBaresVec (-> MEnv Names))
(pub fn (expBaresVec env)
  env.bares)

(pub :: expFullsVec (-> MEnv Names))
(pub fn (expFullsVec env)
  env.fulls)

; The typed module records shared with the resolver.
(pub :: expModsVec (-> MEnv ModuleImports))
(pub fn (expModsVec env)
  env.mods)

; The index over `bares` (namespace.ax's `MangleIdx`), created fresh
; for this expansion and catching up from whatever `bares` holds on
; its first question, so phase D's records cost what the resolver's
; did rather than a scan of `bares` per generated declaration. Unused
; when `bares` is 0 - both sites that would ask it are guarded on
; that already.
(pub :: expMidx (-> MEnv MangleIdx))
(pub fn (expMidx env)
  env.midx)

; The output limits, and why these numbers.
;
; DEPTH is the parser's own `parseMaxDepth`, deliberately the same
; number: expansion must not produce a program the parser would have
; refused. It could not be checked at parse time because the source is
; short - `(macro (g x) <500 deep>)` invoked 120 times nests 620 in the
; file and 60,000 in the tree, and the second number is the one that
; overflows the stack. Measured: that input SIGSEGVs `check`, `run`,
; `symbols` and `axiom lsp`.
;
; NODES bounds fan-out, which no depth limit can see: `(macro (m x) (+ x x))`
; doubles per level, so 26 nested invocations are 154 bytes of source
; and 2^26 nodes - `check` took 41.4s and rose 4x per two levels, and
; the language server simply never answered. The budget is far above
; any real macro (the whole of `stdlib/Pre.ax` expands to depth 1) and
; far below where the wait becomes indistinguishable from a hang.
;
; `expMaxWalk` answers the parser's `parseMaxDepth`
; (`core/parser.ax`), deliberately the same number;
; `scripts/check-diagnostics.sh` holds the equality, and pins
; `expDepthLimit` above at 128 besides it.
(pub :: expMaxWalk Int)
(pub fn (expMaxWalk)
  1024)

(pub :: expMaxNodes Int)
(pub fn (expMaxNodes)
  2000000)

; The THIRD output budget, and the one the two above cannot see.
;
; They are incremented inside `expandExpr`, and `expandProgram` runs
; the DECLARATION phase first - so for a declaration macro that fans
; out, phase E never starts and neither counter is ever touched. The
; depth axis was covered there: `expDeclMacroRounds` stops a template
; that regenerates its own invocation, and it fires. The SIZE axis was
; not, so a template that generates two invocations per round is
; bounded only by memory. It does not draw a diagnostic, it is killed
; by the operating system, at multi-gigabyte RSS, with no output - the
; one failure shape a program compiling code it did not write cannot
; defend against, since `check` had no lever to bound it.
;
; 10,000 against a measured ceiling: `axiom symbols` puts the whole
; self-hosted compiler at 2,535 symbols, and the largest phase-D
; consumer in the tree at 198. `stdlib/` and `core/` declare no
; declaration macros at all, so nothing in this repository approaches
; it - which is exactly the condition under which a floor rots, and is
; why `tests/selfhost/395-decl-macro-width.ax` generates a few hundred
; declarations on purpose: lowering this number into real code has to
; turn a gate red.
;
; ONE THING THIS SURFACES AND DOES NOT FIX. The refusal it produces
; carries 5,001 expansion frames - one AXDL line of 115 KB, almost all
; of it the same macro name. `expInstantiateDecl` pushes a frame per
; instantiation onto a Vec it shares with every other diagnostic and
; never pops it, so the chain records instantiations PERFORMED rather
; than nesting ENTERED. That is not new and is not caused by this
; budget; it was unobservable while a fanning-out macro died of memory
; before anything could print. Unwinding it means truncating a
; structure sixteen goldens read - `490-expansion-backtrace` among
; them - so it is its own change with its own evidence, not a rider on
; this one. The expression side shows what the bound should look like:
; `991-macro-recursion` carries 128 frames, which is its depth limit,
; and means something.
(pub :: expMaxDecls Int)
(pub fn (expMaxDecls)
  10000)

(pub :: expBlown (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expBlown env)
  env.blown)

; Report once, then stay reported: every node after the first refusal
; would otherwise emit its own copy.
(pub :: expBlow (-> MEnv Int String Int))
(pub fn (expBlow env sp msg)
  {
    (if (== env.blown 0)
      {
        (set env.blown 1)
        (expEmit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3024" "macro-expansion-limit" sp msg None
            (Some
              "expansion is a rewrite, so the program the compiler sees is the expanded one; make the macro produce less, or write the repeated part as a function and let the macro expand to a call")
            "the parser's own nesting limit measures the SOURCE, and this limit measures what expansion produced from it"))
      })
    0
  })

(pub :: expSetDepth (-> MEnv Int MEnv))
(pub fn (expSetDepth env d)
  {
    (set env.depth d)
    env
  })

(pub :: expSetMacMod (-> MEnv Int MEnv))
(pub fn (expSetMacMod env m)
  {
    (set env.macmod m)
    env
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A template's FREE identifier, resolved at the macro's definition
; site rather than at the call site.
;
; Without this, one macro means two things. `MacLib` defines `helper`
; and a macro `callHelper` whose template calls it; an entry file that
; imports `MacLib` and defines its own `helper` used to get ITS OWN
; from `(callHelper 5)` — exit 6 — while the identical macro expanded
; inside `MacLib`'s own function got `MacLib`'s — exit 105. Same
; macro, two answers, chosen by where it was written down.
;
; `resolveImports` has already run when this pass does, and it mangles
; an imported `fn`/`::` declaration's name to `Mod$name` while
; stamping the module on word 8. So the definition-site name a
; template's free identifier should resolve to already EXISTS as a
; declaration name, and rewriting the reference to it is enough: both
; resolvers find it without knowing anything about macros.
; `findFnEnt` looks the name up in a table keyed by exactly those
; mangled names, and `mangledFor` passes an already-mangled name
; through unchanged, so the emitted call names the right symbol.
;
; The rewrite is conditional on the qualified name actually being a
; declaration, which is what keeps it from touching anything else: a
; template referring to `+`, to `if`, to a constructor (constructors
; are not mangled) or to a name the entry file owns finds no
; `Mod$name` and stays bare.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expQualify (-> MEnv String String))
(pub fn (expQualify env name)
  ; `m` is the module WORD, so the guard reads it directly - the
  ; `(cast Int m)` this replaced existed only because `expMacMod`
  ; answered a type variable. The String view is re-derived on the
  ; qualified path rather than cast from `m`: a cast at an argument
  ; root classifies evidence 0 and drops the release (MM-LIFE-2e).
  {
    (let m (expMacMod env))
    (if (== m 0)
      name
      {
        (let q (concat (concat (expMacModName env) "$") name))
        (if (== (expDeclNamed (expDeclsVec env) q 0) 1)
          q
          ; MAC-HYG-8's FOURTH hole. `MAC-HYG-7`'s rewrite tries
          ; exactly one spelling - `Mod$name` for the macro's own
          ; module - so a template calling something its module
          ; merely IMPORTED found no such declaration, stayed bare,
          ; and was captured by the caller: the same
          ; one-macro-two-meanings failure `MAC-HYG-6` closed, in
          ; the case `MAC-HYG-6` did not reach.
          ;
          ; This rule said closing it needed import EDGES, which
          ; the merge consumes. It does not. Every declaration in
          ; the merged list carries the module it came from, so
          ; the question "which module declares this name" is
          ; answerable from the list itself - and when exactly ONE
          ; module answers, that is the one the macro's module
          ; reached, because a template that names something
          ; unreachable would not have compiled where it was
          ; written.
          ;
          ; Exactly one. Two candidates is `AX3014`'s ambiguity by
          ; another name, and this rewrite has no more right to
          ; pick between them than a bare reference does, so it
          ; leaves the name alone and the CHECKER reports the
          ; ambiguity at the invocation, carrying the expansion
          ; frame that names the macro. (This used to say the
          ; capture refusal `AX3032` reported it; that code was
          ; retired on 2026-08-16 when the reference stopped being
          ; capturable at all, and `AX3014` was always the one
          ; that fired for two candidates.)
          (expUniqueModuleName (expDeclsVec env) name))
      })
  })

; The single mangled declaration whose BARE name is `name`, or the
; name unchanged. `Mod$name` is the mangled spelling, so the match is
; on the suffix after the `$` and only for declarations that carry a
; module.
(pub :: expUniqueModuleName (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expUniqueModuleName decls name)
  {
    (let hit (expUniqueModuleFrom decls name 0 (as 0 String)))
    (if (== (as hit Int) 0)
      name
      hit)
  })

(pub :: expUniqueModuleFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int String String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expUniqueModuleFrom decls name i found)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    found
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
            (&& (!= (as (nodeModule d) Int) 0)
              (&& (== (nodeVis d) 1) (== (expMangledIs (nodeAName d) name) 1))))
        (if (== (as found Int) 0)
          (expUniqueModuleFrom decls name (+ i 1) (nodeAName d))
          ; a second module declares it: ambiguous, leave bare
          (as 0 String))
        (expUniqueModuleFrom decls name (+ i 1) found))
    }))

; Is `s` the mangled spelling `Mod$name` of the bare `name`?
;
; This is written as a comparison and not as "slice off the module and
; `strEq`" because the slice ALLOCATES, once per declaration and once
; per template identifier the first spelling missed. The self-compile
; already allocates ~3.9 million string headers and frees none of
; them, and check-bootstrap's 540 MiB ceiling is calibrated on exactly
; that count: the slicing version measured 540 MiB against 528 and
; failed the gate, which is the ceiling doing its stated job of
; catching a per-allocation constant rather than a shape change.
(pub :: expMangledIs (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (expMangledIs s name)
  (if (let (Some i) (strFindByte s 36 0))
    (if (!= (- (strLen s) (+ i 1)) (strLen name))
      0
      (expTailEq s (+ i 1) name 0 (strLen name)))
    0))

(pub :: expTailEq (-> String Int String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expTailEq s off name i n)
  (if (>= i n)
    1
    (if (== (strByte s (+ off i)) (strByte name i))
      (expTailEq s off name (+ i 1) n)
      0)))

(pub :: expDeclNamed (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (expDeclNamed decls name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG)) (strEq (nodeAName d) name))
        1
        (expDeclNamed decls name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A fresh identifier. See the header for why `.` is the marker.
(pub :: expFresh (-> MEnv String String))
(pub fn (expFresh env base)
  {
    (let n env.fresh)
    {
      (set env.fresh (+ n 1))
      (concat (concat base ".") (expFmtInt n))
    }
  })

; Decimal rendering. `codegen.ax` has one of these and `Fmt` has
; another, but the compiler's own modules do not import `Fmt` and this
; pass must not depend on codegen.
(pub :: expFmtInt (-> Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expFmtInt n)
  (if (< n 10)
    {
      (let ch (strAlloc 1))
      {
        (__store8 (strData ch) 0 (+ n 48))
        ch
      }
    }
    {
      (let prefix (expFmtInt (/ n 10)))
      {
        (let suffix (strAlloc 1))
        {
          (__store8 (strData suffix) 0 (+ (% n 10) 48))
          (concat prefix suffix)
        }
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Diagnostics.
;
; Every one anchors at the INVOCATION's span, which is a real position
; in the file being compiled. The macro's own definition span would be
; the better secondary, and is what criterion 3 of the roadmap's
; section 4.2 asks for, but a `Diag` carries one unit and a macro is
; usually defined in another file - reporting a span from a different
; source text against this diagnostic's unit would point at a real
; line of the wrong file. That is worse than not pointing, so until
; `Diag` can carry a per-span unit these say the macro's NAME in the
; message and anchor only where they can be trusted.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expEmit (-> MEnv diag::Diag Int))
(pub fn (expEmit env d)
  {
    (vecPush (expOutVec env) d)
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Collecting the macro table.
;
; Declaration order, searched backward, so a later definition of a
; name wins over an earlier one - and since import resolution puts a
; module's declarations ahead of the importing file's, that is also
; what lets an entry file's macro shadow an imported one. It is the
; same rule the emit-time expander used, kept deliberately: changing
; it is a language change, not a bug fix.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expCollectMacros (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expCollectMacros decls acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_MACRO)
          (vecPush acc d))
      }
      (expCollectMacros decls acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Qualified macro names. `Pre::when` reaches this pass as `Pre$when` -
; the flat spelling the parser gives EVERY qualified reference
; (`mangledFromChain`). Macro declarations keep their bare names;
; nothing mangles them; so a qualified invocation resolves by
; SPLITTING the reference instead: the bare part against the macro's
; name, the module part against the module stamped on its declaration
; (word 8). That is `declMatches`' rule for types and constructors,
; re-spelt here because this pass must not depend on the checker.
; Until 2026-08-14, `Pre::when` was AX3001: the macro table had no
; module on the NAME and nothing split the reference
; (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-12).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expModOf (-> String (Option String)))
(pub fn (expModOf s)
  (expModOfFrom s (- (strLen s) 1)))

(pub :: expModOfFrom (-> String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expModOfFrom s i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (== (strByte s i) 36)
      (Some (strSlice s 0 i))
      (expModOfFrom s (- i 1)))))

; A reference as the programmer wrote it, for diagnostics: the parser
; flattened `Mod::name` to `Mod$name`, and quoting THAT back reports a
; spelling that appears nowhere in the source. Bare names pass through
; untouched, which is what keeps every existing golden byte-stable.
(pub :: expSrcSpelling (-> String String))
(pub fn (expSrcSpelling s)
  (match (expModOf s)
    ((None) s)
    ((Some m) (concat (concat m "::") (bareOf s)))))

(pub :: expSameMod (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (expSameMod a b)
  (if (== (as a Int) 0)
    (if (== (as b Int) 0)
      1
      0)
    (if (== (as b Int) 0)
      0
      (if (strEq a b)
        1
        0))))

(pub :: expFindMacro (-> MEnv String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindMacro env name)
  {
    (let site (expSiteName env))
    (match (expModOf name)
      ((Some qm)
        (expFindMacroQual (expMacrosVec env) (bareOf name) qm site
          (- (vecLen (expMacrosVec env)) 1))
      )
      ((None)
        (match (expFindMacroIn (expMacrosVec env) name site (- (vecLen (expMacrosVec env)) 1))
          ((None) None)
          ; An imported macro must not outrank an entry-file
          ; function of the same bare name. `isMacroName` applies
          ; the same veto.
          ((Some mac)
            (if (&& (!= (nodeModule mac) 0)
                  (&& (== (expSameMod (nodeModuleName mac) site) 0)
                    (== (expDeclNamed (expDeclsVec env) name 0) 1)))
              None
              (Some mac))
          ))
      ))
  })

; The backward walk for a QUALIFIED reference: name and module both,
; visible when exported or when the invocation site IS that module.
(pub :: expFindMacroQual (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindMacroQual ms bare qm site i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let m (vecGet ms i))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName m) bare)
            (&& (!= (nodeModule m) 0)
              (&& (strEq (nodeModuleName m) qm)
                (|| (== (nodeVis m) 1) (&& (!= (as site Int) 0) (strEq site qm))))))
        (Some m)
        (expFindMacroQual ms bare qm site (- i 1)))
    }))

; A macro its module does not export is invisible from anywhere else.
;
; `mangleDecl` rewrites only `fn` and `::`, so a macro's name carries
; no module and `resolveDeclsPhase`'s word-5 flag is the only thing
; that knows: `pub`, or named by the import's list. Until this, a
; non-`pub` macro was visible EVERYWHERE - a widening that arrived with
; module visibility itself, because before that the declaration was
; deleted outright and the module could not be imported at all.
;
; `site` is the module of the declaration being expanded, and it is set
; to the MACRO's module while that macro's template is walked, so a
; public macro whose template invokes a private one of its own module
; still expands from outside. That is the same definition-site rule
; `expQualify` applies to a template's free identifiers.
(pub :: expFindMacroIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindMacroIn ms name site i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let m (vecGet ms i))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName m) name)
            (|| (== (nodeVis m) 1)
              (|| (== (nodeModule m) 0) (&& (!= (as site Int) 0) (strEq (nodeModuleName m) site)))))
        (Some m)
        (expFindMacroIn ms name site (- i 1)))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The bound-name stack.
;
; A stack of ScopeBind records. Each lexical binder introduces a fresh
; scope, and references resolve to the innermost visible record. Besides
; keeping local names ahead of macros, the records give literal matching
; and repeated pattern binders stable binding identities.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expBound (-> ScopeBinds String Int))
(pub fn (expBound bs name)
  (expBoundFrom bs name (- (vecLen bs) 1)))

(pub :: expBoundFrom (-> ScopeBinds String Int Int))
(pub fn (expBoundFrom bs name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    0
    {
      (let binding (vecGet bs i))
      (if (strEq binding.name name)
        1
        (expBoundFrom bs name (- i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: expTrunc (-> (Vec a) Int Int))
(pub fn (expTrunc bs n)
  (if (<= (vecLen bs) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPop bs)
      (expTrunc bs n)
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Scope-set hygiene (MAC-HYG-9).
;
; Scope sets answer "which binder?" by matching `(name, scopes)`
; pairs, a reference resolving to the innermost binder whose scopes
; are a subset of its own. Template binders resolve through
; `expScResolveTpl` and answer the spelling their record carries;
; for-bindings answer through `expForBindOf`, and the newer push-order
; stamp of the two hits is the inner binder. These records are the
; only table a reference consults: a binder is a `(name, scopes)` pair
; from the push that renames it to the reference that reads it.
;
; THE INNERMOST RULE (M3). A variable reference takes the inner of
; the template hit and the for hit: the for-binding wins when there
; is no template hit, or its stamp is newer than the hit's - pushed
; later means pushed inside, on stacks whose pops always remove what
; the pushes put under them. For-bindings are visible by ACTIVITY
; (forstack membership), not by chain prefix: a declaration-level
; iteration's bindings are live in generated bodies whose chain never
; grew the iteration scope, and presence is the visibility there.
; Those stale bindings always lose the stamp comparison - a generated
; body starts a fresh record stack, so anything it pushes is newer -
; which is why activity needs no prefix check to stay sound.
; Template binders are visible by subset, and every co-active
; template record's scopes are a prefix of the live chain, because a
; chain pop always truncates the records pushed under it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expScChainVec (-> MEnv Ints))
(pub fn (expScChainVec env)
  env.scChain)

(pub :: expScTplVec (-> MEnv ScopeBinds))
(pub fn (expScTplVec env)
  env.scTpl)

; A fresh scope id.
(pub :: expScNextId (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expScNextId env)
  {
    (let n (+ env.scNext 1))
    {
      (set env.scNext n)
      n
    }
  })

; The next push-order stamp. It orders the template stack against the
; for-stack, which is what the innermost rule compares.
(pub :: expScNextSeq (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expScNextSeq env)
  {
    (let n (+ env.scSeq 1))
    {
      (set env.scSeq n)
      n
    }
  })

; A copy of the current chain. Snapshots are taken at binder pushes;
; the live chain keeps moving, so sharing it would rewrite history.
(pub :: expScChainCopy (-> MEnv Ints))
(pub fn (expScChainCopy env)
  (expScChainCopyIn (expScChainVec env) vecNew 0))

(pub :: expScChainCopyIn (-> Ints Ints Int Ints))
(pub fn (expScChainCopyIn src dst i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    dst
    {
      (vecPush dst (vecGet src i))
      (expScChainCopyIn src dst (+ i 1))
    }))

; Whether `small` is a prefix of `big`: scope visibility, since every
; chain grows by pushes and shrinks only with its records' truncation.
(pub :: expScPrefix (-> Ints Ints Int))
(pub fn (expScPrefix small big)
  (if (> (vecLen small) (vecLen big))
    0
    (expScPrefixIn small big 0)))

(pub :: expScPrefixIn (-> Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (expScPrefixIn small big i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen small))
    1
    (if (!= (vecGet small i) (vecGet big i))
      0
      (expScPrefixIn small big (+ i 1)))))

; Enter an instantiation's scope generation: a fresh scope becomes the
; whole chain, and the template-binder stack starts empty. Answers the
; previous chain for the exit to restore.
; Generations never nest mid-substitution - `substTpl` instantiates
; nothing, nested invocations expand in the output walk afterwards -
; so the save/restore is belt and braces, house style.
(pub :: expScEnterInst (-> MEnv Ints))
(pub fn (expScEnterInst env)
  {
    (let prev env.scChain)
    {
      (set env.scChain vecNew)
      (vecPush (expScChainVec env) (expScNextId env))
      (set env.scTpl vecNew)
      prev
    }
  })

(pub :: expScExitInst (-> MEnv Ints Int))
(pub fn (expScExitInst env prev)
  {
    (set env.scChain prev)
    0
  })

; Enter a declaration instantiation's generation. The chain is reset
; like any instantiation's, but the template-binder stack is NOT:
; each generated body resets it at its own build site
; (`expScResetTpl`), while the declaration's for-bindings stay live
; across it.
(pub :: expScEnterDeclInst (-> MEnv Ints))
(pub fn (expScEnterDeclInst env)
  {
    (let prev env.scChain)
    {
      (set env.scChain vecNew)
      (vecPush (expScChainVec env) (expScNextId env))
      prev
    }
  })

; A generated body starts with no template-binder records. The chain
; is NOT touched: the body substitutes under the declaration's
; generation, whose for-bindings are live here.
(pub :: expScResetTpl (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expScResetTpl env)
  {
    (set env.scTpl vecNew)
    0
  })

; Push a template binder's record. Called with the old spelling and
; the fresh one at the four sites that rename a template binder (let,
; region, lambda parameter, pattern binder); the fresh spelling is
; what references to this binder answer.
(pub :: expScPushTpl (-> MEnv String String Int))
(pub fn (expScPushTpl env name spelling)
  {
    (vecPush (expScTplVec env)
      (ScopeBind
        :name name
        :scopes (expScChainCopy env)
        :seq (expScNextSeq env)
        :spelling spelling))
    0
  })

; Truncate the records pushed under a binder's body. The mark is taken
; before the binder is pushed, at the same four sites.
(pub :: expScTruncTpl (-> MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expScTruncTpl env mark)
  (expTrunc (expScTplVec env) mark))

; Push a for-loop's iteration scope for one element's walk. The id is
; per element walk rather than per loop: records never outlive their
; element (the for-stack truncates beside us), so the two are
; observationally identical, and nesting falls out of the pairing.
(pub :: expScPushLoop (-> MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expScPushLoop env)
  {
    (vecPush (expScChainVec env) (expScNextId env))
    0
  })

(pub :: expScTruncLoop (-> MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expScTruncLoop env mark)
  (expTrunc (expScChainVec env) mark))

; The innermost template binder whose scopes are visible in the live
; chain. A miss carries no record or vector position.
(pub :: expScResolveTpl (-> MEnv String (Option ScopeBind)))
(pub fn (expScResolveTpl env name)
  {
    (let tpl (expScTplVec env))
    (expScResolveTplFrom tpl name (expScChainVec env) (- (vecLen tpl) 1))
  })

(pub :: expScResolveTplFrom (-> ScopeBinds String Ints Int (Option ScopeBind)))
(pub fn (expScResolveTplFrom tpl name chain i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let binding (vecGet tpl i))
      (if (&& (strEq binding.name name) (== (expScPrefix binding.scopes chain) 1))
        (Some binding)
        (expScResolveTplFrom tpl name chain (- i 1)))
    }))

; Template and iteration binders share a push-order counter. An inner
; iteration shadows the template binder; a miss allows other lookup tiers.
(:: expTemplateWinner (-> (Option ScopeBind) (Option ForBind) (Option ScopeBind)))
(fn (expTemplateWinner template iteration)
  (if (let (Some binding) template)
    (if (let (Some element) iteration)
      (if (<= element.seq binding.seq)
        template
        None)
      template)
    None))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Parameter lookup. The LAST occurrence wins, matching the emit-time
; expander - but a duplicate parameter is now AX3020, so the rule is
; unobservable on any program that compiles.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expParamIndex (-> Names String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expParamIndex params name)
  (expParamIndexFrom params name (- (vecLen params) 1)))

(pub :: expParamIndexFrom (-> Names String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expParamIndexFrom params name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr params i) name)
      (Some i)
      (expParamIndexFrom params name (- i 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A BINDER POSITION HOLDING A MACRO PARAMETER.
;
; A template binder is renamed so it cannot capture the caller's names
; (the header's forward half). A binder that IS a macro parameter is
; the opposite case and needs the opposite treatment: the CALLER named
; it, so it must capture exactly what the caller wrote.
;
; Renaming one anyway is what made every binding form impossible:
;
;   (macro (bind! x e body) (let ((x e)) body))
;   (bind! v 41 (+ v 1))
;
; `x` was gensymed to `x.N` and `body` was spliced verbatim, so the
; output bound `x.N` and read `v` - `AX3001 undefined variable v`, on a
; macro whose whole job is to bind `v`. The bug hid behind the case
; that happens to work: when the template both binds and reads through
; the SAME parameter - `(macro (m x e) (let ((x e)) x))` - both of the
; template's `x`s resolve to the gensym and the expansion answers
; correctly, for the wrong reason, with the caller's
; chosen name appearing nowhere. So the defect was invisible to every
; macro that did not take a separate body.
;
; The rule is `substName`'s, which has been right about `set` targets
; all along: a parameter in a name position answers the ARGUMENT's
; name, and an argument that is not a name is refused rather than
; dropped. No scope record is pushed - a reference to the parameter
; elsewhere in the template already substitutes to the same identifier
; through the ordinary parameter path, so a record would be a second
; route to one answer.
;
; The reverse half still holds and is not this function's business: a
; template's FREE identifier that the caller's binder happens to shadow
; resolves at the definition site through `expQualify` and the def-scope
; stamp, so `(bind! helper 1 ...)` does not steal the template's own
; `helper`.
;
; docs/macro-system.md MAC-HYG-10. `tests/diagnostics/590-macro-binder-target.ax`
; pins the refusal; `tests/stdlib/371-err-module.ax` is the first
; program that depends on the rule and does not compile without it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expBinderParam (-> String Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expBinderParam name params args env isp)
  (match (expParamIndex params name)
    ((None)
      (as 0 String)
    )
    ((Some idx)
      {
        (let a (vecGet args idx))
        (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
          (nodeAName a)
          {
            (expEmit env
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3035" "macro-binder-target" isp
                "a macro parameter used as a binder was given something that is not a variable"
                None
                (Some
                  "pass a variable name for this parameter, or rewrite the template so the parameter is not a binder")
                "a `let`, `lambda` or pattern binder is a NAME, so a parameter standing in one can only be given a name - this is the same rule `set` targets follow (AX3022)"))
            name
          })
      }
    )))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Pattern binders.
;
; A match arm's pattern binds names, and a template's arm binders have
; to be renamed for the same reason a template's `let` binder does:
; `(macro (unwrapOr o d) (match o ((Some v) d) ((None) d)))` called
; with `d` = a caller's `v` used to answer the payload instead of the
; caller's value, because the arm's `v` captured it. Measured: 8
; against the 42 the caller wrote.
;
; The shapes a pattern arrives in: a bare TAG_E_VAR is a BINDER -
; stage0 binds a bare identifier in pattern position even when it
; names a constructor, which is why `(Nil)` needs TAG_P_CON0 to mean
; the constructor. A constructor pattern with arguments is an
; TAG_E_APP spine whose ultimate head is the constructor's name, so
; the spine's head is walked past rather than renamed.
; TAG_P_CONNAMED holds its sub-patterns in word c and its field NAMES
; in word b; the field names are the struct's, not binders.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expRenamePat (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expRenamePat p params args env isp)
  (if (nodeSame p nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag p))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        ; ...with three exceptions, because three bare names in
        ; pattern position are not binders. `true` and `false` are
        ; literal TESTS - `emitPattern` compares the scrutinee
        ; against 1 and 0 for them (codegen.ax) and
        ; `checkPatternTyped` binds nothing (typecheck.ax) - and
        ; renaming one to `true.1` turned the test into a binder,
        ; so the arm matched EVERYTHING and every later arm became
        ; unreachable. Measured before this line existed:
        ;
        ;   (macro (isTrue b) (match b ((true) 1) ((false) 0)))
        ;   (isTrue false)                    ; answered 1
        ;   (match false ((true) 1) ((false) 0))  ; answers 0
        ;
        ; The same match, written by hand and written by a macro,
        ; disagreeing - which is the one thing a hygiene mechanism
        ; exists to prevent. `_` is renamed harmlessly today (it is
        ; a binder nobody references) but is excluded with them,
        ; because the checker special-cases it too and a `_.1` in a
        ; scope is a difference with no reason to exist.
        ;
        ; Nothing else is exempt: a bare constructor name in
        ; pattern position IS a binder in this language - that is
        ; why `(Nil)` needs the parentheses - so renaming it is
        ; correct and is what the `(macro (m x) (match x ((Nil) 0)))`
        ; hygiene case depends on.
        (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName p) "true")
              (|| (strEq (nodeAName p) "false") (strEq (nodeAName p) "_")))
          (mkEVarAt (nodeAName p) isp)
          ; A pattern binder that is a macro PARAMETER takes the
          ; caller's name and is not renamed - the arm's body may
          ; be another parameter's syntax, which spells that name
          ; and must bind to it. This is what lets a macro bind a
          ; value under the name its caller chose.
          {
            (let pb (expBinderParam (nodeAName p) params args env isp))
            (if (!= (as pb Int) 0)
              (mkEVarAt pb isp)
              {
                (let new (expFresh env (nodeAName p)))
                {
                  (expScPushTpl env (nodeAName p) new)
                  (mkEVarAt new isp)
                }
              })
          })
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (expRenamePatSpine p params args env isp)
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (nodeBuildPConnamed (nodeAText p) (nodeBChildren p)
              (expRenamePatVec (nodeCChildren p) params args env isp) isp)
            p)))
    }))

; The head of a constructor spine is the constructor's NAME and must
; survive; every argument is a sub-pattern.
(pub :: expRenamePatSpine (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expRenamePatSpine p params args env isp)
  (if (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_APP)
    (mkEApp (expRenamePatSpine (nodeAChild p) params args env isp)
      (expRenamePat (nodeBChild p) params args env isp))
    p))

(pub :: expRenamePatVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expRenamePatVec ps params args env isp)
  (expRenamePatVecIn ps params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: expRenamePatVecIn
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expRenamePatVecIn ps params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    acc
    (expRenamePatVecIn ps params args env isp
      (vecPush acc (expRenamePat (vecGet ps i) params args env isp)) (+ i 1))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Substitution.
;
; The template with every parameter reference replaced by its
; argument's syntax tree, every binder the template introduces renamed
; to a fresh name, and every node it builds carrying the INVOCATION's
; span.
;
; The span rule is uniform and deliberate. A template node's own span
; indexes the file the macro was DEFINED in, and a `Diag` carries one
; unit, so a diagnostic anchored there would point at a real line of
; the wrong file - which reads as a correct diagnostic about
; unrelated code and is worse than pointing nowhere. Arguments keep
; their own spans, because those are call-site nodes and already
; index the right file. Naming the macro as well as the call site is
; roadmap section 4.2's criterion 3 and needs `Diag` to carry a unit
; per span; the header records why that is a separate change.
;
; Nodes are REBUILT rather than mutated. The emit-time expander
; returned the template's own nodes for every tag it did not know,
; which made one template node reachable from every call site - safe
; only for as long as nothing writes to a node, and renaming writes.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; ==================================================================
; The syntax/* query vocabulary (MAC-CAP-5/6), v1: syntax/join in
; declaration NAME position, syntax/constructors as an iteration
; sequence, syntax/for in match-ARM position. Every query is answered
; at INSTANTIATION time, from the declaration list the expander
; already holds - reading that table is not evaluation (MAC-CAP-6) -
; and every miss, misuse, or out-of-template occurrence is AX3028 at
; the offending node, never a default value. The vocabulary is CLOSED:
; a `syntax/`-prefixed head this file does not implement refuses by
; name, and the prefix is reserved in declaration names outright
; (`expCheckDeclNames`; corpus population was zero when the
; reservation landed, 2026-08-14).
; ==================================================================
; One ACTIVE iteration binding: the binder's spelling, the element it
; currently denotes, the sequence's identity key ("constructors
; Color"), the element's index, and the push-order stamp the innermost
; rule compares against a template binder's. The key and index exist
; for `syntax/same`.
(pub struct ForBind
  (name : String)
  (elem : String)
  (key : String)
  (idx : Int)
  (seq : Int))

; One template binder's scope-set record, and the only record a
; reference to it consults. `spelling` is the `name.N` the binder
; answers downstream, assigned from `fresh` at the push site, so a
; binder's output spelling is fixed by push order.
; `scopes` snapshots the chain at the push. `seq` is the
; push-order stamp from the same counter for-bindings draw from.
(pub type ForBinds (Vec ForBind))

(pub struct ScopeBind
  (name : String)
  (scopes : (Vec Int))
  (seq : Int)
  (spelling : String))

(pub type ScopeBinds (Vec ScopeBind))

; An identifier keeps its original binding across substitution. Scopes
; describe lexical visibility; key identifies the selected binding, or
; an unbound spelling. No source positions take part in the comparison.
(pub struct SyntaxIdentity
  (name : String)
  (scopes : (Vec Int))
  (key : String)
  (module : Int))

(pub :: expPushLocal (-> ScopeBinds MEnv String Int))
(pub fn (expPushLocal bs env name)
  {
    (let scopes
      (if (== (vecLen bs) 0)
        vecNew
        {
          (let previous (vecGet bs (- (vecLen bs) 1)))
          (expScChainCopyIn previous.scopes vecNew 0)
        }))
    (vecPush scopes (expScNextId env))
    (vecPush bs (ScopeBind :name name :scopes scopes :seq (expScNextSeq env) :spelling name))
    0
  })

(pub :: expLocalBinding (-> ScopeBinds String Int (Option ScopeBind)))
(pub fn (expLocalBinding bs name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let binding (vecGet bs i))
      (if (strEq binding.name name)
        (Some binding)
        (expLocalBinding bs name (- i 1)))
    }))

; Copy metadata as well as children. Argument syntax may carry field
; labels, integer widths and definition-site stamps already.
(pub :: expSyntaxRebuild (-> ASTNode AstPayload AstPayload AstPayload ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSyntaxRebuild e a b c)
  {
    (let n (nodeClone e))
    (nodeSetPayload n 1 a)
    (nodeSetPayload n 2 b)
    (nodeSetPayload n 3 c)
    n
  })

(pub :: expSyntaxKey (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int String))
(pub fn (expSyntaxKey e decls site)
  (if (!= (nodeSyntaxIdentity e) 0)
    (memGetWordStr (nodeSyntaxIdentity e) 2)
    (expCanonKey decls
      (if (== (nodeDefScope e) 1)
        (nodeModule e)
        site)
      (nodeAName e))))

(pub :: expCaptureVar (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expCaptureVar e env bs)
  {
    (let local
      (:: (if (== (nodeDefScope e) 1)
        None
        (expLocalBinding bs (nodeAName e) (- (vecLen bs) 1))) (Option ScopeBind)))
    (let prior (nodeSyntaxIdentity e))
    (let origin
      (if (== (nodeDefScope e) 1)
        (nodeModule e)
        (if (== prior 0)
          env.site
          (memGetWord prior 3))))
    (let key
      (if (let (Some binding) local)
        (concat "L:" (expFmtInt binding.seq))
        (if (&& (!= prior 0) (strStartsWith (memGetWordStr prior 2) "L:"))
          (memGetWordStr prior 2)
          (expCanonKey (expDeclsVec env) origin (nodeAName e)))))
    (let n (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstText (nodeAName e)) (AstWord 0) (AstWord 0)))
    (writeNodeWord n 12
      (as (SyntaxIdentity
            :name (nodeAName e)
            :scopes
              (if (let (Some binding) local)
                binding.scopes
                (if (== prior 0)
                  vecNew
                  (memGetWordVec prior 1)))
            :key key
            :module origin) Int))
    n
  })

; Snapshot syntax without invoking macros or running queries. Nested
; lexical forms get their own scopes, so a name inside an argument has
; the same binding whether a rule inspects it now or forwards it later.
(pub :: expCaptureSyntax (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expCaptureSyntax e env bs)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (expCaptureVar e env bs)
        (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
          {
            (let val (expCaptureSyntax (nodeBChild e) env bs))
            (let mark (vecLen bs))
            (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
            (let body (expCaptureSyntax (nodeCChild e) env bs))
            (let n
              (expSaveSyntaxBindings
                (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstText (nodeAName e)) (AstChild val) (AstChild body)) bs
                mark))
            (expTrunc bs mark)
            n
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
            {
              (let mark (vecLen bs))
              (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
              (let body (expCaptureSyntax (nodeBChild e) env bs))
              (let n
                (expSaveSyntaxBindings
                  (expSyntaxRebuild e
                    (if (== tag TAG_M_ARM)
                      (AstChild (nodeAChild e))
                      (AstNames (nodeANames e)))
                    (AstChild body) (AstWord 0))
                  bs mark))
              (expTrunc bs mark)
              n
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
              (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChildren (expCaptureVec (nodeAChildren e) env bs))
                (AstWord 0) (AstWord 0))
              (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeAChild e) env bs))
                  (AstChildren (expCaptureVec (nodeBChildren e) env bs)) (AstWord 0))
                (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                  (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeAChild e) env bs))
                    (AstChildren (expCaptureVec (nodeBChildren e) env bs)) (AstWord 0))
                  (if (== tag TAG_M_ARM)
                    {
                      (let mark (vecLen bs))
                      (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
                      (let body (expCaptureSyntax (nodeBChild e) env bs))
                      (let n
                        (expSaveSyntaxBindings
                          (expSyntaxRebuild e
                            (if (== tag TAG_M_ARM)
                              (AstChild (nodeAChild e))
                              (AstNames (nodeANames e)))
                            (AstChild body) (AstWord 0))
                          bs mark))
                      (expTrunc bs mark)
                      n
                    }
                    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_APP) (== tag TAG_E_IF))
                      (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeAChild e) env bs))
                        (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeBChild e) env bs))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
                          (AstWords (nodeCWords e))
                          (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeCChild e) env bs))))
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_SET) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstText (nodeAName e))
                          (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeBChild e) env bs)) (AstWord (nodeC e)))
                        (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== tag TAG_E_TRY))
                          (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeAChild e) env bs))
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                              (AstText (nodeBName e))
                              (AstWord (nodeB e)))
                            (AstWord (nodeC e)))
                          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_SETF) (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE))
                            (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeAChild e) env bs))
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                                (AstText (nodeBName e))
                                (AstChildren (nodeBChildren e)))
                              (AstChild (expCaptureSyntax (nodeCChild e) env bs)))
                            e)))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: expCaptureVec (-> Nodes MEnv ScopeBinds Nodes))
(pub fn (expCaptureVec xs env bs)
  {
    (let acc vecNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
      (vecPush acc (expCaptureSyntax (vecGet xs i) env bs)))
    acc
  })

; Reusing captured syntax reuses its own binders. Two substitutions of
; one lambda therefore name one parameter binding; two separately
; written lambdas introduce different bindings. Caller-supplied names
; can still bind free caller syntax when the output is walked.
; Captured binder nodes store an owning ScopeBinds vector in arena slot 12.
; Other syntax-node shapes use that slot for a SyntaxIdentity record.
(:: expSavedSyntaxBindings (-> ASTNode ScopeBinds))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (expSavedSyntaxBindings node)
  (as (nodeSyntaxIdentityWords node) ScopeBinds))

(pub :: expEnterSyntaxBindings (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds Int))
(pub fn (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
  (if (!= (nodeSyntaxIdentity e) 0)
    {
      (let saved (expSavedSyntaxBindings e))
      (let mut i 0)
      (while (< i (vecLen saved))
        {
          (vecPush bs (vecGet saved i))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      0
    }
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM)
      (expPushNames (nodeANames e) bs env 0)
      (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_M_ARM)
        (expPatBinders (nodeAChild e) bs env)
        (expPushLocal bs env (nodeAName e))))))

(pub :: expSaveSyntaxBindings (-> ASTNode ScopeBinds Int ASTNode))
; Slot 12 keeps the vector's owning header and element refmap. Its word
; accessor is the arena boundary; binder-node readers restore ScopeBinds.
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expSaveSyntaxBindings e bs mark)
  {
    (let saved vecNew)
    (for i in mark..(vecLen bs)
      (vecPush saved (vecGet bs i)))
    (writeNodeWords e 12 (as saved Ints))
    e
  })

(pub :: expForStackVec (-> MEnv ForBinds))
(pub fn (expForStackVec env)
  env.forstack)

(pub :: expForLookup (-> MEnv String (Option String)))
(pub fn (expForLookup env name)
  (if (let (Some binding) (expForBindOf env name))
    (Some binding.elem)
    None))

(pub :: expUpperFirst (-> String String))
(pub fn (expUpperFirst s)
  (if (== (strLen s) 0)
    s
    {
      (let b (strByte s 0))
      (if (&& (>= b 97) (<= b 122))
        {
          (let out (strDup s))
          {
            (memPutByte (strData out) 0 (- b 32))
            out
          }
        }
        s)
    }))

; `lens` + `Point` is `lensPoint`, `get` + `x` is `getX`: the second
; part's first letter is upcased, nothing else moves.
(pub :: expJoinName (-> String String String))
(pub fn (expJoinName a b)
  (concat a (expUpperFirst b)))

; The join marker the parser writes into a declaration's name slot:
; "syntax/join a b", space-joined, unambiguous because a space can
; never appear in a lexed identifier.
;
; NESTED joins are the same string in PREFIX form: `(syntax/join
; (syntax/join get S) f)` is "syntax/join syntax/join get S f", and
; prefix notation over a fixed arity needs no brackets to stay
; unambiguous - which is why the encoding did not have to change, only
; the reader. Splitting on the FIRST space was the reader that could
; not see it, and it is what limited a generated name to two parts.
(pub :: expIsJoinMarker (-> String Int))
(pub fn (expIsJoinMarker s)
  (if (strStartsWith s "syntax/join ")
    1
    0))

(pub :: expMarkerSpaceAt (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expMarkerSpaceAt s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    (strLen s)
    (if (== (strByte s i) 32)
      i
      (expMarkerSpaceAt s (+ i 1)))))

; Does a nested marker head start at `i`?
(pub :: expMarkerJoinAt (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expMarkerJoinAt s i)
  (if (> (+ i 12) (strLen s))
    0
    (if (strEq (strSlice s i 12) "syntax/join ")
      1
      0)))

; One operand of the marker, as the index just past it: a nested join
; consumes its own two operands, a plain identifier runs to the next
; space. This is the whole of the prefix parse.
(pub :: expMarkerOperandEnd (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expMarkerOperandEnd s from)
  (if (== (expMarkerJoinAt s from) 1)
    (expMarkerOperandEnd s (+ (expMarkerOperandEnd s (+ from 12)) 1))
    (expMarkerSpaceAt s from)))

; Part 0 or part 1 of the marker's payload (the two joined names, each
; possibly a marker of its own - `expSubstName` recurses on whatever
; comes back, so depth costs nothing here).
(pub :: expMarkerPart (-> String Int String))
(pub fn (expMarkerPart s which)
  {
    (let from 12)
    {
      (let sp (expMarkerOperandEnd s from))
      (if (== which 0)
        (strSlice s from (- sp from))
        (strSlice s (+ sp 1) (- (strLen s) (+ sp 1))))
    }
  })

; A query naming a declaration that doesn't exist YET. Phase D expands
; declaration-position invocations in rounds, in list order, so a
; `(deriveEq Mode)` written above the `(defFlag Mode)` that generates
; `Mode` asked before the answer existed and was refused with AX3028,
; though MAC-LANG-3a says macros are order-independent (AN-40,
; `tests/selfhost/1008-macro-query-order.ax`). While a round may still
; generate declarations (`defer`, word 29), the query records the miss
; in `deferHits` (word 30) and answers nothing; `expInstantiateDecl`
; then throws that instantiation away and the invocation is tried in
; the next round. Only when a round generates nothing is the last round
; run with `defer` off, so a name nothing declares is still this
; refusal, with the same words.
(pub :: expQueryMissing (-> MEnv Int String String a Int))
(pub fn (expQueryMissing env sp msg help m)
  (if (== env.defer 1)
    {
      (set env.deferHits 1)
      0
    }
    (expQueryErr env sp msg help m)))

; The family's one diagnostic. `m` is the module whose file the span
; indexes (0 = the entry file), resolved to a unit exactly as the
; other expander diagnostics resolve theirs.
(pub :: expQueryErr (-> MEnv Int String String a Int))
(pub fn (expQueryErr env sp msg help m)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3028" "syntax-query" sp msg None (Some help)
          "the query vocabulary is CLOSED and compiler-implemented (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5/6): every query is answered from the declaration list at expansion time, and a query with no answer is this refusal, never a default value")
        m))
    0
  })

; Resolve a query-argument identifier: an active for-binding first
; (innermost wins), then a macro parameter - whose argument must be a
; bare identifier, the same rule a name position enforces - then the
; identifier itself.
(pub :: expResolveQueryName (-> MEnv String Names Nodes Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expResolveQueryName env s params args isp)
  {
    (let foundFor (expForLookup env s))
    (if (let (Some fe) foundFor)
      (Some fe)
      (match (expParamIndex params s)
        ((None) (Some s))
        ((Some idx)
          {
            (let a (vecGet args idx))
            (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
              (Some (nodeAName a))
              {
                (expQueryErr env isp
                  (concat (concat "a syntax/* query's argument must be a bare identifier; `" s)
                    "` was given an expression")
                  "pass a plain name in this argument position" (expMacMod env))
                None
              })
          }
        )))
  })

; The same resolution from a query argument's NODE rather than its
; spelling, which is what lets one query's answer feed another's
; argument: `(syntax/defined (syntax/join show T))` asks about the
; name `(deriveShow T)` would generate, and `syntax/join` is the only
; query whose answer is a single identifier, so it is the only one
; that can stand here. A bare identifier takes the path above
; unchanged; the join form nests, because each side resolves through
; this same function.
;
; A missing name records its refusal before returning None. Nested joins
; resolve left to right and stop at the first refusal.
(pub :: expQueryArgName (-> MEnv ASTNode Names Nodes Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expQueryArgName env a params args isp)
  (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
    (expResolveQueryName env (nodeAName a) params args isp)
    (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_APP)
      {
        (let h (spineHead a))
        (let ps (spineArgVec a vecNew))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
              (&& (strEq (nodeAName h) "syntax/join") (== (vecLen ps) 2)))
          {
            (let l (expQueryArgName env (vecGet ps 0) params args isp))
            (if (let (Some l) l)
              {
                (let r (expQueryArgName env (vecGet ps 1) params args isp))
                (if (let (Some r) r)
                  (Some (expJoinName l r))
                  None)
              }
              None)
          }
          {
            (expQueryErr env
              (if (!= (nodeSpan a) 0)
                (nodeSpan a)
                isp)
              "a syntax/* query's argument must be a bare identifier or a `(syntax/join a b)`"
              "pass a plain name here, or join two of them with `(syntax/join a b)`"
              (expMacMod env))
            None
          })
      }
      {
        (expQueryErr env
          (if (!= (nodeSpan a) 0)
            (nodeSpan a)
            isp)
          "a syntax/* query's argument must be a bare identifier or a `(syntax/join a b)`"
          "pass a plain name here, or join two of them with `(syntax/join a b)`" (expMacMod env))
        None
      })))

; `(syntax/fields S)`: the field names of struct S, in declaration
; order - any visible module's, the same rule as expConstructorsOf.
(pub struct ExpQuerySequence
  (key : String)
  (values : Names))

(pub :: expFieldsOf (-> MEnv String Int (Option ExpQuerySequence)))
(pub fn (expFieldsOf env s isp)
  {
    (let d (expFindDeclNamed env s 1))
    (if (let (Some d) d)
      (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT)
        {
          (let names vecNew)
          (let fs (nodeBChildren d))
          {
            (for i in 0..(vecLen fs)
              (vecPush names (nodeAName (vecGet fs i))))
            (Some (ExpQuerySequence :key (concat "fields " s) :values names))
          }
        }
        {
          (expQueryErr env isp
            (concat (concat "`" s) "` is not a `struct` declaration, so it has no fields")
            "syntax/fields answers structs; a data type's query is syntax/constructors"
            (expMacMod env))
          None
        })
      {
        (let any (expFindDeclNamed env s 0))
        (if (let (Some any) any)
          {
            (expQueryErr env isp
              (concat (concat (concat "`" s) "` is private to module `")
                (concat (nodeModuleName any) "`"))
              "a query answers what an entry-file reference could name; mark the struct `pub`, or derive by hand inside its module"
              (expMacMod env))
            None
          }
          {
            (expQueryMissing env isp
              (concat (concat "no declaration named `" s) "` for syntax/fields to answer")
              "the query reads the declaration list; check the spelling, and declare (or import) the struct before deriving over it"
              (expMacMod env))
            None
          })
      })
  })

; `(syntax/constructors T)`: the constructor names of entry-file
; `data` type T, in declaration order. Two passes over the
; declaration list - entry-only for the ANSWER, any-module for the
; refusal's wording - mirroring the declaration-macro v1 limit.
(pub :: expConstructorsOf (-> MEnv String Int (Option ExpQuerySequence)))
(pub fn (expConstructorsOf env t isp)
  {
    (let d (expFindDeclNamed env t 1))
    (if (let (Some d) d)
      (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA)
        {
          (let names vecNew)
          (let cs (nodeBChildren d))
          {
            (for i in 0..(vecLen cs)
              (vecPush names (nodeAName (vecGet cs i))))
            (Some (ExpQuerySequence :key (concat "constructors " t) :values names))
          }
        }
        {
          (expQueryErr env isp
            (concat (concat "`" t) "` is not a `data` declaration, so it has no constructors")
            "syntax/constructors answers `data` types; a struct's query is syntax/fields"
            (expMacMod env))
          None
        })
      {
        (let any (expFindDeclNamed env t 0))
        (if (let (Some any) any)
          {
            (expQueryErr env isp
              (concat (concat (concat "`" t) "` is private to module `")
                (concat (nodeModuleName any) "`"))
              "a query answers what an entry-file reference could name; mark the type `pub`, or derive by hand inside its module"
              (expMacMod env))
            None
          }
          {
            (expQueryMissing env isp
              (concat (concat "no declaration named `" t) "` for syntax/constructors to answer")
              "the query reads the declaration list; check the spelling, and declare (or import) the type before deriving over it"
              (expMacMod env))
            None
          })
      })
  })

; Type-namespace lookup for a query subject. visibleOnly=1 answers
; what a reference AT THE INVOCATION SITE could name - the same
; visibility rule the checker's own `tyFind` applies, enforced
; here so a private type refuses loudly AT THE INVOCATION instead of
; scattering checker refusals over the generated code. visibleOnly=0
; finds anything, for the refusal's wording.
;
; The site, not the entry file: a module deriving over its OWN type
; (MAC-EXP-16's module-side invocation) names a type its neighbours
; can see whatever anyone else may. Reading the rule as "entry-file
; visible" made a `pub data` refuse inside the module that declared
; it, the moment an importer's name list did not mention it - the
; filter is a fact about the importer, and the query is asked on the
; other side of it.
(pub :: expFindDeclNamed (-> MEnv String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindDeclNamed env t visibleOnly)
  (expFindDeclNamedFrom (expDeclsVec env) t visibleOnly
    (expModuleWordOpt
      (if (== (expQSite env) 0)
        env.site
        (expQSite env)))
    0))

(pub :: expFindDeclNamedFrom (-> Nodes String Int (Option String) Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindDeclNamedFrom decls t visibleOnly site i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT))
            (&& (strEq (nodeAName d) t)
              (|| (== visibleOnly 0)
                (|| (== (expModEq (nodeModuleOpt d) None) 1)
                  (|| (== (expModEq (nodeModuleOpt d) site) 1) (== (nodeVis d) 1))))))
        (Some d)
        (expFindDeclNamedFrom decls t visibleOnly site (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The arity of entry-file constructor C - the field count its
; declaration carries - or None with the refusal emitted. Same two-pass
; shape as the type lookups. `who` names the query in the miss
; message, because two of them count the same thing for different
; reasons and a diagnostic that always says `syntax/binders` sends a
; reader of `(syntax/arity Ghost)` to the wrong row of the table.
(pub :: expCtorArity (-> MEnv String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expCtorArity env c isp)
  (expCtorArityFor env c isp "syntax/binders" "count"))

; English's possessive, because the two queries that share this
; message end differently: `syntax/binders'` and `syntax/arity's`.
; The golden that pinned the first spelling is what caught the naive
; concatenation - "syntax/binders's" - which is the kind of wording
; regression a re-bless would otherwise have absorbed silently.
(pub :: expPossessive (-> String String))
(pub fn (expPossessive w)
  (if (== (strByte w (- (strLen w) 1)) 115)
    (concat w "'")
    (concat w "'s")))

(pub :: expCtorArityFor (-> MEnv String Int String String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expCtorArityFor env c isp who verb)
  {
    (let k (expFindCtorNamed env c 1))
    (if (let (Some k) k)
      (Some (nodeB k))
      (if (let (Some _) (expFindCtorNamed env c 0))
        {
          (expQueryErr env isp
            (concat (concat "`" c) "` is a constructor of a type its module keeps private")
            "a query answers what an entry-file reference could name; mark the type `pub`, or derive by hand inside its module"
            (expMacMod env))
          None
        }
        {
          (expQueryErr env isp
            (concat (concat (concat "no constructor named `" c) (concat "` for " who))
              (concat " to " verb))
            (concat (concat "the query reads the declaration list; " (expPossessive who))
              " first argument names a data constructor")
            (expMacMod env))
          None
        }))
  })

(pub :: expFindCtorNamed (-> MEnv String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindCtorNamed env c visibleOnly)
  (expFindCtorFrom (expDeclsVec env) c visibleOnly 0))

(pub :: expFindCtorFrom (-> Nodes String Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindCtorFrom decls c visibleOnly i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA)
            (|| (== visibleOnly 0)
              (|| (== (expModEq (nodeModuleOpt d) None) 1) (== (nodeVis d) 1))))
        {
          (let k (expFindCtorIn (nodeBChildren d) c 0))
          (if (let (Some k) k)
            (Some k)
            (expFindCtorFrom decls c visibleOnly (+ i 1)))
        }
        (expFindCtorFrom decls c visibleOnly (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: expFindCtorIn (-> Nodes String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expFindCtorIn cs c i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    None
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet cs i)) c)
      (Some (vecGet cs i))
      (expFindCtorIn cs c (+ i 1)))))

; `(syntax/binders C p)`: arity-of-C fresh identifiers derived from
; prefix p - spelled `p#i`, which no user identifier can carry
; (`#` does not lex in names). The spelling is DETERMINISTIC, which
; is what "the same sequence at every mention within one expansion"
; requires: the pattern mention introduces `p#i` as binders (renamed
; by MAC-HYG-2 like any template binder), and the fold's mention
; resolves to the same spellings, which scope resolution then answers
; with the renamed binders - hygiene falls out of the existing order.
(pub :: expBindersOf (-> MEnv String String Int (Option ExpQuerySequence)))
(pub fn (expBindersOf env c p isp)
  (if (let (Some n) (expCtorArity env c isp))
    {
      (let names vecNew)
      (let mut i 0)
      {
        (while (< i n)
          {
            (vecPush names (concat (concat p "#") (fmtInt i)))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        (Some (ExpQuerySequence :key (concat (concat (concat "binders " c) " ") p) :values names))
      }
    }
    None))

; Evaluate an iteration SEQUENCE expression: `(syntax/constructors
; T)`, `(syntax/fields S)`, or `(syntax/binders C p)`. The answer is
; an owning identity key and name vector, or None with the refusal
; already recorded. A present sequence may have no names.
(pub :: expEvalSeq (-> MEnv ASTNode Names Nodes Int (Option ExpQuerySequence)))
(pub fn (expEvalSeq env seq params args isp)
  {
    (let head (spineHead seq))
    (let qargs (spineArgVec seq vecNew))
    (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
      {
        (expQueryErr env
          (if (!= (nodeSpan seq) 0)
            (nodeSpan seq)
            isp)
          "syntax/for's sequence must be a query spine"
          "write the sequence as `(syntax/constructors T)`, `(syntax/fields S)`, or `(syntax/binders C p)`"
          (expMacMod env))
        None
      }
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/constructors")
            (&& (== (vecLen qargs) 1) (== (nodeTag (vecGet qargs 0)) TAG_E_VAR)))
        {
          (let t (expResolveQueryName env (nodeAName (vecGet qargs 0)) params args isp))
          (if (let (Some t) t)
            (expConstructorsOf env t isp)
            None)
        }
        (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/fields")
              (&& (== (vecLen qargs) 1) (== (nodeTag (vecGet qargs 0)) TAG_E_VAR)))
          {
            (let s (expResolveQueryName env (nodeAName (vecGet qargs 0)) params args isp))
            (if (let (Some s) s)
              (expFieldsOf env s isp)
              None)
          }
          (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/binders")
                (&& (== (vecLen qargs) 2)
                  (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet qargs 0)) TAG_E_VAR)
                    (== (nodeTag (vecGet qargs 1)) TAG_E_VAR))))
            {
              (let c (expResolveQueryName env (nodeAName (vecGet qargs 0)) params args isp))
              (if (let (Some c) c)
                {
                  (let p (expResolveQueryName env (nodeAName (vecGet qargs 1)) params args isp))
                  (if (let (Some p) p)
                    (expBindersOf env c p isp)
                    None)
                }
                None)
            }
            {
              (expQueryErr env
                (if (!= (nodeSpan seq) 0)
                  (nodeSpan seq)
                  isp)
                (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName head))
                  "` is not an iteration sequence this vocabulary answers, or its arguments are not bare identifiers")
                "sequences are `(syntax/constructors T)`, `(syntax/fields S)`, and `(syntax/binders C p)`; several of them zip in one iteration as `((x xs) (y ys))`"
                (expMacMod env))
              None
            }))))
  })

; For-variable substitution on the PATTERN side, run before the
; binder renaming: a for-variable substitutes in CONSTRUCTOR
; positions - a parenthesised nullary test, a named-field pattern's
; head, a fieldful spine's head - and a `(syntax/binders C p)`
; standing as a pattern ARGUMENT splices its arity-many `p#i` names
; as separate binder variables, which the renamer then renames like
; any template binder. A bare variable spelled like the for-variable
; stays what a bare variable in pattern position is, a binder, which
; then shadows the iteration variable in its arm - the same rule a
; template binder follows.
(pub :: expSubstPatFor (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstPatFor p params args env isp)
  ; No empty-stack fast path: `(syntax/binders C x)` is legal with a
  ; LITERAL constructor name and no enclosing syntax/for at all, so
  ; the walk must run for every template arm. (Only instantiation
  ; reaches this function - substArmsIn has no other caller.)
  (if (nodeSame p nilNode)
    p
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag p))
      (if (== tag TAG_P_CON0)
        (if (let (Some element) (expForLookup env (nodeAName p)))
          (nodeBuildPCon0 element 0 0 isp)
          p)
        (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
          (nodeBuildPConnamed
            (if (let (Some element) (expForLookup env (nodeAName p)))
              element
              (nodeAName p))
            (nodeBChildren p) (expSubstPatForVec (nodeCChildren p) params args env isp) isp)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
            (expSubstPatForSpine p params args env isp)
            p)))
    }))

(pub :: expSubstPatForSpine (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstPatForSpine p params args env isp)
  {
    (let head (spineHead p))
    (let pargs (spineArgVec p vecNew))
    {
      (let newHead
        (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (let (Some element) (expForLookup env (nodeAName head)))
            (mkEVarAt element isp)
            head)
          head))
      (let out vecNew)
      {
        (for a in pargs
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_APP)
                {
                  (let ah (spineHead a))
                  (&& (== (nodeTag ah) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName ah) "syntax/binders"))
                })
            {
              (let sr (expEvalSeq env a params args isp))
              (if (let (Some sr) sr)
                (expPushBinderVars out sr.values isp 0)
                (vecPush out (mkEVarAt "_" isp)))
            }
            (vecPush out (expSubstPatFor a params args env isp))))
        ; AN EMPTY BINDER SPLICE IS A NULLARY CONSTRUCTOR, NOT A
        ; BINDER. `spineArgVec` of an application never answers
        ; nothing, so `out` is empty only when the one argument was a
        ; `(syntax/binders C f)` that supplied no binders - the
        ; arity-0 case that lets one template cover every arity.
        ; Rebuilt as a spine it collapsed back to the bare head, and a
        ; bare identifier in pattern position BINDS (`expRenamePat`'s
        ; header says so): the arm matched every value instead of one
        ; constructor.
        ;
        ; It answered wrongly and silently. `(deriveShow Colour)` over
        ; an all-nullary `data` generated `(match v (Red "Red")
        ; (Green "Green") (Blue "Blue"))`, whose first arm swallows
        ; everything - `showColour (Green)` answered "Red", at exit 0.
        ; `tests/selfhost/380-syntax-scalar-queries.ax` could not see
        ; it: the only type it derives `show` for is `Shape`, whose
        ; nullary `Dot` is the LAST arm, where a wildcard and the
        ; right answer are indistinguishable.
        (if (&& (== (vecLen out) 0) (== (nodeTag newHead) TAG_E_VAR))
          (nodeBuildPCon0 (nodeAText newHead) 0 0 isp)
          (expRebuildSpine newHead out isp 0))
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: expPushBinderVars (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expPushBinderVars out names isp i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      (vecPush out (mkEVarAt (vecGetStr names i) isp))
      (expPushBinderVars out names isp (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expSubstPatForVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSubstPatForVec ps params args env isp)
  {
    (let acc vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen ps)
        (vecPush acc (expSubstPatFor (vecGet ps i) params args env isp)))
      acc
    }
  })

; The FULL binding record for an active for-variable - `syntax/same`
; needs the sequence key and the element index, not just the element.
(pub :: expForBindOf (-> MEnv String (Option ForBind)))
(pub fn (expForBindOf env name)
  {
    (let bindings (expForStackVec env))
    (expForBindOfFrom bindings name (- (vecLen bindings) 1))
  })

(pub :: expForBindOfFrom (-> ForBinds String Int (Option ForBind)))
(pub fn (expForBindOfFrom bindings name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let binding (vecGet bindings i))
      (if (strEq binding.name name)
        (Some binding)
        (expForBindOfFrom bindings name (- i 1)))
    }))

(:: expSameMissing (-> MEnv ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (expSameMissing env argument isp)
  {
    (expQueryErr env isp
      (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName argument)) "` is not bound by an enclosing syntax/for")
      "syntax/same compares iteration variables; bind both with syntax/for first" (expMacMod env))
    (mkEInt 0)
  })

; `(syntax/same f g)`: whether two iteration variables currently
; denote the same element - the same declaration SLOT, so both sides
; must range over the same sequence (equal keys) and the answer is
; index equality. Comparing across different sequences is refused
; rather than answered false: a false from mismatched sequences is a
; wrong-answer generator, and spelling alone never suffices
; (MAC-CAP-5's own rule). Answers the node to splice - `true`,
; `false`, or a refusal's poison 0-literal.
(pub :: expEvalSame (-> MEnv Nodes Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalSame env qargs isp)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 2)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/same compares exactly two iteration variables"
        "write `(syntax/same f g)` with both names bound by an enclosing syntax/for"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let an (vecGet qargs 0))
      (let bn (vecGet qargs 1))
      (if (|| (!= (nodeTag an) TAG_E_VAR) (!= (nodeTag bn) TAG_E_VAR))
        {
          (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/same's arguments must be bare iteration variables"
            "write `(syntax/same f g)` with both names bound by an enclosing syntax/for"
            (expMacMod env))
          (mkEInt 0)
        }
        (if (let (Some ba) (expForBindOf env (nodeAName an)))
          (if (let (Some bb) (expForBindOf env (nodeAName bn)))
            (if (strEq ba.key bb.key)
              (if (== ba.idx bb.idx)
                (mkEVarAt "true" isp)
                (mkEVarAt "false" isp))
              {
                (expQueryErr env isp
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName an)) "` ranges over `")
                      (concat (concat ba.key "` and `") (nodeAName bn)))
                    (concat (concat "` over `" bb.key) "`"))
                  "syntax/same compares positions within ONE sequence; iterate both variables over the same query"
                  (expMacMod env))
                (mkEInt 0)
              })
            (expSameMissing env bn isp))
          (expSameMissing env an isp)))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The three SCALAR queries (MAC-CAP-5's `syntax/name`, `syntax/arity`
; and `syntax/defined`), which answer one node where one value is
; needed - the position `syntax/constructors` and `syntax/fields`
; refuse in, because a sequence has no room there.
;
; Each landed with the macro that needs it, which is MAC-CAP-6's
; closure rule read literally: `stdlib/Pre.ax`'s `deriveShow` spends
; `syntax/name`, its `deriveArity` spends `syntax/arity`, and
; `showOr` spends `syntax/defined`. A query with no consumer is a
; vocabulary entry nothing measures.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `(syntax/name x)`: the identifier `x` as a STRING LITERAL. The
; resolution is the argument resolver's, so the useful spelling is
; the one inside an iteration - `(syntax/name C)` under
; `(syntax/for (C (syntax/constructors T)) ...)` answers each
; constructor's own name, which is what a generated `show` prints.
; The answer carries the invocation's span like every other node
; substitution builds (MAC-EXP-14).
(pub :: expEvalName (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalName env qargs params args isp)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 1)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/name takes exactly one identifier" "write `(syntax/name x)`"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let n (expQueryArgName env (vecGet qargs 0) params args isp))
      (if (let (Some n) n)
        ; A TAG_E_STR node carries the LEXEME, quotes and escapes
        ; included - `internString` stores it verbatim and the
        ; emitter unescapes on the way out - so a synthesised
        ; literal has to be spelled the way the lexer would have
        ; spelled it. Without the quotes the emitter strips the
        ; first and last BYTES of the name itself: `Circle`
        ; printed as `ircl`, at exit 0, which is the wrong-answer
        ; class this repository's fixtures assert values to catch.
        ; An identifier needs no escaping - `"` and `\` cannot lex
        ; in one - so quoting is the whole conversion.
        (nodeBuildEStr (concat (concat "\"" n) "\"") 0 0 isp)
        (mkEInt 0))
    }))

; `(syntax/arity C)`: the field count of constructor `C`, as an
; integer literal. Reads the same declaration slot `syntax/binders`
; counts, and refuses identically on a missing or private one - the
; two queries differ in what they DO with the number, not in what
; they can see.
(pub :: expEvalArity (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalArity env qargs params args isp)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 1)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/arity takes exactly one constructor name"
        "write `(syntax/arity C)`" (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let c (expQueryArgName env (vecGet qargs 0) params args isp))
      (if (let (Some c) c)
        {
          (let n (expCtorArityFor env c isp "syntax/arity" "measure"))
          (if (let (Some n) n)
            (nodeBuildEInt n 0 0 isp)
            (mkEInt 0))
        }
        (mkEInt 0))
    }))

; Does `n` name a visible top-level declaration - a function or its
; signature, a `data` or `struct` type, or one of that type's
; constructors? Those are exactly the names a generated body could
; REFER to, which is the question a conditional derivation is
; actually asking. A module's declaration answers under its mangled
; spelling, so the lookup goes through `expQualify` - the same
; definition-site rule a template's free identifier follows
; (MAC-HYG-6), and the reason `(syntax/defined println)` is true
; inside a macro whose file imports `IO` and false in one that does
; not.
(pub :: expIsDefinedName (-> MEnv String Int))
(pub fn (expIsDefinedName env n)
  (if (== (expDeclNamed (expDeclsVec env) n 0) 1)
      1
    (let (Some _) (expFindDeclNamed env n 1))
      1
    (let (Some _) (expFindCtorNamed env n 1))
      1
    {
      (let q (expQualify env n))
      (if (strEq q n)
        0
        1)
    }))

; `(syntax/defined n)`: `true` or `false`, decided here and spliced.
; A `syntax/defined` standing as an `if` condition is folded at
; expansion time exactly as `syntax/same` is (substTpl's TAG_E_IF
; arm), so the branch that survives is the only one the checker ever
; sees - which is what makes conditional derivation *conditional*
; rather than "both branches must type".
(pub :: expEvalDefined (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalDefined env qargs params args isp)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 1)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/defined takes exactly one name"
        "write `(syntax/defined n)`, or `(syntax/defined (syntax/join f T))` for a generated one"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let n (expQueryArgName env (vecGet qargs 0) params args isp))
      (if (let (Some n) n)
        (if (== (expIsDefinedName env n) 1)
          (mkEVarAt "true" isp)
          (mkEVarAt "false" isp))
        (mkEInt 0))
    }))

; ==================================================================
; syntax/format — the compile-time format-string parser
; ==================================================================
;
; `(syntax/format e)` is the one query that reads a LITERAL rather
; than the declaration list, and it is what makes `(println "x =
; {x}")` a rewrite instead of a runtime parse. It is the whole of the
; compiler's involvement in formatting: the printing macros in
; `stdlib/IO.ax` and `format` in `stdlib/Fmt.ax` are four lines each
; on top of it (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-10).
;
; WHY THIS IS A COMPILER PRIMITIVE AND NOT A MACRO. Tier 1 is a
; rewrite system over syntax NODES. A format string arrives as one
; node - a `TAG_E_STR` whose payload is an opaque lexeme - and the
; query vocabulary has no way to take it apart: there is no
; `syntax/`-anything that indexes a string, and adding one would be
; a string-processing language inside the template language. The
; decomposition therefore happens here, in compiler code, over a
; literal the compiler already holds. That is NOT tier 2: no user
; code runs, the input is a literal rather than a program, and the
; output is a tree built by this function. The threat-model line the
; roadmap draws (§4.2) is "the compiler must never execute arbitrary
; code from source files", and parsing a string the compiler lexed
; is on the safe side of it, exactly as `syntax/constructors`
; reading a `data` declaration is.
;
; WHAT IT ANSWERS. Two shapes, decided by what the argument IS:
;
;   a string literal   parse it, and answer the concatenation of its
;                      literal runs with a rendering call per hole
;   anything else      answer `(format# e)` - one call, dispatched
;                      on the argument's STATIC type
;
; The second case is why taking the name `println` for a macro cost
; no call site: every `(println expr)` written before this existed
; still means what it meant, because the rendering of a `String` is
; the identity - the call is elided outright. It is also `format`'s
; whole definition - `(format x)` is "render x", which is what
; `format!("{}", x)` spells in Rust.
;
; `syntax/formatln` is the same thing with a newline folded into the
; last literal run at expansion time, so `(println "hi")` emits one
; `write` of `"hi\n"`: one syscall and no allocation, against the two
; writes the `println` FUNCTION did.
;
; THE HOLE GRAMMAR, and why it is this one:
;
;   {name}                    render `name` by its static type
;   {name:SPEC}               render it the way SPEC says
;   {{  }}                    a literal brace
;
;   SPEC := [align] ['0'] [width] ['.' precision] [type]
;   align     := '<' | '^' | '>'          (default '>')
;   type      := 'x' | 'X'                (hexadecimal)
;
; A specifier is not a formatting MINI-LANGUAGE interpreted at run
; time - there is no run time here. Each part chooses a different
; `Fmt` FUNCTION, and the choice is made once, during expansion:
;
;   {n}        (format# n)
;   {n:x}      (fmtHex n)
;   {x:.2}     (fmtFloatPrec x 2)
;   {n:>8}     (fmtPadLeft (format# n) 8)
;   {s:<12}    (fmtPadRight (format# s) 12)
;   {s:^12}    (fmtPadCenter (format# s) 12)
;   {n:04}     (fmtPadZerosLeft (format# n) 4)
;   {x:>10.2}  (fmtPadLeft (fmtFloatPrec x 2) 10)
;
; This is what replaces a print function per type. `printInt` existed
; because rendering an `Int` was a different CALL from rendering a
; `String`; the rendering call is now chosen by the specifier and the
; argument's static type, so there is one `println` and no `printInt`.
;
; It is also where "validated at compile time" stops being a slogan.
; The parser rejects a malformed specifier (AX3031), and the CHECKER
; rejects a well-formed one applied to the wrong type, because the
; function the specifier chose has a type: `{s:.2}` on a `String`
; reaches `fmtFloatPrec`'s `Float` parameter and is `AX3004` at the
; invocation. No specifier can be "ignored at run time", because none
; of them survives to run time.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. There is no argument list: `{name}` names a
; binding at the INVOCATION, and a macro that took N arguments would
; need repetition patterns (MAC-LANG-15..18), which do not exist.
; Capture is not a workaround for their absence - it is the form the
; language's own examples are written in - but the absence is why
; there is no positional `{}`, and `{}` is refused by name rather
; than left to fail as an empty identifier.
;
; TYPES ARE NOT CONSULTED HERE, and cannot be (MAC-CAP-7). A hole
; becomes a CALL to the reserved head `format#`, and which function
; that head is rewritten to is the checker's business, decided after
; expansion from the argument's static type. A hole naming a type
; with no rendering is `AX3025` at the invocation, with the expansion
; backtrace pointing at the macro - which is the correct place for
; it, and is why this needs no diagnostic of its own for the case.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A valid parsed specifier. Alignment and zero-padding flags retain
; their compact byte storage; absent precision and radix use `None`.
(pub struct FmtSpec
  (align : UInt8)
  (zero : UInt8)
  (width : Int)
  (prec : (Option Int))
  (ty : (Option UInt8)))

(:: fmtSpecTypeAllowed (-> (Option UInt8) Bool))
(fn (fmtSpecTypeAllowed ty)
  (if (let (Some radix) ty)
    (|| (== radix (as 88 UInt8)) (== radix (as 120 UInt8)))
    true))

(:: fmtSpecPrecisionAllowed (-> (Option Int) Bool))
(fn (fmtSpecPrecisionAllowed precision)
  (if (let (Some count) precision)
    (>= count 0)
    true))

(pub :: mkFmtSpec (-> Int Int Int (Option Int) (Option UInt8) FmtSpec))
;@axiom:pre((&& (|| (== align 60) (|| (== align 62) (== align 94))) (&& (&& (>= zero 0) (<= zero 1)) (&& (fmtSpecPrecisionAllowed prec) (fmtSpecTypeAllowed ty)))))
(pub fn (mkFmtSpec align zero width prec ty)
  (FmtSpec :align (as align UInt8) :zero (as zero UInt8) :width width :prec prec :ty ty))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: fmtSpecAlign (-> FmtSpec Int))
(pub fn (fmtSpecAlign spec)
  (as spec.align Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: fmtSpecZero (-> FmtSpec Int))
(pub fn (fmtSpecZero spec)
  (as spec.zero Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: fmtSpecWidth (-> FmtSpec Int))
(pub fn (fmtSpecWidth spec)
  spec.width)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: fmtSpecPrec (-> FmtSpec (Option Int)))
(pub fn (fmtSpecPrec spec)
  spec.prec)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: fmtSpecType (-> FmtSpec (Option UInt8)))
(pub fn (fmtSpecType spec)
  spec.ty)

; The generated call `(f x)`, with `f` resolved the way a template's
; free identifier resolves - at the macro's DEFINITION site
; (MAC-HYG-6). `concat`, `fmtInt` and the `fmtPad*` family are the
; names this query invents, and none of them is written in any
; template, so they cannot go through substTpl's path and have to take
; the same rule explicitly.
;
; THIS COMMENT HAS BEEN WRONG ABOUT CAPTURE TWICE, in opposite
; directions, so what follows is a measurement each time.
;
; It first said a capture would be "loud, a type error at the
; invocation". It is not: a well-typed hijack is silent and answers
; wrongly, and the two programs that showed it were
;
;   (:: show (-> a String)) (fn (show x) "HIJACKED")
;   ... (println "n={n}")            printed  n=HIJACKED
;
;   (:: concat (-> String String String))
;   (fn (concat a b) "HIJACKED")
;   ... (println "n={n}")            printed  HIJACKED
;
; both at exit 0. It then said, from that measurement, that an entry
; file defining `show` OR `concat` captures - and kept saying so
; after `expQualify` grew MAC-HYG-8's fourth rule, which is what
; retired the second half. That rule rewrites a free identifier to
; `Mod$name` when exactly ONE module in the merged declaration list
; declares it; `Str` is the only module declaring `concat`, and the
; entry file's own declaration is not a module's, so the lowering
; emits `Str$concat` and the hijack is not reached. Re-measured on
; the 0.7.3 binary, which predates this file's 0.7.4 changes: the
; second program above prints `n=42`, and `symbols --calls` on `main`
; reads `#calls=IO$writeStr,Str$concat,Sys$stdout`.
;
; The first half is closed differently, and by this commit: the
; rendering head is `format#` (`expFmtShow`, below) and does not come
; through here at all. tests/selfhost/383-format-capture.ax measures
; both halves, so neither can go back to being a paragraph.
(pub :: expFmtCall (-> MEnv String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtCall env f x sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEVarAt (expQualify env f) sp) x))

; The rendering call for one hole: `(format# x)`, and `format#` is a
; head no program can write.
;
; This does NOT go through `expQualify`, and that is the whole point.
; `expQualify` resolves a template's free identifier at the macro's
; definition site, which is the right rule for `concat` and
; `fmtInt` - they are library functions and a program may legitimately
; own the name. `format#` is not a library function: nothing declares
; it, `typecheck.ax`'s `showIsHead` claims the head and rewrites it
; from the argument's static type, and no node carrying it survives
; checking. Qualifying it would only give a module a chance to shadow
; a name that is not its to shadow.
;
; `#` is not an identifier character - `(:: format# ...)` is `AX1001
; unexpected character `#`` from the lexer - so the spelling is
; unwritable in source and no declaration, in any module, can reach
; it. It is spelled with the same `#` as the generated renderers
; `show#Point`, `show#(Option Int)`, for the same reason and with no
; collision: those are real emitted functions with a type name after
; the `#`, this is a bare head with nothing after it.
;
; Until 0.7.4 this was `(expFmtCall env "show" v vsp)` and the head was
; the ordinary, writable name `show`. An entry file declaring
; `(:: show (-> a String))` captured every hole in the program,
; silently and at exit 0 - measured, and pinned by
; tests/selfhost/383-format-capture.ax, which now measures that it
; does not.
; It takes no `env`, unlike every other member of this family.
; That absence is the signature of the rule: there is no environment
; in which this head means anything but itself.
(pub :: expFmtShow (-> ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtShow v sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEVarAt "format#" sp) v))

; `(f x n)` - the shape every width and precision specifier lowers
; to, since each of them is a `Fmt` function of a rendered String (or
; a Float) and a count.
(pub :: expFmtCall2 (-> MEnv String ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtCall2 env f x y sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEVarAt (expQualify env f) sp) x) y))

; Append `node` to the concatenation built so far. 0 means "nothing
; yet", so a one-part format string produces that part and no call:
; `(println "hi")` emits exactly the literal it was given.
(pub :: expFmtCat (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtCat env acc node sp)
  (if (nodeSame acc nilNode)
    node
    (mkEApp (expFmtCall env "concat" acc sp) node)))

; The same, for a literal RUN that may be empty - which it is at every
; boundary a hole sits on. `"{a}{b}"` has three runs and all three are
; empty, and concatenating them cost three `concat` calls per line
; printed, at run time, to append nothing. An empty run contributes
; nothing to the answer and is dropped instead.
(pub :: expFmtCatRun (-> MEnv ASTNode String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtCatRun env acc run sp)
  (if (== (strLen run) 0)
    acc
    (expFmtCat env acc (expFmtLit run sp) sp)))

; A literal run, as the node the lexer would have produced for it.
; `TAG_E_STR` carries the LEXEME - quotes and escapes included, which
; is why the runs are sliced out of the original lexeme rather than
; unescaped and re-escaped: every escape the source wrote survives
; byte for byte, and none is interpreted twice.
;
; WORD B IS THE "ALREADY PARSED" MARK, and it is what stops a format
; string being parsed TWICE. Expansion runs to a fixpoint over its own
; output, so a macro whose result is a literal hands that literal to
; whatever encloses it: `(println (format "a{{b}}"))` collapsed the
; inner `format` to the literal `a{b}` and then let the outer
; `println` parse THAT, reading the `{b}` a doubled brace had just
; produced as a hole - `undefined variable b`, for a program with no
; `b` and no hole in it. A literal this pass built is not source and
; must not be re-read as source. The parser writes 0 here
; (`parser.ax` line 645) and nothing else reads the word: the checker
; looks at the tag, and codegen at the lexeme.
(pub :: expFmtLit (-> String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtLit body sp)
  (nodeBuildEStr (concat (concat "\"" body) "\"") 1 0 sp))

; The end of the identifier starting at `i`, or `i` itself when there
; is no identifier there. The charset is the LEXER's, not a second
; one invented here: a hole may name anything the language can name,
; so `{empty-list}` and `{ok?}`-minus-the-`?` follow `isIdentChar`
; and drift with it rather than against it.
(pub :: expFmtIdentEnd (-> String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expFmtIdentEnd body i n)
  (if (>= i n)
    i
    (if (isIdentStart (strByte body i))
      (expFmtIdentScan body (+ i 1) n)
      i)))

(pub :: expFmtIdentScan (-> String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expFmtIdentScan body i n)
  (if (>= i n)
    i
    (if (isIdentChar (strByte body i))
      (expFmtIdentScan body (+ i 1) n)
      i)))

; A malformed format string. The span is computed INSIDE the literal
; - `base` is the offset of the body's first byte, so a caret lands
; on the offending brace or hole rather than on the whole
; invocation, which is the one thing a format string's diagnostics
; have to get right to be worth having.
;
; The unit is `declmod` (word 11) and NOT `macmod`, and the
; difference is the whole diagnostic. `macmod` is the module the
; MACRO was written in - `IO` - and these spans index the file the
; format string was written in, which is the file being expanded.
; Stamped with `macmod` the renderer resolves a caller's byte offset
; against `stdlib/IO.ax`'s text and reports a location inside the
; standard library, which is what this did before the offsets were
; checked against real source: `IO.ax:3:17` for a mistake four lines
; into the user's own file. Word 11 exists for exactly this and is
; the reason it is not overwritten by the template walk.
(pub :: expFmtErr (-> MEnv Int Int String String Int))
(pub fn (expFmtErr env from to msg help)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3031" "malformed-format-string" (mkSpan from to) msg None
          (Some help)
          "a format string is parsed at expansion time, so every hole in it is checked before the program runs; `{{` and `}}` write a literal brace")
        env.declmod))
    0
  })

; The largest width or precision a format specifier may name.
;
; Implementation-defined, like every other limit this compiler
; enforces (macro-system.md 11.2), and stated in the diagnostic rather
; than left for a reader to look up. One million is far past any
; layout - the widest column in this repository's own output is 10 -
; and far below where the accumulator that reads the digits could
; wrap.
(pub :: expFmtMaxCount Int)
(pub fn (expFmtMaxCount)
  1000000)

(pub :: expFmtMaxCountMsg String)
(pub fn (expFmtMaxCountMsg)
  "1000000")

; The specifier between `:` and `}`, as a `FmtSpec`.
;
; Parsed in the order the grammar writes it, each part optional, and
; anything left over at the end is a refusal rather than a silent
; ignore - a format specifier that is quietly dropped is the exact
; failure this whole design exists to make impossible.
(pub :: expFmtParseSpec (-> MEnv String Int Int Int (Option FmtSpec)))
(pub fn (expFmtParseSpec env body from to base)
  {
    (let mut p from)
    (let mut align 62)
    (let mut zero 0)
    (let mut width 0)
    (let mut prec (:: None (Option Int)))
    (let mut ty (:: None (Option UInt8)))
    (let mut ok 1)
    {
      (if (&& (< p to)
            (|| (== (strByte body p) 60) (|| (== (strByte body p) 94) (== (strByte body p) 62))))
        {
          (set align (strByte body p))
          (set p (+ p 1))
        })
      ; A leading `0` is the zero-pad FLAG, not the first digit of the
      ; width: `{n:04}` is width 4 zero-padded, exactly as in Rust,
      ; and a width of literally zero pads nothing anyway.
      (if (&& (< p to) (== (strByte body p) 48))
        {
          (set zero 1)
          (set p (+ p 1))
        })
      ; The accumulator STOPS at the cap rather than wrapping, and the
      ; cap is then reported. Unbounded, these two loops were the only
      ; way a format string could take the compiler off a cliff:
      ; `{n:99999999999999999999}` wrapped the width to a huge value
      ; and the program trapped in the allocator at exit 70 with no
      ; diagnostic, and the same digits as a PRECISION made the
      ; expansion hang - `powTen` counting to a wrapped bound. Both are
      ; AX3031 now, at expansion time, and the message names the limit
      ; it enforced (macro-system.md 11.2's rule for every limit this
      ; compiler states).
      (while (&& (< p to) (isDigit (strByte body p)))
        {
          (set width (if (> width expFmtMaxCount)
            width
            (+ (* width 10) (- (strByte body p) 48))))
          (set p (+ p 1))
        })
      (if (&& (== ok 1) (> width expFmtMaxCount))
        (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base from) (+ base to)
                  (concat "a format width above " expFmtMaxCountMsg)
                  "a width is a byte count the padding allocates; this one is a typo rather than a layout")))
      (if (&& (== ok 1) (&& (< p to) (== (strByte body p) 46)))
        {
          (set p (+ p 1))
          (if (|| (>= p to) (! (isDigit (strByte body p))))
            ; Include the dot when no digits follow it.
            (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base (- p 1)) (+ base to)
                      "a `.` in a format specifier needs a digit count after it"
                      "write `{x:.2}` for two decimal places"))
            {
              (let mut digits 0)
              (while (&& (< p to) (isDigit (strByte body p)))
                {
                  (set digits (if (> digits expFmtMaxCount)
                    digits
                    (+ (* digits 10) (- (strByte body p) 48))))
                  (set p (+ p 1))
                })
              (if (> digits expFmtMaxCount)
                (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base from) (+ base to)
                          (concat "a format precision above " expFmtMaxCountMsg)
                          "a precision is a digit count; `{x:.2}` is two decimal places"))
                (set prec (Some digits)))
            })
        })
      (if (&& (== ok 1) (< p to))
        (if (|| (== (strByte body p) 120) (== (strByte body p) 88))
          {
            (set ty (Some (as (strByte body p) UInt8)))
            (set p (+ p 1))
          }
          (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base p) (+ base (+ p 1))
                    (concat (concat "`" (strSlice body p 1)) "` is not a format-specifier type")
                    "the types are `x` and `X`, both hexadecimal. Alignment and width come first (`{n:>8x}`) and a precision is `.N` (`{x:.2}`)"))))
      (if (&& (== ok 1) (< p to))
        (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base p) (+ base to)
                  "this is left over after the format specifier"
                  "a specifier is [align][0][width][.precision][type], in that order - `{x:>08.2}`")))
      (if (== ok 1)
        (Some (mkFmtSpec align zero width prec ty))
        None)
    }
  })

; A HOLE NAMES A BINDING AT THE INVOCATION, ALWAYS - and it cannot
; name a macro parameter. `(macro (logIt v) (println "value is {v}"))`
; does not interpolate `logIt`'s argument; it emits a bare `v`, which
; is `AX3001` at the call unless the caller happens to have one.
;
; That is structural rather than an omission. A string literal is a
; LEAF to `substTpl`, copied through untouched, so by the time
; `println`'s own expansion reaches the query the enclosing macro's
; parameters are long out of scope - and they could not be spliced in
; anyway, because a hole is a place for an EXPRESSION and a literal
; has room only for text. Making it work would mean scanning every
; string in every template for braces, which would change the meaning
; of every template that prints a brace for some other reason.
;
; A macro that wants to render its own argument composes instead:
;
;   (macro (logIt v) (println (concat "value is " (format v))))
;
; The failure is loud, at the macro's own line, and pinned by
; tests/diagnostics/570-format-refusals.ax.
; The rendering call for one hole: a conversion, then a padding.
;
; Both layers are chosen HERE, at expansion time, so the generated
; code contains no test of a format flag - `{n:>8x}` becomes exactly
; `(fmtPadLeft (fmtHex n) 8)` and carries no specifier with it.
(pub :: expFmtHole (-> MEnv ASTNode FmtSpec Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtHole env v spec vsp)
  {
    (let rendered
      (if (let (Some radix) spec.ty)
        (expFmtCall env
          (if (== radix (as 120 UInt8))
            "fmtHex"
            "fmtHexUpper")
          v vsp)
        (if (let (Some precision) spec.prec)
          (expFmtCall2 env "fmtFloatPrec" v (mkEInt precision) vsp)
          (expFmtShow v vsp))))
    (if (== spec.width 0)
      rendered
      (if (== spec.zero (as 1 UInt8))
        (expFmtCall2 env "fmtPadZerosLeft" rendered (mkEInt spec.width) vsp)
        (expFmtCall2 env
          (if (== spec.align (as 60 UInt8))
            "fmtPadRight"
            (if (== spec.align (as 94 UInt8))
              "fmtPadCenter"
              "fmtPadLeft"))
          rendered (mkEInt spec.width) vsp)))
  })

(pub :: expEvalFormat (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalFormat env qargs params args isp nl)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 1)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/format takes exactly one argument"
        "write `(syntax/format e)`; the printing macros pass their single parameter to it"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let a (vecGet qargs 0))
      {
        (let e (expFmtArgNode a params args))
        (if (|| (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_STR) (!= (as (nodeB e) Int) 0))
          ; Not a source literal: one rendering call, dispatched on the
          ; static type. This is the arm that keeps `(println x)`
          ; meaning what it meant before `println` was a macro,
          ; and (via the word-b mark) the arm that stops an
          ; expansion's own output being parsed a second time.
          {
            (let sp
              (if (!= (nodeSpan e) 0)
                (nodeSpan e)
                isp))
            {
              (let r (expFmtShow e sp))
              (if (== nl 0)
                r
                (expFmtCat env r (expFmtLit "\\n" sp) sp))
            }
          }
          (expFmtParse env e isp nl))
      }
    }))

; The argument as the caller wrote it: a macro parameter standing in
; the template resolves to the argument node, and anything else is
; itself. Unlike `expQueryArgName` this wants the NODE, because a
; format string is a literal rather than a name.
(pub :: expFmtArgNode (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtArgNode a params args)
  (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
    a
    (match (expParamIndex params (nodeAName a))
      ((None) a)
      ((Some idx) (vecGet args idx)))))

; The end of the hole opened at `i`: the offset of its closing `}`,
; or `n` when there is none. Scanned rather than assumed, because a
; specifier can contain `<`, `>`, `.` and digits but never a brace.
(pub :: expFmtCloseAt (-> String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expFmtCloseAt body i n)
  (if (>= i n)
    n
    (if (== (strByte body i) 125)
      i
      (expFmtCloseAt body (+ i 1) n))))

(pub :: expFmtParse (-> MEnv ASTNode Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFmtParse env lit isp nl)
  ; The body's first byte, in source: past the opening quote.
  ; A literal whose span the expander lost falls back to the
  ; invocation's, and then every caret is the invocation -
  ; degraded, never wrong.
  {
    (let lex (nodeAName lit))
    (let lsp
      (if (!= (nodeSpan lit) 0)
        (nodeSpan lit)
        isp))
    {
      (let body (strSlice lex 1 (- (strLen lex) 2)))
      (let base (+ (spanStart lsp) 1))
      {
        (let n (strLen body))
        {
          (let mut acc nilNode)
          (let mut chunk "")
          (let mut i 0)
          (let mut ok 1)
          {
            (while (&& (< i n) (== ok 1))
              {
                (let ch (strByte body i))
                (if (== ch 92)
                  ; A backslash escape is copied as a PAIR, so its
                  ; second byte can never be read as a brace and
                  ; the run stays exactly what the source wrote.
                  {
                    (let w
                      (if (< (+ i 1) n)
                        2
                        1))
                    {
                      (set chunk (concat chunk (strSlice body i w)))
                      (set i (+ i w))
                    }
                  }
                  (if (== ch 123)
                    (if (&& (< (+ i 1) n) (== (strByte body (+ i 1)) 123))
                      {
                        (set chunk (concat chunk "{"))
                        (set i (+ i 2))
                      }
                      {
                        (let j (expFmtIdentEnd body (+ i 1) n))
                        (if (== j (+ i 1))
                          (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base i) (+ base (+ i 1))
                                    (if (&& (< (+ i 1) n) (== (strByte body (+ i 1)) 125))
                                      "an empty hole `{}` names nothing to interpolate"
                                      "this `{` opens a hole that does not name a binding")
                                    "a hole names a binding in scope at the CALL - `{count}` interpolates `count`. There are no positional arguments and no argument list: write the name in the string, or `{{` for a literal brace"))
                          (if (>= j n)
                            (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base i) (+ base n) "this `{` is never closed"
                                      "close the hole with `}`, or write `{{` for a literal brace"))
                            ; The name is done. Either the hole
                            ; closes here, or a `:` introduces
                            ; the specifier, or the byte is
                            ; simply wrong.
                            {
                              (let close
                                (if (== (strByte body j) 125)
                                  j
                                  (if (== (strByte body j) 58)
                                    (expFmtCloseAt body (+ j 1) n)
                                    (- 0 1))))
                              (if (< close 0)
                                ; The span is `{name` - it
                                ; opens at the brace and stops
                                ; BEFORE the offending byte,
                                ; which the message quotes
                                ; instead. That byte is very
                                ; often a space (`{a b}`), and
                                ; `check-diagnostics` refuses
                                ; to bless a primary span that
                                ; begins or ends on
                                ; whitespace - correctly: a
                                ; caret under a space marks
                                ; nothing a reader can see.
                                (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base i) (+ base j)
                                          (concat
                                            (concat "this hole is not closed: `"
                                              (strSlice body j 1))
                                            "` is neither `}` nor `:`")
                                          "a hole is `{name}`, or `{name:SPEC}` with a format specifier - `{n:>8}`, `{x:.2}`, `{n:04x}`"))
                                (if (>= close n)
                                  (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base i) (+ base n)
                                            "this `{` is never closed"
                                            "close the hole with `}` after its format specifier"))
                                  {
                                    (let parsedSpec
                                      (if (== close j)
                                        (Some (mkFmtSpec 62 0 0 None None))
                                        (expFmtParseSpec env body (+ j 1) close base)))
                                    (if (let (Some spec) parsedSpec)
                                      {
                                        (set acc (expFmtCatRun env acc chunk lsp))
                                        (set chunk "")
                                        (set acc (expFmtCat env acc
                                                   (expFmtHole env
                                                     (mkEVarAt
                                                       (strSlice body (+ i 1) (- j (+ i 1)))
                                                       (mkSpan (+ base (+ i 1)) (+ base j)))
                                                     spec (mkSpan (+ base (+ i 1)) (+ base j)))
                                                   lsp))
                                        (set i (+ close 1))
                                      }
                                      (set ok 0))
                                  }))
                            }))
                      })
                    (if (== ch 125)
                      (if (&& (< (+ i 1) n) (== (strByte body (+ i 1)) 125))
                        {
                          (set chunk (concat chunk "}"))
                          (set i (+ i 2))
                        }
                        (set ok (expFmtErr env (+ base i) (+ base (+ i 1))
                                  "this `}` closes a hole that was never opened"
                                  "write `}}` for a literal closing brace")))
                      {
                        (set chunk (concat chunk (strSlice body i 1)))
                        (set i (+ i 1))
                      })))
              })
            ; The newline joins the trailing literal run rather than
            ; becoming a concatenation of its own, which is what makes
            ; `(println "hi")` one `write` of one literal.
            (set chunk (if (== nl 1)
              (concat chunk "\\n")
              chunk))
            (if (== ok 0)
              (mkEInt 0)
              ; A trailing run, and the empty-format case: with no
              ; holes at all `acc` is still 0 here, so the answer
              ; is the literal itself and `(println "hi")` emits
              ; exactly one node.
              (if (&& (nodeSame acc nilNode) (== (strLen chunk) 0))
                (expFmtLit "" lsp)
                (expFmtCatRun env acc chunk lsp)))
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })

; The name of the expansion-time query at the head of an `if`
; condition, or None when the condition is an ordinary runtime
; expression. Exactly two queries answer a Bool, and both are folded
; by substTpl's TAG_E_IF arm.
(pub :: expCondQuery (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (expCondQuery condition)
  (if (!= (nodeTag condition) TAG_E_APP)
    None
    {
      (let head (spineHead condition))
      (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
          None
        (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/same")
          (Some "syntax/same")
        (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/defined")
          (Some "syntax/defined")
        None)
    }))

; `(syntax/join a b)` where an expression stands: the joined
; identifier as a reference, with any further arguments of the
; flattened spine applied to it. The name goes through `expQualify`
; like every other free identifier a template mentions (MAC-HYG-6),
; which is a no-op for the usual case - the declaration the join
; names was generated in the entry file and is not mangled - and the
; right answer when a module generated it.
(pub :: expEvalJoinRef (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalJoinRef env qargs params args isp)
  (if (< (vecLen qargs) 2)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/join takes exactly two identifiers"
        "write `(syntax/join a b)`, or `((syntax/join a b) x)` to call what it names"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let l (expQueryArgName env (vecGet qargs 0) params args isp))
      (if (let (Some l) l)
        {
          (let r (expQueryArgName env (vecGet qargs 1) params args isp))
          (if (let (Some r) r)
            {
              (let f (mkEVarAt (expQualify env (expJoinName l r)) isp))
              (let out vecNew)
              {
                (for i in 2..(vecLen qargs)
                  (vecPush out (substTpl (vecGet qargs i) params args env isp)))
                (markGeneratedCall (expRebuildSpine f out isp 0))
              }
            }
            (mkEInt 0))
        }
        (mkEInt 0))
    }))

; Is this expression a `(syntax/for (x seq) tpl)` spine?
(pub :: expIsForSpine (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expIsForSpine e)
  {
    (let h (spineHead e))
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName h) "syntax/for"))
      1
      0)
  })

; `(syntax/fold f z (x xs) tpl)` and its parallel form
; `(syntax/fold f z ((x xs) (y ys)) tpl)`: the right-fold
; `(f tpl1 (f tpl2 ... z))`, tpl instantiated once per element under
; the fold variables, nested under a TWO-argument head - `&&` and
; `==` are ordinary registered functions, so the built spine is a
; plain application. An empty sequence answers z alone. Parallel
; sequences MUST have equal length; a mismatch is the diagnostic the
; spec promises, at the invocation.
;
; The binding-form grammar, shared by `syntax/for` and `syntax/fold`:
; the single form's spine is APP(VAR x, seq) - head IS the binder; the
; parallel form's is APP(APP(VAR x, s1), (y s2), ...) - the same head,
; the first sequence as argument 0, and each further pair an
; APP(VAR, seq) of its own. expIterPairs normalises both to a vector
; of (binder, seqExpr) pairs.
;
; ONE normaliser for both forms is the point: `for` and `fold` are the
; two constructs that turn a sequence into program text, and a zip
; that agreed for one and not the other would be a rule with two
; spellings. `who` names the construct in the refusals, which is the
; only thing that differs between them.
(pub struct ExpIterBinding
  (name : String)
  (sequence : ASTNode))

(pub struct ExpIterSequence
  (name : String)
  (values : Names)
  (key : String))

(pub :: expIterPairs (-> MEnv ASTNode Int String (Option (Vec ExpIterBinding))))
(pub fn (expIterPairs env binds isp who)
  {
    (let h (spineHead binds))
    (let bargs (spineArgVec binds vecNew))
    (if (|| (!= (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR) (== (vecLen bargs) 0))
      {
        (expQueryErr env isp
          (concat (concat "`" who) "`'s binding form is `(x xs)` or `((x xs) (y ys) ...)`")
          "bind each iteration variable to one sequence" (expMacMod env))
        None
      }
      {
        (let pairs vecNew)
        {
          {
            (let p0 (ExpIterBinding :name (nodeAName h) :sequence (vecGet bargs 0)))
            (vecPush pairs p0)
          }
          (expIterPairsRest env bargs isp pairs 1 who)
        }
      })
  })

(pub :: expIterPairsRest
  (-> MEnv Nodes Int (Vec ExpIterBinding) Int String (Option (Vec ExpIterBinding))))
(pub fn (expIterPairsRest env bargs isp pairs i who)
  (if (>= i (vecLen bargs))
    (Some pairs)
    {
      (let b (vecGet bargs i))
      {
        (let bh (spineHead b))
        (let bas (spineArgVec b vecNew))
        (if (|| (!= (nodeTag b) TAG_E_APP) (|| (!= (nodeTag bh) TAG_E_VAR) (!= (vecLen bas) 1)))
          {
            (expQueryErr env isp
              (concat (concat "each parallel `" who) "` binding is a `(y ys)` pair")
              "bind each iteration variable to one sequence" (expMacMod env))
            None
          }
          {
            (let p (ExpIterBinding :name (nodeAName bh) :sequence (vecGet bas 0)))
            {
              (vecPush pairs p)
              (expIterPairsRest env bargs isp pairs (+ i 1) who)
            }
          })
      }
    }))

; The binding form of an ARM- or DECLARATION-position `syntax/for`,
; evaluated: pairs normalised, each sequence answered, and every
; sequence required to have the same length. Answers the (var, names,
; key) triples `expIterPush` binds from, or None with the refusal already
; recorded.
;
; TWO spans, because two kinds of thing go wrong here and the author
; changes a different line for each (MAC-DIAG-5). A malformed binding
; form is a fact about the MACRO's text - `shapeSp` - and the author
; edits the template. A SKEW is a fact about the arguments: the
; template pairs the sequences, the invocation decides their lengths,
; so it anchors at the invocation and carries the frame naming the
; macro. `syntax/fold` has always reported its skew at the invocation;
; this is the same rule stated rather than inherited.
(pub :: expIterSeqsOf (-> MEnv ASTNode Names Nodes Int Int (Option (Vec ExpIterSequence))))
(pub fn (expIterSeqsOf env binds params args shapeSp isp)
  {
    (let pairs (expIterPairs env binds shapeSp "syntax/for"))
    (if (let (Some pairs) pairs)
      {
        (let seqs (expIterSeqs env pairs params args shapeSp vecNew 0))
        (if (let (Some seqs) seqs)
          {
            (let n (expIterLen seqs))
            (if (== (expIterSameLen seqs n 1) 0)
              {
                (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/for's parallel sequences have different lengths"
                  "parallel sequences zip in lockstep and must have equal length (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5)"
                  (expMacMod env))
                None
              }
              (Some seqs))
          }
          None)
      }
      None)
  })

; How many elements one evaluated binding set iterates.
(pub :: expIterLen (-> (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int))
(pub fn (expIterLen seqs)
  {
    (let first (vecGet seqs 0))
    (vecLen first.values)
  })

(pub :: expEvalFold (-> MEnv Nodes Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expEvalFold env qargs params args isp)
  (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 4)
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/fold takes a head, a zero, a binding form, and one template"
        "write `(syntax/fold f z (x xs) tpl)` or the parallel `((x xs) (y ys))` form"
        (expMacMod env))
      (mkEInt 0)
    }
    {
      (let pairs (expIterPairs env (vecGet qargs 2) isp "syntax/fold"))
      (if (let (Some pairs) pairs)
        {
          (let seqs (expIterSeqs env pairs params args isp vecNew 0))
          (if (let (Some seqs) seqs)
            {
              (let n (expIterLen seqs))
              (if (== (expIterSameLen seqs n 1) 0)
                {
                  (expQueryErr env isp "syntax/fold's parallel sequences have different lengths"
                    "parallel sequences zip in lockstep and must have equal length (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5)"
                    (expMacMod env))
                  (mkEInt 0)
                }
                {
                  (let fh (substTpl (vecGet qargs 0) params args env isp))
                  (expFoldBuild env pairs seqs params args isp fh (vecGet qargs 3)
                    (substTpl (vecGet qargs 1) params args env isp) (- n 1))
                })
            }
            (mkEInt 0))
        }
        (mkEInt 0))
    }))

; Evaluate each pair's sequence; answers a vec of (var, names, key)
; triples, or None after the first refused query.
(pub :: expIterSeqs
  (-> MEnv (Vec ExpIterBinding) Names Nodes Int (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int
    (Option (Vec ExpIterSequence))))
(pub fn (expIterSeqs env pairs params args isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pairs))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let p (vecGet pairs i))
      {
        (let sr (expEvalSeq env p.sequence params args isp))
        (if (let (Some sr) sr)
          {
            (let t (ExpIterSequence :name p.name :values sr.values :key sr.key))
            {
              (vecPush acc t)
              (expIterSeqs env pairs params args isp acc (+ i 1))
            }
          }
          None)
      }
    }))

(pub :: expIterSameLen (-> (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expIterSameLen seqs n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seqs))
    1
    (if (==
          {
            (let entry (vecGet seqs i))
            (vecLen entry.values)
          }
          n)
      (expIterSameLen seqs n (+ i 1))
      0)))

; Build `(f tpl_i acc)` from the right: acc starts as z, and index i
; walks N-1 down to 0 so tpl_1 ends up outermost.
(pub :: expFoldBuild
  (-> MEnv (Vec ExpIterBinding) (Vec ExpIterSequence) Names (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode ASTNode
    ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFoldBuild env pairs seqs params args isp fh tpl acc i)
  (if (< i 0)
    acc
    {
      (let mark (vecLen (expForStackVec env)))
      (let markC (vecLen (expScChainVec env)))
      {
        (expIterPush env seqs i 0)
        (expScPushLoop env)
        {
          (let ti (substTpl tpl params args env isp))
          {
            (expTrunc (expForStackVec env) mark)
            (expScTruncLoop env markC)
            (expFoldBuild env pairs seqs params args isp fh tpl
              (nodeBuildEApp (nodeBuildEApp fh ti nilWords isp) acc nilWords isp) (- i 1))
          }
        }
      }
    }))

; Push every parallel binding for element `idx` - all of them, so the
; template sees the whole zipped tuple while it is instantiated, and
; one mark/truncate around the group pops them together.
(pub :: expIterPush (-> MEnv (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expIterPush env seqs idx j)
  (if (>= j (vecLen seqs))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet seqs j))
      {
        (vecPush (expForStackVec env)
          (ForBind
            :name t.name
            :elem (vecGet t.values idx)
            :key t.key
            :idx idx
            :seq (expScNextSeq env)))
        (expIterPush env seqs idx (+ j 1))
      }
    }))

; Splice an ARGUMENT-position `(syntax/for BINDS tpl)` into the
; enclosing application's argument vector: the template expression
; instantiated once per element, each pushed where the form stood -
; `(S (syntax/for (g (syntax/fields S)) e))` becomes `(S e1 e2 ...)`.
; The spine shape is APP(APP(syntax/for, BINDS), tpl): argument 0
; is the binding form - `expIterPairs`' grammar, one pair or several
; zipped - and argument 1 the template. Anything else refuses.
(pub :: expSpliceForArg (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceForArg f params args env isp out)
  ; The form's OWN span where it has one, as the arm- and
  ; declaration-position splices already do: a skewed zip is a
  ; fact about the macro's text, so the caret belongs on the
  ; `syntax/for` that wrote it rather than on the invocation
  ; that reached it.
  {
    (let qargs (spineArgVec f vecNew))
    (let fsp
      (if (!= (nodeSpan f) 0)
        (nodeSpan f)
        isp))
    (if (!= (vecLen qargs) 2)
      {
        (expQueryErr env fsp
          "argument-position syntax/for takes a binding form and ONE template expression"
          "write `(syntax/for (x seq) tpl)`, or `((x xs) (y ys))` to zip two sequences"
          (expMacMod env))
        (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
        0
      }
      {
        (let seqs (expIterSeqsOf env (vecGet qargs 0) params args fsp isp))
        (if (let (Some seqs) seqs)
          (expSpliceForArgElems (vecGet qargs 1) params args env isp out seqs (expIterLen seqs) 0)
          {
            (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
            0
          })
      })
  })

(pub :: expSpliceForArgElems
  (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceForArgElems tpl params args env isp out seqs n j)
  (if (>= j n)
    0
    {
      (let mark (vecLen (expForStackVec env)))
      (let markC (vecLen (expScChainVec env)))
      {
        (expIterPush env seqs j 0)
        (expScPushLoop env)
        (vecPush out (substTpl tpl params args env isp))
        (expTrunc (expForStackVec env) mark)
        (expScTruncLoop env markC)
        (expSpliceForArgElems tpl params args env isp out seqs n (+ j 1))
      }
    }))

; An application in a template, substituted SPINE-AT-ONCE rather than
; through the binary halves: the flatten is what lets an ARGUMENT
; whose own head is `syntax/for` splice several arguments into the
; enclosing call - `(S (syntax/for (g (syntax/fields S)) e))` becomes
; `(S e1 e2)` - and what lets a scalar query at the head answer in
; place. The binary recursion could see neither: the enclosing
; spine's head is `S`, not the query, and one node cannot return two.
; Rebuilt left-nested, the shape every consumer reads; inner
; applications reach this same function through their own walk, so
; nothing is processed twice.
(pub :: substTplApp (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (substTplApp tpl params args env isp)
  {
    (let head (spineHead tpl))
    (let qargs (spineArgVec tpl vecNew))
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR) (strStartsWith (nodeAName head) "syntax/"))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/same")
        (expEvalSame env qargs isp)
        (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/fold")
          (expEvalFold env qargs params args isp)
          (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/name")
            (expEvalName env qargs params args isp)
            (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/arity")
              (expEvalArity env qargs params args isp)
              (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/defined")
                (expEvalDefined env qargs params args isp)
                (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/format")
                  (expEvalFormat env qargs params args isp 0)
                  (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/formatln")
                    (expEvalFormat env qargs params args isp 1)
                    (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/join")
                      ; A join in EXPRESSION position is a REFERENCE to the
                      ; declaration a join in name position generates -
                      ; `((syntax/join show T) x)` calls `showColor`. Spines are
                      ; flattened, so the call arrives here as one spine headed
                      ; by the query with the joined parts first and the call's
                      ; own arguments after; rebuilding is what separates them.
                      ; Without this a macro could NAME a generated declaration
                      ; and never call one, which is why `showOr` needs it and
                      ; `deriveEq` did not.
                      (expEvalJoinRef env qargs params args isp)
                      (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/for")
                        {
                          (expQueryErr env isp
                            "syntax/for stands where several results can splice - a match arm, a template declaration, an argument - not where ONE value is needed"
                            "iterate with syntax/for in arm, declaration, or argument position, or reduce with syntax/fold; a scalar position has no room for a sequence"
                            (expMacMod env))
                          (mkEInt 0)
                        }
                        (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/constructors")
                              (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/fields")
                                (strEq (nodeAName head) "syntax/binders")))
                          {
                            (expQueryErr env isp
                              (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName head))
                                "` answers a SEQUENCE, and this position needs one value")
                              "sequence queries stand in a syntax/for's sequence slot or a syntax/fold's binding"
                              (expMacMod env))
                            (mkEInt 0)
                          }
                          {
                            (expQueryErr env isp
                              (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName head))
                                "` is not a query this position answers")
                              "the applied queries are syntax/same, syntax/fold, syntax/name, syntax/arity, syntax/defined, syntax/format and syntax/formatln here, and syntax/constructors / syntax/fields / syntax/binders in an iteration's sequence slot"
                              (expMacMod env))
                            (mkEInt 0)
                          }))))))))))
      {
        (let out vecNew)
        (let mut i 0)
        {
          (while (< i (vecLen qargs))
            {
              (let a (vecGet qargs i))
              {
                (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_APP) (== (expIsForSpine a) 1))
                  (expSpliceForArg a params args env isp out)
                  ; MAC-LANG-16: `x ...` splices the forms `x` absorbed.
                  ; The ellipsis lexes as an identifier, so it is the
                  ; NEXT argument in the spine rather than a suffix of
                  ; this one - which is also how `expRuleShapes` prints
                  ; it back.
                  (if (== (expEllipsisAt env qargs i) 1)
                    {
                      (if (let (Some forms) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName a)))
                        (expSpliceSeq env forms params args isp out 0)
                        {
                          (expEllipsisDiag env (nodeAName a)
                            "has no repetition binding in this scope"
                            "keep the repeated name under its matching ellipsis" isp)
                          (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
                          0
                        })
                      (set i (+ i 1))
                    }
                    ; MAC-LANG-16, second half: `(f a) ...` rebuilds the
                    ; form once per absorbed element, with each binder
                    ; taking its element. An APP here that is not a
                    ; for-spine (checked above) and is followed by
                    ; `...` is that - or a misuse, which the splice
                    ; diagnoses rather than silently dropping.
                    (if (== (expNestedEllipsisAt env qargs i) 1)
                      {
                        (expSpliceNested env a params args isp out)
                        (set i (+ i 1))
                      }
                      (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName a) "..."))
                        {
                          (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "follows nothing that repeats"
                            "put `...` directly after the name of a repeating element, or drop it"
                            isp)
                          (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
                        }
                        (vecPush out (substTpl a params args env isp))))))
                (set i (+ i 1))
              }
            })
          (expSubstCall tpl head out params args env isp)
        }
      })
  })

; Does the TEMPLATE bind this name around the substitution in flight?
(pub :: expTplBound (-> MEnv String Int))
(pub fn (expTplBound env name)
  {
    (let tb env.tplbinds)
    (if (== (as tb Int) 0)
      0
      (expTplBoundIn tb name 0))
  })

(pub :: expTplBoundIn (-> Strings String Int Int))
(pub fn (expTplBoundIn tb name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tb))
    0
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr tb i) name)
      1
      (expTplBoundIn tb name (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: expSeqBindsVec (-> MEnv SeqBinds))
(pub fn (expSeqBindsVec env)
  env.seqbinds)

; Is `qargs[i]` a sequence-bound name whose next neighbour is `...`?
(pub :: expEllipsisAt (-> MEnv Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (expEllipsisAt env qargs i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (vecLen qargs))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet qargs i))
      (let n (vecGet qargs (+ i 1)))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
            (&& (== (nodeTag n) TAG_E_VAR)
              (&& (strEq (nodeAName n) "...")
                (if (let (Some _) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName a)))
                  true
                  false))))
        1
        0)
    }))

; Each absorbed form, substituted and pushed where the pair stood.
(pub :: expSpliceSeq (-> MEnv (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceSeq env forms params args isp out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen forms))
    0
    {
      (vecPush out (substTpl (vecGet forms i) params args env isp))
      (expSpliceSeq env forms params args isp out (+ i 1))
    }))

; Is `qargs[i]` a form followed by `...` - the nested half of the
; check above, for `(f a) ...` where the flat one needs a bare
; name? Shape only: whether anything inside repeats is the splice's
; own diagnostic, so a misuse still names itself rather than
; falling through to a silent rebuild.
(pub :: expNestedEllipsisAt (-> MEnv Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (expNestedEllipsisAt env qargs i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (vecLen qargs))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet qargs i))
      (let n (vecGet qargs (+ i 1)))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_APP)
            (&& (== (nodeTag n) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName n) "...")))
        1
        0)
    }))

; Every sequence-bound name inside a template form, in first-seen
; order. Duplicates are harmless downstream (one index's value
; twice), so this collects rather than dedupes.
(pub :: expNestedSeqNames (-> ASTNode Names MEnv Names))
(pub fn (expNestedSeqNames form acc env)
  {
    (let tg (nodeTag form))
    (if (== tg TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (let (Some _) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName form)))
        {
          (vecPush acc (nodeAName form))
          acc
        }
        acc)
      (if (== tg TAG_E_APP)
        (expNestedSeqNames (nodeBChild form) (expNestedSeqNames (nodeAChild form) acc env) env)
        acc))
  })

; `(f a) ...` in a template: rebuild the form once per absorbed
; element, with each sequence-bound name taking its element. All
; sequences share one length by construction - one value per
; absorbed argument - so the first names it. The per-element
; substitution appends singles onto the shared params/args (last
; wins, so they shadow correctly) and pops them after: nothing
; appends to those vecs during substitution - matching builds
; fresh ones, `expHoleArgs` builds its own - so the truncation
; restores exactly what was there.
;
; Two diagnosed misuses, neither silent: no sequence inside (the
; `...` follows nothing that repeats), and a `...` inside (a repeat
; beneath a repeat, refused on patterns at the macro's line and
; here at the use). The interior check walks the whole form, not
; just one application level: a marker nested inside a `match`,
; an `if` or any other form would otherwise re-splice the whole
; sequence inside every element.
; Resolve every repeated name before mutating substitution arguments. Empty
; bindings are present; missing names or unequal row lengths refuse the splice.
(:: expSequenceRows (-> MEnv Names Int Int NodesRows (Option NodesRows)))
(fn (expSequenceRows env names isp i rows)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    (Some rows)
    {
      (let name (vecGet names i))
      (if (let (Some forms) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) name))
        {
          (let compatible
            (if (== (vecLen rows) 0)
              true
              (== (vecLen forms) (vecLen (vecGet rows 0)))))
          (if compatible
            {
              (vecPush rows forms)
              (expSequenceRows env names isp (+ i 1) rows)
            }
            {
              (expEllipsisDiag env name "repeats a different number of forms"
                "keep parallel repeated bindings at the same repetition depth" isp)
              None
            })
        }
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env name "has no repetition binding in this scope"
            "keep the repeated name under its matching ellipsis" isp)
          None
        })
    }))

(pub :: expSpliceNested (-> MEnv ASTNode Names Nodes Int Nodes Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceNested env app params args isp out)
  {
    (let names (expNestedSeqNames app vecNew env))
    (if (== (vecLen names) 0)
      {
        (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "follows nothing that repeats"
          "put `...` directly after the form whose binders repeat, or drop it" isp)
        (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
        0
      }
      (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat app) 1)
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "nests a repeat inside a repeat"
            "repeat one level only - splice `(f a) ...`, never `(f (g a ...) ...)`" isp)
          (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
          0
        }
        (if (let (Some rows) (expSequenceRows env names isp 0 vecNew))
          {
            (let first (vecGet rows 0))
            (let markP (vecLen params))
            (let markA (vecLen args))
            (for j in 0..(vecLen first)
              {
                (for k in 0..(vecLen names)
                  {
                    (vecPush params (vecGet names k))
                    (vecPush args (vecGet (vecGet rows k) j))
                  })
                (vecPush out (substTpl app params args env isp))
                (expTrunc params markP)
                (expTrunc args markA)
              })
            0
          }
          {
            (vecPush out (mkEInt 0))
            0
          })))
  })

(pub :: expRebuildSpine (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expRebuildSpine f outArgs isp i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen outArgs))
    f
    (expRebuildSpine (nodeBuildEApp f (vecGet outArgs i) nilWords isp) outArgs isp (+ i 1))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; MAC-EXP-14b: a macro parameter standing where a template keeps a
; NAME rather than substituting an expression. Two positions pass
; their name through verbatim - the field of `.` and `set`, and the
; effect list of `handle` - so a parameter there is read as a literal name and
; its argument is evaluated and discarded. AX3074 refuses it, at the
; invocation under the macro's frame: the template says a parameter
; and a literal at once, and each meaning has a spelling that says
; it - another name for the parameter when the literal is meant, an
; enclosing `syntax/for` when a field is chosen per invocation. A field
; name that iteration binds substitutes from it (section 10.3's
; getters), so that case stays quiet; the effect list consults no such
; binding, and anything naming a parameter is refused.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expWarnNamePos (-> Names String Int MEnv Int String String Int))
(pub fn (expWarnNamePos params name forShielded env isp position help)
  (if (isNone (expParamIndex params name))
    0
    (if (&& (== forShielded 1)
          (if (let (Some _) (expForLookup env name))
            true
            false))
      0
      (expEmit env
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3074" "macro-parameter-name-position" isp
          (concat
            (concat (concat "this macro's template uses its parameter `" name)
              (concat "` as " position))
            ", which is kept as written rather than replaced by its argument")
          None (Some help)
          "the field name and `handle`'s effect list substitute nothing (macro-system.md MAC-EXP-14b)")))))

; The first parameter name a `handle` effect list mentions, or null.
; The list holds spanned var nodes (the parser keeps one per name so
; AX3016 can anchor at it); the warning anchors at the invocation
; instead, because a template node's span indexes the defining file
; and this diagnostic is reported against the invoking one.
(pub :: expHandleParamName (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expHandleParamName effs params i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    (as 0 String)
    {
      (let e (vecGet effs i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR) (isSome (expParamIndex params (nodeAName e))))
        (nodeAName e)
        (expHandleParamName effs params (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A template literal rebuilt with the invocation's span
; (MAC-EXP-14a): keeping the defining file's byte offsets renders
; against the invoking file's line table. Every literal kind carries
; its value in word A; an integer literal also carries its mark in
; word B (2 on the unit value `()`), which the rebuild keeps, or a
; template's `()` would come out as the integer 0.
(pub :: expRebuildLit (-> ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expRebuildLit tpl isp)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag tpl))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_INT)
      (nodeBuildEInt (nodeA tpl) (nodeB tpl) 0 isp)
      (if (== tag TAG_E_STR)
        (nodeBuildEStr (nodeAText tpl) 0 0 isp)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_FLT)
          (nodeBuildEFlt (nodeA tpl) 0 0 isp)
          (nodeBuildEChar (nodeA tpl) 0 0 isp))))
  })

(pub :: substTpl (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (substTpl tpl params args env isp)
  (if (nodeSame tpl nilNode)
    nilNode
    ; --- leaves: rebuilt with the invocation span (MAC-EXP-14) -----
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag tpl))
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT)
            (|| (== tag TAG_E_STR) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_E_CHAR))))
        (expRebuildLit tpl isp)
        ; --- a name: the inner binder, a parameter, or itself, ---
        ; --- in that order -----------------------------------------
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          ; M3 innermost-wins: the template hit answers unless a
          ; for-binding of the same spelling carries a newer
          ; push-order stamp - pushed later, so pushed inside -
          ; in which case the reference falls through to the
          ; for-branch below, where it always went on a miss.
          {
            (let template (expScResolveTpl env (nodeAName tpl)))
            (let iteration (expForBindOf env (nodeAName tpl)))
            (if (let (Some winner) (expTemplateWinner template iteration))
              (mkEVarAt winner.spelling isp)
              {
                (let foundFor (expForLookup env (nodeAName tpl)))
                (if (let (Some fe) foundFor)
                  ; The element itself may be a renamed binder:
                  ; a fold variable over `(syntax/binders C x)`
                  ; resolves to `x#i`, whose pattern mention the
                  ; renamer has already renamed - the template
                  ; tier is consulted for the ELEMENT so the
                  ; body reference lands on the renamed binder.
                  (mkEVarAt
                    (if (let (Some binding) (expScResolveTpl env fe))
                      binding.spelling
                      fe)
                    isp)
                  (match (expParamIndex params (nodeAName tpl))
                    ((None)
                      ; A `syntax/`-spelled name that survived
                      ; the lookups above is a query this
                      ; vocabulary does not answer, or one
                      ; standing in a position it does not
                      ; own - the vocabulary is CLOSED
                      ; (MAC-CAP-6), so it refuses by name
                      ; instead of drifting into expQualify
                      ; and dying later as an undefined
                      ; variable that blames the wrong thing.
                      (if (strStartsWith (nodeAName tpl) "syntax/")
                        {
                          (expQueryErr env isp
                            (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName tpl))
                              "` is not a query this position answers")
                            "every query is APPLIED - `(syntax/name x)`, `(syntax/constructors T)` - so a bare `syntax/` name is a query missing its arguments, or a spelling the vocabulary does not answer"
                            (expMacMod env))
                          (mkEInt 0)
                        }
                        ; A name bound to a SEQUENCE reaching a
                        ; position that takes one form is
                        ; MAC-LANG-16's depth rule broken. It
                        ; would otherwise fall through to the
                        ; free-identifier arm below and die as
                        ; `AX3001 undefined variable`, blaming
                        ; the name rather than the missing `...`.
                        (if (let (Some _) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName tpl)))
                          {
                            (expEllipsisDiag env (nodeAName tpl)
                              "repeats, and this position takes one form"
                              "write `... ` after it to splice every form it took, or match it with a non-repeating element"
                              isp)
                            (mkEInt 0)
                          }
                          ; Neither a template binder nor a
                          ; parameter, so a FREE identifier:
                          ; resolved where the macro was
                          ; written, not where it was called.
                          ; A FREE identifier of the template,
                          ; so it means what it meant where the
                          ; macro was WRITTEN - top level, since
                          ; a macro is a top-level declaration.
                          ; Qualification says WHICH top-level
                          ; thing when a module can name it;
                          ; word 10 says "top level" even when
                          ; nothing can, which is the entry-file
                          ; case qualification never reached.
                          {
                            (let v (mkEVarAt (expQualify env (nodeAName tpl)) isp))
                            (if (== (expTplBound env (nodeAName tpl)) 1)
                              v
                              {
                                (writeNodeText v 8 (expMacModName env))
                                (setNodeDefScope v)
                              })
                          }))
                    )
                    ((Some idx) (vecGet args idx))))
              })
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
            (substTplApp tpl params args env isp)
            (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
              ; A condition that is a `syntax/same` or `syntax/defined`
              ; spine is decided at expansion time, and the CHOSEN
              ; BRANCH is spliced in the if's place - so
              ; `(if (syntax/same f g) v s.g)` expands to `v` on the
              ; diagonal and `s.g` off it, which is what makes section
              ; 10.3's "withX expands to (Point v s.y)" literally true
              ; rather than behaviourally true. For `syntax/defined`
              ; the folding is not a nicety but the whole point: the
              ; branch that did NOT survive names a declaration that
              ; does not exist, so a runtime `if` over both would be
              ; AX3001 on the arm the query exists to avoid.
              {
                (let cq (expCondQuery (nodeAChild tpl)))
                (if (let (Some cq) cq)
                  {
                    (let v
                      (if (strEq cq "syntax/same")
                        (expEvalSame env (spineArgVec (nodeAChild tpl) vecNew) isp)
                        (expEvalDefined env (spineArgVec (nodeAChild tpl) vecNew) params args isp)))
                    (if (&& (== (nodeTag v) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName v) "true"))
                      (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp)
                      (if (&& (== (nodeTag v) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName v) "false"))
                        (substTpl (nodeCChild tpl) params args env isp)
                        ; the refusal's poison: keep the tree shaped
                        v))
                  }
                  (nodeBuildEIf (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                    (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp)
                    (substTpl (nodeCChild tpl) params args env isp) isp))
              }
              ; --- binders: value first, then body under the new name ----
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                ; A binder that is a macro parameter keeps the caller's
                ; name and takes no scope record (`expBinderParam`); one the
                ; template introduced is gensymed and recorded.
                {
                  (let val (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp))
                  (let markS (vecLen (expScTplVec env)))
                  (let pb (expBinderParam (nodeAName tpl) params args env isp))
                  {
                    (let new
                      (if (!= (as pb Int) 0)
                        pb
                        (expFresh env (nodeAName tpl))))
                    {
                      (if (== (as pb Int) 0)
                        (expScPushTpl env (nodeAName tpl) new))
                      {
                        (let body (substTpl (nodeCChild tpl) params args env isp))
                        {
                          (expScTruncTpl env markS)
                          (nodeBuildBinding tag new val body isp)
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  {
                    (let markS (vecLen (expScTplVec env)))
                    (let ps (substLamParams (nodeANames tpl) params args env isp))
                    {
                      (let body (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp))
                      {
                        (expScTruncTpl env markS)
                        (nodeBuildELam ps body 0 isp)
                      }
                    }
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                    (nodeBuildEBegin (substVec (nodeAChildren tpl) params args env isp) 0 0 isp)
                    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                        ; --- `(region r body)`: `r` is a BINDER ------------
                        ; A macro parameter in that position keeps the
                        ; caller's name (`expBinderParam`); one the
                        ; template introduced is gensymed, exactly as a
                        ; `let`'s is, so a template's `r` never shadows
                        ; an invocation site's and never draws AX3058
                        ; against it. The scope record is pushed for the
                        ; body's diagnostics: nested introduced scopes keep
                        ; distinct names after expansion.
                        {
                          (let markS (vecLen (expScTplVec env)))
                          (let pb (expBinderParam (nodeAName tpl) params args env isp))
                          {
                            (let new
                              (if (!= (as pb Int) 0)
                                pb
                                (expFresh env (nodeAName tpl))))
                            {
                              (if (== (as pb Int) 0)
                                (expScPushTpl env (nodeAName tpl) new))
                              {
                                (let body (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp))
                                {
                                  (expScTruncTpl env markS)
                                  (nodeBuildERegion new body isp isp)
                                }
                              }
                            }
                          }
                        }
                        (nodeBuildEWhile (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                          (substVec (nodeBChildren tpl) params args env isp) 0 isp))
                      ; --- `(set name value)`: the name is a REFERENCE ------------
                      ; A null target (AX3022, MAC-CAP-3a) poisons the whole
                      ; node rather than emitting the template's own name.
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                        {
                          (let tgt (substName (nodeAName tpl) params args env isp))
                          (if (== (as tgt Int) 0)
                            (mkEInt 0)
                            (nodeBuildESet tgt (substTpl (nodeBChild tpl) params args env isp) 0
                              isp))
                        }
                        ; A FIELD NAME substitutes only from an active for-binding -
                        ; `s.f` with f iterating (syntax/fields S) names each field
                        ; in turn (section 10.3's getters). A macro PARAMETER in
                        ; field-name position stays verbatim, as it always has: the
                        ; name lives in word b as a bare string, and rewriting it
                        ; against params would change what existing templates mean.
                        ; That silence is AX3074's (MAC-EXP-14b), warned below.
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
                          (nodeBuildETry (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp) 0 0 isp)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            {
                              (expWarnNamePos params (nodeBName tpl) 1 env isp "a field name"
                                "rename the parameter to mean the literal field, or bind the field name with an enclosing `syntax/for`")
                              (nodeBuildEField (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                                (if (let (Some element) (expForLookup env (nodeBName tpl)))
                                  element
                                  (nodeBName tpl))
                                0 isp)
                            }
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                              {
                                (expWarnNamePos params (nodeBName tpl) 1 env isp "a field name"
                                  "rename the parameter to mean the literal field, or bind the field name with an enclosing `syntax/for`")
                                (nodeBuildESetf (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                                  (if (let (Some element) (expForLookup env (nodeBName tpl)))
                                    element
                                    (nodeBName tpl))
                                  (substTpl (nodeCChild tpl) params args env isp) isp)
                              }
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                                {
                                  (let en (expHandleParamName (nodeBChildren tpl) params 0))
                                  {
                                    (if (! (== (as en Int) 0))
                                      (expWarnNamePos params en 0 env isp "a handled effect name"
                                        "rename the parameter to mean the literal effect"))
                                    (nodeBuildEHandle
                                      (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                                      (nodeBChildren tpl)
                                      (substTpl (nodeCChild tpl) params args env isp) isp)
                                  }
                                }
                                (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                                  (nodeBuildEMatch (substTpl (nodeAChild tpl) params args env isp)
                                    (substArms (nodeBChildren tpl) params args env isp) 0 isp)
                                  ; `cond` is AX2004, so no template holds
                                  ; one: the refusal below owns the arm.
                                  ; --- the refusal ------------------------------------------
                                  ; Reached only by a tag added to the parser and not to this
                                  ; function. Returning `tpl` here is what made eight separate
                                  ; miscompiles silent; refusing is what stops the ninth.
                                  {
                                    (expEmit env
                                      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3021" "macro-template-unsupported"
                                        isp
                                        "this macro template contains a form the expander cannot substitute into"
                                        None
                                        (Some
                                          "rewrite the template without this form, or add a case for it to `substTpl` in core/expand.ax")
                                        "the expansion was refused rather than emitted, because substituting nothing here would silently leave the template's own identifiers in the generated code"))
                                    (mkEInt 0)
                                  }))))))))))))))
    }))

; A lambda's parameter list is a Vec of NAMES. Each is a binder the
; template introduces and is renamed - unless it is a MACRO parameter,
; in which case the caller named it and it keeps that name
; (`expBinderParam`), which is what lets a template take the binder of
; the lambda it builds from its own caller.
(pub :: substLamParams (-> Names Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Strings))
(pub fn (substLamParams ps params args env isp)
  (substLamParamsIn ps params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: substLamParamsIn (-> Names Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Strings Int Strings))
(pub fn (substLamParamsIn ps params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    acc
    {
      (let pb (expBinderParam (vecGetStr ps i) params args env isp))
      (if (!= (as pb Int) 0)
        (substLamParamsIn ps params args env isp (vecPush acc pb) (+ i 1))
        {
          (let new (expFresh env (vecGetStr ps i)))
          {
            (expScPushTpl env (vecGetStr ps i) new)
            (substLamParamsIn ps params args env isp (vecPush acc new) (+ i 1))
          }
        })
    }))

; `(set name v)` names a binder rather than evaluating an expression.
; A renamed template binder answers its new name; a macro parameter
; answers the argument's name, which only exists if the argument is
; itself a plain identifier - `(set (f 1) 2)` has no meaning and the
; parser would not have produced it, but a PARAMETER standing in that
; position can be handed any expression at all, so the case is real
; and is refused rather than dropped.
(pub :: substName (-> String Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (substName name params args env isp)
  {
    (let template (expScResolveTpl env name))
    (if (let (Some binding) template)
      binding.spelling
      (match (expParamIndex params name)
        ((None) (expQualify env name))
        ((Some idx)
          {
            (let a (vecGet args idx))
            (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
              (nodeAName a)
              {
                (expEmit env
                  (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3022" "macro-set-target" isp
                    "a macro parameter used as the target of `set` was given something that is not a variable"
                    None
                    (Some
                      "pass a variable name for this parameter, or rewrite the template so the parameter is not a `set` target")
                    "`set` assigns to a binding, so its target has to be a name; the argument here is an expression"))
                ; Poison, not the parameter's own name: returning `name`
                ; would emit the template's identifier into the tree,
                ; which only the driver's error gate keeps from codegen
                ; (MAC-CAP-3a). A null name tells the caller to poison
                ; the whole node, whose span 0 then suppresses cascades.
                (as 0 String)
              })
          }
        )))
  })

(pub :: substVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (substVec xs params args env isp)
  (substVecIn xs params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: substVecIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (substVecIn xs params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    acc
    (substVecIn xs params args env isp (vecPush acc (substTpl (vecGet xs i) params args env isp))
      (+ i 1))))

; A match arm: the pattern's binders are renamed and the body is
; substituted UNDER those renames, so a template binder and a
; substituted argument that happen to share a name stay distinct.
(pub :: substArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (substArms arms params args env isp)
  ; An arm followed by the marker diverts to the splice loop;
  ; otherwise this is the ordinary arm walk, byte for byte what it
  ; was. The pre-scan costs one pass over short vectors and keeps
  ; the hot path - and its goldens - untouched.
  (if (== (armsHasSpliceMarker arms 0) 1)
    (substArmsSplice arms params args env isp vecNew 0)
    (substArmsIn arms params args env isp vecNew 0)))

; Is element `i` a plain arm followed by the marker? `syntax/for`
; forms manage their own repetition and never trigger this, even
; beside a marker - the marker after one falls through to whatever
; a stray marker does today, which is none of this function's
; new business.
(pub :: armsHasSpliceMarker (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (armsHasSpliceMarker arms i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (vecLen arms))
    0
    (if (&& (!= (nodeTag (vecGet arms i)) TAG_M_FOR) (== (isEllipsisArm (vecGet arms (+ i 1))) 1))
      1
      (armsHasSpliceMarker arms (+ i 1)))))

; `arm ...` in an arm list: rebuild `arm` once per absorbed
; element, with each sequence-bound name taking its element - the
; match-arm half of what `expSpliceNested` does for application
; spines, and the half the §10.5 machine macro's step function
; needs. Ordinary arms delegate back to `substArmsIn` on a
; singleton, so the single-arm logic lives in exactly one place;
; a marker on its own diagnoses (mirroring the ordinary-code
; refusal) and splices a poison arm, so no shape falls through to
; a null-bodied arm the checker would walk into.
;
; Two diagnosed misuses, neither silent: no sequence in the arm
; (the `...` follows nothing that repeats), and a constructor
; pattern in the arm (its binders are invisible to the
; expression-side walk below, so splicing it would leave them
; unbound - refused plainly rather than miscompiled).
(pub :: substArmsSplice
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (substArmsSplice arms params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (isEllipsisArm arm) 1)
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env "..."
            "starts nothing - a repetition marker follows the arm it repeats"
            "drop it, or move it directly after the repeated arm" isp)
          (substArmsSplice arms params args env isp
            (vecPush acc (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
            (+ i 1))
        }
        (if (&& (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
              (&& (< (+ i 1) (vecLen arms)) (== (isEllipsisArm (vecGet arms (+ i 1))) 1)))
          (substArmsSplice arms params args env isp
            (expAppendBuilt acc (expSpliceOneArm arm params args env isp) 0) (+ i 2))
          {
            (let one
              (substArmsIn
                {
                  (let single vecNew)
                  {
                    (vecPush single arm)
                    single
                  }
                }
                params args env isp vecNew 0))
            (substArmsSplice arms params args env isp (expAppendBuilt acc one 0) (+ i 1))
          }))
    }))

; One arm spliced: per-element rebuilds through the ordinary
; single-arm path, with each sequence-bound name taking its
; element. Answers a vec of arms; on a diagnosed misuse the vec
; holds one poison arm (`_` answering 0, mirroring the
; ordinary-code refusal), because the diagnostic is the signal and
; a failed match would only add a follow-on error.
; The per-element substitution appends singles onto the shared
; params/args (last wins) and pops them after: nothing appends to
; those vecs during substitution, so the truncation restores
; exactly what was there.
;
; Two diagnosed misuses, neither silent: no sequence in the arm,
; and a repeat inside the arm's body. Constructor patterns splice
; like anything else - a fixed test rebuilds as itself and a
; sequence-bound field tests its element (`expSubstPatSeq`) - with
; a parameter standing in binder position for a non-name still
; refused downstream as `AX3035`.
(pub :: expSpliceOneArm (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSpliceOneArm arm params args env isp)
  {
    (let seqs (expSeqBindsVec env))
    (if (== (vecLen seqs) 0)
      {
        (let poison vecNew)
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "follows nothing that repeats"
            "repetition lives in a rule-form macro's match: move the match there, or drop the marker"
            isp)
          (vecPush poison (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
          poison
        }
      }
      (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild arm)) 1)
        {
          (let poison vecNew)
          {
            (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "nests a repeat inside a repeat"
              "repeat one level only - splice `arm ...`, never an arm that splices again inside"
              isp)
            (vecPush poison
              (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
            poison
          }
        }
        {
          (let first (vecGet seqs 0))
          (let n (vecLen first.forms))
          {
            (let markP (vecLen params))
            {
              (let markA (vecLen args))
              {
                (let mut j 0)
                {
                  (let mut bad 0)
                  {
                    (let out vecNew)
                    {
                      (while (< j n)
                        (if (== bad 1)
                          (set j n)
                          {
                            (let mut k 0)
                            {
                              (while (< k (vecLen seqs))
                                {
                                  (let binding (vecGet seqs k))
                                  (vecPush params binding.name)
                                  (vecPush args (vecGet binding.forms j))
                                  (set k (+ k 1))
                                })
                              ; The pattern with this element's values
                              ; in test position BEFORE the single-arm
                              ; path renames binders: a bare
                              ; sequence-bound name there must become
                              ; the constructor test it names, not a
                              ; binder matching everything
                              ; (expSubstPatSeq). The body keeps the
                              ; ordinary substitution below.
                              ; A singleton substitution cannot fail -
                              ; every arm shape builds exactly one - so
                              ; this is the tripwire, not the path: poison
                              ; the match loudly rather than dropping an
                              ; arm silently.
                              {
                                (let one
                                  (substArmsIn
                                    {
                                      (let single vecNew)
                                      {
                                        (vecPush single
                                          (nodeBuildMArm
                                            (expSubstPatSeq (nodeAChild arm) (expSeqBindsVec env) j
                                              isp)
                                            (nodeBChild arm) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
                                        single
                                      }
                                    }
                                    params args env isp vecNew 0))
                                (if (!= (vecLen one) 1)
                                  {
                                    (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "an arm rebuild failed mid-splice"
                                      "this is an internal inconsistency, not a program error - the single-arm path builds exactly one arm"
                                      isp)
                                    (vecPush out
                                      (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0
                                        (nodeSpan arm)))
                                    (set bad 1)
                                    (set j n)
                                  }
                                  {
                                    (vecPush out (vecGet one 0))
                                    (set j (+ j 1))
                                  })
                              }
                              (while (> (vecLen params) markP)
                                (vecPop params))
                              (while (> (vecLen args) markA)
                                (vecPop args))
                            }
                          }))
                      out
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }))
  })

; A spliced arm's pattern with one element's values in test
; position, BEFORE the single-arm path below renames binders. A
; bare identifier in pattern position BINDS (expRenamePat's
; header), so substituting the element there answers a binder
; matching everything - the same silent wildcard
; expSubstPatForSpine's note records for the `syntax/for` path,
; where `(deriveShow Colour)` generated a first arm that swallowed
; every scrutinee at exit 0. A sequence-bound bare name whose
; element is itself a bare name therefore rebuilds as TAG_P_CON0,
; the nullary-constructor test `(...)` parses as; every other
; shape is left for the ordinary binder path, which owns it.
;
; Only the splice loop above reads this; ordinary arms never do.
;
; Parsed constructor patterns ride along: a fixed nullary test
; (`TAG_P_CON0`) names no sequence and rebuilds as itself, and a
; named pattern (`TAG_P_CONNAMED`) maps this same rule over its
; field sub-patterns - a sequence-bound field with a bare-name
; element tests that constructor, anything else stays for the
; ordinary binder path below, which owns it (and refuses a
; parameter standing in binder position for a non-name, `AX3035`,
; rather than binding silently).
(pub :: expSubstPatSeq (-> ASTNode SeqBinds Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstPatSeq p seqs j isp)
  (if (nodeSame p nilNode)
    p
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag p))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (let (Some elems) (expSeqLookup seqs (nodeAName p)))
          {
            (let e (vecGet elems j))
            (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
              (nodeBuildPCon0 (nodeAName e) 0 0 (nodeSpan p))
              p)
          }
          p)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (expSubstPatSeqSpine p seqs j isp)
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (nodeBuildPConnamed (nodeAName p) (nodeBChildren p)
              (expSubstPatSeqVec (nodeCChildren p) seqs j isp) (nodeSpan p))
            p)))
    }))

(pub :: expSubstPatSeqVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) SeqBinds Int Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSubstPatSeqVec ps seqs j isp)
  (expSubstPatSeqVecIn ps seqs j isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: expSubstPatSeqVecIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) SeqBinds Int Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSubstPatSeqVecIn ps seqs j isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    acc
    (expSubstPatSeqVecIn ps seqs j isp (vecPush acc (expSubstPatSeq (vecGet ps i) seqs j isp))
      (+ i 1))))

; One application spine: the head names the constructor and must
; survive as a reference (expRenamePatSpine's rule), so a
; sequence-bound head takes the element's name bare; every
; argument is a test position and takes the full rule above.
(pub :: expSubstPatSeqSpine (-> ASTNode SeqBinds Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstPatSeqSpine p seqs j isp)
  {
    (let head (spineHead p))
    (let pargs (spineArgVec p vecNew))
    {
      (let newHead
        (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (let (Some elems) (expSeqLookup seqs (nodeAName head)))
            {
              (let e (vecGet elems j))
              (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
                (mkEVarAt (nodeAName e) (nodeSpan head))
                head)
            }
            head)
          head))
      (let out vecNew)
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen pargs)
          (vecPush out (expSubstPatSeq (vecGet pargs i) seqs j isp)))
        (expRebuildSpine newHead out isp 0)
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: substArmsIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (substArmsIn arms params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
        {
          (expSpliceForArms arm params args env isp acc)
          (substArmsIn arms params args env isp acc (+ i 1))
        }
        ; For-variable substitution runs BEFORE the binder
        ; renaming, on EVERY arm while an iteration is active -
        ; deriveEq's inner `(match b ((C) true) ...)` is the
        ; for-arm's BODY, so its patterns arrive here through
        ; the ordinary TAG_E_MATCH walk, not through the
        ; TAG_M_FOR dispatch above.
        {
          (let markS (vecLen (expScTplVec env)))
          {
            (let pat
              (expRenamePat (expSubstPatFor (nodeAChild arm) params args env isp) params args env
                isp))
            {
              (let body (substTpl (nodeBChild arm) params args env isp))
              {
                (expScTruncTpl env markS)
                (substArmsIn arms params args env isp (vecPush acc (nodeBuildMArm pat body 0 isp))
                  (+ i 1))
              }
            }
          }
        })
    }))

; Splice a TAG_M_FOR: evaluate the sequence, then instantiate the
; template arms once per element with the binding pushed - through
; substArmsIn itself, so a NESTED syntax/for inside the template arms
; splices the same way. Each element's instantiation gets its own
; binder renames (the mark/trunc inside substArmsIn), so two spliced
; arms can never share a renamed binder. A refused sequence splices
; one poison arm - `_` answering 0 - because the REPL runs the
; checker even after expansion refused, and the tree it walks must be
; well-formed.
(pub :: expSpliceForArms (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceForArms farm params args env isp acc)
  {
    (let seqs
      (expIterSeqsOf env (nodeBChild farm) params args
        (if (!= (nodeSpan farm) 0)
          (nodeSpan farm)
          isp)
        isp))
    (if (let (Some seqs) seqs)
      (expSpliceForElems farm params args env isp acc seqs (expIterLen seqs) 0)
      {
        (vecPush acc (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" isp) (mkEInt 0) 0 isp))
        0
      })
  })

(pub :: expSpliceForElems
  (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expSpliceForElems farm params args env isp acc seqs n j)
  (if (>= j n)
    0
    {
      (let mark (vecLen (expForStackVec env)))
      (let markC (vecLen (expScChainVec env)))
      {
        (expIterPush env seqs j 0)
        (expScPushLoop env)
        (substArmsIn (nodeCChildren farm) params args env isp acc 0)
        (expTrunc (expForStackVec env) mark)
        (expScTruncLoop env markC)
        (expSpliceForElems farm params args env isp acc seqs n (+ j 1))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Application spines.
;
; `(when a b)` is App(App(when, a), b), so a macro's arguments are the
; spine's, collected left to right. The emit-time expander flattened
; the spine before it ever consulted the macro table, which is why
; `((m a) b)` handed BOTH a and b to a one-parameter `m` and the outer
; application vanished. The spine is still flattened here - a
; seven-argument call is a seven-deep spine and has to keep working -
; but a spine longer than the macro's arity now re-applies the surplus
; to the expansion instead of dropping it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The walk over ORDINARY code. Its two jobs are to find macro
; invocations and to know which names are locally bound at each point,
; because a local outranks a macro of the same name.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandExpr (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
; The budgeted wrapper. `expandExprInner` is the walk itself; this
; counts the node, tracks the walk's depth on the way in and out, and
; stops the recursion at the limits rather than at the guard page.
; Only expansion-produced nodes count (MAC-EXP-11a): the parser's own
; limits already bound source depth, and counting source nodes lets a
; large macro-free file hit a budget whose message blames macros that
; do not exist. Inside an instantiation (`expDepth` > 0) every node
; visited is expansion-produced (or reached through one), so both
; budgets apply there and only there.
(pub fn (expandExpr e env bs)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    nilNode
    (if (== env.blown 1)
      e
      (if (== (expDepth env) 0)
        (expandExprInner e env bs)
        {
          (let d (+ env.walk 1))
          (let n (+ env.nodes 1))
          {
            (set env.walk d)
            (set env.nodes n)
            {
              (let r
                (if (> d expMaxWalk)
                  {
                    (expBlow env (nodeSpan e)
                      (concat
                        (concat "macro expansion nested deeper than the limit of "
                          (expFmtInt expMaxWalk))
                        " forms"))
                    e
                  }
                  (if (> n expMaxNodes)
                    {
                      (expBlow env (nodeSpan e)
                        (concat
                          (concat "macro expansion produced more than the limit of "
                            (expFmtInt expMaxNodes))
                          " forms"))
                      e
                    }
                    (expandExprInner e env bs))))
              {
                (set env.walk (- d 1))
                r
              }
            }
          }
        }))))

(pub :: expandExprInner (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandExprInner e env bs)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (let sp (nodeSpan e))
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT)
            (|| (== tag TAG_E_STR) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_E_CHAR))))
        e
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (expandVarRef e env bs)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
            (expandApp e env bs)
            (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
              (nodeBuildEIf (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs) (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs)
                (expandExpr (nodeCChild e) env bs) sp)
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (let val (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs))
                  (let mark (vecLen bs))
                  {
                    (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
                    {
                      (let body (expandExpr (nodeCChild e) env bs))
                      {
                        (expTrunc bs mark)
                        (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstText (nodeAName e)) (AstChild val) (AstChild body))
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  {
                    (let mark (vecLen bs))
                    {
                      (expEnterSyntaxBindings e env bs)
                      {
                        (let body (qmBody env (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs)))
                        {
                          (expTrunc bs mark)
                          (expSyntaxRebuild e (AstNames (nodeANames e)) (AstChild body) (AstWord 0))
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                    (nodeBuildEBegin (expandVec (nodeAChildren e) env bs) 0 0 sp)
                    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                        (nodeBuildERegion (nodeAText e) (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs)
                          (nodeC e) sp)
                        (nodeBuildEWhile (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs)
                          (expandVec (nodeBChildren e) env bs) 0 sp))
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                        (nodeBuildESet (nodeAText e) (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs) 0 sp)
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                          (nodeBuildEField (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs) (nodeBName e) 0 sp)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
                            (nodeBuildETry (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs) 0 0 sp)
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                              (nodeBuildESetf (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs) (nodeBName e)
                                (expandExpr (nodeCChild e) env bs) sp)
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                                (nodeBuildEHandle (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs)
                                  (nodeBChildren e) (expandExpr (nodeCChild e) env bs) sp)
                                (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                                  (nodeBuildEMatch (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs)
                                    (expandArms (nodeBChildren e) env bs) 0 sp)
                                  ; Anything else has no expression children to walk - a pattern
                                  ; node reached out of position, or a tag this pass does not
                                  ; recognise. Unlike a TEMPLATE, ordinary code holds no
                                  ; parameter references, so passing it through is safe.
                                  ; (`cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                                  ; none, so no arm walks one.)
                                  e))))))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: expPushNames (-> Names ScopeBinds MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expPushNames ns bs env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ns))
    0
    {
      (expPushLocal bs env (vecGetStr ns i))
      (expPushNames ns bs env (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expandVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expandVec xs env bs)
  (expandVecIn xs env bs vecNew 0))

(pub :: expandVecIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expandVecIn xs env bs acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    acc
    (expandVecIn xs env bs (vecPush acc (expandExpr (vecGet xs i) env bs)) (+ i 1))))

; A match arm's pattern binds names for its body. They are NOT renamed
; here - this is the user's own code, not a template - but they must
; enter the bound set, or a macro sharing a name with an arm binder
; would rewrite the binder's uses.
(pub :: expandArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expandArms arms env bs)
  (expandArmsIn arms env bs vecNew 0))

(pub :: expandArmsIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expandArmsIn arms env bs acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    ; A bare `...` where an arm stands splices in a rule template
    ; and means nothing in ordinary code: refuse it the way the
    ; `syntax/for` refusal below does, with a well-formed poison
    ; arm so the checker walks something shaped like an arm.
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (isEllipsisArm arm) 1)
        {
          (expQueryErr env (nodeSpan arm)
            "`...` splices a repeated template arm, and stands in no ordinary match"
            "repetition lives in a rule-form macro's template: move the match there, or write the arms by hand"
            env.declmod)
          (expandArmsIn arms env bs
            (vecPush acc (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
            (+ i 1))
        }
        (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
          ; `syntax/for` in an ordinary match - the user's own code,
          ; outside any macro template - has no iteration to splice
          ; and is refused, then REPLACED with a well-formed poison
          ; arm: the REPL runs the checker even after expansion
          ; refused, and checkMatch reads every arm as TAG_M_ARM.
          {
            (expQueryErr env (nodeSpan arm)
              "syntax/for stands in a macro template's match, not in ordinary code"
              "iteration is an expansion-time facility: move the match into a declaration macro's template, or write the arms by hand"
              env.declmod)
            (expandArmsIn arms env bs
              (vecPush acc
                (nodeBuildMArm (mkEVarAt "_" (nodeSpan arm)) (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
              (+ i 1))
          }
          {
            (let mark (vecLen bs))
            {
              (expEnterSyntaxBindings arm env bs)
              {
                (let body (expandExpr (nodeBChild arm) env bs))
                {
                  (expTrunc bs mark)
                  (expandArmsIn arms env bs
                    (vecPush acc
                      (expSyntaxRebuild arm (AstChild (nodeAChild arm)) (AstChild body)
                        (AstWord 0)))
                    (+ i 1))
                }
              }
            }
          }))
    }))

(pub :: expPatBinders (-> ASTNode ScopeBinds MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expPatBinders p bs env)
  (if (nodeSame p nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag p))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        {
          (if (&& (! (strEq (nodeAName p) "_"))
                (&& (! (strEq (nodeAName p) "true")) (! (strEq (nodeAName p) "false"))))
            (expPushLocal bs env (nodeAName p)))
          0
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (expPatBindersSpine p bs env)
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (expPatBindersVec (nodeCChildren p) bs env 0)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: expPatBindersSpine (-> ASTNode ScopeBinds MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expPatBindersSpine p bs env)
  (if (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (expPatBindersSpine (nodeAChild p) bs env)
      (expPatBinders (nodeBChild p) bs env)
      0
    }
    0))

(pub :: expPatBindersVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) ScopeBinds MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expPatBindersVec ps bs env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    0
    {
      (expPatBinders (vecGet ps i) bs env)
      (expPatBindersVec ps bs env (+ i 1))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A bare identifier. A zero-parameter macro is invoked this way -
; `(five)` erases its parens and arrives here - so the name is looked
; up, but only after the bound set has had its say.
;
; The bound set does NOT have its say over a reference the template
; owns: word 10 (`nodeDefScope`) marks a free identifier `substTpl`
; resolved at the macro's definition site, and a caller's `let` of
; the same spelling is a different variable rather than a rebinding
; of it. Letting it win here is what made a helper - or a recursive
; self-call - invoked from a template stop expanding the moment the
; caller bound the name: the unexpanded call survived to codegen,
; where `check` had said OK and `opt` died on a symbol nothing
; defined. `checkVar` and `emitVar` already skip locals for a stamped
; reference; this walk is the third resolver and asks the same bit.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandVarRef (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandVarRef e env bs)
  (if (strEq (nodeAName e) "|>")
    (expPipeBad env (nodeSpan e) "a pipeline needs a value and at least one stage")
    (if (&& (== (expBound bs (nodeAName e)) 1) (== (nodeDefScope e) 0))
      e
      ; `(asm)` with no arm parses as the bare name.
      (if (strEq (nodeAName e) "asm")
        {
          (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan e)
            "`asm` needs at least one arm, such as `(asm (aarch64 \"nop\"))`")
          (mkEInt 0)
        }
        (match (expFindMacro env (nodeAName e))
          ((None)
            (if (expStructNeedsLabels e env bs)
              (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e)
                "a struct with fields is constructed with labels; use a lambda or named field holes to make a callable constructor")
              e)
          )
          ((Some mac)
            (if (!= (nodeFieldNamesWord mac) 0)
              (if (expMacHasExprRule mac)
                (expandRuleApp mac (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName e)) vecNew env bs (nodeSpan e))
                {
                  (expEmit env
                    (expMarkUnit env
                      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3027" "declaration-macro" (nodeSpan e)
                        (concat (concat "declaration macro `" (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName e)))
                          "` referenced in expression position")
                        None
                        (Some
                          "every rule of this macro generates declarations; invoke it at top level")
                        "an expression position needs a value, and each rule's template here is declarations (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-8)")
                      env.declmod))
                  (mkEInt 0)
                })
              (if (> (vecLen (nodeBNames mac)) 0)
                {
                  (expArityDiag env (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName e)) (vecLen (nodeBNames mac)) 0
                    (nodeSpan e))
                  (mkEInt 0)
                }
                (expandInstantiate mac vecNew env bs (nodeSpan e))))
          ))))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; An application whose head names a macro.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandApp (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandApp e env bs)
  (if (expHasTypeOperand e)
    (expExpandTypedApp e env bs)
    (expandValueApp e env bs)))

(pub :: expandValueApp (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandValueApp e env bs)
  (if (!= (nodeFieldNames e) 0)
    (expandNamedStruct e env bs)
    (if (expStructNeedsLabels (spineHead e) env bs)
      (if (nodeGeneratedCall e)
        (match (expNamedStructDecl env (spineHead e))
          ((ExpStructFound decl)
            (expandNamedStruct
              (setCtorFieldNames e (expNamedFieldNames (nodeBChildren decl) vecNew 0)) env bs)
          )
          (_ (expandNamedStruct e env bs)))
        (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e)
          "struct construction requires a label for every field; positional struct construction is not supported"))
      (expandAppUnlabelled e env bs))))

(pub :: expandAppUnlabelled (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandAppUnlabelled e env bs)
  (if (&& (== (nodeTag (spineHead e)) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName (spineHead e)) "|>"))
    (expandPipe e env bs)
    {
      (let head (spineHead e))
      (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
        (expandAppOrHole e env bs)
        ; A head the template owns (word 10, see `expandVarRef`) is
        ; looked up even when the caller binds the spelling: it is the
        ; macro's own helper or self-call, not the caller's variable.
        (if (&& (== (expBound bs (nodeAName head)) 1) (== (nodeDefScope head) 0))
          (expandAppOrHole e env bs)
          ; `asm` is the built-in inline-assembly macro (MM-FFI-9). It is
          ; asked before the macro table, so no library macro can take
          ; the name, and after the local binders, as a macro is.
          (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "asm")
            (expandAsm e env bs)
            ; `(! b)` is `(if b false true)`, so every later pass reads a
            ; form it already knows. Any other use of `!` stays a call,
            ; and the checker refuses it as it refuses a misused `&&`
            ; (AX3013), from the `Bool -> Bool` row `tcNew` registers.
            ; `true` and `false` resolve to the literals whatever the
            ; caller binds, so the rewrite can't be captured.
            (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName head) "!") (== (spineArgs e 0) 1))
              (nodeBuildEIf (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs) (mkEVarAt "false" (nodeSpan e))
                (mkEVarAt "true" (nodeSpan e)) (nodeSpan e))
              (match (expFindMacro env (nodeAName head))
                ((None) (expandAppOrHole e env bs))
                ((Some mac) (expandMacroApp e head mac env bs)))))))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; INLINE ASSEMBLY (`docs/memory-model.md` MM-FFI-9). `asm` is a
; built-in macro: the compiler computes its expansion, because a
; pattern cannot build the operand constraints LLVM needs.
;
;   (asm (aarch64 "add {r}, {a}, {b}" (out r) (in a x) (in b y))
;        (x86_64 "leaq ({a},{b}), {r}" (out r) (in a x) (in b y)))
;
; lowers to one call of the primitive `__asm.N`. Its first argument is
; a string literal describing every arm, and its N further arguments
; are the arms' operand expressions. No source can spell the `.` in
; that name (the lexer refuses it with AX2001), so only this expansion
; reaches the primitive and the description is always well formed.
; Every later pass sees an ordinary primitive call: the checker types
; each argument as an `Int`, the effect walk adds `Unsafe`
; (`isUnsafePrim`), and codegen emits the arm for the target's
; architecture (`emitPrimAsm`).
;
; An argument is one of three things. An input's value, expanded here.
; An output stored through `into`, as `(set slot 0)`: the checker checks
; it as the `set` it is - a `mut` binding, an `Int`, assignable from
; this frame - and codegen never evaluates it, storing the output in
; that binding instead. Or a `sym` operand, as `(__asm.sym f)`, which
; the checker resolves as a reference to `f` (`checkAsmSym`) and codegen
; reads as the function's symbol.
;
; THE DESCRIPTION, arm after arm with nothing between them:
;
;   ARCH;FLAGS;CONSTRAINTS;NOUT;ANS;OUTS;INS;LINES;FLEN;FILETLEN;TEMPLATE
;
; FLAGS is a letter per option: `p` pure, `m` nomem, `r` readonly, `f`
; preserves_flags, `n` noreturn, `i` intel_syntax, `s` nostack, and
; `a` for `clobber_abi("C")`. CONSTRAINTS are the outputs, the inputs and the
; registers the arm clobbers by name; the memory, flag and ABI clobbers
; are codegen's, which knows the target. NOUT counts the outputs and
; ANS is the one the form answers, or -1. OUTS is `k:i` per output
; stored through `into`: output k, call argument i. INS is the call
; argument of each input in constraint order, `s` marking a `sym`.
; LINES is `start-end` per template line, the line's bytes in FILE,
; which become the call's `!srcloc` so that an assembler error comes
; back to the line that caused it (`asmFailDiags` in driver.ax); it is
; empty when the source file is unknown. FILE and TEMPLATE are
; length-prefixed because they may hold any byte. TEMPLATE keeps the
; literal's source escapes, so it is itself a valid literal body, and
; codegen decodes it with `escBody` like any other literal. `{name}`
; is already `${i}` there, a `const` its decimal text, and a `$` the
; author wrote is `$$`.
;
; A malformed form is one diagnostic, at the part that is wrong: the
; template's own bytes when the literal is where the span says, the
; operand otherwise. It expands to `0` so the checker adds nothing.
; AX3091 is a form that can't be lowered, AX3118 an operand the
; template never names, AX3119 options that contradict each other or
; the form.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The most arguments one form may pass across all its arms: inputs,
; `into` outputs and `sym` operands. The checker registers `__asm.0`
; to `__asm.16` (`tcRegAsmPrims`), so the two numbers move together.
(pub :: expAsmMaxValues Int)
(pub fn (expAsmMaxValues)
  16)

(pub :: expandAsm (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandAsm e env bs)
  {
    (let arms (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (let vals vecNew)
    (let seen vecNew)
    (let mut spec "")
    (let mut bad 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (if (== (vecLen arms) 0)
        (set bad (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan e)
                   "`asm` needs at least one arm, such as `(aarch64 \"nop\")`")))
      (while (&& (== bad 0) (< i (vecLen arms)))
        {
          {
            (let r (expAsmArm (vecGet arms i) env bs vals seen))
            (if (== (strLen r) 0)
              (set bad 1)
              (set spec (concat spec r)))
          }
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (&& (== bad 0) (> (vecLen vals) expAsmMaxValues))
        (set bad (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan e)
                   (concat (concat "this `asm` form passes " (expFmtInt (vecLen vals)))
                     " values across its arms - inputs, `into` outputs and `sym` operands - and the limit is 16"))))
      (if (== bad 1)
        (nodeBuildEInt 0 0 0 (nodeSpan e))
        (expBuildSpine (mkEVarAt (concat "__asm." (expFmtInt (vecLen vals))) (nodeSpan e))
          (expAsmArgv (nodeBuildEStr (concat (concat "\"" spec) "\"") 0 0 (nodeSpan e)) vals) 0))
    }
  })

; The call's arguments: the description, then the operands.
(pub :: expAsmArgv (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expAsmArgv desc vals)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush out desc)
      (for i in 0..(vecLen vals)
        (vecPush out (vecGet vals i)))
      out
    }
  })

; One refusal of an `asm` form, stamped with the unit of the
; declaration being expanded (word 11), as `expandVarRef`'s refusals
; are. Answers 1, so a caller can record the refusal as it reports it.
(pub :: expAsmDiag (-> MEnv Int String String String String Int))
(pub fn (expAsmDiag env sp code slug msg help)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR code slug sp msg None (Some help)
          (concat (concat "run `axiom explain " code) "` for the full form"))
        env.declmod))
    1
  })

; AX3091: a form the compiler can't lower.
(pub :: expAsmBad (-> MEnv Int String Int))
(pub fn (expAsmBad env sp msg)
  (expAsmDiag env sp "AX3091" "inline-asm" msg
    "an `asm` form is `(asm (ARCH \"template\" item...) ...)`, with ARCH `aarch64` or `x86_64`; an item is an operand, `(in name value)`, `(out name)`, `(lateout name)`, `(inout name value)`, `(inlateout name value)`, `(const name n)` or `(sym name f)`, or a `(clobber \"reg\"...)`, `(clobber_abi \"C\")` or `(options ...)`"))

; AX3119: options that contradict each other or the arm.
(pub :: expAsmOptsBad (-> MEnv Int String Int))
(pub fn (expAsmOptsBad env sp msg)
  (expAsmDiag env sp "AX3119" "asm-options" msg
    "`pure` lets the optimiser drop a block whose outputs go unused, so it needs an output and `nomem` or `readonly`; `noreturn` takes no outputs; `att_syntax` and `intel_syntax` are x86_64's; with `clobber_abi` each output names its register"))

; One arm: its description, or "" once it has reported what is wrong.
; `vals` gains the arm's arguments and `seen` its architecture.
(pub :: expAsmArm (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) Strings String))
(pub fn (expAsmArm arm env bs vals seen)
  {
    (let head (spineHead arm))
    (if (|| (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_E_APP) (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR))
      {
        (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan arm)
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_E_VAR) (expAsmArch (nodeAName arm)))
            (concat (concat "each `asm` arm is a list: write `(asm (" (nodeAName arm))
              " \"template\" operand...))`")
            "each `asm` arm is a list opening with its architecture, such as `(aarch64 \"nop\")`"))
        ""
      }
      {
        (let arch (nodeAName head))
        (if (! (expAsmArch arch))
          {
            (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan head)
              (concat (concat "`" arch)
                "` is not an architecture `asm` knows; an arm opens with `aarch64` or `x86_64`"))
            ""
          }
          (if (isSome (expAsmNameAt seen arch 0))
            {
              (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan head)
                (concat (concat "a second `" arch)
                  "` arm; a form has at most one arm per architecture"))
              ""
            }
            {
              (vecPush seen arch)
              (expAsmArmBody arch (spineArgVec arm vecNew) arm env bs vals)
            }))
      })
  })

(pub :: expAsmArch (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmArch a)
  (|| (strEq a "aarch64") (strEq a "x86_64")))

; Is `w` one of the space-separated `words`?
(pub :: expAsmWordIn (-> String String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmWordIn w words)
  {
    (let n (strLen words))
    (let mut s 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut hit false)
    {
      (while (&& (! hit) (<= i n))
        {
          (if (|| (== i n) (== (strByte words i) 32))
            {
              (if (strEq (strSlice words s (- i s)) w)
                (set hit true))
              (set s (+ i 1))
            })
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      hit
    }
  })

; The operand kinds that write a register the form reads back, and
; the ones that take an input slot in the constraint list. `inout` and
; `inlateout` are both.
(pub :: expAsmOutKind (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmOutKind k)
  (expAsmWordIn k "out lateout inout inlateout"))

(pub :: expAsmInKind (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmInKind k)
  (expAsmWordIn k "in inout inlateout sym"))

; One operand of an arm, as written. `kind` is `in`, `out`,
; `lateout`, `inout`, `inlateout`, `const` or `sym`; `reg` the
; register it names, or "" for any general-purpose one; `val` the value
; node of an input, a `const` or a `sym`, else 0; `into` the `mut`
; binding an output is stored in, or "" for the output the form
; answers; `text` a `const`'s decimal value.
(pub struct AsmOp
  (kind : String)
  (name : String)
  (reg : String)
  (node : ASTNode)
  (val : ASTNode)
  (into : String)
  (intoNode : ASTNode)
  (text : String))

; The index of the operand called `nm`, if present.
(pub :: expAsmOpAt (-> (Vec AsmOp) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expAsmOpAt ops nm i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen ops)))
    None
    {
      (let p (vecGet ops i))
      (if (strEq p.name nm)
        (Some i)
        (expAsmOpAt ops nm (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The arm's template and items. A refusal ends the arm: "" comes back
; and nothing is added to `vals`.
(pub :: expAsmArmBody (-> String Nodes ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) String))
(pub fn (expAsmArmBody arch parts arm env bs vals)
  (if (|| (== (vecLen parts) 0) (!= (nodeTag (vecGet parts 0)) TAG_E_STR))
    {
      (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan arm)
        "an `asm` arm's first item after its architecture is the template, a string literal")
      ""
    }
    {
      (let ops vecNew)
      (let regs vecNew)
      (let clobs vecNew)
      (let opts vecNew)
      (let optNodes vecNew)
      (let abis vecNew)
      (let mut bad 0)
      (let mut i 1)
      {
        (while (&& (== bad 0) (< i (vecLen parts)))
          {
            (set bad (expAsmItem arch (vecGet parts i) env bs ops regs clobs opts optNodes abis))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        (if (== bad 0)
          (set bad (expAsmCheckArm arch env ops clobs opts optNodes abis)))
        (if (== bad 1)
          ""
          (expAsmArmDesc arch (vecGet parts 0) env bs vals ops clobs opts abis))
      }
    }))

; One item after the template: an operand, a clobber list,
; `clobber_abi` or an option list, recorded into the arm's vectors.
; Answers 1 once it has reported a refusal, else 0.
(pub :: expAsmItem
  (-> String ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec AsmOp) Strings Strings Strings (Vec ASTNode)
    (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expAsmItem arch it env bs ops regs clobs opts optNodes abis)
  {
    (let kh (spineHead it))
    (if (|| (!= (nodeTag it) TAG_E_APP) (!= (nodeTag kh) TAG_E_VAR))
      (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan it)
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag it) TAG_E_VAR)
              (expAsmWordIn (nodeAName it)
                "in out lateout inout inlateout const sym clobber clobber_abi options"))
          (concat (concat "`(" (nodeAName it))
            ")` is empty: an operand names itself first, and `clobber`, `clobber_abi` and `options` name at least one thing")
          "an `asm` item is an operand such as `(in name value)` or `(out name)`, a `(clobber \"reg\"...)`, a `(clobber_abi \"C\")` or an `(options ...)` list"))
      {
        (let kind (nodeAName kh))
        (let args (spineArgVec it vecNew))
        (if (strEq kind "clobber")
          (expAsmClobbers arch args 0 env regs clobs)
          (if (strEq kind "clobber_abi")
            (expAsmAbi it args env abis)
            (if (strEq kind "options")
              (expAsmOptions args 0 env opts optNodes)
              (if (expAsmWordIn kind "in out lateout inout inlateout const sym")
                (expAsmOperand arch kind it args env bs ops regs)
                (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan kh)
                  (concat (concat "`" kind)
                    "` is not an operand kind; the kinds are `in`, `out`, `lateout`, `inout`, `inlateout`, `const` and `sym`, beside `clobber`, `clobber_abi` and `options`"))))))
      })
  })

; `(clobber_abi "C")`: every register the platform's C calling
; convention lets a callee overwrite, which codegen lists per target
; (`asmAbiClobbers`), so a block may call a C or Axiom function.
(pub :: expAsmAbi (-> ASTNode Nodes MEnv (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expAsmAbi it args env abis)
  (if (> (vecLen abis) 0)
    (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan it) "a second `clobber_abi`; an arm names its calling convention once")
    (if (|| (!= (vecLen args) 1) (!= (nodeTag (vecGet args 0)) TAG_E_STR))
      (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan it)
        "`clobber_abi` takes one calling convention as a string literal: `(clobber_abi \"C\")`")
      {
        (let abi (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName (vecGet args 0))))
        (if (strEq abi "C")
          {
            (vecPush abis it)
            0
          }
          (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args 0))
            (concat (concat "`" abi)
              "` is not a calling convention `clobber_abi` knows; it knows \"C\", the platform's own")))
      })))

; `(options pure nomem ...)`: each a bare word, each once. An arm may
; hold more than one list.
(pub :: expAsmOptions (-> Nodes Int MEnv Strings (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expAsmOptions args i env opts optNodes)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet args i))
      (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
        (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a) "an option is a bare word, as in `(options nomem nostack)`")
        {
          (let w (nodeAName a))
          (if (!
                (expAsmWordIn w
                  "pure nomem readonly preserves_flags noreturn nostack att_syntax intel_syntax"))
            (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a)
              (concat (concat "`" w)
                "` is not an `asm` option; the options are `pure`, `nomem`, `readonly`, `preserves_flags`, `noreturn`, `nostack`, `att_syntax` and `intel_syntax`"))
            (if (isSome (expAsmNameAt opts w 0))
              (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a)
                (concat (concat "`" w) "` is given twice; an arm states an option once"))
              {
                (vecPush opts w)
                (vecPush optNodes a)
                (expAsmOptions args (+ i 1) env opts optNodes)
              }))
        })
    }))

; How an operand kind is written, for a refusal of its shape.
(pub :: expAsmUsage (-> String String))
(pub fn (expAsmUsage kind)
  (if (expAsmWordIn kind "out lateout")
    (concat (concat (concat "`(" kind) " name)` takes a name and, optionally, a register: `(")
      (concat (concat kind " r)` or `(") (concat kind " r \"x0\")`, and may end `into slot`")))
    (if (strEq kind "in")
      "`(in name value)` takes a name, optionally a register, and one value: `(in a x)` or `(in a \"x0\" x)`"
      (if (strEq kind "const")
        "`(const name value)` takes a name and one integer: `(const n 4)`"
        (if (strEq kind "sym")
          "`(sym name function)` takes a name and a top-level function: `(sym f helper)`"
          (concat
            (concat (concat "`(" kind)
              " name value)` takes a name, optionally a register, and one value: `(")
            (concat (concat kind " v x)` or `(")
              (concat kind " v \"x0\" x)`, and may end `into slot`"))))))))

; Why a second operand can't take the name `nm`. An input and an output
; sharing a name is usually one register meant as both, which is what
; `inout` says.
(pub :: expAsmDupWhy (-> String String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmDupWhy k1 k2 nm)
  (if (|| (&& (strEq k1 "in") (expAsmWordIn k2 "out lateout"))
        (&& (strEq k2 "in") (expAsmWordIn k1 "out lateout")))
    (concat (concat "`" nm)
      (concat
        "` names an input and an output; for one register that starts as the value and ends as the result, write `(inout "
        (concat nm " value)`")))
    (concat (concat "a second operand named `" nm) "`; each operand of an arm has its own name")))

; One operand, checked and recorded into `ops`. Answers 1 once it has
; reported a refusal, else 0.
;
; The shapes, each with an optional register after the name except
; `const` and `sym`:
;
;   (in a v)  (out r)  (lateout r)  (inout v x)  (inlateout v x)
;   (const n 4)  (sym f helper)
;
; and an output may end `into slot`, a `mut` binding it is stored in
; rather than answered. `out` and `inout` are early-clobber (`=&r`):
; their register is no input's, so the instructions may write it
; before they have read every input. `lateout` and `inlateout` (`=r`)
; let the register allocator share it with an input, which is right
; only when every input has been read before the output is written.
(pub :: expAsmOperand (-> String String ASTNode Nodes MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec AsmOp) Strings Int))
(pub fn (expAsmOperand arch kind op args env bs ops regs)
  (if (|| (== (vecLen args) 0) (!= (nodeTag (vecGet args 0)) TAG_E_VAR))
    (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan op)
      (concat (concat "`(" kind)
        " ...)` names its operand first, with a bare name the template writes as `{name}`"))
    {
      (let n (vecLen args))
      (let hasInto
        (&& (>= n 3)
          (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet args (- n 2))) TAG_E_VAR)
            (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet args (- n 2))) "into"))))
      (let body
        (if hasInto
          (- n 2)
          n))
      (let hasReg
        (&& (> body 1)
          (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet args 1)) TAG_E_STR)
            (expAsmWordIn kind "in out lateout inout inlateout"))))
      (let want
        (if (expAsmWordIn kind "out lateout")
          1
          2))
      (let nm (nodeAName (vecGet args 0)))
      (if (&& hasInto (! (expAsmOutKind kind)))
        (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args (- n 2)))
          (concat (concat "`into` stores an output, and `(" kind) " ...)` has none"))
        (if (&& hasInto (!= (nodeTag (vecGet args (- n 1))) TAG_E_VAR))
          (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args (- n 1)))
            "`into` names the `mut` binding the output is stored in")
          (if (!= body
                (+ want
                  (if hasReg
                    1
                    0)))
            (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan op) (expAsmUsage kind))
            {
              (let reg
                (if hasReg
                  (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName (vecGet args 1)))
                  ""))
              (let regBad
                (if hasReg
                  (expAsmOperandRegWhy arch (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName (vecGet args 1))))
                  ""))
              (let prev (expAsmOpAt ops nm 0))
              (if (> (strLen regBad) 0)
                (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args 1)) regBad)
                (if (&& hasReg (isSome (expAsmNameAt regs reg 0)))
                  (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args 1))
                    (concat (concat "`" reg)
                      "` is named twice in this arm; an operand's register is its own"))
                  (if (let (Some prev) prev)
                    {
                      (let p (vecGet ops prev))
                      (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan (vecGet args 0)) (expAsmDupWhy p.kind kind nm))
                    }
                    (expAsmOperandValue kind op args env bs ops regs reg hasInto body))))
            })))
    }))

; The rest of an operand once its name and register are good: the
; value a `const` folds to, the function a `sym` names, and the record.
(pub :: expAsmOperandValue
  (-> String ASTNode Nodes MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec AsmOp) Strings String Bool Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmOperandValue kind op args env bs ops regs reg hasInto body)
  {
    (let nm (nodeAName (vecGet args 0)))
    (let val
      (if (expAsmWordIn kind "out lateout")
        nilNode
        (vecGet args (- body 1))))
    (let intoNode
      (if hasInto
        (vecGet args (- (vecLen args) 1))
        nilNode))
    (if (strEq kind "const")
      {
        (let t (expAsmConstText val))
        (if (== (strLen t) 0)
          (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan val)
            "a `const` operand is an integer the compiler can work out: a literal, or `+`, `-` and `*` over literals")
          (expAsmPushOp ops regs
            (AsmOp
              :kind kind
              :name nm
              :reg reg
              :node op
              :val val
              :into ""
              :intoNode nilNode
              :text t)))
      }
      (if (strEq kind "sym")
        (if (!= (nodeTag val) TAG_E_VAR)
          (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan val)
            "a `sym` operand names a top-level function, as in `(sym f helper)`")
          (if (&& (== (expBound bs (nodeAName val)) 1) (== (nodeDefScope val) 0))
            (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan val)
              (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName val))
                "` is a local binding; a `sym` operand names a top-level function, which has a symbol"))
            (expAsmPushOp ops regs
              (AsmOp
                :kind kind
                :name nm
                :reg reg
                :node op
                :val val
                :into ""
                :intoNode nilNode
                :text ""))))
        (expAsmPushOp ops regs
          (AsmOp
            :kind kind
            :name nm
            :reg reg
            :node op
            :val val
            :into
              (if hasInto
                (nodeAName intoNode)
                "")
            :intoNode intoNode
            :text ""))))
  })

(pub :: expAsmPushOp (-> (Vec AsmOp) Strings AsmOp Int))
(pub fn (expAsmPushOp ops regs p)
  {
    (if (> (strLen p.reg) 0)
      (vecPush regs p.reg))
    (vecPush ops p)
    0
  })

; A `const` operand's decimal text, or "" when it is not an integer
; the expander can work out: a literal, or `+`, `-` and `*` over such.
(pub :: expAsmConstText (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (expAsmConstText e)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    (if (expAsmFold e v)
      (expAsmIntText (vecGet v 0))
      "")
  })

(pub :: expAsmFold (-> ASTNode Ints Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmFold e out)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_INT)
    {
      (vecPush out (nodeA e))
      true
    }
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP) (== (nodeTag (spineHead e)) TAG_E_VAR))
      {
        (let op (nodeAName (spineHead e)))
        (let xs (spineArgVec e vecNew))
        (let l vecNew)
        (let r vecNew)
        (if (&& (== (vecLen xs) 2) (expAsmWordIn op "+ - *"))
          (if (&& (expAsmFold (vecGet xs 0) l) (expAsmFold (vecGet xs 1) r))
            {
              (vecPush out
                (if (strEq op "+")
                  (+ (vecGet l 0) (vecGet r 0))
                  (if (strEq op "-")
                    (- (vecGet l 0) (vecGet r 0))
                    (* (vecGet l 0) (vecGet r 0)))))
              true
            }
            false)
          false)
      }
      false)))

; Decimal, with a sign: `expFmtInt` covers the naturals, and the most
; negative word has no positive twin, so the last digit is taken first.
(pub :: expAsmIntText (-> Int String))
(pub fn (expAsmIntText n)
  (if (>= n 0)
    (expFmtInt n)
    {
      (let q (/ n 10))
      (let r (% n 10))
      (concat "-"
        (concat
          (if (== q 0)
            ""
            (expFmtInt (- 0 q)))
          (expFmtInt (- 0 r))))
    }))

; `(clobber "x9" "v0")`: each register the instructions write that no
; operand names. `memory`, `cc` and `flags` are clobbered unless an
; option says otherwise, so naming one adds nothing - and contradicts
; `nomem` or `preserves_flags`, which `expAsmCheckArm` refuses. They
; are kept in `clobs` for that, and out of `regs`.
(pub :: expAsmClobbers (-> String Nodes Int MEnv Strings Strings Int))
(pub fn (expAsmClobbers arch args i env regs clobs)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet args i))
      (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_STR)
        (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a)
          "`clobber` names registers as string literals, such as `(clobber \"x9\")`")
        {
          (let r (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName a)))
          (if (expAsmWordIn r "memory cc flags")
            {
              (vecPush clobs r)
              (expAsmClobbers arch args (+ i 1) env regs clobs)
            }
            {
              (let why (expAsmClobberRegWhy arch r))
              (if (> (strLen why) 0)
                (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a) why)
                (if (isSome (expAsmNameAt regs r 0))
                  (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan a)
                    (concat (concat "`" r)
                      "` is named twice in this arm; a clobbered register can't also hold an operand"))
                  {
                    (vecPush regs r)
                    (vecPush clobs r)
                    (expAsmClobbers arch args (+ i 1) env regs clobs)
                  }))
            })
        })
    }))

; How many operands are outputs.
(pub :: expAsmCountOuts (-> (Vec AsmOp) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmCountOuts ops i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ops))
    acc
    {
      (let p (vecGet ops i))
      (expAsmCountOuts ops (+ i 1)
        (if (expAsmOutKind p.kind)
          (+ acc 1)
          acc))
    }))

; The index of the `k`th output with no `into` (0-based), if present. The
; form answers the first; a second has nowhere to go.
(pub :: expAsmAnswered (-> (Vec AsmOp) Int Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expAsmAnswered ops k i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen ops)))
    None
    {
      (let p (vecGet ops i))
      (if (&& (expAsmOutKind p.kind) (== (strLen p.into) 0))
        (if (== k 0)
          (Some i)
          (expAsmAnswered ops (- k 1) (+ i 1)))
        (expAsmAnswered ops k (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The first output with no register of its own, or the first output at
; all when `any` is set; None when there is none.
(pub :: expAsmFirstOut (-> (Vec AsmOp) Bool Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expAsmFirstOut ops any i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen ops)))
    None
    {
      (let p (vecGet ops i))
      (if (&& (expAsmOutKind p.kind) (|| any (== (strLen p.reg) 0)))
        (Some i)
        (expAsmFirstOut ops any (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The node of option `w`, or 0 when the arm doesn't give it.
(pub :: expAsmOptNode (-> Strings (Vec ASTNode) String ASTNode))
(pub fn (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes w)
  (if (let (Some k) (expAsmNameAt opts w 0))
    (vecGet optNodes k)
    nilNode))

; What the arm's items say together, once each is well formed: one
; answered output at most, and options that agree with each other and
; with the operands (AX3119). Answers 1 once it has refused.
(pub :: expAsmCheckArm (-> String MEnv (Vec AsmOp) Strings Strings (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expAsmCheckArm arch env ops clobs opts optNodes abis)
  {
    (let nOut (expAsmCountOuts ops 0 0))
    (let second (expAsmAnswered ops 1 0))
    (let pure (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "pure"))
    (let nomem (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "nomem"))
    (let readonly (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "readonly"))
    (let noret (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "noreturn"))
    (let keeps (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "preserves_flags"))
    (let att (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "att_syntax"))
    (let intel (expAsmOptNode opts optNodes "intel_syntax"))
    (let bare
      (if (> (vecLen abis) 0)
        (expAsmFirstOut ops false 0)
        None))
    (if (let (Some secondIndex) second)
      {
        (let p (vecGet ops secondIndex))
        (expAsmBad env (nodeSpan p.node)
          (concat (concat "an arm answers one value, and `" p.name)
            (concat "` is a second output with nowhere to go; store it in a `mut` binding: `("
              (concat p.kind (concat " " (concat p.name " into slot)`"))))))
      }
      (if (&& (strEq arch "aarch64") (|| (! (nodeSame att nilNode)) (! (nodeSame intel nilNode))))
        (expAsmOptsBad env
          (nodeSpan
            (if (! (nodeSame att nilNode))
              att
              intel))
          "`att_syntax` and `intel_syntax` choose an x86_64 assembler syntax; aarch64 has one")
        (if (&& (! (nodeSame att nilNode)) (! (nodeSame intel nilNode)))
          (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan intel)
            "`att_syntax` and `intel_syntax` contradict each other; AT&T is the default")
          (if (&& (! (nodeSame nomem nilNode)) (! (nodeSame readonly nilNode)))
            (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan readonly)
              "`nomem` says the instructions touch no memory and `readonly` that they read it; keep the one that is true")
            (if (&& (! (nodeSame pure nilNode)) (! (nodeSame noret nilNode)))
              (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan noret)
                "`pure` and `noreturn` contradict each other: a `pure` block is nothing but its outputs, and a `noreturn` block has none")
              (if (&& (! (nodeSame pure nilNode)) (== nOut 0))
                (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan pure)
                  "a `pure` block's only effect is its outputs, and this arm has none; drop `pure`, or add the output it computes")
                (if (&& (! (nodeSame pure nilNode))
                      (&& (nodeSame nomem nilNode) (nodeSame readonly nilNode)))
                  (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan pure)
                    "`pure` needs `nomem` or `readonly` beside it, to say whether the instructions read memory")
                  (if (&& (! (nodeSame noret nilNode)) (> nOut 0))
                    (if (let (Some firstIndex) (expAsmFirstOut ops true 0))
                      {
                        (let p (vecGet ops firstIndex))
                        (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan p.node)
                          "a `noreturn` block never finishes, so it has no outputs")
                      }
                      (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan noret)
                        "a `noreturn` block never finishes, so it has no outputs"))
                    (if (&& (! (nodeSame nomem nilNode)) (isSome (expAsmNameAt clobs "memory" 0)))
                      (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan nomem)
                        "`nomem` says the instructions touch no memory, and the arm clobbers `memory`; keep the one that is true")
                      (if (&& (! (nodeSame keeps nilNode))
                            (|| (isSome (expAsmNameAt clobs "cc" 0))
                              (isSome (expAsmNameAt clobs "flags" 0))))
                        (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan keeps)
                          "`preserves_flags` says the instructions leave the flags alone, and the arm clobbers them; keep the one that is true")
                        (if (let (Some bareIndex) bare)
                          {
                            (let p (vecGet ops bareIndex))
                            (expAsmOptsBad env (nodeSpan p.node)
                              (concat
                                (concat
                                  "with `clobber_abi`, each output names its register, as in `("
                                  p.kind)
                                (concat " "
                                  (concat p.name
                                    " \"x0\")`; any other could be one the call clobbers"))))
                          }
                          0)))))))))))
  })

; A run of `n` zeros.
(pub :: expAsmZeros (-> Int Ints))
(pub fn (expAsmZeros n)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..n
        (vecPush v 0))
      v
    }
  })

; Each operand's number in LLVM's template, `${k}`: the outputs first,
; in the order written, then the inputs. An `inout` is named by its
; output's number and takes an input slot too, tied to it. A `const`
; is text and has none (-1).
(pub :: expAsmSlots (-> (Vec AsmOp) Int Ints))
(pub fn (expAsmSlots ops nOut)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (let mut o 0)
    (let mut j 0)
    {
      (for p in ops
        {
          (if (expAsmOutKind p.kind)
            {
              (vecPush out o)
              (set o (+ o 1))
            }
            (if (expAsmInKind p.kind)
              (vecPush out (+ nOut j))
              (vecPush out (- 0 1))))
          (if (expAsmInKind p.kind)
            (set j (+ j 1)))
        })
      out
    }
  })

; Is the template literal where its span says, byte for byte? Then an
; offset into its body is a position in the file. A literal a macro
; template produced carries the invocation's span instead, and a
; refusal inside it points at the whole template.
(pub :: expAsmSpanExact (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmSpanExact tnode)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan tnode))
    (if (== sp 0)
      false
      (== (- (spanEnd sp) (spanStart sp)) (strLen (nodeAName tnode))))
  })

; The span of `len` bytes at `off` in the template's body, or the whole
; template when the literal isn't where its span says.
(pub :: expAsmSubSpan (-> ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmSubSpan tnode off len)
  (if (expAsmSpanExact tnode)
    {
      (let at (+ (+ (spanStart (nodeSpan tnode)) 1) off))
      (mkSpan at (+ at len))
    }
    (nodeSpan tnode)))

; A template refusal: the message and where, for the caller to report.
(pub :: expAsmErr (-> Strings Ints ASTNode Int Int String Int))
(pub fn (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode off len msg)
  {
    (vecPush errs msg)
    (vecPush errSps (expAsmSubSpan tnode off len))
    0
  })

; The arm's description once its items agree, or "" once it has
; refused the template.
(pub :: expAsmArmDesc
  (-> String ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) (Vec AsmOp) Strings Strings (Vec ASTNode)
    String))
(pub fn (expAsmArmDesc arch tnode env bs vals ops clobs opts abis)
  {
    (let nOut (expAsmCountOuts ops 0 0))
    (let slots (expAsmSlots ops nOut))
    (let used (expAsmZeros (vecLen ops)))
    (let errs vecNew)
    (let errSps vecNew)
    (let body (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName tnode)))
    (let tmpl (expAsmTemplate arch tnode body ops slots used errs errSps))
    (if (> (vecLen errs) 0)
      {
        (expAsmBad env (vecGet errSps 0) (vecGetStr errs 0))
        ""
      }
      {
        (let lbl (expAsmNamedLabel arch body))
        (if (> (vecLen lbl) 0)
          {
            (expAsmBad env (expAsmSubSpan tnode (vecGet lbl 0) (vecGet lbl 1))
              (concat
                (concat "the template defines the label `"
                  (strSlice body (vecGet lbl 0) (- (vecGet lbl 1) 1)))
                "`, which is defined again wherever the block is copied - in a loop the optimiser unrolls, or a function it inlines; use a numbered label such as `1:`, reached as `1b` or `1f`"))
            ""
          }
          {
            (let k (expAsmFirstUnused ops used 0))
            (if (let (Some unusedIndex) k)
              {
                (expAsmUnused env (vecGet ops unusedIndex))
                ""
              }
              (expAsmArmText arch tnode env bs vals ops slots clobs opts abis tmpl nOut))
          })
      })
  })

; The first operand the template never names that needs naming, if any.
; An operand in a register of its own needn't be named: the
; instructions reach it by that register, as a syscall reaches `x8`.
(pub :: expAsmFirstUnused (-> (Vec AsmOp) Ints Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expAsmFirstUnused ops used i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen ops)))
    None
    {
      (let p (vecGet ops i))
      (if (&& (== (vecGet used i) 0) (== (strLen p.reg) 0))
        (Some i)
        (expAsmFirstUnused ops used (+ i 1)))
    }))

; AX3118: an operand the template never names, so no instruction can
; reach it - an input nothing reads, an output nothing writes, which
; then answers whatever its register held.
(pub :: expAsmUnused (-> MEnv AsmOp Int))
(pub fn (expAsmUnused env p)
  (expAsmDiag env (nodeSpan p.node) "AX3118" "asm-operand-unused"
    (concat (concat "the template never writes `{" p.name)
      (concat "}`, so "
        (if (expAsmOutKind p.kind)
          "no instruction can write this output"
          (if (strEq p.kind "in")
            "no instruction can read this input"
            "the operand does nothing"))))
    "write `{name}` where an instruction uses the operand, delete it, or give it a register of its own, as in `(in a \"x8\" v)`, when an instruction uses that register without naming it"))

; The arm's description: constraints, arguments and where its lines
; are. Inputs are expanded here, in the order written, which is the
; order codegen evaluates them in.
(pub :: expAsmArmText
  (-> String ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) (Vec AsmOp) Ints Strings Strings (Vec ASTNode)
    String Int String))
(pub fn (expAsmArmText arch tnode env bs vals ops slots clobs opts abis tmpl nOut)
  {
    (let mut outc "")
    (let mut inc "")
    (let mut ins "")
    (let mut outs "")
    (let mut ans (- 0 1))
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen ops))
        {
          (let p (vecGet ops i))
          {
            (if (expAsmOutKind p.kind)
              {
                (set outc (expAsmJoin outc (expAsmOutCon p)))
                (if (== (strLen p.into) 0)
                  (set ans (vecGet slots i))
                  {
                    (set outs (expAsmJoin outs
                                (concat (concat (expFmtInt (vecGet slots i)) ":")
                                  (expFmtInt (vecLen vals)))))
                    (vecPush vals (nodeBuildESet p.into (mkEInt 0) 0 (nodeSpan p.intoNode)))
                  })
              })
            (set i (+ i 1))
          }
        })
      (set i 0)
      (while (< i (vecLen ops))
        {
          (let p (vecGet ops i))
          {
            (if (expAsmInKind p.kind)
              {
                (set inc (expAsmJoin inc (expAsmInCon p (vecGet slots i))))
                (set ins (expAsmJoin ins
                           (concat
                             (if (strEq p.kind "sym")
                               "s"
                               "")
                             (expFmtInt (vecLen vals)))))
                (vecPush vals
                  (if (strEq p.kind "sym")
                    (expBuildSpine (mkEVarAt "__asm.sym" (nodeSpan p.node)) (expAsmOne p.val) 0)
                    (expandExpr p.val env bs)))
              })
            (set i (+ i 1))
          }
        })
      {
        (let cons (expAsmJoin (expAsmJoin outc inc) (expAsmClobberCons clobs 0 "")))
        (let file (expAsmFileOf env))
        (let lines
          (if (|| (== (strLen file) 0) (== (nodeSpan tnode) 0))
            ""
            (expAsmLines tnode (strUnquoteLit (nodeAName tnode)))))
        (concat
          (concat (concat (concat arch ";") (concat (expAsmFlags opts abis) ";"))
            (concat (concat cons ";") (concat (expFmtInt nOut) ";")))
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat (expAsmIntText ans) ";") (concat outs ";"))
              (concat (concat ins ";") (concat lines ";")))
            (concat (concat (concat (expFmtInt (strLen file)) ";") file)
              (concat (concat (expFmtInt (strLen tmpl)) ";") tmpl))))
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: expAsmOne (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expAsmOne x)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush v x)
      v
    }
  })

; An output's constraint: early-clobber (`=&`) for `out` and `inout`,
; plain (`=`) for the late kinds, in a named register or any.
(pub :: expAsmOutCon (-> AsmOp String))
(pub fn (expAsmOutCon p)
  (concat
    (if (expAsmWordIn p.kind "out inout")
      "=&"
      "=")
    (if (> (strLen p.reg) 0)
      (concat (concat "{" p.reg) "}")
      "r")))

; An input's constraint: a register, the number of the output an
; `inout` is tied to, or `s` for a symbol.
(pub :: expAsmInCon (-> AsmOp Int String))
(pub fn (expAsmInCon p slot)
  (if (strEq p.kind "sym")
    "s"
    (if (strEq p.kind "in")
      (if (> (strLen p.reg) 0)
        (concat (concat "{" p.reg) "}")
        "r")
      (expFmtInt slot))))

; `~{reg}` for each register the arm clobbers by name; `memory` and the
; flags are codegen's to add or leave out.
(pub :: expAsmClobberCons (-> Strings Int String String))
(pub fn (expAsmClobberCons clobs i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen clobs))
    acc
    {
      (let name (vecGetStr clobs i))
      ; LLVM 18 recognises the link-register clobber as `lr`, not `x30`.
      (let r
        (if (strEq name "x30")
          "lr"
          name))
      (expAsmClobberCons clobs (+ i 1)
        (if (expAsmWordIn r "memory cc flags")
          acc
          (expAsmJoin acc (concat (concat "~{" r) "}"))))
    }))

(pub :: expAsmFlags (-> Strings (Vec ASTNode) String))
(pub fn (expAsmFlags opts abis)
  (concat
    (concat (concat (expAsmFlag opts "pure" "p") (expAsmFlag opts "nomem" "m"))
      (concat (expAsmFlag opts "readonly" "r") (expAsmFlag opts "preserves_flags" "f")))
    (concat (concat (expAsmFlag opts "noreturn" "n") (expAsmFlag opts "intel_syntax" "i"))
      (concat (expAsmFlag opts "nostack" "s")
        (if (> (vecLen abis) 0)
          "a"
          "")))))

(pub :: expAsmFlag (-> Strings String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmFlag opts w letter)
  (if (isSome (expAsmNameAt opts w 0))
    letter
    ""))

; The file the form is written in, from the unit table and the module
; of the declaration being expanded. An absent module has no file;
; inline assembly encodes that as an empty file name.
(pub :: expAsmFileOf (-> MEnv String))
(pub fn (expAsmFileOf env)
  (expAsmFileFor env.units (expModuleWordOpt env.declmod)))

(pub :: expAsmFileFor (-> Units (Option String) String))
(pub fn (expAsmFileFor units module)
  (match (expUnitOfMod units module)
    ((None) "")
    ((Some u) (unitFile (vecGet units u)))))

; A line break at `i` in a literal body: 2 for the `\n` escape, 1 for a
; raw newline byte, 0 for anything else.
(pub :: expAsmNewline (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmNewline s i)
  (if (== (strByte s i) 10)
    1
    (if (&& (== (strByte s i) 92) (&& (< (+ i 1) (strLen s)) (== (strByte s (+ i 1)) 110)))
      2
      0)))

; `start-end` for each line of the template, in file bytes, joined with
; commas. LLVM numbers the lines of an inline-assembly string from 1 and
; a placeholder adds none, so line k here is line k there. A line with
; no bytes, or a template that isn't where its span says, gets the
; whole template's span.
(pub :: expAsmLines (-> ASTNode String String))
(pub fn (expAsmLines tnode s)
  {
    (let n (strLen s))
    (let mut acc "")
    (let mut ls 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (<= i n)
        {
          (let brk
            (if (== i n)
              1
              (expAsmNewline s i)))
          (if (> brk 0)
            {
              {
                (let sp
                  (if (> i ls)
                    (expAsmSubSpan tnode ls (- i ls))
                    (nodeSpan tnode)))
                (set acc (expAsmJoin acc
                           (concat (concat (expFmtInt (spanStart sp)) "-")
                             (expFmtInt (spanEnd sp)))))
              }
              (set i (+ i brk))
              (set ls i)
            }
            (if (&& (== (strByte s i) 92) (< (+ i 1) n))
              (set i (+ i 2))
              (set i (+ i 1))))
        })
      acc
    }
  })

; A byte a label's name may hold.
(pub :: expAsmLabelByte (-> Int Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmLabelByte c)
  (if (|| (&& (>= c 97) (<= c 122)) (&& (>= c 65) (<= c 90)))
    true
    (if (&& (>= c 48) (<= c 57))
      true
      (|| (== c 95) (|| (== c 46) (== c 36))))))

; The first label the template defines by name, as `[offset, length]`
; in `s` with the colon, or an empty Vec. A statement opening with a
; name and a colon defines one; a numbered label (`1:`) is local and
; may be defined any number of times, which is what makes it the one a
; template can use. Statements end at a line break, and on x86_64 at
; `;` too. AArch64 keeps `;` out: Darwin's assembler reads it as a
; comment, where `nop ; note: why` defines nothing.
(pub :: expAsmNamedLabel (-> String String Ints))
(pub fn (expAsmNamedLabel arch s)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (let n (strLen s))
    (let mut ls 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (&& (<= i n) (== (vecLen out) 0))
        {
          (let brk
            (if (== i n)
              1
              (if (&& (strEq arch "x86_64") (== (strByte s i) 59))
                1
                (expAsmNewline s i))))
          (if (> brk 0)
            {
              (expAsmLabelIn s ls i out)
              (set i (+ i brk))
              (set ls i)
            }
            (if (&& (== (strByte s i) 92) (< (+ i 1) n))
              (set i (+ i 2))
              (set i (+ i 1))))
        })
      out
    }
  })

; The statement in `s` from `ls` to `le`: past its blanks, a name, and
; a colon straight after it make a label, pushed as `[offset, length]`
; unless the name is all digits.
(pub :: expAsmLabelIn (-> String Int Int Ints Int))
(pub fn (expAsmLabelIn s ls le out)
  {
    (let mut j ls)
    (let mut digits true)
    {
      (while (&& (< j le)
               (|| (== (strByte s j) 32)
                 (|| (== (strByte s j) 9)
                   (&& (== (strByte s j) 92) (&& (< (+ j 1) le) (== (strByte s (+ j 1)) 116))))))
        (set j (+ j
                 (if (== (strByte s j) 92)
                   2
                   1))))
      {
        (let mut k j)
        {
          (while (&& (< k le) (expAsmLabelByte (strByte s k)))
            {
              (if (|| (< (strByte s k) 48) (> (strByte s k) 57))
                (set digits false))
              (set k (+ k 1))
            })
          (if (&& (> k j) (&& (< k le) (&& (== (strByte s k) 58) (! digits))))
            {
              (vecPush out j)
              (vecPush out (+ (- k j) 1))
              0
            }
            0)
        }
      }
    }
  })

; The template with `{name}` as `${k}` (or `${k:m}` for `{name:m}`), a
; `const` as its text, a `sym` as `${k:c}`, `{{` and `}}` as braces, and
; `$` as `$$`, working on the literal's body with its source escapes
; left in place: a backslash and the byte after it are copied as they
; stand. Each operand the template names is marked in `used`. A problem
; is pushed onto `errs` with its span, and the scan stops.
(pub :: expAsmTemplate (-> String ASTNode String (Vec AsmOp) Ints Ints Strings Ints String))
(pub fn (expAsmTemplate arch tnode s ops slots used errs errSps)
  {
    (let mut out "")
    (let mut i 0)
    (let n (strLen s))
    {
      (while (&& (< i n) (== (vecLen errs) 0))
        {
          (let c (strByte s i))
          (if (&& (== c 92) (< (+ i 1) n))
            {
              (set out (concat out (strSlice s i 2)))
              (set i (+ i 2))
            }
            (if (== c 36)
              {
                (set out (concat out "$$"))
                (set i (+ i 1))
              }
              (if (&& (== c 123) (&& (< (+ i 1) n) (== (strByte s (+ i 1)) 123)))
                {
                  (set out (concat out "{"))
                  (set i (+ i 2))
                }
                (if (== c 123)
                  {
                    (let close (expAsmFindClose s (+ i 1)))
                    (if (< close 0)
                      (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode i 1
                        "a `{` in the template opens an operand and nothing closes it; write `{{` for a literal brace")
                      {
                        (set out (concat out
                                   (expAsmPlaceholder arch tnode s (+ i 1) close ops slots used
                                     errs errSps)))
                        (set i (+ close 1))
                      })
                  }
                  (if (== c 125)
                    (if (&& (< (+ i 1) n) (== (strByte s (+ i 1)) 125))
                      {
                        (set out (concat out "}"))
                        (set i (+ i 2))
                      }
                      (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode i 1
                        "a `}` in the template closes nothing; write `}}` for a literal brace"))
                    {
                      (set out (concat out (strSlice s i 1)))
                      (set i (+ i 1))
                    })))))
        })
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: expAsmFindClose (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmFindClose s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    (- 0 1)
    (if (== (strByte s i) 125)
      i
      (if (== (strByte s i) 123)
        (- 0 1)
        (expAsmFindClose s (+ i 1))))))

; `{name}` or `{name:m}`, whose name runs from `from` to `close`: the
; operand's `${k}` or `${k:m}`, a `const`'s text, or a `sym`'s
; `${k:c}`, the bare symbol on both architectures. The modifiers pick a
; view of a register: `w` and `x` on aarch64, `b`, `h`, `w`, `k` and
; `q` on x86_64. A refusal points at the braces and what is in them.
(pub :: expAsmPlaceholder
  (-> String ASTNode String Int Int (Vec AsmOp) Ints Ints Strings Ints String))
(pub fn (expAsmPlaceholder arch tnode s from close ops slots used errs errSps)
  {
    (let inner (strSlice s from (- close from)))
    (let colon (expAsmColon inner 0))
    (let nm
      (if (< colon 0)
        inner
        (strSlice inner 0 colon)))
    (let m
      (if (< colon 0)
        ""
        (strSlice inner (+ colon 1) (- (strLen inner) (+ colon 1)))))
    (let k (expAsmOpAt ops nm 0))
    (let off (- from 1))
    (let len (+ (- close from) 2))
    (if (let (Some k) k)
      {
        (let p (vecGet ops k))
        {
          (vecSet used k 1)
          (if (expAsmWordIn p.kind "const sym")
            (if (> (strLen m) 0)
              {
                (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode off len
                  (concat (concat (concat "`" nm) "` is a `")
                    (concat p.kind "` operand, which takes no modifier")))
                ""
              }
              (if (strEq p.kind "const")
                p.text
                (concat (concat "${" (expFmtInt (vecGet slots k))) ":c}")))
            (if (&& (> (strLen m) 0) (! (expAsmModifier arch m)))
              {
                (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode off len
                  (concat (concat (concat "`:" m) "` is not an operand modifier on ")
                    (concat arch
                      (if (strEq arch "aarch64")
                        "; the modifiers are `w` and `x`"
                        "; the modifiers are `b`, `h`, `w`, `k` and `q`"))))
                ""
              }
              (concat (concat "${" (expFmtInt (vecGet slots k)))
                (if (> (strLen m) 0)
                  (concat (concat ":" m) "}")
                  "}"))))
        }
      }
      {
        (expAsmErr errs errSps tnode off len
          (concat (concat "the template writes `{" nm)
            "}`, and no operand of this arm has that name; write `{{` and `}}` for literal braces"))
        ""
      })
  })

; `s` and `t` joined with a comma, either of them possibly empty.
(pub :: expAsmJoin (-> String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmJoin s t)
  (if (== (strLen t) 0)
    s
    (if (== (strLen s) 0)
      t
      (concat (concat s ",") t))))

; Why `r` can't hold an operand on `arch`, or "" when it can. An
; operand lives in a general-purpose register the compiler's frame and
; the platform don't own.
(pub :: expAsmOperandRegWhy (-> String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmOperandRegWhy arch r)
  {
    (let why (expAsmReservedWhy arch r))
    (if (> (strLen why) 0)
      why
      (if (strEq arch "aarch64")
        (if (&& (expAsmA64Gpr r)
              (if (strEq r "x30")
                false
                true))
          ""
          (if (strEq r "x30")
            "`x30` is the link register; name it in `clobber` if the instructions write it"
            (concat (concat "`" r)
              "` is not a register an aarch64 operand can name; use `x0` to `x28`, except `x18`")))
        (if (expAsmX64Gpr r)
          ""
          (concat (concat "`" r)
            "` is not a register an x86_64 operand can name; use `rax`, `rbx`, `rcx`, `rdx`, `rsi`, `rdi` or `r8` to `r15`"))))
  })

; Why `r` can't be clobbered on `arch`, or "" when it can: a
; general-purpose register, the link register, or a vector register.
(pub :: expAsmClobberRegWhy (-> String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmClobberRegWhy arch r)
  {
    (let why (expAsmReservedWhy arch r))
    (if (> (strLen why) 0)
      why
      (if (strEq arch "aarch64")
        (if (|| (expAsmA64Gpr r) (expAsmNumbered r "v" 0 31))
          ""
          (concat (concat "`" r)
            "` is not a register an aarch64 `clobber` can name; use `x0` to `x30`, except `x18` and `x29`, or `v0` to `v31`"))
        (if (|| (expAsmX64Gpr r) (expAsmNumbered r "xmm" 0 15))
          ""
          (concat (concat "`" r)
            "` is not a register an x86_64 `clobber` can name; use a general-purpose register other than `rsp` and `rbp`, or `xmm0` to `xmm15`"))))
  })

; The registers no block may touch, and why.
(pub :: expAsmReservedWhy (-> String String String))
(pub fn (expAsmReservedWhy arch r)
  (if (strEq arch "aarch64")
    (if (|| (strEq r "sp") (strEq r "x31"))
      "`sp` is the stack pointer; an `asm` block must leave it as it found it"
      (if (strEq r "x29")
        "`x29` is the frame pointer, which the compiler's frame owns"
        (if (strEq r "x18")
          "`x18` is the platform register, reserved on Darwin; use another"
          "")))
    (if (strEq r "rsp")
      "`rsp` is the stack pointer; an `asm` block must leave it as it found it"
      (if (strEq r "rbp")
        "`rbp` is the frame pointer, which the compiler's frame owns"
        ""))))

; `x0` to `x30`: the reserved ones are refused first, by name.
(pub :: expAsmA64Gpr (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmA64Gpr r)
  (expAsmNumbered r "x" 0 30))

(pub :: expAsmX64Gpr (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmX64Gpr r)
  (if (|| (strEq r "rax") (|| (strEq r "rbx") (|| (strEq r "rcx") (strEq r "rdx"))))
    true
    (if (|| (strEq r "rsi") (strEq r "rdi"))
      true
      (expAsmNumbered r "r" 8 15))))

; `r` is `prefix` followed by a decimal in [lo, hi], written without a
; leading zero.
(pub :: expAsmNumbered (-> String String Int Int Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmNumbered r prefix lo hi)
  {
    (let p (strLen prefix))
    (let n (strLen r))
    (if (|| (<= n p) (|| (> (- n p) 2) (! (strStartsWith r prefix))))
      false
      (if (&& (== (- n p) 2) (== (strByte r p) 48))
        false
        {
          (let mut v 0)
          (let mut ok true)
          {
            (for i in p..n
              {
                (let c (strByte r i))
                (if (&& (>= c 48) (<= c 57))
                  (set v (+ (* v 10) (- c 48)))
                  (set ok false))
              })
            (&& ok (&& (>= v lo) (<= v hi)))
          }
        }))
  })

(pub :: expAsmColon (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (expAsmColon s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    (- 0 1)
    (if (== (strByte s i) 58)
      i
      (expAsmColon s (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: expAsmNameAt (-> Strings String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expAsmNameAt names nm i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen names)))
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr names i) nm)
      (Some i)
      (expAsmNameAt names nm (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: expAsmModifier (-> String String Bool))
(pub fn (expAsmModifier arch m)
  (if (strEq arch "aarch64")
    (|| (strEq m "w") (strEq m "x"))
    (if (|| (strEq m "b") (|| (strEq m "h") (strEq m "w")))
      true
      (|| (strEq m "k") (strEq m "q")))))

(pub :: expandAppPlain (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandAppPlain e env bs)
  (nodeBuildEApp (expandExpr (nodeAChild e) env bs) (expandExpr (nodeBChild e) env bs) nilWords
    (nodeSpan e)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `_` HOLES. An application's direct argument list may contain a bare
; `_` in place of an argument - `(add 5 _)` stands for the value a
; hand-written `(lambda (y) (add 5 y))` would build, one fresh
; parameter per hole, left to right. Checked here rather than in the
; checker: by the time `checkApp` sees the tree, `(add 5 _)` and the
; hand-written lambda are the identical shape, so AX3013 keeps
; meaning exactly what it always meant - a bare name applied to too
; few arguments, with no `_` anywhere in the call - and needs no new
; case to notice holes.
;
; NOT "no hidden allocations" (a claim this feature does not make):
; the desugared lambda is a closure record and allocates exactly as
; an equivalent hand-written one would. The merit is EXPLICITNESS -
; the closure forms exactly where a `_` is written, visible to a
; reader or an agent without tracing an implicit currying rule.
;
; The check is SPINE-LOCAL: `spineArgVec` returns only the arguments
; of the call `e` sits at the top of, never reaching into an argument
; that is itself a call. `(add 5 (mul 2 _))` therefore has no hole at
; `add`'s level - its second argument is the whole `(mul 2 _)` node,
; not `_` itself - and `add`'s spine is expanded plainly; `mul`'s own
; visit, reached when that argument is expanded in the ordinary way,
; finds the hole and wraps only the inner call:
; `(add 5 (lambda (h) (mul 2 h)))`. A hole binds to its INNERMOST
; enclosing call, which is the only rule the two examples the design
; gives are both consistent with.
;
; Head-position `_` - `(_ 5)` - is left alone: this pass only scans
; the ARGUMENT vector, never `spineHead`'s result, so a hole cannot
; appear there. Bare `_` in expression position stays `checkVar`'s
; ordinary undefined-variable case - measured on a program with
; `(add 5 _)` where `add` takes 2 arguments, before this pass existed:
; `E AX3001 ... undefined variable `_``, `?...:"a similarly named
; binding `+` is in scope; did you mean this?"` - and a hole that
; would need a name outside this scan (an application's own callee
; position) still gets exactly that diagnostic today.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandAppOrHole (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandAppOrHole e env bs)
  {
    (let argv (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (if (== (expHoleCount argv 0) 0)
      (expandAppPlain e env bs)
      {
        (let params vecNew)
        (let newArgv vecNew)
        {
          (expHoleArgs argv 0 env bs params newArgv)
          (mkLamCurried params (expBuildSpine (expandExpr (spineHead e) env bs) newArgv 0)
            (nodeSpan e))
        }
      })
  })

; Is this argument node exactly the wildcard identifier - not merely
; an expression that CONTAINS one further down?
(pub :: expIsHole (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expIsHole a)
  (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
    (if (strEq (nodeAName a) "_")
      1
      0)
    0))

(pub :: expHoleCount (-> Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (expHoleCount argv i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen argv))
    0
    (+
      (if (== (expIsHole (vecGet argv i)) 1)
        1
        0)
      (expHoleCount argv (+ i 1)))))

; One pass over the argument list: a hole mints a fresh parameter
; (`expFresh`, the same generator macro hygiene uses, so the name
; carries the `.` no Axiom source can spell - a collision with
; anything the caller wrote is impossible by the same construction
; the header above documents) and both the parameter and the rebuilt
; call's argument at that position reference it; anything else is
; expanded the ordinary way, exactly as `expandAppPlain` would have
; done at that position. `params` and `newArgv` are filled by side
; effect - `vecPush` mutates in place, the style `spineArgVec` itself
; already uses.
;
; `params` holds raw STRINGS, not nodes - `mkLamCurried` (parser.ax)
; expects the same shape `parseIdentList` builds for a hand-written
; `(lambda (x y) ..)`, and `bindLamParams` reads `(vecGet params i)`
; straight into `pushScope`'s `name` slot with no `nodeAName` in
; between. A node handle pushed there instead type-checks (both are
; `Int`) and then fails every lookup: measured pushing `(mkEVarAt
; fresh sp)` in this slot instead of `fresh`, `(add 5 _)` reported
; `undefined variable `_h.0`` on the reference this same pass had just
; built - a node's word-1 name string compared against the node's own
; handle, which can never `strEq`.
(pub :: expHoleArgs (-> Nodes Int MEnv ScopeBinds Strings (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expHoleArgs argv i env bs params newArgv)
  (if (>= i (vecLen argv))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet argv i))
      (if (== (expIsHole a) 1)
        {
          (let fresh (expFresh env "_h"))
          {
            (vecPush params fresh)
            (vecPush newArgv (mkEVarAt fresh (nodeSpan a)))
            (expHoleArgs argv (+ i 1) env bs params newArgv)
          }
        }
        {
          (vecPush newArgv (expandExpr a env bs))
          (expHoleArgs argv (+ i 1) env bs params newArgv)
        })
    }))

; The inverse of `spineArgVec`: fold a rebuilt argument list back onto
; a head, left to right - the same left-nesting the parser itself
; builds for `(f a b c)`.
(pub :: expBuildSpine (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expBuildSpine head argv i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen argv))
    head
    (expBuildSpine (mkEApp head (vecGet argv i)) argv (+ i 1))))

; Whether any rule of the rule-form macro `mac` has an expression
; template, or any has declarations: what decides the refusal a macro
; gets in a position none of its rules can fill.
(pub :: expMacHasExprRule (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (expMacHasExprRule mac)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) false)
    ((Some rules) (expRulesHaveExpr rules 0))))

(pub :: expMacHasDeclRule (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (expMacHasDeclRule mac)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) false)
    ((Some rules) (expRulesHaveDecls rules 0))))

(pub :: expRulesHaveExpr (-> MacroRules Int Bool))
(pub fn (expRulesHaveExpr rules i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
    false
    (if (isSome (expRuleExpr (vecGet rules i)))
      true
      (expRulesHaveExpr rules (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: expRulesHaveDecls (-> MacroRules Int Bool))
(pub fn (expRulesHaveDecls rules i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
    false
    (if (> (vecLen (expRuleTpl (vecGet rules i))) 0)
      true
      (expRulesHaveDecls rules (+ i 1)))))

; A declaration-only rule has no expression template.
(pub :: expRuleExpr (-> MacroRule (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expRuleExpr r)
  r.expression)

; A rule-form macro invoked in expression position, with `args`: the
; first rule whose pattern matches is instantiated, and it must have an
; expression template. A macro with none at all keeps the refusal it
; has always drawn, before any rule is tried.
(pub :: expandRuleApp (-> ASTNode String Nodes MEnv ScopeBinds Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandRuleApp mac name args env bs isp)
  {
    (let rules
      (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
        ((None) vecNew)
        ((Some rules) rules)))
    (if (expMacHasExprRule mac)
      {
        (let m
          (expRuleForForms rules (expCaptureVec args env bs) 0
            (expMatchCtx (expDeclsVec env) env.site (nodeModule mac))))
        (if (let (Some m) m)
          (match (expRuleExpr (expMatchOf m))
            ((None)
              {
                (expEmit env
                  (expMarkUnit env
                    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3027" "declaration-macro" isp
                      (concat (concat "the rule of macro `" name)
                        "` that matches here generates declarations, so it can't stand in expression position")
                      None
                      (Some
                        "invoke it at top level, or give the macro an earlier rule for this shape whose template is one expression")
                      "each rule's template is declarations or one expression, and where the macro is invoked picks which it needs (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-14)")
                    env.declmod))
                (mkEInt 0)
              }
            )
            ((Some template) (expandExprRuleInstantiate mac template m env bs isp)))
          {
            (expNoRuleDiag env name (expRuleShapes rules name 0 "") isp)
            (mkEInt 0)
          })
      }
      {
        (expEmit env
          (expMarkUnit env
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3027" "declaration-macro" isp
              (concat (concat "declaration macro `" name) "` invoked in expression position") None
              (Some "every rule of this macro generates declarations; invoke it at top level")
              "an expression position needs a value, and each rule's template here is declarations (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-8)")
            env.declmod))
        (mkEInt 0)
      })
  })

(pub :: expandMacroApp (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandMacroApp e head mac env bs)
  ; A rule-form macro (word 9) selects its rule by match, and the rule
  ; it selects must have an expression template to stand here; arity is
  ; exact in rule position, so there is no surplus-applies story here
  ; (that story is the head-list form's alone).
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (let params (nodeBNames mac))
    (let isp
      (if (!= (nodeSpan head) 0)
        (nodeSpan head)
        (nodeSpan e)))
    (if (!= (nodeFieldNamesWord mac) 0)
      (expandRuleApp mac (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName head)) args env bs isp)
      (if (< (vecLen args) (vecLen params))
        {
          (expArityDiag env (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName head)) (vecLen params) (vecLen args) isp)
          (mkEInt 0)
        }
        ; Exactly `arity` arguments feed the macro; a longer spine
        ; applies the surplus to whatever the macro produced.
        {
          (let taken (takeArgs args (vecLen params) env bs))
          (applyRest (expandInstantiate mac taken env bs isp) args (vecLen params) env bs)
        }))
  })

(pub :: takeArgs (-> Nodes Int MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (takeArgs args n env bs)
  (takeArgsIn args n env bs vecNew 0))

; Bare identifiers remain syntax until their substituted use is walked.
; A template may place one in a field label, type query or call head.
; Value uses still pass through expandVarRef in the output walk.
(pub :: takeArgsIn (-> Nodes Int MEnv ScopeBinds (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (takeArgsIn args n env bs acc i)
  (if (>= i n)
    acc
    (takeArgsIn args n env bs
      (vecPush acc
        (if (== (nodeTag (vecGet args i)) TAG_E_VAR)
          (expCaptureSyntax (vecGet args i) env bs)
          (expandExpr (expCaptureSyntax (vecGet args i) env bs) env bs)))
      (+ i 1))))

(pub :: applyRest (-> ASTNode Nodes Int MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (applyRest f args n env bs)
  (if (>= n (vecLen args))
    f
    (applyRest (mkEApp f (expandExpr (vecGet args n) env bs)) args (+ n 1) env bs)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Instantiate: substitute, then expand the result, so a template that
; invokes another macro expands too. The depth counter is what turns
; `(macro (loopy x) (loopy x))` from a segfault into a diagnostic.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandInstantiate (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ScopeBinds Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandInstantiate mac args env bs isp)
  {
    (let d (+ (expDepth env) 1))
    (if (> d expDepthLimit)
      {
        (expEmit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3019" "macro-recursion-limit" isp
            (concat (concat "expanding `" (nodeAName mac))
              "` exceeded the macro expansion depth limit")
            None
            (Some
              "check whether this macro, directly or through another, invokes itself with no case that stops")
            "expansion is a rewrite, so a macro that always rewrites to itself never terminates; the limit is 128"))
        (mkEInt 0)
      }
      ; This template's iteration stack starts EMPTY: a nested
      ; macro's template must not see the enclosing template's
      ; for-variables, exactly as macmod is scoped. Restored
      ; before the output walk - a binding is dead the moment
      ; substTpl returns.
      ; The frame, recorded before the walk so that an outer
      ; macro's frame precedes any macro its template invokes.
      ; Word 19 - the call site's binders - used to be saved, set and
      ; restored here, for the capture check in `substTpl`'s
      ; free-identifier arm. That check was retired on 2026-08-16
      ; with `AX3032`: a template's free identifier resolves at the
      ; macro's own scope now, so what the call site binds cannot
      ; capture it and does not need carrying. The slot stays
      ; because the word numbering is additive by house rule -
      ; removing a field in the middle renumbers every one after it -
      ; and it is written by nothing and read by nothing.
      {
        (let prevMod (expMacMod env))
        (let prevSite env.site)
        (let prevFors env.forstack)
        (let prevSc (expScEnterInst env))
        (let _frame
          (vecPush env.frames
            (ExpFrame
              :site (expModuleWordOpt env.declmod)
              :span isp
              :name (nodeAName mac)
              :defSpan (nodeSpan mac)
              :defMod (nodeModuleOpt mac))))
        (let _setmod (expSetMacMod env (nodeModule mac)))
        (let _setsite (set env.site (nodeModule mac)))
        (let _setfors (set env.forstack vecNew))
        {
          (let body (substTpl (nodeCChild mac) (nodeBNames mac) args env isp))
          {
            (expSetMacMod env prevMod)
            (set env.forstack prevFors)
            (expScExitInst env prevSc)
            (expSetDepth env d)
            {
              (let out (expandExpr body env bs))
              {
                (set env.site prevSite)
                (expSetDepth env (- d 1))
                out
              }
            }
          }
        }
      })
  })

; Instantiate a rule's EXPRESSION template (MAC-LANG-14, slot 6): the
; match `m` already selected the rule and built the
; parallel names/vals plus the sequence binds. Substitution runs
; through `substTpl` like any expression template, so hygiene, spans
; and the definition-site rule are unchanged; the result is expanded
; so a template invoking another macro expands too. Sequences ride
; on env word 20, exactly as declaration instantiation does, and the
; iteration stack starts empty for the same reason.
(pub :: expandExprRuleInstantiate (-> ASTNode ASTNode ExpMatch MEnv ScopeBinds Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandExprRuleInstantiate mac template m env bs isp)
  {
    (let d (+ (expDepth env) 1))
    (if (> d expDepthLimit)
      {
        (expEmit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3019" "macro-recursion-limit" isp
            (concat (concat "expanding `" (nodeAName mac))
              "` exceeded the macro expansion depth limit")
            None
            (Some
              "check whether this macro, directly or through another, invokes itself with no case that stops")
            "expansion is a rewrite, so a macro that always rewrites to itself never terminates; the limit is 128"))
        (mkEInt 0)
      }
      {
        (let prevMod (expMacMod env))
        (let prevSite env.site)
        (let prevFors env.forstack)
        (let prevSeqs env.seqbinds)
        (let prevSc (expScEnterInst env))
        (let _frame
          (vecPush env.frames
            (ExpFrame
              :site (expModuleWordOpt env.declmod)
              :span isp
              :name (nodeAName mac)
              :defSpan (nodeSpan mac)
              :defMod (nodeModuleOpt mac))))
        (let _setmod (expSetMacMod env (nodeModule mac)))
        (let _setsite (set env.site (nodeModule mac)))
        (let _setfors (set env.forstack vecNew))
        (let _setseqs (set env.seqbinds (expMatchSeqs m)))
        {
          (let body (substTpl template (expMatchNames m) (expMatchVals m) env isp))
          {
            (expSetMacMod env prevMod)
            (set env.forstack prevFors)
            (set env.seqbinds prevSeqs)
            (expScExitInst env prevSc)
            (expSetDepth env d)
            {
              (let out (expandExpr body env bs))
              {
                (set env.site prevSite)
                (expSetDepth env (- d 1))
                out
              }
            }
          }
        }
      })
  })

; MAC-HYG-8's first hole CLOSED, and `AX3032` retired with it
; (2026-08-16).
;
; This is where the capture refusal lived. A template's free
; identifier means what it meant where the macro was WRITTEN; for a
; module macro `expQualify` enforces that by rewriting the name to
; `Mod$name`, which nothing at a call site can shadow, and for an
; ENTRY-FILE macro there was nothing to qualify to - entry
; declarations are left bare, deliberately - so a binder of that name
; at the invocation captured the reference. Measured before the
; refusal existed: `(macro (useHelper v) (helper v))` invoked under
; `(let ((helper (lambda (y) 0))) ...)` answered 0 where the macro's
; own `helper` answers 40, at exit 0, with no diagnostic from any
; pass. The refusal made that loud, and said so in its own note: the
; permanent fix is scope sets, and until they land the refusal is what
; keeps the hole from being silent.
;
; It RESOLVES now. `substTpl` stamps every template free identifier
; with `setNodeDefScope`, and both resolvers - `checkVar`, and
; `emitVar` through `dispatchCall` - skip the local scope for a
; reference so stamped. That is scope sets' operational content for
; this one case: a macro is a top-level declaration, so its template's
; free identifiers had NO enclosing local scope where they were
; written, and a caller's `let` of the same spelling is a different
; variable rather than a rebinding of theirs.
;
; The refusal would now reject correct programs, so it is deleted
; rather than left unreachable. **`AX3032` is burned**: out of the
; registry, `explain` no longer answers it, and the number is never
; reused - the standing rule for a code, retired or not. The refusal's
; fixture, `590-macro-capture` (deleted, with its three goldens), is
; now `tests/selfhost/394-macro-entry-capture.ax` - the same program
; measuring the answer instead of the refusal.
; MAC-LANG-18's refusal: an invocation matching no rule names the
; macro and lists the SHAPES it accepts.
;
; It used to list arities - "takes 1 or 3 arguments" - which was all
; arity-selection could say, and which says nothing at all once two
; rules of one arity differ by shape. The shapes are the rules'
; patterns as the author wrote them, so the reader compares their
; invocation against the thing they would have to change.
(pub :: expNoRuleDiag (-> MEnv String String Int Int))
(pub fn (expNoRuleDiag env name shapes isp)
  (expEmit env
    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3018" "macro-arity" isp
      (concat (concat (concat "no rule of macro `" name) "` matches this invocation; it accepts ")
        shapes)
      None (Some "write the invocation in one of the shapes above")
      "rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose pattern matches wins (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-18): an invocation matching none is this refusal rather than a fall-through. A plain identifier in a shape matches any argument; a literal matches its own VALUE, and a parenthesised shape matches a form of that many elements - but not a form headed by a keyword (`if`, `let`, `lambda`, `match`), which the parser gives a shape of its own")))

(pub :: expArityDiag (-> MEnv String Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expArityDiag env name want got isp)
  (expEmit env
    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3018" "macro-arity" isp
      (concat
        (concat (concat (concat (concat "macro `" name) "` takes ") (expFmtInt want))
          " arguments, but was given ")
        (expFmtInt got))
      None (Some "pass exactly as many arguments as the macro declares parameters")
      "a macro is a rewrite, not a function: a missing argument would leave the parameter's own name in the generated code, and a surplus one would be dropped without being evaluated")))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Declarations.
;
; Only a function has a body to walk. The body is written back into
; word 3 IN PLACE, which matters: `resolveImports` answers a merged
; list that shares its declaration nodes with the entry list the
; checker is also handed, so rebuilding the nodes would expand one
; view of the program and leave the other unexpanded.
;
; A macro's own template is NOT expanded here. It is expanded once per
; invocation, against that invocation's arguments; expanding it in
; place would rewrite it against no arguments at all.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `?`: propagate an error
;
; `e?` answers the `Ok` payload of the Result `e`; when `e` is an `Err`,
; the enclosing function or lambda answers that `Err` instead
; (docs/reference.md, "Propagate an error with `?`"). Axiom has no
; early return, so the expander produces a `match` whose `Ok` arm
; holds the rest of the body. Each body
; is rewritten once its macros have expanded (`qmBody`, called from
; `expandDecl` and the lambda arm of `expandExprInner`):
;
;   * in TAIL position - a body, a `let`'s body, a block's last
;     element, an `if`'s branch, a `match` arm - the walk goes on, so
;     the propagated `Err` is always the body's own answer;
;   * anywhere else the FIRST `?` in evaluation order is lifted out of
;     the expression around it (`qmLift`), and each sibling evaluated
;     before it is bound to a fresh `q.N` first, so effects keep
;     their order: `(f (g) (h)?)` still runs `(g)` before `(h)`;
;   * a `?` in a branch, a `let` body or a loop is rewritten so the
;     form answers a `Result`: `Err` skips the rest, and the `Ok`
;     payload is the value the form would have had. A `region` or a
;     `handle` propagates through its body in the same way.
;   * an inconsistent internal AST stops lowering with AX3146. The
;     error prevents emission, and any leftover `?` remains poison.
;
; A lambda is its own body: its `?`s answer the lambda, and the walk
; here never looks inside one (`qmHas`).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct QmSt
  (mut names : (Vec String))
  (mut vals : (Vec ASTNode))
  (mut scrut : (Option ASTNode))
  (mut tname : String)
  (mut tsp : Int)
  (mut failed : Bool))

;; Does `e` hold a `?` outside any lambda?
;
; Memoised on the node's word 7, which nothing has written yet at
; this point in the pipeline (the region walk stamps it later): 1
; means no `?` below, 2 means one, 3 means the walk is inside this
; node. A macro that uses its parameter twice shares the argument
; between both uses, so an expansion is a DAG and a walk without the
; memo visits one node once per path: a 40-deep nesting of such a
; macro hung the compiler (tests/fuzz/lsp/lint-dag.lspfuzz). `qmBody`
; clears the marks when it is done (`qmClear`).
(pub :: qmHas (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (qmHas e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    false
    {
      (let m (nodeAxtagsWord e))
      (if (== m 1)
        false
        (if (|| (== m 2) (== m 3))
          (== m 2)
          {
            (writeNodeWord e 7 3)
            {
              (let r (qmHasCore e))
              {
                (writeNodeWord e 7
                  (if r
                    2
                    1))
                r
              }
            }
          }))
    }))

(pub :: qmHasCore (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (qmHasCore e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    false
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
          true
        (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (qmHas (nodeBChild e)))
        (== tag TAG_E_IF)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (|| (qmHas (nodeBChild e)) (qmHas (nodeCChild e))))
        (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
          (|| (qmHas (nodeBChild e)) (qmHas (nodeCChild e)))
        (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
          (qmHasVec (nodeAChildren e) 0)
        (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (qmHasVec (nodeBChildren e) 0))
        (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
          (qmHas (nodeBChild e))
        (== tag TAG_E_SET)
          (qmHas (nodeBChild e))
        (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
          (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
        (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (qmHas (nodeCChild e)))
        (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (qmHas (nodeCChild e)))
        (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
          (|| (qmHas (nodeAChild e)) (qmHasArms (nodeBChildren e) 0))
        false)
    }))

(pub :: qmHasVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Bool))
(pub fn (qmHasVec xs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    false
    (if (qmHas (vecGet xs i))
      true
      (qmHasVec xs (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: qmHasArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Bool))
(pub fn (qmHasArms arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    false
    (if (qmHas (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)))
      true
      (qmHasArms arms (+ i 1)))))

; Clear `qmHas`'s marks below `e`: a node whose mark is already 0 was
; never marked, or is cleared, so the walk stops there.
(pub :: qmClear (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (qmClear e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeAxtagsWord e) 0)
      0
      {
        (let tag (nodeTag e))
        {
          (writeNodeWord e 7 0)
          ; Only words that hold NODES are walked: a `let`'s word 1 and
          ; a `set`'s are names, and a field access's word 2 is one.
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_TRY) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== tag TAG_E_REGION)))
            (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
              (qmClear (nodeBChild e))
              (qmClear (nodeAChild e)))
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
              {
                (qmClear (nodeBChild e))
                (qmClear (nodeCChild e))
              }
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_APP) (|| (== tag TAG_E_SETF) (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)))
                {
                  (qmClear (nodeAChild e))
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
                    (qmClear (nodeBChild e))
                    (qmClear (nodeCChild e)))
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                  {
                    (qmClear (nodeAChild e))
                    (qmClear (nodeBChild e))
                    (qmClear (nodeCChild e))
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                    (qmClear (nodeBChild e))
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (qmClearVec (nodeAChildren e) 0)
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                        {
                          (qmClear (nodeAChild e))
                          (qmClearVec (nodeBChildren e) 0)
                        }
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                          {
                            (qmClear (nodeAChild e))
                            (qmClearArms (nodeBChildren e) 0)
                          }))))))))
          0
        }
      })))

(pub :: qmClearVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (qmClearVec xs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    0
    {
      (qmClear (vecGet xs i))
      (qmClearVec xs (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: qmClearArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (qmClearArms arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (qmClear (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)))
      (qmClearArms arms (+ i 1))
    }))

;; Rewrite every `?` in a body; a body with none comes back unchanged.
(pub :: qmBody (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmBody env e)
  {
    (let r
      (if (qmHas e)
        (qmRw env e)
        e))
    {
      (qmClear e)
      (qmClear r)
      r
    }
  })

; `e` is in tail position.
(pub :: qmRw (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmRw env e)
  (if (! (qmHas e))
    e
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (let sp (nodeSpan e))
      (if (&& (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM)) (! (qmHas (nodeBChild e))))
          (nodeBuildBinding tag (nodeAName e) (nodeBChild e) (qmRw env (nodeCChild e)) sp)
        (&& (== tag TAG_E_IF) (! (qmHas (nodeAChild e))))
          (nodeBuildEIf (nodeAChild e) (qmRw env (nodeBChild e)) (qmRw env (nodeCChild e)) sp)
        (&& (== tag TAG_E_MATCH) (! (qmHas (nodeAChild e))))
          (nodeBuildEMatch (nodeAChild e) (qmRwArms env (nodeBChildren e) vecNew 0) 0 sp)
        (&& (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN) (qmOnlyLast (nodeAChildren e)))
          (nodeBuildEBegin (qmRwLast env (nodeAChildren e)) 0 0 sp)
        (qmLift env e))
    }))

(pub :: qmRwArms (-> MEnv (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (qmRwArms env arms acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (qmRwArms env arms
        (vecPush acc (nodeBuildMArm (nodeAChild arm) (qmRw env (nodeBChild arm)) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
        (+ i 1))
    }))

; Only the last element of a block holds a `?`.
(pub :: qmOnlyLast (-> (Vec ASTNode) Bool))
(pub fn (qmOnlyLast xs)
  {
    (let n (vecLen xs))
    (if (== n 0)
      false
      {
        (let mut ok true)
        {
          (for i in 0..(- n 1)
            (if (qmHas (vecGet xs i))
              (set ok false)))
          ok
        }
      })
  })

(pub :: qmRwLast (-> MEnv (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (qmRwLast env xs)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (let n (vecLen xs))
    {
      (for i in 0..n
        (vecPush out
          (if (== i (- n 1))
            (qmRw env (vecGet xs i))
            (vecGet xs i))))
      out
    }
  })

; Lift the first `?` in evaluation order out of `e`, which is in tail
; position: the bindings of what ran before it, then a `match` on its
; operand whose `Ok` arm continues the rewritten rest.
(pub :: qmLift (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmLift env e)
  {
    (let st (QmSt :names vecNew :vals vecNew :scrut None :tname "" :tsp 0 :failed false))
    {
      (let hole (qmExtract env st e))
      (if st.failed
        e
        (match st.scrut
          ((None) e)
          ((Some scrutinee)
            (qmWrap st 0
              (nodeBuildEMatch scrutinee
                (qmArms env (expFresh env "q") st.tname (qmRw env hole) st.tsp) 0 st.tsp))
          )))
    }
  })

; The two arms: `((Err er) (Err er))` and `((Ok t) rest)`. The `Err`
; arm carries the `?`'s span, so a body whose declared result is not a
; `Result` is reported at the `?`.
(pub :: qmArms (-> MEnv String String ASTNode Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (qmArms env er t rest sp)
  {
    (let arms vecNew)
    (if (== env.carrier 1)
      {
        (vecPush arms (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildPCon0 "None" 0 0 sp) (mkEVarAt "None" sp) 0 sp))
        (vecPush arms
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Some" sp) (mkEVarAt t sp) nilWords sp) rest 0
            sp))
        arms
      }
      {
        (vecPush arms
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Err" sp) (mkEVarAt er sp) nilWords sp)
            (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Err" sp) (mkEVarAt er sp) nilWords sp) 0 sp))
        (vecPush arms
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Ok" sp) (mkEVarAt t sp) nilWords sp) rest 0 sp))
        arms
      })
  })

; The recorded bindings around `inner`, outermost first: a named one is
; a `let`, an unnamed one a statement run for its effect.
(pub :: qmWrap (-> QmSt Int ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmWrap st i inner)
  (if (>= i (vecLen st.names))
    inner
    {
      (let name (vecGetStr st.names i))
      (let v (vecGet st.vals i))
      (if (== (strLen name) 0)
        {
          (let xs vecNew)
          {
            (vecPush xs v)
            (vecPush xs (qmWrap st (+ i 1) inner))
            (nodeBuildEBegin xs 0 0 (nodeSpan v))
          }
        }
        (mkELetAt name v (qmWrap st (+ i 1) inner) (nodeSpan v)))
    }))

; A value evaluated before the `?`: a literal stays where it is, and
; anything else is bound to a fresh name so it still runs first.
(pub :: qmBindBefore (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmBindBefore env st x)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag x))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT) (|| (== tag TAG_E_STR) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_E_CHAR))))
      x
      {
        (let n (expFresh env "q"))
        {
          (vecPush st.names n)
          (vecPush st.vals x)
          (mkEVarAt n (nodeSpan x))
        }
      })
  })

; A statement evaluated before the `?`, for its effect only.
(pub :: qmRunBefore (-> QmSt ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (qmRunBefore st x)
  {
    (vecPush st.names "")
    (vecPush st.vals x)
    0
  })

(pub :: qmTail (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (qmTail xs i)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (for k in i..(vecLen xs)
        (vecPush out (vecGet xs k)))
      out
    }
  })

; Every tag admitted by qmHasCore has a qmExtract arm. Reaching this
; fallback means an internal caller or memo broke that precondition.
; Keep both the failure state and error: neither a value nor a warning
; may let the compiler emit a partly lowered tree.
(pub :: qmInvariant (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmInvariant env st e)
  {
    (set st.failed true)
    (expEmit env
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3146" "propagate-invariant" (nodeSpan e)
          "the compiler could not lower an inconsistent propagation node" None
          (Some "report this compiler bug with the smallest reproducing program")
          "the propagation scan and extractor must agree on every supported AST form; this is an internal compiler error")
        "propagation lowering stopped here"))
    e
  })

; The enclosing function's signature chooses the carrier. `Option`
; propagates with `Some` and `None`. Anything else, including a lambda
; whose type is not written yet, propagates with `Ok` and `Err`.
; The names are collected once. Looking each function up by scanning
; every declaration made expansion quadratic in the module.
(:: qmOptionNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) Strings))
(fn (qmOptionNames decls)
  (qmOptionNamesFrom decls 0 vecNew))

(:: qmOptionNamesFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Strings Strings))
(fn (qmOptionNamesFrom decls i out)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    out
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG) (qmTyOption (qmArrowResult (nodeTyChild d))))
        (vecPush out (nodeAName d)))
      (qmOptionNamesFrom decls (+ i 1) out)
    }))

(:: qmFnReturnsOption (-> MEnv String Bool))
(fn (qmFnReturnsOption env name)
  (== (strVecHas env.optNames name) 1))

(:: qmArrowResult (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmArrowResult t)
  (if (&& (! (nodeSame t nilNode)) (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR))
    (qmArrowResult (nodeBChild t))
    t))

(:: qmTyOption (-> ASTNode Bool))
(fn (qmTyOption t)
  (if (|| (nodeSame t nilNode) (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON))
    false
    (strEq (nodeAName t) "Option")))

; `(Ok e)` or `(Some e)`, from the enclosing function's carrier.
; The span is the expression's, so a later diagnostic can still point
; at it.
(:: qmOk (-> MEnv ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (qmOk env e sp)
  (nodeBuildEApp
    (mkEVarAt
      (if (== env.carrier 1)
        "Some"
        "Ok")
      sp)
    e nilWords sp))

; `produced` is a `Result`. Record it as the scrutinee and answer the
; name its `Ok` payload will be bound to.
(:: qmAsValue (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (qmAsValue env st produced sp)
  {
    (let t (expFresh env "q"))
    {
      (set st.scrut (Some produced))
      (set st.tname t)
      (set st.tsp sp)
      (mkEVarAt t sp)
    }
  })

; Match `scrut`. The `Err` arm returns that error. The `Ok` arm binds
; `okName` and runs `okBody`.
(:: qmBindResult (-> MEnv ASTNode String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (qmBindResult env scrut okName okBody sp)
  {
    (let err (expFresh env "q"))
    (nodeBuildEMatch scrut (qmArms env err okName okBody sp) 0 sp)
  })

; `(Ok ...)` and `(Err ...)` are already `Result`s. Wrapping one again
; turns a tail `(Ok n)` into `(Ok (Ok n))`.
(:: qmIsResult (-> MEnv ASTNode Bool))
(fn (qmIsResult env e)
  (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
    (if (&& (== env.carrier 1) (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR))
      (strEq (nodeAName e) "None")
      false)
    {
      (let head (nodeAChild e))
      (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
        false
        (if (== env.carrier 1)
          (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "Some") (strEq (nodeAName head) "None"))
          (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "Ok") (strEq (nodeAName head) "Err"))))
    }))

; `e` as a `Result`: no `?` becomes `(Ok e)`, and a `?` becomes an
; `Err` that skips the rest or an `Ok` of the value `e` would have had.
; An expression that is already `(Ok ...)` or `(Err ...)` stays as it is
; when it contains no further `?`.
(:: qmPropagate (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagate env e)
  (if (&& (qmIsResult env e) (! (qmHas e)))
    e
    (if (|| (nodeSame e nilNode) (! (qmHas e)))
      (qmOk env e (nodeSpan e))
      (qmProp env e))))

(:: qmPropagateIf (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateIf env e)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let cond (nodeAChild e))
    (if (qmHas cond)
      {
        (let c (expFresh env "q"))
        (qmBindResult env (qmPropagate env cond) c
          (nodeBuildEIf (mkEVarAt c sp) (qmPropagate env (nodeBChild e))
            (qmPropagate env (nodeCChild e)) sp)
          sp)
      }
      (nodeBuildEIf cond (qmPropagate env (nodeBChild e)) (qmPropagate env (nodeCChild e)) sp))
  })

(:: qmPropagateBinding (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateBinding env e)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let init (nodeBChild e))
    (let body (nodeCChild e))
    (if (qmHas init)
      {
        (let v (expFresh env "q"))
        (qmBindResult env (qmPropagate env init) v
          (nodeBuildBinding (nodeTag e) (nodeAName e) (mkEVarAt v sp) (qmPropagate env body) sp) sp)
      }
      (nodeBuildBinding (nodeTag e) (nodeAName e) init (qmPropagate env body) sp))
  })

(:: qmPropArms (-> MEnv (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(fn (qmPropArms env arms acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (qmPropArms env arms
        (vecPush acc
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeAChild arm) (qmPropagate env (nodeBChild arm)) 0 (nodeSpan arm)))
        (+ i 1))
    }))

(:: qmPropagateMatch (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateMatch env e)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let scrut (nodeAChild e))
    (if (qmHas scrut)
      {
        (let v (expFresh env "q"))
        (qmBindResult env (qmPropagate env scrut) v
          (nodeBuildEMatch (mkEVarAt v sp) (qmPropArms env (nodeBChildren e) vecNew 0) 0 sp) sp)
      }
      (nodeBuildEMatch scrut (qmPropArms env (nodeBChildren e) vecNew 0) 0 sp))
  })

; Statements before the first `?` run first. That statement's `Err`
; skips the rest. A later statement supplies the block's value.
(:: qmPropagateBegin (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateBegin env e)
  {
    (let xs (nodeAChildren e))
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let k (qmFirstWith xs 0))
    (let n (vecLen xs))
    (let before vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..k
        (vecPush before (vecGet xs i)))
      (let focus (qmPropagate env (vecGet xs k)))
      (let rest
        (if (== k (- n 1))
          focus
          {
            (let tail (qmTail xs (+ k 1)))
            (qmBindResult env focus "_"
              (if (== (vecLen tail) 1)
                (qmPropagate env (vecGet tail 0))
                (qmPropagateBegin env (nodeBuildEBegin tail 0 0 sp)))
              sp)
          }))
      (if (== (vecLen before) 0)
        rest
        {
          (vecPush before rest)
          (nodeBuildEBegin before 0 0 sp)
        })
    }
  })

; The guard is false once an earlier iteration stored an `Err`, so the
; condition and the body stop. The `Ok` payload is 0, which is what a
; finished loop answers.
(:: qmFlagGuard (-> MEnv String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (qmFlagGuard env flag cond sp)
  {
    (let arms vecNew)
    (let yes
      (if (== env.carrier 1)
        "Some"
        "Ok"))
    {
      (vecPush arms
        (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt yes sp) (mkEVarAt "_" sp) nilWords sp) cond 0 sp))
      (vecPush arms
        (if (== env.carrier 1)
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildPCon0 "None" 0 0 sp) (mkEVarAt "false" sp) 0 sp)
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Err" sp) (mkEVarAt "_" sp) nilWords sp)
            (mkEVarAt "false" sp) 0 sp)))
      (nodeBuildEMatch (mkEVarAt flag sp) arms 0 sp)
    }
  })

(:: qmFlagStep (-> MEnv String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (qmFlagStep env flag boxed sp)
  {
    (let err (expFresh env "q"))
    (let arms vecNew)
    (let yes
      (if (== env.carrier 1)
        "Some"
        "Ok"))
    {
      (vecPush arms
        (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt yes sp) (mkEVarAt "_" sp) nilWords sp) (mkEInt 0) 0
          sp))
      (vecPush arms
        (if (== env.carrier 1)
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildPCon0 "None" 0 0 sp)
            (nodeBuildESet flag (mkEVarAt "None" sp) 0 sp) 0 sp)
          (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Err" sp) (mkEVarAt err sp) nilWords sp)
            (nodeBuildESet flag (nodeBuildEApp (mkEVarAt "Err" sp) (mkEVarAt err sp) nilWords sp) 0
              sp)
            0 sp)))
      (nodeBuildEMatch boxed arms 0 sp)
    }
  })

(:: qmPropagateWhile (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateWhile env e)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let flag (expFresh env "q"))
    (let stmts (nodeBChildren e))
    (let body
      (if (== (vecLen stmts) 1)
        (vecGet stmts 0)
        (nodeBuildEBegin stmts 0 0 sp)))
    (let loopBody vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush loopBody (qmFlagStep env flag (qmPropagate env body) sp))
      (let seq vecNew)
      {
        (vecPush seq (nodeBuildEWhile (qmFlagGuard env flag (nodeAChild e) sp) loopBody 0 sp))
        (vecPush seq (mkEVarAt flag sp))
        (nodeBuildELetm flag (qmOk env (mkEInt 0) sp) (nodeBuildEBegin seq 0 0 sp) sp)
      }
    }
  })

; Lift the first `?` and wrap the value that remains in `Ok`.
; The region's value is the body's value. A `?` in the body makes
; that value a `Result`; the region checker then allows it to leave
; only when both sides are scalars.
(:: qmPropagateRegion (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateRegion env e)
  (nodeBuildERegion (nodeAText e) (qmPropagate env (nodeBChild e)) (nodeC e) (nodeSpan e)))

; A handler's value is its body's value, so a `?` in the body
; propagates through the handler the same way.
(:: qmPropagateHandle (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropagateHandle env e)
  (nodeBuildEHandle (qmPropagate env (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBChildren e) (nodeCChild e) (nodeSpan e)))

(:: qmPropLift (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmPropLift env e)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (let st (QmSt :names vecNew :vals vecNew :scrut None :tname "" :tsp 0 :failed false))
    {
      (let hole (qmExtract env st e))
      (if st.failed
        e
        (match st.scrut
          ((None) (qmOk env e sp))
          ((Some scrutinee)
            {
              (let continued (qmRw env hole))
              (qmWrap st 0
                (nodeBuildEMatch scrutinee
                  (qmArms env (expFresh env "q") st.tname
                    (if (qmIsResult env continued)
                      continued
                      (qmOk env continued sp))
                    st.tsp)
                  0 st.tsp))
            }
          )))
    }
  })

(:: qmProp (-> MEnv ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (qmProp env e)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag e))
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
      {
        (let t (expFresh env "q"))
        (qmBindResult env (qmRw env (nodeAChild e)) t (qmOk env (mkEVarAt t sp) sp) sp)
      }
      (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
        (qmPropagateIf env e)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
          (qmPropagateMatch env e)
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            (qmPropagateBinding env e)
            (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
              (qmPropagateBegin env e)
              (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                (qmPropagateWhile env e)
                (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                  (qmPropagateRegion env e)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                    (qmPropagateHandle env e)
                    (qmPropLift env e)))))))))
  })

; Find the first `?` under `e` in evaluation order, record it and what
; ran before it in `st`, and answer `e` with that `?` replaced by the
; name its `Ok` payload will be bound to. `e` holds a `?`.
(pub :: qmExtract (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmExtract env st e)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag e))
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
        (if (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
          (nodeBuildETry (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) 0 0 sp)
          {
            (let t (expFresh env "q"))
            {
              (let scrutinee (nodeAChild e))
              (set st.scrut (if (nodeSame scrutinee nilNode)
                None
                (Some scrutinee)))
              (set st.tname t)
              (set st.tsp sp)
              (mkEVarAt t sp)
            }
          })
      (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        (qmExtractApp env st e)
      (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
        (if (qmHas (nodeBChild e))
          (nodeBuildBinding tag (nodeAName e) (qmExtract env st (nodeBChild e)) (nodeCChild e) sp)
          (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateBinding env e) sp))
      (== tag TAG_E_IF)
        (if (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
          (nodeBuildEIf (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBChild e) (nodeCChild e) sp)
          (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateIf env e) sp))
      (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
        (if (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
          (nodeBuildEMatch (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBChildren e) 0 sp)
          (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateMatch env e) sp))
      (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
        (qmExtractBlock env st e)
      (== tag TAG_E_SET)
        (nodeBuildESet (nodeAText e) (qmExtract env st (nodeBChild e)) 0 sp)
      (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
        (nodeBuildEField (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBName e) 0 sp)
      (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
        (if (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
          (nodeBuildESetf (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBName e) (nodeCChild e) sp)
          {
            (let base (qmBindBefore env st (nodeAChild e)))
            (nodeBuildESetf base (nodeBName e) (qmExtract env st (nodeCChild e)) sp)
          })
      (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
        (if (qmHas (nodeAChild e))
          (nodeBuildEWhile (qmExtract env st (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBChildren e) 0 sp)
          (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateWhile env e) sp))
      (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
        (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateRegion env e) sp)
      (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
        (qmAsValue env st (qmPropagateHandle env e) sp)
      (qmInvariant env st e))
  })

; An application, read as its spine: the head, then each argument, in
; the order they run. Everything before the child that holds the `?`
; is bound first; a head that is a plain name is never bound, since
; naming a function evaluates nothing.
(pub :: qmExtractApp (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmExtractApp env st e)
  {
    (let args vecNew)
    {
      (let hd (qmSpine e args))
      (if (qmHas hd)
        (qmRebuild (qmExtract env st hd) args 0)
        {
          (let k (qmFirstWith args 0))
          (let h2
            (if (== (nodeTag hd) TAG_E_VAR)
              hd
              (qmBindBefore env st hd)))
          (let out vecNew)
          {
            (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
              (vecPush out
                (if (< i k)
                  (qmBindBefore env st (vecGet args i))
                  (if (== i k)
                    (qmExtract env st (vecGet args i))
                    (vecGet args i)))))
            (qmRebuild h2 out 0)
          }
        })
    }
  })

; The head of `e`'s application spine, with its arguments pushed onto
; `args` in order.
(pub :: qmSpine (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmSpine e args)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (let hd (qmSpine (nodeAChild e) args))
      {
        (vecPush args (nodeBChild e))
        hd
      }
    }
    e))

(pub :: qmRebuild (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmRebuild f args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    f
    (qmRebuild (mkEApp f (vecGet args i)) args (+ i 1))))

(pub :: qmFirstWith (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (qmFirstWith xs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    i
    (if (qmHas (vecGet xs i))
      i
      (qmFirstWith xs (+ i 1)))))

; A block: the statements before the one that holds the `?` run first,
; and the rest stays a block in the `Ok` arm.
(pub :: qmExtractBlock (-> MEnv QmSt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (qmExtractBlock env st e)
  {
    (let xs (nodeAChildren e))
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (let k (qmFirstWith xs 0))
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
          (if (< i k)
            (qmRunBefore st (vecGet xs i))
            (vecPush out
              (if (== i k)
                (qmExtract env st (vecGet xs i))
                (vecGet xs i)))))
        (if (== (vecLen out) 1)
          (vecGet out 0)
          (nodeBuildEBegin out 0 0 (nodeSpan e)))
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: expandDecl (-> ASTNode MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expandDecl d env)
  (if (nodeSame d nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
      {
        (let bs vecNew)
        {
          (expPushNames (nodeBNames d) bs env 0)
          (let saved env.carrier)
          {
            (set env.carrier (if (qmFnReturnsOption env (nodeAName d))
              1
              0))
            (writeNodeChild d 3 (qmBody env (expandExpr (nodeCChild d) env bs)))
            (set env.carrier saved)
            0
          }
        }
      }
      ; An `impl` method's expression and a `trait` method's DEFAULT
      ; body were the two expression positions this arm did not reach,
      ; and both were walked here from 2026-08-14 until traits went in
      ; 0.6.0. Without that walk a macro invoked in either was never
      ; expanded at all: `check` said OK - the checker's macro-head arm
      ; answers a silent wildcard - and `opt` died on a call to a
      ; symbol nothing defined (MAC-EXP-3a).
      0)))

(pub :: expandDeclsIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expandDeclsIn decls env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    ; The invocation site's module, for macro visibility - and the
    ; declaration's module for the frame table, which word 9's
    ; template-walk overwrites must not touch.
    {
      (set env.site (nodeModule (vecGet decls i)))
      (set env.declmod (nodeModule (vecGet decls i)))
      (expandDecl (vecGet decls i) env)
      (set env.site 0)
      (set env.declmod 0)
      (expandDeclsIn decls env (+ i 1))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A macro declaration is checked once, here, rather than at each
; invocation: two parameters with the same name make every reference
; to that name resolve to the LAST argument, silently. Measured on the
; emit-time expander: `(macro (dup a a) (+ a a))` invoked as
; `(dup 10 20)` answered 40.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stamp a diagnostic with the unit of the module its span indexes,
; answering the diagnostic so the call wraps a `mkDiagNote` in place.
; A module with no unit uses the entry-file diagnostic convention.
(pub :: expMarkUnit (-> MEnv diag::Diag a diag::Diag))
(pub fn (expMarkUnit env dg m)
  {
    (diagSetUnit dg (expUnitForDiag env.units (expModuleWordOpt (as m Int))))
    dg
  })

(pub :: expCheckMacros (-> MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckMacros env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen (expMacrosVec env)))
    0
    {
      (expCheckDupParams (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env 0)
      (expCheckRuleParams (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env 0)
      (expCheckRuleArities (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env 0)
      (expCheckRuleEllipsis (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env 0)
      (expCheckRuleLiterals (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env)
      ; Declaration templates only: a rule whose template is only an
      ; expression has an empty declaration vector.
      (expCheckTplKinds (vecGet (expMacrosVec env) i) env 0)
      (expCheckMacros env (+ i 1))
    }))

; A binder may constrain several forms at one repetition depth. A name
; shared between the fixed prefix and the repeating pattern would mean
; both one form and a sequence, so it remains AX3020.
(pub :: expCheckRuleParams (-> ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckRuleParams mac env i)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some rules)
      (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
        0
        {
          (let r (vecGet rules i))
          (if (let (Some k) (expRepIndex r))
            {
              (let fixed vecNew)
              (for j in 0..k
                (expRuleBindersOf (vecGet (expRulePats r) j) fixed))
              (let ns (expUniqueNames fixed))
              (let reps (expUniqueNames (expRuleBindersOf (vecGet (expRulePats r) k) vecNew)))
              (for n in 0..(vecLen reps)
                (if (isSome (expParamIndex ns (vecGetStr reps n)))
                  (expEmit env
                    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3020" "macro-duplicate-parameter" (nodeSpan mac)
                      (concat (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac)) "` binds the parameter `")
                        (concat (vecGetStr reps n) "` at two repetition depths"))
                      None (Some "give the fixed and repeated bindings different names")
                      "a pattern binder may constrain several forms at one depth; one form and a sequence need separate names"))))
            })
          (expCheckRuleParams mac env (+ i 1))
        })
    )))

(pub :: expUniqueNames (-> Names Names))
(pub fn (expUniqueNames ns)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen ns)
      (if (isNone (expParamIndex out (vecGetStr ns i)))
        (vecPush out (vecGetStr ns i))))
    out
  })

; AN UNREACHABLE RULE is refused at the macro's own line.
;
; This refusal used to be "two rules of one ARITY", and its stated
; reason - "arity is what selects a rule, so the second is
; unreachable" - stopped being true the moment a pattern could be
; something other than a binder (MAC-LANG-15). Two rules of one arity
; and DIFFERENT SHAPES are both reachable, and refusing them would
; refuse the whole point: the specification's own `simplify` example
; (§10.4) is seven rules of arity one.
;
; What survives is the part that was always the real claim. A rule
; whose every element is a plain binder matches EVERY invocation of
; its arity - it is irrefutable - so nothing of that arity after it
; can ever be reached. That is the narrowed test, it refuses exactly
; what the old one refused among all-binder rules, and it admits
; exactly what patterns are for.
; MAC-LANG-16 admits ONE repeating element per rule. With two,
; "the arguments left over" stops having an answer - `(m a ... b ...)`
; can split its arguments anywhere - and a macro system that picks for
; you is one whose expansion nobody can predict.
(pub :: expCheckRuleEllipsis (-> ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckRuleEllipsis mac env i)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some rules)
      (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
        0
        {
          ; MAC-LANG-16, second half: a repeating element that is a
          ; PATTERN binds each of its binders to a sequence of its
          ; own, in lockstep over the absorbed tail
          ; (`expMatchRuleAt`). What is still refused is a repeat
          ; BENEATH a repeat: `...` inside the repeating pattern
          ; would need sequences of sequences, which is a third
          ; thing and is not built.
          {
            (let k (expRepIndex (vecGet rules i)))
            (if (match k
              ((None) false)
              ((Some index) (== (patHasEllipsis (vecGet (expRulePats (vecGet rules i)) index)) 1)))
              (expEmit env
                (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3034" "macro-ellipsis" (nodeSpan mac)
                  (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac)) "` repeats beneath a repeat") None
                  (Some
                    "repeat one level only - `(m (f a) ...)` - and take deeper shapes apart across rules")
                  "a repeated pattern binds each binder to one sequence; a `...` inside it would bind sequences of sequences (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16)")))
          }
          (if (> (expRepCount (expRuleReps (vecGet rules i)) 0 0) 1)
            (expEmit env
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3034" "macro-ellipsis" (nodeSpan mac)
                (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac)) "` has a rule with more than one `...`")
                None
                (Some
                  "keep one repeating element per rule; the arguments a second one would take are the ones the first already took")
                "a repeating element absorbs every argument left after the fixed ones (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16), so a second has nothing defined to absorb and where the split fell would be the expander's guess")))
          (expCheckRuleEllipsis mac env (+ i 1))
        })
    )))

; A literal no pattern spells is dead text - and the likeliest
; reading is a typo for a name the patterns spell, in which case
; the rule binds where the author meant to match. Refused at the
; macro's own line (`AX3066`), beside the other definition-time
; rule checks. Literals are list-scoped, so the used set is the
; UNION across rules: a literal spelled in any rule is live.
(pub :: expCheckRuleLiterals (-> ASTNode MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expCheckRuleLiterals mac env)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some rules)
      (if (== (vecLen rules) 0)
        0
        {
          (let union vecNew)
          {
            (expCollectUsed rules union 0)
            (expCheckLiteralsUsed mac rules union env)
          }
        })
    )))

(pub :: expCollectUsed (-> MacroRules Names Int Int))
(pub fn (expCollectUsed rules union i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
    0
    {
      (let rule (vecGet rules i))
      (expCollectUsedVec rule.literals.used union 0)
      (expCollectUsed rules union (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expCollectUsedVec (-> Names Names Int Int))
(pub fn (expCollectUsedVec used union i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen used))
    0
    {
      (if (== (strVecHas union (vecGetStr used i)) 0)
        (vecPush union (vecGet used i)))
      (expCollectUsedVec used union (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expCheckLiteralsUsed (-> ASTNode MacroRules Names MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expCheckLiteralsUsed mac rules union env)
  (if (== (vecLen rules) 0)
    0
    {
      (let rule (vecGet rules 0))
      (expCheckLiteralsUsedIn mac rule.literals.declared union env 0)
    }))

(pub :: expCheckLiteralsUsedIn (-> ASTNode Names Names MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckLiteralsUsedIn mac lits union env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen lits))
    0
    (if (== (strVecHas union (vecGetStr lits i)) 1)
      (expCheckLiteralsUsedIn mac lits union env (+ i 1))
      {
        (expEmit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3066" "macro-literal" (nodeSpan mac)
            (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac))
              (concat "` declares the literal `"
                (concat (vecGetStr lits i) "` but no pattern spells it")))
            None (Some "spell it in a pattern, or drop it from the header")
            "a declared literal that matches nothing is dead text, and the likeliest reading is a typo for a name the patterns spell - in which case the rule binds where the author meant to match"))
        (expCheckLiteralsUsedIn mac lits union env (+ i 1))
      })))

(pub :: expRepCount (-> Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expRepCount reps i n)
  (if (>= i (vecLen reps))
    n
    (expRepCount reps (+ i 1) (+ n (vecGet reps i)))))

(pub :: expEllipsisDiag (-> MEnv String String String Int Int))
(pub fn (expEllipsisDiag env name msg help isp)
  (expEmit env
    (expMarkUnit env
      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3034" "macro-ellipsis" isp
        (concat (concat (concat "`" name) "` ") msg) None (Some help)
        "a template MUST use a repeated binding under an ellipsis of the same depth (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16): a name bound to a SEQUENCE is many forms, and every position that takes one form needs to be told which")
      env.declmod)))

(pub :: expCheckRuleArities (-> ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckRuleArities mac env i)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some rules)
      (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
        0
        {
          (if (let (Some j) (expShadowingRule rules i 0))
            (expEmit env
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3033" "macro-unreachable-rule" (nodeSpan mac)
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac))
                      "` has a rule that can never match: rule ")
                    (concat (expFmtInt (+ i 1)) " is shadowed by rule "))
                  (expFmtInt (+ j 1)))
                None
                (Some
                  "give the earlier rule a pattern that can FAIL - a literal, or a parenthesised shape of two or more elements - or delete this one. A one-element `(p)` is the bare binder `p`: the parentheses are the same ones an argument loses, so it still matches everything")
                "rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose pattern matches wins (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-18); an earlier rule of the same arity whose elements are all plain binders matches every invocation of that arity, so nothing of that arity after it is reachable")))
          ; Its OWN code since 2026-08-16. This shared
          ; `AX3020` while the refusal was "two rules of
          ; one arity", on the stated ground that both
          ; cases say "this macro declares the same thing
          ; twice" (MAC-LANG-14). Under MAC-LANG-15 they
          ; do not: a repeated PARAMETER is one name meaning
          ; two things, an unreachable RULE is a distinct
          ; thing that can never run, and a slug reading
          ; `macro-duplicate-parameter` on the second is a
          ; machine-readable field that is simply wrong.
          (expCheckRuleArities mac env (+ i 1))
        })
    )))

; The index of an earlier IRREFUTABLE rule that takes every
; invocation rule `i` could take; None when no earlier rule covers it.
;
; Without a repeat both rules take exactly one arity, so the test is
; equality. WITH one the earlier rule's arity is a floor and it takes
; everything from there up, so it starves any later rule whose own
; floor is at least as high - which is how `(m a ...)` first makes
; every rule after it dead.
(pub :: expShadowingRule (-> MacroRules Int Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expShadowingRule rules i j)
  (if (>= j i)
    None
    {
      (let earlier (vecGet rules j))
      (let later (vecGet rules i))
      (let covers
        (match (expRepIndex earlier)
          ((Some floor)
            (>=
              (match (expRepIndex later)
                ((Some laterFloor) laterFloor)
                ((None) (vecLen (expRulePats later))))
              floor)
          )
          ((None)
            (match (expRepIndex later)
              ((Some _) false)
              ((None) (== (vecLen (expRulePats earlier)) (vecLen (expRulePats later)))))
          )))
      (if (&& (== (expRuleIrrefutable earlier) 1) covers)
        (Some j)
        (expShadowingRule rules i (+ j 1)))
    }))

; A rule-form macro's template is checked ONCE, at the macro's own
; line: v1 generates `fn` and `::` declarations and further macro
; invocations, and any other declaration kind in a template is AX3021
; here, before any invocation exists - the loud-at-definition shape
; MAC-SAFE-4 uses for the empty form, and the reachable producer
; AX3021 was promised (MAC-CAP-2: the ninth form refuses in the commit
; that adds it).
; Every rule's template, not just the first: a kind refusal is a
; property of the macro, and a macro is now a list of rules
; (MAC-LANG-14).
(pub :: expCheckTplKinds (-> ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckTplKinds mac env i)
  (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some rules)
      (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
        0
        {
          (expCheckTplKindVec mac (expRuleTpl (vecGet rules i)) env 0)
          (expCheckTplKinds mac env (+ i 1))
        })
    )))

; The template half of a rule, named rather than indexed at every site.
(pub :: expRuleTpl (-> MacroRule Nodes))
(pub fn (expRuleTpl r)
  r.declarations)

; One pattern and source spelling per top-level element (MAC-LANG-15).
(pub :: expRulePats (-> MacroRule Nodes))
(pub fn (expRulePats r)
  r.patterns)

(pub :: expRuleSpells (-> MacroRule Names))
(pub fn (expRuleSpells r)
  r.spellings)

; Repeat flags (MAC-LANG-16): 1 where the element repeats, 0 where it does
; not. A rule with a repeating element matches a RANGE of arities
; rather than one, which is why `expRuleForForms` stopped comparing
; lengths and started asking this.
(pub :: expRuleReps (-> MacroRule Ints))
(pub fn (expRuleReps r)
  r.repeats)

; The optional index of the rule's repeating element. v1 admits ONE, and
; `expCheckRuleEllipsis` refuses the rest: with two, "the remaining
; arguments" stops having an answer - `(m a ... b ...)` can split its
; arguments anywhere, and a macro system that picks for you is a macro
; system whose expansion nobody can predict.
(pub :: expRepIndex (-> MacroRule (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expRepIndex r)
  (expRepIndexFrom (expRuleReps r) 0))

(pub :: expRepIndexFrom (-> Ints Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expRepIndexFrom reps i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen reps))
    None
    (if (== (vecGet reps i) 1)
      (Some i)
      (expRepIndexFrom reps (+ i 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; LITERALS (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-17, subset): a rule pattern may
; spell a literal identifier, which matches an argument spelling the
; SAME binding - and binds nothing.
;
; Spelling with a reservation: a literal occurrence is an `E_VAR`
; whose name is `syntax/literal ` plus the spelling (note the
; SPACE - no lexed identifier contains one, so no template name can
; resolve to it, the same trick `syntax/pat` and the
; `syntax/join a b` marker strings use). The parser rewrites
; declared literals to this spelling when it parses the rules, so
; arity, tree shape and every reader that counts elements is
; untouched: a literal is still one `E_VAR` node in exactly the
; position its spelling stood.
;
; Each argument identifier carries its lexical scopes and selected
; binding. Captured caller syntax retains its module when forwarded;
; template free identifiers retain their definition-site stamp. Global
; identities include functions, macros, constructors, effects and extern
; items. Unbound DSL keywords agree by spelling.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A builtin operator spelling: the eighteen primitives the checker
; registers for every program (typecheck.ax `regFn`), which resolve
; although no declaration spells them. The list going stale fails
; LOUD, never wrong: an unlisted primitive falls through to the
; unbound spelling both sides agree on while nothing shadows it,
; and refuses to match where something does.
(pub :: expBuiltinOp (-> String Int))
(pub fn (expBuiltinOp name)
  (if (strEq name "+")
    1
    (if (strEq name "-")
      1
      (if (strEq name "*")
        1
        (if (strEq name "/")
          1
          (if (strEq name "%")
            1
            (if (strEq name "&")
              1
              (if (strEq name "|")
                1
                (if (strEq name "^")
                  1
                  (if (strEq name "<<")
                    1
                    (if (strEq name ">>")
                      1
                      (if (strEq name "==")
                        1
                        (if (strEq name "!=")
                          1
                          (if (strEq name "<")
                            1
                            (if (strEq name ">")
                              1
                              (if (strEq name "<=")
                                1
                                (if (strEq name ">=")
                                  1
                                  (if (strEq name "&&")
                                    1
                                    (if (strEq name "||")
                                      1
                                      (if (strEq name "!")
                                        1
                                        0))))))))))))))))))))

; One name's canonical key at one module: `D:` plus what the name
; resolves to where it stands - the module's own declaration, the
; single visible declarer, or the builtin - and `U:` plus the bare
; spelling where it resolves to nothing (or to two declarers, whose
; ambiguity the checker reports at the use). Keys compare with
; `strEq`: equal keys are one binding, and two unbound spellings
; agree exactly when they spell alike.
;
; A qualified spelling (`Pre$+` for source `Pre::+`) resolves
; directly: the module names its declaration, so no search runs.
; A builtin through a qualifier is still the builtin - `Pre::+`
; and `+` are one binding although the spellings differ, which is
; the case the key exists to catch.
(pub :: expCanonKey (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int String String))
(pub fn (expCanonKey decls modW name)
  {
    (let qual (expModOf name))
    (let bare (bareOf name))
    (let own
      (expBindingIn decls bare
        (match qual
          ((None) modW)
          ((Some module)
            (as module Int)
          ))
        0 0 ""))
    (let imported
      (match qual
        ((None) (expBindingIn decls bare modW 1 0 ""))
        ((Some _) "")))
    (if (! (strEq own ""))
      own
      (if (strStartsWith imported "M:")
        imported
        (if (== (expBuiltinOp bare) 1)
          (concat "D:builtin:" bare)
          (if (isSome qual)
            (concat "U:" name)
            (if (strEq imported "")
              (concat "U:" name)
              imported)))))
  })

; Exact module lookup precedes visible imports. Signatures and bodies
; share one key; constructors include their owning type, so two modules'
; homonymous constructors remain different bindings.
(pub :: expBindingIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int Int String String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expBindingIn decls name modW imports i found)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    found
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (let eligible
        (if (== imports 0)
          (expSameMod (nodeModuleName d) (as modW String))
          (if (&& (!= (nodeModule d) 0) (== (nodeVis d) 1))
            1
            0)))
      (let key
        (if (== eligible 1)
          (expDeclBindingKey d name)
          ""))
      (if (|| (strEq key "")
            (|| (&& (strStartsWith found "D:") (strStartsWith key "C:"))
              (&& (strStartsWith found "M:") (! (strStartsWith key "M:")))))
        (expBindingIn decls name modW imports (+ i 1) found)
        (if (|| (strStartsWith key "M:")
              (|| (strEq found "")
                (|| (strEq found key) (&& (strStartsWith key "D:") (strStartsWith found "C:")))))
          (expBindingIn decls name modW imports (+ i 1) key)
          (concat "A:" name)))
    }))

(pub :: expBindingName (-> ASTNode String String))
(pub fn (expBindingName d name)
  (if (== (nodeModule d) 0)
    name
    (concat (concat (nodeModuleName d) "$") name)))

(pub :: expDeclBindingKey (-> ASTNode String String))
(pub fn (expDeclBindingKey d name)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag d))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_FN) (|| (== tag TAG_D_SIG) (== tag TAG_D_MACRO)))
      (if (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) name)
        (concat
          (if (== tag TAG_D_MACRO)
            "M:"
            "D:")
          (expBindingName d name))
        "")
      (if (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT)
        (if (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) name)
          (concat "C:" (expBindingName d name))
          "")
        (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
          (expCtorBindingKey (nodeBChildren d) d name 0)
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT) (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN))
            (if (isSome (expParamIndex (nodeBNames d) name))
              (concat "D:" (expBindingName d name))
              "")
            ""))))
  })

(pub :: expCtorBindingKey (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode String Int String))
(pub fn (expCtorBindingKey cs d name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    ""
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet cs i)) name)
      (concat (concat "C:" (expBindingName d (bareOf (nodeAName d)))) (concat ":" name))
      (expCtorBindingKey cs d name (+ i 1)))))

; The match context rule selection threads: the merged declaration
; list both canonicalizations read, and the invocation-site and
; macro-definition modules they resolve at. One vec rather than
; three parameters, so the seven functions from selection to leaf
; match grow by one slot each instead of three.
(pub struct ExpMatchContext
  (decls : Nodes)
  (site : Int)
  (definition : Int))

(pub :: expMatchCtx (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int ExpMatchContext))
(pub fn (expMatchCtx decls siteW defW)
  (ExpMatchContext :decls decls :site siteW :definition defW))

(pub :: expIsLiteral (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expIsLiteral p)
  (if (!= (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR)
    0
    (if (strStartsWith (nodeAName p) "syntax/literal ")
      1
      0)))

(pub :: expLitName (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (expLitName p)
  (strSlice (nodeAName p) 15 (- (strLen (nodeAName p)) 15)))

(pub :: expMatchLiteral (-> String ASTNode ExpMatchContext Int))
; One literal against one argument form: the canonical keys at the
; invocation site and at the macro's definition must agree. Anything
; that is not a bare name cannot spell a binding, so only an
; `E_VAR` argument can match.
(pub fn (expMatchLiteral nm a lctx)
  (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
    0
    (if (&& (! (strStartsWith (expSyntaxKey a lctx.decls lctx.site) "A:"))
          (strEq (expSyntaxKey a lctx.decls lctx.site) (expCanonKey lctx.decls lctx.definition nm)))
      1
      0)))

; Repeated pattern binders constrain syntax instead of discarding the
; first value. Identifiers compare their captured bindings, literals
; their values, and compound forms their ordered children and labels.
(pub :: expSyntaxSame (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expSyntaxSame a b)
  (if (nodeSame a b)
    1
    (if (|| (nodeSame a nilNode) (nodeSame b nilNode))
      0
      (if (!= (nodeTag a) (nodeTag b))
        0
        (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (strEq (expSyntaxKey a vecNew 0) (expSyntaxKey b vecNew 0))
            1
            0)
          (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_STR)
            (expStrLitEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
            (if (|| (== (expSyntaxSlotKind (nodeTag a) 1) 0) (== (expSyntaxSlotsSame a b 1) 0))
              0
              (expSyntaxStringsSame (nodeFieldNamesNames a) (nodeFieldNamesNames b)))))))))

; Slot kinds: 0 is an unused word, 1 a scalar, 2 a name, 3 a node,
; 4 a node vector and 5 a name vector. Positions and checker metadata
; do not change syntax equality.
(pub :: expSyntaxSlotKind (-> Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expSyntaxSlotKind tag slot)
  (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_E_CHAR)))
    (if (|| (== slot 1) (&& (== tag TAG_E_INT) (== slot 2)))
      1
      0)
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_APP) (|| (== tag TAG_E_IF) (== tag TAG_M_ARM)))
      (if (&& (== slot 3) (!= tag TAG_E_IF))
        0
        3)
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
        (if (== slot 1)
          2
          3)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
          (if (== slot 1)
            5
            (if (== slot 2)
              3
              0))
          (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
            (if (== slot 1)
              4
              0)
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_MATCH))
              (if (== slot 1)
                3
                (if (== slot 2)
                  4
                  0))
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_SET) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                (if (== slot 1)
                  2
                  (if (== slot 2)
                    3
                    0))
                (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== tag TAG_E_SETF))
                  (if (== slot 2)
                    2
                    (if (&& (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== slot 3))
                      0
                      3))
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
                    (if (== slot 1)
                      3
                      0)
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                      (if (== slot 2)
                        4
                        3)
                      (if (== tag TAG_P_CON0)
                        (if (== slot 1)
                          2
                          0)
                        (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
                          (if (== slot 1)
                            2
                            (if (== slot 2)
                              5
                              4))
                          0)))))))))))))

(pub :: expSyntaxSlotsSame (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (expSyntaxSlotsSame a b slot)
  (if (> slot 3)
    1
    {
      (let kind (expSyntaxSlotKind (nodeTag a) slot))
      (let same
        (if (== kind 0)
            1
          (== kind 1)
            (if (== (nodeWireWord a slot) (nodeWireWord b slot))
              1
              0)
          (== kind 2)
            (if (strEq (nodeTextAt a slot) (nodeTextAt b slot))
              1
              0)
          (== kind 3)
            (expSyntaxSame (nodeChildAt a slot) (nodeChildAt b slot))
          (== kind 4)
            (expSyntaxVecSame (nodeChildrenAt a slot) (nodeChildrenAt b slot))
          (expSyntaxStringsSame (nodeNamesAt a slot) (nodeNamesAt b slot))))
      (if (== same 0)
        0
        (expSyntaxSlotsSame a b (+ slot 1)))
    }))

(pub :: expSyntaxVecSame (-> Nodes Nodes Int))
(pub fn (expSyntaxVecSame a b)
  (if (addrEq (addrOf a) (addrOf b))
    1
    (if (|| (vecIsNull a) (vecIsNull b))
      0
      {
        (let av a)
        (let bv b)
        (let mut i 0)
        (let mut same
          (if (== (vecLen av) (vecLen bv))
            1
            0))
        (while (&& (== same 1) (< i (vecLen av)))
          {
            (set same (expSyntaxSame (vecGet av i) (vecGet bv i)))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        same
      })))

(pub :: expSyntaxStringsSame (-> Names Names Int))
(pub fn (expSyntaxStringsSame a b)
  (if (addrEq (addrOf a) (addrOf b))
    1
    (if (|| (vecIsNull a) (vecIsNull b))
      0
      {
        (let av a)
        (let bv b)
        (let mut i 0)
        (let mut same
          (if (== (vecLen av) (vecLen bv))
            1
            0))
        (while (&& (== same 1) (< i (vecLen av)))
          {
            (if (! (strEq (vecGetStr av i) (vecGetStr bv i)))
              (set same 0))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        same
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; MATCHING (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-15).
;
; The whole of what a match produces is the two PARALLEL VECTORS
; substitution has always taken - a `Vec String` of names and a
; `Vec node` of forms, read by `expParamIndex` and then `vecGet`. That
; is why nothing below `expInstantiateDecl` changes: for an
; all-binder pattern the vectors a match builds are the same contents
; in the same order as the rule's own slot 0 and the invocation's own
; argument vector, which is the regression argument for the 49
; rule-form macros already in the tree.
;
; The order is left-to-right because both sides walk left-to-right,
; and `expParamIndex` is a name lookup rather than a position, so the
; only thing the order has to be is CONSISTENT between the two
; vectors - which building them in one pass makes true by
; construction.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; One pattern element against one argument FORM. 1 on a match, 0 on a
; refusal. Binders are pushed as they are met; a refusal may leave a
; partial push behind, which is why a failed rule's vectors are
; DISCARDED rather than truncated (`expRuleForForms` makes a fresh
; pair per rule).
(pub :: expMatchPat (-> ASTNode ASTNode Names Nodes ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchPat p a names vals lctx)
  {
    (let pt (nodeTag p))
    (if (== pt TAG_E_VAR)
      ; A literal matches one binding and binds nothing: no push onto
      ; either vector, so a literal never allocates a parameter slot
      ; and never collides with a binder of the same spelling.
      (if (== (expIsLiteral p) 1)
        (expMatchLiteral (expLitName p) a lctx)
        ; `_` matches anything and binds nothing - which is also why
        ; several `_` in one rule are legal where two of any other
        ; name are not.
        (if (strEq (nodeAName p) "_")
          1
          (match (expParamIndex names (nodeAName p))
            ((Some prior) (expSyntaxSame (vecGet vals prior) a))
            ((None)
              {
                (vecPush names (nodeAName p))
                (vecPush vals a)
                1
              }
            ))))
      (if (== pt TAG_E_INT)
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_INT) (== (as (nodeA a) Int) (as (nodeA p) Int)))
          1
          0)
        (if (== pt TAG_E_CHAR)
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_CHAR) (== (as (nodeA a) Int) (as (nodeA p) Int)))
            1
            0)
          (if (== pt TAG_E_FLT)
            ; Its BITS, which is what the node holds - `parseLit` ran
            ; `floatLexemeBits` on the way in - so this is a comparison of
            ; VALUES and `1.0` matches `1.00`. The same is true one arm up:
            ; `parseLit` ran `strToInt`, so `10` matches `1_0`. Measured
            ; both ways round, because an earlier revision of this comment
            ; claimed the opposite.
            (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_FLT) (== (as (nodeA a) Int) (as (nodeA p) Int)))
              1
              0)
            (if (== pt TAG_E_STR)
              ; A string is the ONE literal whose node keeps its lexeme
              ; verbatim - quotes and escapes included, by a design the
              ; whole compiler relies on, with the emitter unescaping on the
              ; way out. So `strEq` on the nodes would compare SPELLINGS
              ; where every other literal kind compares values, and
              ; `(m T "\t")` would refuse an argument written as a real tab.
              ; Measured: it did, answering the fall-through rule while the
              ; identically-spelled argument took this one. Decoding here
              ; rather than changing the node keeps that design and makes
              ; the five arms agree.
              (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_STR) (== (expStrLitEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName p)) 1))
                1
                0)
              (if (== pt TAG_E_APP)
                (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_APP)
                  0
                  {
                    (let ps (spineArgVec p vecNew))
                    (let as (spineArgVec a vecNew))
                    (if (!= (vecLen ps) (vecLen as))
                      0
                      (if (== (expMatchPat (spineHead p) (spineHead a) names vals lctx) 0)
                        0
                        (expMatchVec ps as 0 names vals lctx)))
                  })
                ; A tag no pattern can carry. `parseMacroPat` builds only the five
                ; above, so this is unreachable from source and answers a refusal
                ; rather than a crash if that ever stops being true.
                0))))))
  })

; Do two string LITERALS denote the same string? Compares decoded
; bytes over the lexeme bodies, so a spelling difference that is not a
; value difference is not a pattern difference.
(pub :: expStrLitEq (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (expStrLitEq a b)
  (expStrLitEqAt a 1 (- (strLen a) 1) b 1 (- (strLen b) 1)))

(pub :: expStrLitEqAt (-> String Int Int String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expStrLitEqAt a i ae b j be)
  (if (>= i ae)
    (if (>= j be)
      1
      0)
    (if (>= j be)
      0
      {
        (let ca (strByte a i))
        (let cb (strByte b j))
        {
          (let na
            (if (&& (== ca 92) (< (+ i 1) ae))
              2
              1))
          (let nb
            (if (&& (== cb 92) (< (+ j 1) be))
              2
              1))
          (if (==
                (if (== na 2)
                  (unescByte (strByte a (+ i 1)))
                  ca)
                (if (== nb 2)
                  (unescByte (strByte b (+ j 1)))
                  cb))
            (expStrLitEqAt a (+ i na) ae b (+ j nb) be)
            0)
        }
      })))

; The byte a source escape denotes; anything unrecognised stands for
; itself, which is what `\\`, `\'` and `\"` need. The same table
; `codegen.ax` carries under this name - duplicated because `codegen`
; imports `expand` and not the other way round.
(pub :: expMatchVec (-> Nodes Nodes Int Names Nodes ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchVec ps as i names vals lctx)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    1
    (if (== (expMatchPat (vecGet ps i) (vecGet as i) names vals lctx) 0)
      0
      (expMatchVec ps as (+ i 1) names vals lctx))))

; One successful rule owns every binding collected by matching it.
(pub struct ExpMatch
  (rule : MacroRule)
  (names : Names)
  (values : Nodes)
  (sequences : SeqBinds))

(pub :: expMatchOf (-> ExpMatch MacroRule))
(pub fn (expMatchOf matched)
  matched.rule)

(pub :: expMatchNames (-> ExpMatch Names))
(pub fn (expMatchNames matched)
  matched.names)

(pub :: expMatchVals (-> ExpMatch Nodes))
(pub fn (expMatchVals matched)
  matched.values)

; MAC-LANG-18: the rules are tried IN ORDER and the FIRST whose
; pattern matches wins. Arity is now a pre-filter rather than the
; selector - a rule whose element count differs cannot match, and
; skipping it is cheaper than walking it.
(pub :: expRuleForForms (-> MacroRules Nodes Int ExpMatchContext (Option ExpMatch)))
(pub fn (expRuleForForms rules args i lctx)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
    None
    ; Arity is a pre-filter still, but a repeating rule's arity is
    ; a FLOOR rather than an equality: `(m a b ...)` takes one
    ; argument and then any number.
    {
      (let r (vecGet rules i))
      (let k (expRepIndex (vecGet rules i)))
      (if (match k
        ((None) (!= (vecLen (expRulePats r)) (vecLen args)))
        ((Some index) (< (vecLen args) index)))
        (expRuleForForms rules args (+ i 1) lctx)
        {
          (let names vecNew)
          (let vals vecNew)
          (let seqs vecNew)
          (if (== (expMatchRuleAt r args names vals seqs lctx) 0)
            (expRuleForForms rules args (+ i 1) lctx)
            (Some (ExpMatch :rule r :names names :values vals :sequences seqs)))
        })
    }))

; One rule against the invocation's arguments, splitting at the
; repeating element if there is one. The elements BEFORE it match one
; argument each; the element itself absorbs every argument left, as a
; SEQUENCE binding rather than a value binding.
(pub :: expMatchRuleAt (-> MacroRule Nodes Names Nodes SeqBinds ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchRuleAt r args names vals seqs lctx)
  {
    (let ps (expRulePats r))
    (match (expRepIndex r)
      ((None) (expMatchVec ps args 0 names vals lctx))
      ((Some k)
        (if (== (expMatchFixed ps args 0 k names vals lctx) 0)
          0
          {
            (let pk (vecGet ps k))
            (if (== (nodeTag pk) TAG_E_VAR)
              {
                (vecPush seqs (SeqBind :name (nodeAName pk) :forms (expTailFrom args k vecNew)))
                1
              }
              ; A repeating PATTERN: every absorbed argument matches the
              ; pattern, and each of its binders takes the sequence of
              ; what it bound, in lockstep (MAC-LANG-16). A match
              ; failure on any element fails the rule, exactly as a
              ; fixed-element mismatch does.
              (expMatchNestTail pk args k seqs lctx))
          })
      ))
  })

; The first `n` elements, one argument each.
(pub :: expMatchFixed (-> Nodes Nodes Int Int Names Nodes ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchFixed ps args i n names vals lctx)
  (if (>= i n)
    1
    (if (== (expMatchPat (vecGet ps i) (vecGet args i) names vals lctx) 0)
      0
      (expMatchFixed ps args (+ i 1) n names vals lctx))))

(pub :: expTailFrom (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec a) (Vec a)))
(pub fn (expTailFrom args i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc (vecGet args i))
      (expTailFrom args (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

; A repeating PATTERN against the absorbed tail, one element at a
; time (MAC-LANG-16, second half). Each tail argument matches the
; pattern with a FRESH names/vals pair - sharing one across elements
; would let `expParamIndex`'s last-wins silently discard every
; binding but the tail's - and each of the pattern's binders takes
; the sequence of what it bound, in tail order. All sequences have
; the same length by construction: one value per absorbed argument.
; An empty tail matches with every sequence empty.
;
; The binder set comes from the pattern itself
; (`expRuleBindersOf`), so a match that bound something else - which
; the same pattern cannot produce, but a future matcher change
; might - fails rather than misaligns. `_` never appears: it binds
; nothing and `expRuleBindersOf` skips it, so a pattern repeating
; over `_` is a shape filter with no sequences. `...` never appears
; either: a repeat beneath a repeat is refused at the macro's line
; before matching runs.
(pub :: expMatchNestTail (-> ASTNode Nodes Int SeqBinds ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchNestTail pat args i seqs lctx)
  {
    (let binders (expUniqueNames (expRuleBindersOf pat vecNew)))
    {
      (let accs (expFreshAccs binders 0 vecNew))
      (if (== (expMatchNestEach pat args i binders accs lctx) 0)
        0
        {
          (expSealSeqs binders accs seqs 0)
          1
        })
    }
  })

; One owning syntax vector per binder, in binder order.
(pub :: expFreshAccs (-> Names Int NodesRows NodesRows))
(pub fn (expFreshAccs binders i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen binders))
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc vecNew)
      (expFreshAccs binders (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

; Every absorbed argument against the pattern; each success appends
; one value per binder.
(pub :: expMatchNestEach (-> ASTNode Nodes Int Names NodesRows ExpMatchContext Int))
(pub fn (expMatchNestEach pat args i binders accs lctx)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    1
    {
      (let names vecNew)
      {
        (let vals vecNew)
        (if (== (expMatchPat pat (vecGet args i) names vals lctx) 0)
          0
          (if (== (expNestAppend binders accs names vals 0) 0)
            0
            (expMatchNestEach pat args (+ i 1) binders accs lctx)))
      }
    }))

; One element's bindings into the accumulators, by binder order. 0
; when the element did not bind something the pattern declares -
; which the same pattern cannot do, so this is the tripwire not the
; path.
(pub :: expNestAppend (-> Names NodesRows Names Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (expNestAppend binders accs names vals i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen binders))
    1
    (match (expParamIndex names (vecGetStr binders i))
      ((None) 0)
      ((Some at)
        {
          (vecPush (vecGet accs i) (vecGet vals at))
          (expNestAppend binders accs names vals (+ i 1))
        }
      ))))

; Accumulators into `SeqBind`s, in binder order.
(pub :: expSealSeqs (-> Names NodesRows SeqBinds Int Int))
(pub fn (expSealSeqs binders accs seqs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen binders))
    1
    {
      (vecPush seqs (SeqBind :name (vecGetStr binders i) :forms (vecGet accs i)))
      (expSealSeqs binders accs seqs (+ i 1))
    }))

; A name bound to a SEQUENCE of argument forms. Distinct from the
; flat `names`/`vals` pair because that pair spends each name on
; exactly one node - `expParamIndex` then `vecGet` - which is the
; representation `MAC-LANG-16` could not use and said so.
(pub struct SeqBind
  (name : String)
  (forms : Nodes))

(pub type SeqBinds (Vec SeqBind))

(pub :: expMatchSeqs (-> ExpMatch SeqBinds))
(pub fn (expMatchSeqs matched)
  matched.sequences)

; A present empty sequence still binds its name; a miss has no forms.
(pub :: expSeqLookup (-> SeqBinds String (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expSeqLookup seqs name)
  (expSeqLookupFrom seqs name 0))

(pub :: expSeqLookupFrom (-> SeqBinds String Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expSeqLookupFrom seqs name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seqs))
    None
    {
      (let binding (vecGet seqs i))
      (if (strEq binding.name name)
        (Some binding.forms)
        (expSeqLookupFrom seqs name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Is every top-level element of this rule a distinct BINDER? Such a rule
; matches every invocation of its arity, so any later rule of that
; arity is unreachable - which is the narrowed form of the refusal
; `MAC-LANG-14` shipped when arity was the whole selector.
(pub :: expRuleIrrefutable (-> MacroRule Int))
(pub fn (expRuleIrrefutable r)
  (if (== (expPatsIrrefutable (expRulePats r) 0) 0)
    0
    {
      (let ns (expRuleBinders (expRulePats r) 0 vecNew))
      (if (== (vecLen ns) (vecLen (expUniqueNames ns)))
        1
        0)
    }))

(pub :: expPatsIrrefutable (-> Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (expPatsIrrefutable ps i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    1
    ; A literal is refutable although it is an `E_VAR`: it matches
    ; one binding, not any form.
    (if (== (expIsLiteral (vecGet ps i)) 1)
      0
      (if (== (nodeTag (vecGet ps i)) TAG_E_VAR)
        (expPatsIrrefutable ps (+ i 1))
        0))))

; The SHAPES a macro accepts, for MAC-LANG-18's refusal: one
; parenthesised form per rule, named after the macro, joined by
; " or ". This replaces a list of arities, which could only ever say
; how many arguments and never which shape.
(pub :: expRuleShapes (-> MacroRules String Int String String))
(pub fn (expRuleShapes rules name i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rules))
    acc
    (expRuleShapes rules name (+ i 1)
      {
        (let one (expOneShape (vecGet rules i) name))
        (if (== (strLen acc) 0)
          one
          (concat (concat acc " or ") one))
      })))

(pub :: expOneShape (-> MacroRule String String))
(pub fn (expOneShape r name)
  (concat (expShapeElems (expRuleSpells r) 0 (concat "(" name)) ")"))

(pub :: expShapeElems (-> Strings Int String String))
(pub fn (expShapeElems spells i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen spells))
    acc
    (expShapeElems spells (+ i 1) (concat (concat acc " ") (vecGetStr spells i)))))

; Every binder occurrence, including constraints and nested patterns.
; Repetition deduplicates this list to create one sequence per name;
; the reachability check counts duplicates as equality constraints.
;
; Not `expPatBinders`, which walks a MATCH ARM's pattern and therefore
; skips the spine HEAD - correct there, where the head is a
; constructor, and wrong here, where `(nm T)` binds `nm` as much as it
; binds `T`.
(pub :: expRuleBinders (-> Nodes Int Names Names))
(pub fn (expRuleBinders ps i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    acc
    {
      (expRuleBindersOf (vecGet ps i) acc)
      (expRuleBinders ps (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: expRuleBindersOf (-> ASTNode Names Names))
(pub fn (expRuleBindersOf p acc)
  ; A literal binds nothing: a reserved spelling never allocates a
  ; parameter slot, or the name would collide with a binder of the
  ; same spelling downstream.
  (if (== (expIsLiteral p) 1)
    acc
    (if (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (strEq (nodeAName p) "_")
        acc
        {
          (vecPush acc (nodeAName p))
          acc
        })
      (if (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (expRuleBindersOf (nodeAChild p) acc)
          (expRuleBindersOf (nodeBChild p) acc)
          acc
        }
        acc))))

; `expRuleForArity` and `expRuleArities` lived here until 2026-08-16.
; They were rule selection and its diagnostic when ARITY was the
; selector: the first rule whose parameter count matched, and the list
; of counts an invocation matching none was told about.
;
; `MAC-LANG-15` replaced both. `expRuleForForms` selects by MATCHING a
; rule's patterns against the argument forms - arity survives inside it
; as a pre-filter, and as a FLOOR rather than an equality once a rule
; can repeat (`MAC-LANG-16`) - and `expRuleShapes` prints the rules'
; patterns rather than their lengths, because a list of counts says
; nothing to an author who passed a count some rule declares.
;
; Recorded rather than silently removed: an arity-only selector is the
; thing this rule shipped first, and knowing it was deleted rather than
; forgotten is what keeps a reader from re-deriving it.
; One template declaration's KIND, recursively: a syntax/for is
; accepted and its interior checked with the same rule, so a `data`
; hiding inside an iteration still refuses at the macro's own line.
(pub :: expCheckTplKind (-> ASTNode ASTNode MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expCheckTplKind mac t env)
  (if (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_FN)
        (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_SIG)
          (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_MACROCALL)
            (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_DATA)
              (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_STRUCT)
                (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_ALIAS) (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_EFFECT)))))))
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_SYNFOR)
      (expCheckTplKindVec mac (nodeCChildren t) env 0)
      (expEmit env
        (expMarkUnit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3021" "macro-template-unsupported"
            (if (== (nodeSpan t) 0)
              (nodeSpan mac)
              (nodeSpan t))
            (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac))
              "`'s template may generate `fn`, `::`, `data`, `struct`, `type` and `effect` declarations, further macro invocations, or a syntax/for over them - this form is not supported yet")
            None (Some "generate the declaration by hand, or split it out of the template")
            "trait, import and nested macro templates refuse here, at the macro's own line, rather than passing through silently (MAC-CAP-2). An import inside a template would reopen resolution ordering and a nested macro the phase-D fixpoint, which is why those two are refusals by decision rather than by schedule")
          (nodeModule mac))))))

(pub :: expCheckTplKindVec (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckTplKindVec mac ts env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ts))
    0
    {
      (expCheckTplKind mac (vecGet ts i) env)
      (expCheckTplKindVec mac ts env (+ i 1))
    }))

; The `syntax/` prefix is RESERVED in declaration names: a declared
; fn, signature, or macro spelled `syntax/...` would shadow the query
; vocabulary's spellings in the positions the parser reserves for
; them, and the near-miss `(fn (syntax/join a b) body)` - one paren
; short of a joined NAME - would otherwise silently declare a
; function named syntax/join with parameters a and b. Template
; declarations never enter this list (they live in the macro's word
; 9), so a marker name arriving here is one written OUTSIDE a macro.
; Corpus population when the reservation landed: zero.
(pub :: expCheckDeclNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckDeclNames decls env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        {
          (let tag (nodeTag d))
          (if (== tag TAG_D_SYNFOR)
            ; Declaration-position iteration OUTSIDE any macro
            ; template - refused, then re-tagged INERT, because
            ; the checker walks whatever this list holds and a
            ; tag it does not know is a garbage traversal in the
            ; one pipeline that runs it ungated (the REPL).
            {
              (expQueryErr env (nodeSpan d)
                "syntax/for stands in a declaration macro's template, not at top level"
                "iteration is an expansion-time facility: move these declarations into a rule-form macro's template, or write them by hand"
                (nodeModule d))
              (writeNodeWord d 0 TAG_NIL)
            }
            ; `data` and `struct` joined this list when their
            ; templates landed: their names go through `parseDeclName`
            ; now, so a marker can reach the declaration list from
            ; outside a macro exactly as a fn's could, and the
            ; constructors of a `data` are checked with it.
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_FN)
                  (|| (== tag TAG_D_SIG)
                    (|| (== tag TAG_D_MACRO) (|| (== tag TAG_D_DATA) (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT)))))
              {
                (if (strStartsWith (nodeAName d) "syntax/")
                  (expQueryErr env (nodeSpan d)
                    (if (== (expIsJoinMarker (nodeAName d)) 1)
                      "a `(syntax/join ...)` name stands in a declaration macro's template, not in ordinary code"
                      (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName d))
                        "` declares a name in the reserved `syntax/` prefix"))
                    "the syntax/ spellings belong to the query vocabulary (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5/6); pick another name, or move the declaration into a macro template"
                    (nodeModule d)))
                (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
                  (expCheckCtorNames (nodeBChildren d) d env 0))
              }))
        }
        (expCheckDeclNames decls env (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; A `data`'s constructor names go through the same reserved-prefix
; test its type name does - `((syntax/join Mk N) Int)` is a legal
; constructor spelling inside a template and nowhere else.
(pub :: expCheckCtorNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckCtorNames cs d env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    0
    {
      {
        (let c (vecGet cs i))
        (if (strStartsWith (nodeAName c) "syntax/")
          (expQueryErr env
            (if (== (nodeSpan c) 0)
              (nodeSpan d)
              (nodeSpan c))
            (if (== (expIsJoinMarker (nodeAName c)) 1)
              "a `(syntax/join ...)` constructor name stands in a declaration macro's template, not in ordinary code"
              (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName c))
                "` declares a constructor in the reserved `syntax/` prefix"))
            "the syntax/ spellings belong to the query vocabulary (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5/6); pick another name, or move the declaration into a macro template"
            (nodeModule d)))
      }
      (expCheckCtorNames cs d env (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expCheckDupParams (-> ASTNode MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckDupParams mac env i)
  ; A RULE-FORM macro's node B is rule zero's slot 0, which since
  ; MAC-LANG-15 holds a `syntax/pat` MARKER wherever an element is not
  ; a plain binder - so two `_` in one rule read as one name twice.
  ; `expCheckRuleParams` is the check for that form and walks the
  ; patterns instead, so this one answers the head-list form only
  ; rather than duplicating it wrongly.
  (if (!= (nodeFieldNamesWord mac) 0)
    0
    (expCheckDupParamsIn mac (nodeBNames mac) env i)))

(pub :: expCheckDupParamsIn (-> ASTNode Names MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expCheckDupParamsIn mac ps env i)
  {
    (let ps ps)
    (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
      0
      {
        (if (match (expParamIndex ps (vecGetStr ps i))
          ((None) false)
          ((Some at) (!= at i)))
          (expEmit env
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3020" "macro-duplicate-parameter" (nodeSpan mac)
              (concat (concat (concat "macro `" (nodeAName mac)) "` declares the parameter `")
                (concat (vecGetStr ps i) "` more than once"))
              None (Some "give each parameter a distinct name")
              "every reference to a repeated parameter resolves to the LAST argument, so the earlier ones are silently discarded")))
        ; `expCheckDupParamsIn`, not `expCheckDupParams`: the latter
        ; re-reads `(nodeB mac)`, so a caller that passed its OWN
        ; vector had it silently swapped for rule zero's after the
        ; first element. Measured before this line changed: a
        ; duplicate in a later rule at any index but 0 - `((mk x)
        ; ...) ((mk a b b) ...)` - checked CLEAN, where the same
        ; duplicate at index 0 refused.
        (expCheckDupParamsIn mac ps env (+ i 1))
      })
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The pass.
;
; Called between `resolveImports` and `checkModule`, on the MERGED
; declaration list, with the same diagnostic Vec the checker will add
; to. Answers nothing: the declarations are rewritten in place.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: expandProgram
  (-> Nodes Nodes Diags Frames Units Names Names ModuleImports GeneratedOrigins))
(pub fn (expandProgram entryDecls decls out frames units bares fulls mods)
  {
    (let env
      (MEnv
        :macros (expCollectMacros decls vecNew 0)
        :out out
        :fresh 0
        :depth 0
        :decls decls
        :macmod 0
        :walk 0
        :nodes 0
        :blown 0
        :site 0
        :frames frames
        :declmod 0
        :units units
        :forstack vecNew
        :bares bares
        :fulls fulls
        :mods mods
        :qsite 0
        :gens vecNew
        :callbs 0
        :seqbinds vecNew
        :tplbinds vecNew
        :declnodes 0
        :midx mangleIdxNew
        :scNext 0
        :scChain vecNew
        :scTpl vecNew
        :scSeq 0
        :retired28 0
        :defer 0
        :deferHits 0
        :structNames internNewTyped
        :structBuckets vecNew
        :carrier 0
        :optNames (qmOptionNames decls)))
    {
      (expCheckMacros env 0)
      (expCheckDeclNames decls env 0)
      ; Phase D before phase E (MAC-EXP-16): declaration-position
      ; invocations resolve to a fixpoint FIRST, so the declarations
      ; they generate are in the list before any body is walked - a
      ; generated fn's body can invoke expression macros, and a later
      ; declaration can call a generated function, order-independently
      ; like everything else at top level.
      (expandDeclPhase entryDecls decls env)
      (expIndexStructDecls env)
      (expandDeclsIn decls env 0)
      ; Contracts last, over the list expansion finished with: a
      ; macro-generated `fn` carries its own AXTAGs and gets its
      ; contract lowered like any written one, and a contract whose
      ; expression invokes a macro is not a shape this lowers - the
      ; expression comes from a comment, which no expansion round
      ; reads.
      (expLowerContracts decls (contractSigIndex decls) 0)
      env.gens
    }
  })

; ==================================================================
; PHASE D - declaration macros (MAC-CAP-8, MAC-EXP-16/17).
;
; A rule-form macro's template is a vector of DECLARATION nodes,
; parsed by the real declaration parsers at the macro's own line
; (word 9 of the macro node; an expression macro's word 9 is 0). A
; TAG_D_MACROCALL resolves against the same table, with the same
; visibility and the same qualified-name split, and instantiates by
; DECLARATION-level substitution:
;
;   - a name position (a generated fn/sig's name, a fn's parameters,
;     a nested invocation's head) substitutes a macro parameter for
;     its argument, which MUST be a bare identifier - a generated
;     declaration's name has to be spellable;
;   - a body substitutes through `substTpl`, so hygiene, spans and
;     the definition-site rule are the expression machinery's,
;     unchanged;
;   - a signature's type substitutes structurally (TCON/TVAR whose
;     name is a parameter), and its per-arrow float flags are
;     RECOMPUTED from the substituted node - the type-alias slice's
;     lesson: a type rewritten without its flags prints 0.00 at exit
;     0.
;
; The FIRST generated declaration overwrites the invocation node in
; place - the entry list and the merged list share node handles, the
; same invariant expression expansion rewrites through - and the rest
; append to BOTH lists, because checkDuplicates and checkReservedNames
; walk the entry list specifically: a generated second `main` or a
; generated `axiom_alloc` must be refused exactly as a written one.
; Every generated node carries vis 1 (resolveImports stamped the
; written entry declarations before this pass ran), module 0, and the
; INVOCATION's span handle, which is what joins backtrace frames and
; anchors `symbols` (MAC-TOOL-4).
; ==================================================================
(pub :: expDeclMacroRounds Int)
(pub fn (expDeclMacroRounds)
  128)

(pub :: expandDeclPhase (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int))
(pub fn (expandDeclPhase entryDecls decls env)
  {
    ; Rounds run with queries deferring (`expQueryMissing`) while
    ; a round can still generate the declaration they name.
    (set env.defer 1)
    (expandDeclRounds entryDecls decls env 0)
    (set env.defer 0)
    0
  })

; Pops `v` back to `n` elements.
(pub :: expTruncate (-> (Vec a) Int Int))
(pub fn (expTruncate v n)
  (if (<= (vecLen v) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPop v)
      (expTruncate v n)
    }))

; Is an invocation still waiting - neither expanded nor refused?
; After a round that generated nothing, only a deferred one can be.
(pub :: expAnyWaiting (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expAnyWaiting decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_MACROCALL) (== (nodeCWord d) 0))
        1
        (expAnyWaiting decls (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: expandDeclRounds (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expandDeclRounds entryDecls decls env round)
  (if (>= round expDeclMacroRounds)
    {
      (let d (expFirstMacroCall decls 0))
      {
        (expEmit env
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3019" "macro-recursion-limit"
            (if (nodeSame d nilNode)
              0
              (nodeSpan d))
            "declaration-macro expansion exceeded 128 rounds" None
            (Some
              "check whether a declaration macro, directly or through another, generates an invocation of itself with no case that stops")
            "each round resolves every declaration-position invocation the previous round generated; a template that always generates another invocation never terminates"))
        0
      }
    }
    (if (== (expBlown env) 1)
      0
      (if (== (expandDeclRound entryDecls decls env 0 0) 0)
        ; Nothing generated. A deferred invocation's subject can no
        ; longer appear, so one more round with deferring off reports
        ; it (AX3028), exactly as a single pass always did.
        (if (&& (== env.defer 1) (== (expAnyWaiting decls 0) 1))
          {
            (set env.defer 0)
            (expandDeclRounds entryDecls decls env (+ round 1))
          }
          0)
        (expandDeclRounds entryDecls decls env (+ round 1))))))

(pub :: expFirstMacroCall (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expFirstMacroCall decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    nilNode
    (if (== (nodeTag (vecGet decls i)) TAG_D_MACROCALL)
      (vecGet decls i)
      (expFirstMacroCall decls (+ i 1)))))

; One round; answers 1 when any invocation instantiated. A REFUSED
; invocation is marked (word 3 = 1) and reported once - it stays in
; the list, the driver's error gate keeps it from the checker.
(pub :: expandDeclRound (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expandDeclRound entryDecls decls env i progress)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    progress
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_MACROCALL) (== (nodeCWord d) 0))
        (if (== (expandDeclCall entryDecls decls env d) 1)
          (expandDeclRound entryDecls decls env (+ i 1) 1)
          (expandDeclRound entryDecls decls env (+ i 1) progress))
        (expandDeclRound entryDecls decls env (+ i 1) progress))
    }))

(pub :: expDeclCallErr (-> MEnv ASTNode String String String Int))
(pub fn (expDeclCallErr env d msg help note)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3027" "declaration-macro" (nodeSpan d) msg None (Some help) note)
        (nodeModule d)))
    (writeNodeWord d 3 1)
    0
  })

(pub :: expandDeclCall (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expandDeclCall entryDecls decls env d)
  ; A MODULE-SIDE invocation is expanded like an entry-file one and
  ; then put into the module's namespace by hand (`expModuleize`),
  ; because the pass that would have done it - import resolution -
  ; ran before this one. What it needs from that pass is the two
  ; mangling records and the module's import filter; a caller that
  ; supplies neither (the REPL's re-expansion, codegen's) cannot have
  ; an imported module's declaration in front of it, and the refusal
  ; below says so rather than generating a declaration nothing can
  ; name.
  (if (&& (!= (nodeModule d) 0) (vecIsNull env.bares))
    (expDeclCallErr env d
      "a declaration-macro invocation inside an imported module needs the module's mangling records, and this pipeline has none"
      "invoke the macro from the entry file"
      "module-side invocation puts generated declarations into the module's namespace, which needs the bare-to-mangled records import resolution builds; a pipeline that re-expands an already-resolved program does not carry them")
    ; The invocation SITE decides which macros are visible
    ; (MAC-LANG-9): a module's own private macro is reachable from
    ; that module and nowhere else. Phase E sets this per
    ; declaration in `expandDeclsIn`; phase D has to set it here, or
    ; a module invoking its own private macro reads as an unknown
    ; declaration head - which is what it did on the first probe.
    ; EXACT arity in declaration position - there is no
    ; "surplus applies to the result" story here, since the
    ; result is declarations, not a value - and with
    ; several rules the arity is what SELECTS one
    ; (MAC-LANG-14). An invocation matching none names
    ; every arity the macro offers.
    {
      (let prevSite env.site)
      (let _s (set env.site (nodeModule d)))
      {
        (let r
          (match (expFindMacro env (nodeAName d))
            ((None)
              (expDeclCallErr env d
                (concat (concat "`" (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName d)))
                  "` is neither a declaration keyword nor a visible macro")
                "a top-level form is a declaration - fn, ::, data, struct, type, effect, import, macro - or an invocation of a declaration macro"
                "this used to be a bare `syntax error` that also stopped the parse at the first unknown head, hiding every later diagnostic in the file")
            )
            ((Some mac)
              (if (|| (== (nodeFieldNamesWord mac) 0) (! (expMacHasDeclRule mac)))
                (expDeclCallErr env d
                  (concat (concat "macro `" (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName d)))
                    "` has an expression template and cannot stand in declaration position")
                  "give it a rule whose template is declarations - `(macro name ((name args ...) (fn ...) ...))`"
                  "an expression template is one expression; a declaration position needs declarations (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-8)")
                {
                  (let m
                    (expRuleForForms
                      (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
                        ((None) vecNew)
                        ((Some rules) rules))
                      (expCaptureVec (nodeBChildren d) env vecNew) 0
                      (expMatchCtx decls (nodeModule d) (nodeModule mac))))
                  (if (let (Some m) m)
                    (if (== (vecLen (expRuleTpl (expMatchOf m))) 0)
                      (expDeclCallErr env d
                        (concat (concat "the rule of macro `" (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName d)))
                          "` that matches here has an expression template, so it can't stand in declaration position")
                        "invoke it where a value goes, or give the macro an earlier rule for this shape whose template is declarations"
                        "each rule's template is declarations or one expression, and where the macro is invoked picks which it needs (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-14)")
                      (expInstantiateDecl entryDecls decls env d mac m))
                    {
                      (expNoRuleDiag env (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName d))
                        (expRuleShapes
                          (match (nodeMacroRules mac)
                            ((None) vecNew)
                            ((Some rules) rules))
                          (expSrcSpelling (nodeAName d)) 0 "")
                        (nodeSpan d))
                      (writeNodeWord d 3 1)
                      0
                    })
                })
            )))
        {
          (set env.site prevSite)
          r
        }
      }
    }))

; Takes the MATCH rather than the rule: the two parallel vectors it
; hands to `expBuildDecls` are the match's, not the rule's own slot 0
; and the invocation's own arguments. For an all-binder pattern those
; are the same contents in the same order, which is why the 49
; rule-form macros already in the tree emit identical IR.
(pub :: expInstantiateDecl (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ASTNode ASTNode ExpMatch Int))
(pub fn (expInstantiateDecl entryDecls decls env d mac m)
  ; What a deferred build (`expQueryMissing`) must leave as it found
  ; it: the diagnostics it emitted, the frame pushed below, and the
  ; fresh-name counter its hygiene renaming advanced.
  {
    (let isp (nodeSpan d))
    (let tags (nodeAxtagsOpt d))
    (let prevMod (expMacMod env))
    (let prevSite env.site)
    (let prevDecl env.declmod)
    (let prevFors env.forstack)
    (let prevSeqs env.seqbinds)
    (let prevSc (expScEnterDeclInst env))
    (let outLen0 (vecLen (expOutVec env)))
    (let framesLen0 (vecLen env.frames))
    (let fresh0 env.fresh)
    (let _h (set env.deferHits 0))
    {
      (set env.seqbinds (expMatchSeqs m))
      (expSetMacMod env (nodeModule mac))
      (set env.site (nodeModule mac))
      (set env.qsite (nodeModule d))
      (set env.declmod 0)
      (set env.forstack vecNew)
      ; The frame's SITE is the module the invocation was written in,
      ; not the literal 0 this carried while phase D was entry-file
      ; only: `expAttachTo` joins a frame to a diagnostic by span AND
      ; by module, so a module-side invocation's frames could never
      ; match once the diagnostic carried its own module - which is
      ; both halves of MAC-DIAG-4's backtrace and MAC-TOOL-5's fix
      ; suppression, silently absent for exactly the invocations this
      ; slice made legal. The expression path (`expandDecl`) has always
      ; read its site from the declaration being expanded.
      (vecPush env.frames
        (ExpFrame
          :site (nodeModuleOpt d)
          :span isp
          :name (nodeAName mac)
          :defSpan (nodeSpan mac)
          :defMod (nodeModuleOpt mac)))
      {
        (let made
          (expBuildDecls (expRuleTpl (expMatchOf m)) (expMatchNames m) (expMatchVals m) env isp
            vecNew 0))
        {
          (set env.seqbinds prevSeqs)
          (expSetMacMod env prevMod)
          (expScExitInst env prevSc)
          (set env.site prevSite)
          (set env.qsite 0)
          (set env.declmod prevDecl)
          (set env.forstack prevFors)
          ; The width budget is NOT counted here any more, and the
          ; reason is that counting it here could only ever refuse an
          ; instantiation that had ALREADY been built in full. The
          ; total moved once per INSTANTIATION, so a single one was
          ; unbounded - and a declaration-position `syntax/for` nests,
          ; so what one invocation builds is a PRODUCT the limit never
          ; saw until the product was in memory. The allocator is a
          ; bump allocator with no free, so that is not a slow compile.
          ;
          ; Measured on this tree before the count moved, `axiom check`
          ; only, on a two-level declaration-position `syntax/for`
          ; whose product is exact - 2 declarations per cell:
          ;
          ;    12,000 decls  0.04 s    11 MB   AX3024 after the fact
          ;    40,000 decls  0.04 s    32 MB   AX3024 after the fact
          ;   200,000 decls  0.10 s   153 MB   AX3024 after the fact
          ;   800,000 decls  0.39 s   604 MB   AX3024 after the fact
          ; 3,200,000 decls  1.27 s  2003 MB   KILLED at the cap, no
          ;                                    diagnostic, no output
          ;
          ; Peak memory tracked the PRODUCT across three orders of
          ; magnitude while the message read "more than the limit of
          ; 10000" - the limit named a number it was not enforcing.
          ; The 3.2 M row is the failure AX3024's long form says this
          ; budget exists to prevent, reproduced with the budget in
          ; place. `expBuildDecls` counts each declaration as it pushes
          ; it now, so the same nest stops at 10,001 declarations: all
          ; five rows above became 0.04 s and 10-11 MB.
          ;
          ; `expBlow` still sets the same word the expression budgets
          ; set, so the `expBlown` test just below and the one that
          ; stops the round fixpoint carry the refusal out with no new
          ; control flow here.
          (if (== env.deferHits 1)
            ; A query named a declaration that doesn't exist yet:
            ; nothing of this build is kept, and the invocation stays
            ; unrefused for the next round.
            {
              (expTruncate (expOutVec env) outLen0)
              (expTruncate env.frames framesLen0)
              (set env.fresh fresh0)
              (set env.deferHits 0)
              0
            }
            (if (== (expBlown env) 1)
              {
                (writeNodeWord d 3 1)
                0
              }
              (if (let (Some made) made)
                (if (== (vecLen made) 0)
                  (expInstantiateNothing entryDecls decls env d)
                  {
                    (let mod (nodeModule d))
                    (if (== (as mod Int) 0)
                      {
                        (expCopyDeclInto d (vecGet made 0))
                        (expAppendRest entryDecls decls made 1)
                        (expGiveTags d made tags)
                        (expRecordGens env made (nodeAName mac) 0)
                        ; The entry file wins the bare slot, generated
                        ; declarations included - `recordEntryFns` does
                        ; this for the ones the parser produced, before
                        ; any import is read, and these did not exist
                        ; then.
                        (expClaimEntryNames made env 0)
                        1
                      }
                      ; Module-side: every generated declaration joins
                      ; the module's namespace before anything reads the
                      ; list, and NONE of them joins the entry file's -
                      ; checkDuplicates and checkReservedNames walk that
                      ; one, and a module's declarations have never been
                      ; theirs to judge.
                      {
                        (let vis0 (expModuleize made env mod))
                        {
                          (expCopyDeclInto d (vecGet made 0))
                          (writeNodeWord d 5 vis0)
                          (expAppendRestMod decls made 1)
                          (expGiveTags d made tags)
                          (expRecordGens env made (nodeAName mac) 0)
                          1
                        }
                      })
                  })
                {
                  (writeNodeWord d 3 1)
                  0
                })))
        }
      }
    }
  })

; An instantiation that generated no declarations: a `syntax/for` over
; an empty sequence, or a `decl ...` splice over an empty tail, which
; MAC-LANG-16 allows. The invocation leaves the program: from `decls`,
; and for the entry file from `entryDecls` too, the two lists
; `expAppendRest` keeps it in. Answering 1 runs another round, which
; reaches the declaration that moved into its place.
;
; AXTAGs written above it would attach to nothing. `expGiveTags`
; re-homes them onto the first generated declaration, and there is
; none, so they are refused here rather than dropped.
(pub :: expInstantiateNothing (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) MEnv ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expInstantiateNothing entryDecls decls env d)
  (if (match (nodeAxtagsOpt d)
    ((None) false)
    ((Some tags) (> (vecLen tags) 0)))
    (expDeclCallErr env d
      "this invocation generates no declarations, so the AXTAGs above it attach to nothing"
      "remove the AXTAGs, or give the invocation arguments that generate a declaration"
      "an invocation's AXTAGs move to the first declaration it generates; with none generated, a claim written here would be accepted and never checked")
    {
      (expDropDecl decls d 0)
      (if (== (as (nodeModule d) Int) 0)
        (expDropDecl entryDecls d 0))
      1
    }))

; Remove declaration `d` from `v`, found by identity, keeping the
; order of the rest.
(pub :: expDropDecl (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (expDropDecl v d i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (nodeSame (vecGet v i) d)
      (expDropAt v i)
      (expDropDecl v d (+ i 1)))))

; Shift `v[i+1..]` down one place and drop the last slot.
(pub :: expDropAt (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expDropAt v i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (vecLen v))
    {
      (vecPop v)
      0
    }
    {
      (vecSet v i (vecGet v (+ i 1)))
      (expDropAt v (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: isEllipsisDecl (-> ASTNode Int))
; A bare `...` among template declarations: the splice marker a rule
; template leaves for instantiation (`decl ...` rebuilding one
; declaration per absorbed element, MAC-LANG-16). The parser builds
; exactly what `(...)` builds - a declaration-macro invocation of
; `...` with no arguments - so no wire format changes: it is a
; TAG_D_MACROCALL like any nested invocation. Only the splice loop
; below and phase D read it: the loop consumes it beside a preceding
; declaration, and anywhere else it resolves as a macro named `...`
; and refuses as AX3027, which is what `(...)` has always drawn.
(pub fn (isEllipsisDecl d)
  (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_MACROCALL)
    0
    (if (strEq (nodeAName d) "...")
      (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren d)) 0)
        1
        0)
      0)))

; One template declaration spliced: per-element rebuilds through the
; ordinary single-declaration path, with each sequence-bound name
; taking its element. Some owns the generated declarations, including an
; empty sequence. None records a refused splice or failed element and
; aborts the whole instantiation. Per-element substitution appends singles
; onto the shared
; params/args (last wins) and pops them after: nothing appends to
; those vecs during substitution, so the truncation restores exactly
; what was there. Mirrors `expSpliceOneArm` one level up.
;
; Two diagnosed misuses, neither silent: no sequence in scope (the
; `...` follows nothing that repeats), and a repeat INSIDE the
; declaration (a spine, arm, constructor or declaration `...` of its
; own - using one sequence at two depths, refused rather than
; rebuilt n-squared).
(pub :: expSpliceOneDecl (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expSpliceOneDecl t params args env isp)
  {
    (let seqs (expSeqBindsVec env))
    (if (== (vecLen seqs) 0)
      {
        (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "follows nothing that repeats"
          "repetition lives in a rule-form macro's match: move the match there, or drop the marker"
          isp)
        None
      }
      (if (== (tplDeclHasRepeat t) 1)
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "nests a repeat inside a repeat"
            "repeat one level only - splice one declaration per element, never a declaration that splices again inside"
            isp)
          None
        }
        {
          (let first (vecGet seqs 0))
          (let n (vecLen first.forms))
          {
            (let markP (vecLen params))
            {
              (let markA (vecLen args))
              {
                (let mut j 0)
                {
                  (let out vecNew)
                  {
                    (let mut bad 0)
                    {
                      (while (< j n)
                        (if (== bad 1)
                          (set j n)
                          {
                            (let mut k 0)
                            {
                              (while (< k (vecLen seqs))
                                {
                                  (let binding (vecGet seqs k))
                                  (vecPush params binding.name)
                                  (vecPush args (vecGet binding.forms j))
                                  (set k (+ k 1))
                                })
                              {
                                (let g (expBuildOneDecl t params args env isp))
                                (if (let (Some g) g)
                                  {
                                    (vecPush out g)
                                    (set j (+ j 1))
                                  }
                                  {
                                    (set bad 1)
                                    (set j n)
                                  })
                              }
                              (while (> (vecLen params) markP)
                                (vecPop params))
                              (while (> (vecLen args) markA)
                                (vecPop args))
                            }
                          }))
                      (if (== bad 1)
                        None
                        (Some out))
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }))
  })

; Does a template declaration hold a repeat of its own (a spine,
; arm, constructor or declaration `...` inside it)? The splice loop
; above refuses one rather than rebuilding it per element, because
; the interior marker would re-splice the whole sequence inside
; every element. A `syntax/for` interior is walked, not exempted:
; its queries are independent, but a rule-`...` marker written
; inside it still consumes a rule sequence at the wrong depth.
(pub :: tplDeclHasRepeat (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tplDeclHasRepeat t)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag t))
    (if (== tag TAG_D_FN)
      (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeCChild t))
      (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
        (tplCtorsHaveRepeat (nodeBChildren t) 0)
        (if (== tag TAG_D_MACROCALL)
          (tplVecHasRepeat (nodeBChildren t) 0)
          (if (== tag TAG_D_SYNFOR)
            (tplDeclsHaveRepeat (nodeCChildren t) 0)
            0))))
  })

(pub :: tplDeclsHaveRepeat (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tplDeclsHaveRepeat ds i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ds))
    0
    (if (== (tplDeclHasRepeat (vecGet ds i)) 1)
      1
      (tplDeclsHaveRepeat ds (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tplCtorsHaveRepeat (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tplCtorsHaveRepeat cs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    0
    (if (== (isEllipsisCtor (vecGet cs i)) 1)
      1
      (tplCtorsHaveRepeat cs (+ i 1)))))

; Does a template expression hold a rule-`...` marker? Any bare
; `...` variable is one: nothing can bind that spelling, so no
; program means it as a reference. Arms are walked for their bodies
; and for a marker standing where an arm stands; a `syntax/for`
; arm's arms likewise. Patterns hold no marker positions and types
; hold no expressions, so neither is walked.
(pub :: tplExprHasRepeat (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tplExprHasRepeat e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (strEq (nodeAName e) "...")
          1
          0)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
            1
            (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e)))
          (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
            (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
              1
              (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e)) 1)
                1
                (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeCChild e))))
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
              (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e)) 1)
                1
                (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeCChild e)))
              (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e))
                (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                  (tplVecHasRepeat (nodeAChildren e) 0)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                    (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
                      1
                      (tplVecHasRepeat (nodeBChildren e) 0))
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                      (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e))
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                        (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild e))
                        (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== tag TAG_E_TRY))
                          (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e))
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                            (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
                              1
                              (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeCChild e)))
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                              (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
                                1
                                (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeCChild e)))
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                                (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeAChild e)) 1)
                                  1
                                  (tplArmsHaveRepeat (nodeBChildren e) 0))
                                ; `cond` is AX2004: no template holds
                                ; one, so nothing here repeats one.
                                0)))))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: tplVecHasRepeat (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tplVecHasRepeat es i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen es))
    0
    (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (vecGet es i)) 1)
      1
      (tplVecHasRepeat es (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tplArmsHaveRepeat (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tplArmsHaveRepeat arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (isEllipsisArm arm) 1)
        1
        (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
          (if (== (tplArmsHaveRepeat (nodeCChildren arm) 0) 1)
            1
            (tplArmsHaveRepeat arms (+ i 1)))
          (if (== (tplExprHasRepeat (nodeBChild arm)) 1)
            1
            (tplArmsHaveRepeat arms (+ i 1)))))
    }))

; One spliced declaration's siblings, drained through the same push
; discipline the single-declaration path below uses: the template's
; AXTAGs travel to every declaration it generated, each one counts
; against the width budget as it exists, and the walk continues two
; past the marker.
(pub :: expDrainBuilt
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int Int
    (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expDrainBuilt built tmpl tpl params args env isp acc i j)
  (if (>= j (vecLen built))
    (expBuildDecls tpl params args env isp acc (+ i 2))
    {
      (let g (vecGet built j))
      {
        (nodeCopyWord g tmpl 7)
        (vecPush acc g)
        {
          (let tot (+ env.declnodes 1))
          {
            (set env.declnodes tot)
            (if (> tot expMaxDecls)
              {
                (expBlow env isp
                  (concat
                    (concat "macro expansion produced more than the limit of "
                      (expFmtInt expMaxDecls))
                    " declarations"))
                None
              }
              (expDrainBuilt built tmpl tpl params args env isp acc i (+ j 1)))
          }
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: expBuildDecls
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildDecls tpl params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tpl))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let t (vecGet tpl i))
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_D_SYNFOR)
        ; Declaration-position iteration: the interior
        ; declarations instantiated once per element, spliced
        ; where the form stood - through this same function, so
        ; nested iterations compose exactly as the arm-position
        ; splice does.
        (if (! (expBuildForDecls t params args env isp acc))
          None
          (expBuildDecls tpl params args env isp acc (+ i 1)))
        ; A `decl ...` splice: the declaration rebuilds once per
        ; absorbed element, spliced where the pair stood. A
        ; `syntax/for` form manages its own repetition and never
        ; triggers this, even beside a marker. A marker on its own
        ; diagnoses - it follows nothing - and aborts the
        ; instantiation, so no shape falls through to a macro
        ; invocation of `...` the next round would refuse as AX3027.
        (if (== (isEllipsisDecl t) 1)
          {
            (expEllipsisDiag env "..."
              "starts nothing - a repetition marker follows the declaration it repeats"
              "drop it, or move it directly after the repeated declaration" isp)
            None
          }
          (if (&& (< (+ i 1) (vecLen tpl)) (== (isEllipsisDecl (vecGet tpl (+ i 1))) 1))
            {
              (let built (expSpliceOneDecl t params args env isp))
              (if (let (Some built) built)
                (expDrainBuilt built t tpl params args env isp acc i 0)
                None)
            }
            {
              (let g (expBuildOneDecl t params args env isp))
              (if (let (Some g) g)
                {
                  ; The template's AXTAGs travel to the declaration it
                  ; generated. `g` is a NEW node - everything after
                  ; expansion checks it, not the template - so without this
                  ; a tag written inside a template is parsed, attached,
                  ; and then dropped at instantiation. The same shape as an
                  ; impl method losing its tag when it is lowered.
                  (nodeCopyWord g t 7)
                  (vecPush acc g)
                  ; ONE declaration, counted the moment it exists. This is
                  ; the per-node discipline `expandExpr` has always used on
                  ; the expression axis (word 7 against `expMaxNodes`), and
                  ; it is what makes `expMaxDecls` a bound rather than a
                  ; report: every declaration a nest produces passes
                  ; through this push, at every level, so the running total
                  ; crosses the limit at declaration 10,001 instead of at
                  ; whatever the product happened to be. A nest that
                  ; reached 2 GB and an operating-system kill in 1.27 s
                  ; refuses here in 0.04 s and 10 MB.
                  ;
                  ; The span is the INVOCATION's, threaded down unchanged
                  ; from `expInstantiateDecl`, so the refusal points where
                  ; it always did and carries the same expansion frames -
                  ; `401-decl-macro-size-limit`'s golden is unchanged by
                  ; the move.
                  {
                    (let tot (+ env.declnodes 1))
                    {
                      (set env.declnodes tot)
                      (if (> tot expMaxDecls)
                        {
                          (expBlow env isp
                            (concat
                              (concat "macro expansion produced more than the limit of "
                                (expFmtInt expMaxDecls))
                              " declarations"))
                          None
                        }
                        (expBuildDecls tpl params args env isp acc (+ i 1)))
                    }
                  }
                }
                None)
            })))
    }))

(pub :: expBuildForDecls (-> ASTNode Names Nodes MEnv Int Nodes Bool))
(pub fn (expBuildForDecls f params args env isp acc)
  {
    (let seqs
      (expIterSeqsOf env (nodeBChild f) params args
        (if (!= (nodeSpan f) 0)
          (nodeSpan f)
          isp)
        isp))
    (if (let (Some seqs) seqs)
      (expBuildForElems f params args env isp acc seqs (expIterLen seqs) 0)
      false)
  })

(pub :: expBuildForElems (-> ASTNode Names Nodes MEnv Int Nodes (Vec ExpIterSequence) Int Int Bool))
(pub fn (expBuildForElems f params args env isp acc seqs n j)
  (if (>= j n)
    true
    ; The per-ELEMENT half of the same budget. The push site above
    ; refuses the element that crosses the limit, but an element costs
    ; more than the declarations it yields - `expBuildForDecls`
    ; re-evaluates the sequence and pushes a for-stack frame per
    ; element, and on a nest the OUTER loop keeps stepping through
    ; elements whose bodies are entire inner iterations. Testing the
    ; blown flag here stops the whole nest at the crossing element
    ; rather than letting each surviving outer element pay for another
    ; inner sweep; on the 3,200,000-declaration probe that is the
    ; difference between 0.04 s and never returning.
    (if (== (expBlown env) 1)
      false
      {
        (let mark (vecLen (expForStackVec env)))
        (let markC (vecLen (expScChainVec env)))
        {
          (expIterPush env seqs j 0)
          (expScPushLoop env)
          {
            (let r (expBuildDecls (nodeCChildren f) params args env isp acc 0))
            {
              (expTrunc (expForStackVec env) mark)
              (expScTruncLoop env markC)
              (if (let (Some r) r)
                (expBuildForElems f params args env isp acc seqs n (+ j 1))
                false)
            }
          }
        }
      })))

; A name position: a macro parameter substitutes its argument, which
; must be a bare identifier. A refusal returns None after recording its
; diagnostic; a present name may be empty in compiler-generated syntax.
(pub :: expSubstName (-> String Names Nodes MEnv Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expSubstName s params args env isp)
  ; The join marker resolves each of its two names through this same
  ; function - so a side that is a parameter follows the
  ; bare-identifier rule and draws the same diagnostic - and joins
  ; the results: `eq` + `Color` is `eqColor`.
  (if (== (expIsJoinMarker s) 1)
    {
      (let ra (expSubstName (expMarkerPart s 0) params args env isp))
      (if (let (Some ra) ra)
        {
          (let rb (expSubstName (expMarkerPart s 1) params args env isp))
          (if (let (Some rb) rb)
            (Some (expJoinName ra rb))
            None)
        }
        None)
    }
    {
      (let foundFor (expForLookup env s))
      (if (let (Some fe) foundFor)
        (Some fe)
        (match (expParamIndex params s)
          ((None) (Some s))
          ((Some idx)
            {
              (let a (vecGet args idx))
              (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
                (Some (nodeAName a))
                {
                  (expEmit env
                    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3027" "declaration-macro" isp
                      (concat
                        (concat "a generated declaration's name must be a bare identifier; `" s)
                        "` was given an expression")
                      None (Some "pass a plain name in this argument position")
                      "the parameter stands in a NAME position of the template - a declaration head or a parameter list - and only an identifier can be a name"))
                  None
                })
            }
          )))
    }))

(pub :: expSubstNames (-> Names Names Nodes MEnv Int (Option Names)))
(pub fn (expSubstNames ss params args env isp)
  (expSubstNamesIn ss params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: expSubstNamesIn (-> Names Names Nodes MEnv Int Names Int (Option Names)))
(pub fn (expSubstNamesIn ss params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ss))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let n (expSubstName (vecGetStr ss i) params args env isp))
      (if (let (Some n) n)
        {
          (vecPush acc n)
          (expSubstNamesIn ss params args env isp acc (+ i 1))
        }
        None)
    }))

; Structural substitution over a TYPE node: a TCON or TVAR whose name
; is a macro parameter becomes the argument identifier - lowercase a
; variable, uppercase a constructor, the grammar's own rule. Rebuilt,
; never mutated (MAC-HYG-4).
(pub :: expSubstTy (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstTy t params args env isp)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    ; A JOIN in type position. `(syntax/join nm Handle)` parses
    ; here as a type CONSTRUCTOR applied to two arguments, and
    ; without this arm it substituted into a three-part
    ; application - the type a `type` template's own alias name
    ; has, which is exactly the name a signature beside it wants
    ; to write. Each side resolves through `expSubstName`, so a
    ; parameter, a for-variable and a literal all work, and the
    ; answer is one constructor with no arguments.
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag t))
      (if (&& (== tag TAG_T_CON)
            (&& (strEq (nodeAName t) "syntax/join")
              (if (== (nodeB t) 0)
                false
                (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 2))))
        {
          (let l (expSubstTyName (vecGet (nodeBChildren t) 0) params args env isp))
          (if (let (Some l) l)
            {
              (let r (expSubstTyName (vecGet (nodeBChildren t) 1) params args env isp))
              (if (let (Some r) r)
                {
                  (let j (expJoinName l r))
                  (if (== (lexStartsLower j) 1)
                    (mkTVar j)
                    (mkTCon j vecNew))
                }
                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
            }
            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_T_CON)
          {
            (let n (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
            (if (let (Some n) n)
              (if (== (lexStartsLower n) 1)
                (mkTVar n)
                (mkTCon n (expSubstTyVec (nodeBChildren t) params args env isp)))
              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_T_VAR)
            {
              (let n (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
              (if (let (Some n) n)
                (if (== (lexStartsLower n) 1)
                  (mkTVar n)
                  (mkTCon n vecNew))
                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_T_ARR)
              (mkTArr (expSubstTy (nodeAChild t) params args env isp)
                (expSubstTy (nodeBChild t) params args env isp))
              (if (== tag TAG_T_TUP)
                (mkTTup (expSubstTyVec (nodeAChildren t) params args env isp))
                (if (== tag TAG_T_PTR)
                  (mkTPtr (expSubstTy (nodeAChild t) params args env isp))
                  t))))))
    }))

; One side of a type-position join: a bare type name, resolved the way
; a name position resolves. A nested join is a type node of its own,
; so it recurses through `expSubstTy` and reads its answer back off
; the constructor it built.
(pub :: expSubstTyName (-> ASTNode Names Nodes MEnv Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expSubstTyName t params args env isp)
  (if (|| (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON) (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR))
    (if (if (== (nodeB t) 0)
      true
      (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0))
      (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp)
      {
        (let sub (expSubstTy t params args env isp))
        (if (== (nodeTag sub) TAG_T_ERR)
          None
          (Some (nodeAName sub)))
      })
    {
      (expQueryErr env isp "a `(syntax/join ...)` in type position joins two type NAMES"
        "write `(syntax/join a b)` with a name on each side, or a join of two on either"
        (expMacMod env))
      None
    }))

(pub :: expSubstTyVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Nodes))
(pub fn (expSubstTyVec v params args env isp)
  (if (== (as v Int) 0)
    vecNew
    (expSubstTyVecIn v params args env isp vecNew 0)))

(pub :: expSubstTyVecIn (-> Nodes Names Nodes MEnv Int Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (expSubstTyVecIn v params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc (expSubstTy (vecGet v i) params args env isp))
      (expSubstTyVecIn v params args env isp acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; Per-arrow float flags, recomputed from the SUBSTITUTED type - one
; flag per source arrow position, the parser's own rule, over the same
; count the template's flags carried.
(pub :: expSigFlags (-> ASTNode Int Ints))
(pub fn (expSigFlags ty n)
  (expSigFlagsIn ty n vecNew))

(pub :: expSigFlagsIn (-> ASTNode Int Ints Ints))
(pub fn (expSigFlagsIn ty n acc)
  (if (<= n 0)
    acc
    (if (&& (! (nodeSame ty nilNode)) (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR))
      {
        (vecPush acc (expTyIsFloatCon (nodeAChild ty)))
        (expSigFlagsIn (nodeBChild ty) (- n 1) acc)
      }
      {
        (vecPush acc (expTyIsFloatCon ty))
        (expSigFlagsIn ty (- n 1) acc)
      })))

(pub :: expTyIsFloatCon (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expTyIsFloatCon t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON) (strEq (nodeAName t) "Float"))
      1
      0)))

; The span a generated declaration CARRIES AS ITS OWN: the name
; argument's, when the name came from a parameter - `(defPair p q)`
; generates a declaration whose name the user really spelled at `q`,
; and `axiom symbols`, go-to-definition and the duplicate-definition
; diagnostic all read the declaration's span as "where the name is".
; A name with NO source spelling - a `(syntax/join ...)` marker, a
; template-fixed name - records span 0, which AXSYM renders as its
; sanctioned positionless `-` row: the alternative was the invocation
; head's span, and the AXSYM position sweep rightly refused a symbol
; claimed at bytes that spell something else (it caught exactly that
; on the first fixed-name probe). The costs are owned: no
; go-to-definition target for a computed name, and a duplicate
; definition against one reports spanless.
; Body nodes still carry the invocation span (MAC-EXP-14) - this is
; only the declaration node itself.
(pub :: expNameSpan (-> String Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (expNameSpan s params args)
  (match (expParamIndex params s)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some idx)
      {
        (let a (vecGet args idx))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR) (!= (nodeSpan a) 0))
          (nodeSpan a)
          0)
      }
    )))

; One template declaration instantiated. fn and :: are the v1 surface;
; a nested TAG_D_MACROCALL re-tags for the next round; the default arm
; REFUSES (MAC-CAP-2's rule: a rewriter's unknown case is never a
; passthrough) - though `expCheckMacros` already refused the template
; at the macro's own line, so reaching this arm is the belt after the
; braces.
(pub :: expBuildOneDecl (-> ASTNode Names Nodes MEnv Int (Option ASTNode)))

; What `pub` a generated declaration is born with. The entry file's
; own declarations are all visible - nothing imports them, and import
; resolution stamps every one of them 1 - so an entry-side generation
; keeps the 1 it has always had, whatever the template says. A
; MODULE-side generation is the case where the question has an answer:
; the template's own `pub` is what decides whether anything outside
; that module may name the product, exactly as it decides for a
; declaration written by hand there.
;
; The axis is the INVOCATION SITE (word 17), and reading word 9 here
; was wrong in both directions, measured: word 9 holds the MACRO's
; defining module by the time a template is built - `expInstantiateDecl`
; moves it there so a nested private macro resolves at its definition
; site - so the test asked "was this macro DEFINED in the entry file".
; An entry file invoking an imported macro whose template omits `pub`
; got a generated `fn` stamped 0, `defIdxBuild` indexes only visible
; `fn`s, and the program failed with `AX3015 zeroColor has a signature
; but no definition` - a program the compiler that predates this slice
; compiles. The mirror leaked: a module invoking an ENTRY-FILE macro
; exported every product whatever its template said.
(pub :: expGenVis (-> MEnv ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expGenVis env t)
  (if (== (as (expQSite env) Int) 0)
    1
    (nodeVis t)))

(pub fn (expBuildOneDecl t params args env isp)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag t))
    (if (== tag TAG_D_FN)
      {
        (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
        (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
        (if (let (Some nm) nm)
          {
            (let ps (expSubstNames (nodeBNames t) params args env isp))
            (if (let (Some ps) ps)
              {
                (let prevTb env.tplbinds)
                (let _pushTb (set env.tplbinds ps))
                (let _scReset (expScResetTpl env))
                (let body (substTpl (nodeCChild t) params args env isp))
                (let _popTb (set env.tplbinds prevTb))
                {
                  (let g (nodeBuildDFn nm ps body nsp))
                  {
                    (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                    (nodeCopyWord g t 9)
                    (Some g)
                  }
                }
              }
              None)
          }
          None)
      }
      (if (== tag TAG_D_SIG)
        {
          (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
          (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
          (if (let (Some nm) nm)
            {
              (let ty (expSubstTy (nodeTyChild t) params args env isp))
              {
                (let g
                  (nodeBuildDSig nm
                    (expSigFlags ty
                      (if (== (nodeBWord t) 0)
                        0
                        (vecLen (nodeBWords t))))
                    0 nsp))
                {
                  (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                  (writeNodeChild g 6 ty)
                  (Some g)
                }
              }
            }
            None)
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
          ; A `data` template (MAC-CAP-7: a macro that generates TYPE
          ; declarations). The type's name and each constructor's name
          ; are name positions; each field type is a type position; the
          ; per-field float flags are RECOMPUTED from the substituted
          ; types, which is MAC-EXP-17's rule applied one level down
          ; from a signature. The type-PARAMETER list is copied
          ; verbatim: a parameter standing for a type variable would be
          ; a binder a macro introduces into the type namespace, and
          ; nothing in MAC-HYG-* renames those yet.
          {
            (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
            (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
            (if (let (Some nm) nm)
              {
                (let cs (expBuildCtors (nodeBChildren t) params args env isp))
                (if (let (Some cs) cs)
                  {
                    (let g (nodeBuildDData nm cs 0 nsp))
                    {
                      (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                      (setNodeTyWord g (nodeTy t))
                      (Some g)
                    }
                  }
                  None)
              }
              None)
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT)
            ; A `struct` template. Field NAMES are name positions like
            ; everything else, so `(syntax/for (f (syntax/fields S)) ...)`
            ; can build one struct out of another's fields.
            {
              (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
              (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
              (if (let (Some nm) nm)
                {
                  (let fs (expBuildStructFields (nodeBChildren t) params args env isp))
                  (if (let (Some fs) fs)
                    ; Word c is the representation markers
                    ; (`collectStructMarkers`): copied as written, since
                    ; a marker is a keyword and never a name position,
                    ; so `word` and `shared` survive a template.
                    {
                      (let g (nodeBuildDStruct nm fs (nodeCChildren t) nsp))
                      {
                        (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                        (Some g)
                      }
                    }
                    None)
                }
                None)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_D_ALIAS)
              ; A `type` template. The alias's name is a name position, its
              ; TARGET is a type position, and the type-parameter list is
              ; copied verbatim for the reason the `data` arm gives. Nothing
              ; else is needed: `tcExpandSigAliases` rewrites uses of an
              ; alias during checking, and phase D has finished by then, so
              ; a generated alias is indistinguishable from a written one -
              ; which is what MAC-EXP-17 says and what makes this arm four
              ; lines rather than a pass.
              {
                (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
                (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
                (if (let (Some nm) nm)
                  {
                    (let ty (expSubstTy (nodeBChild t) params args env isp))
                    (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
                      None
                      {
                        (let g (nodeBuildDAlias nm ty (nodeCNames t) nsp))
                        {
                          (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                          (Some g)
                        }
                      })
                  }
                  None)
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT)
                ; An `effect` template. The effect's name and each operation's
                ; name are name positions; each operation's declared arrow is a
                ; type position. The two vectors are rebuilt in lockstep - the
                ; bare names every existing reader indexes (codegen's evidence
                ; slots, the ambiguity table, AX3017's count) and the fields
                ; that carry the types - because a reader that found them
                ; disagreeing would read one operation's name beside another's
                ; arrow.
                {
                  (let nsp (expNameSpan (nodeAName t) params args))
                  (let nm (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
                  (if (let (Some nm) nm)
                    {
                      (let ops vecNew)
                      (let ofs vecNew)
                      (if (!
                            (expBuildEffectOps (nodeBNames t) (nodeCChildren t) params args env isp
                              ops ofs 0))
                        None
                        {
                          (let g (nodeBuildDEffect nm ops ofs nsp))
                          {
                            (writeNodeWord g 5 (expGenVis env t))
                            (Some g)
                          }
                        })
                    }
                    None)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_D_MACROCALL)
                  {
                    (let hd (expSubstName (nodeAName t) params args env isp))
                    (if (let (Some hd) hd)
                      {
                        (let as (expSubstArgs (nodeBChildren t) params args env isp))
                        (Some (nodeBuildDMacrocall hd as 0 isp))
                      }
                      None)
                  }
                  {
                    (expEmit env
                      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3021" "macro-template-unsupported" isp
                        "a declaration template may generate `fn`, `::`, `data`, `struct`, `type` and `effect` declarations, or further macro invocations - this form is not supported yet"
                        None
                        (Some "generate the declaration by hand, or extend the template surface")
                        "trait, import and nested macro templates are refusals rather than silent passthroughs, so the commit that adds one adds it loudly (MAC-CAP-2)"))
                    None
                  })))))))
  })

; An `effect` template's operations, both vectors at once. A refusal
; returns false after recording its diagnostic.
(pub :: expBuildEffectOps (-> Names Nodes Names Nodes MEnv Int Names Nodes Int Bool))
(pub fn (expBuildEffectOps ops ofs params args env isp outOps outFs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ops))
    true
    {
      (let nm (expSubstName (vecGet ops i) params args env isp))
      (if (let (Some nm) nm)
        {
          (let f
            (if (< i (vecLen ofs))
              (vecGet ofs i)
              nilNode))
          {
            (vecPush outOps nm)
            (vecPush outFs
              (if (nodeSame f nilNode)
                nilNode
                (nodeBuildFieldType nm
                  (if (== (nodeB f) 0)
                    nilNode
                    (expSubstTy (nodeBChild f) params args env isp))
                  0 isp)))
            (expBuildEffectOps ops ofs params args env isp outOps outFs (+ i 1))
          }
        }
        false)
    }))

; Each constructor of a `data` template: name substituted, field
; types substituted, arity and float flags recomputed from what came
; out. A struct-variant's field names (word 9) ride along unchanged -
; they are already identifiers, and nothing in the substitution can
; reach them.
(pub :: expBuildCtors (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildCtors cs params args env isp)
  ; A `...` marker after a form diverts to the splice loop;
  ; otherwise this is the ordinary one-form-per-constructor walk,
  ; byte for byte what it was. The pre-scan costs one pass over
  ; short vectors and keeps the hot path - and its goldens -
  ; untouched.
  (if (== (csHasSpliceMarker cs 0) 1)
    (expBuildCtorsSplice cs params args env isp vecNew 0)
    (expBuildCtorsIn cs params args env isp vecNew 0)))

; Is element `i` followed by the marker - a nullary constructor
; spelling `...`, exactly what `(...)` parses as? The marker on its
; own (leading, doubled, trailing nothing) is not a splice site:
; it builds as the ordinary `...` constructor it has always been.
(pub :: csHasSpliceMarker (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (csHasSpliceMarker cs i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (vecLen cs))
    0
    (if (== (isEllipsisCtor (vecGet cs (+ i 1))) 1)
      1
      (csHasSpliceMarker cs (+ i 1)))))

; The `...` marker: a nullary constructor spelling `...`, exactly
; what `(...)` parses as and what a bare `...` now parses as. Only
; the splice loop reads it; everywhere else it is a name like any
; other, which is why real code keeps the acceptance it had.
(pub :: isEllipsisCtor (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isEllipsisCtor c)
  (if (!= (nodeTag c) TAG_CTOR)
    0
    (if (strEq (nodeAName c) "...")
      1
      0)))

; The same marker one level up: an arm whose pattern is the bare
; variable `...`, exactly what the parser builds. Only the splice
; loop below and the ordinary-code refusal above read it.
(pub :: isEllipsisArm (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isEllipsisArm a)
  (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_M_ARM)
    0
    {
      (let p (nodeAChild a))
      (if (!= (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR)
        0
        (if (strEq (nodeAName p) "...")
          1
          0))
    }))

(pub :: expBuildCtorsIn
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildCtorsIn cs params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let c (vecGet cs i))
      {
        (let cn (expSubstName (nodeAName c) params args env isp))
        (if (let (Some cn) cn)
          ; `expNameSpan`, not the template constructor's own
          ; span: a name that came from a join has NO source
          ; spelling, and pointing at the template's `(` made
          ; `symbols` claim bytes that spell something else -
          ; caught by the AXSYM position sweep, which is the
          ; check that cannot be satisfied by re-blessing
          ; anything. Positionless is the honest answer, and
          ; the same one a generated `fn` gives.
          {
            (let ftys
              (if (== (nodeTy c) 0)
                vecNew
                (expSubstTyVec (nodeTyChildren c) params args env isp)))
            {
              (let g
                (nodeBuildCtor cn (vecLen ftys) (expCtorFlags ftys)
                  (expNameSpan (nodeAName c) params args)))
              {
                (setNodeTyChildren g ftys)
                (nodeCopyWord g c 9)
                (expBuildCtorsIn cs params args env isp (vecPush acc g) (+ i 1))
              }
            }
          }
          None)
      }
    }))

; `C ...` in a constructor list: rebuild `C` once per absorbed
; element, with each sequence-bound name taking its element - the
; declaration-list half of what `expSpliceNested` does for
; application spines, and the half the §10.5 machine macro's `data`
; needs. Ordinary elements delegate back to `expBuildCtors` on a
; singleton, so the single-constructor logic lives in exactly one
; place and the no-marker path above never reaches here.
;
; Two diagnosed misuses, neither silent: no sequence in `C` (the
; `...` follows nothing that repeats), and a `...` leaf inside `C`
; (a repeat beneath a repeat - no `...` can be sequence-bound,
; since a repeating pattern cannot contain one, so a leaf spelling
; it is literal). A form whose names all resolve the ordinary way
; never triggers the loop: `csHasSpliceMarker` only fires beside a
; marker, and the marker beside nothing repeatable diagnoses.
(pub :: expBuildCtorsSplice
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildCtorsSplice cs params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cs))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let c (vecGet cs i))
      (if (&& (< (+ i 1) (vecLen cs)) (== (isEllipsisCtor (vecGet cs (+ i 1))) 1))
        {
          (let built (expSpliceOneCtor c params args env isp))
          (if (let (Some built) built)
            (expBuildCtorsSplice cs params args env isp (expAppendBuilt acc built 0) (+ i 2))
            None)
        }
        {
          (let one
            (expBuildCtors
              {
                (let single vecNew)
                {
                  (vecPush single c)
                  single
                }
              }
              params args env isp))
          (if (let (Some one) one)
            (if (== (vecLen one) 0)
              None
              (expBuildCtorsSplice cs params args env isp (expAppendBuilt acc one 0) (+ i 1)))
            None)
        })
    }))

; A built vec appended onto the accumulator, element by element.
(pub :: expAppendBuilt (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expAppendBuilt acc built i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen built))
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc (vecGet built i))
      (expAppendBuilt acc built (+ i 1))
    }))

; One constructor form spliced: per-element rebuilds through the
; ordinary single-constructor path, with each sequence-bound name
; taking its element. The per-element substitution appends singles
; onto the shared params/args (last wins) and pops them after:
; nothing appends to those vecs during substitution, so the
; truncation restores exactly what was there.
(pub :: expSpliceOneCtor (-> ASTNode Names Nodes MEnv Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expSpliceOneCtor constructor params args env isp)
  {
    (let names (expCtorSeqNames constructor vecNew env))
    (if (== (vecLen names) 0)
      {
        (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "follows nothing that repeats"
          "put `...` directly after a constructor form whose names repeat, or drop it" isp)
        None
      }
      (if (== (expCtorHasEllipsis constructor) 1)
        {
          (expEllipsisDiag env "..." "nests a repeat inside a repeat"
            "repeat one level only in constructor lists too" isp)
          None
        }
        (if (let (Some rows) (expSequenceRows env names isp 0 vecNew))
          {
            (let first (vecGet rows 0))
            (let markP (vecLen params))
            (let markA (vecLen args))
            (let out vecNew)
            (let mut bad false)
            (for j in 0..(vecLen first)
              (if (! bad)
                {
                  (for k in 0..(vecLen names)
                    {
                      (vecPush params (vecGet names k))
                      (vecPush args (vecGet (vecGet rows k) j))
                    })
                  (let one (expBuildCtors (vecPush vecNew constructor) params args env isp))
                  (if (let (Some one) one)
                    (if (!= (vecLen one) 1)
                      (set bad true)
                      (vecPush out (vecGet one 0)))
                    (set bad true))
                  (expTrunc params markP)
                  (expTrunc args markA)
                }))
            (if bad
              None
              (Some out))
          }
          None)))
  })

; Every sequence-bound name a constructor form mentions: its own
; name, then every binder-looking leaf of its field types (absent
; for a nullary constructor, exactly as the single build below
; reads it). A name the match never bound is not collected -
; `expSeqLookup` missing means a literal head or a fixed name,
; which substitutes the ordinary way. Duplicates are harmless
; downstream (one index's value twice), so this collects rather
; than dedupes.
(pub :: expCtorSeqNames (-> ASTNode Names MEnv Names))
(pub fn (expCtorSeqNames c acc env)
  (if (let (Some _) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName c)))
    {
      (vecPush acc (nodeAName c))
      (expTySeqNamesVec (expCtorFieldTys c) acc env 0)
    }
    (expTySeqNamesVec (expCtorFieldTys c) acc env 0)))

; A constructor form's field types, or an empty vector for a
; nullary one - the same guard the single build reads through.
(pub :: expCtorFieldTys (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expCtorFieldTys c)
  (if (== (nodeTy c) 0)
    vecNew
    (nodeTyChildren c)))

; Binder-looking leaves of field-type nodes. `TAG_T_VAR` carries
; the name; the containers recurse structurally; anything else is
; skipped here and refused by the splice above, which owns the
; diagnostic.
(pub :: expTySeqNames (-> ASTNode Names MEnv Names))
(pub fn (expTySeqNames t acc env)
  {
    (let tg (nodeTag t))
    (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
      (if (let (Some _) (expSeqLookup (expSeqBindsVec env) (nodeAName t)))
        {
          (vecPush acc (nodeAName t))
          acc
        }
        acc)
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        (expTySeqNamesVec (nodeBChildren t) acc env 0)
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          (expTySeqNames (nodeBChild t) (expTySeqNames (nodeAChild t) acc env) env)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (expTySeqNamesVec (nodeAChildren t) acc env 0)
            ; A pointer's referent: the only other single-child
            ; shape, and the only arm that reads `nodeA` as a node
            ; rather than a name - `TAG_T_VAR` and `TAG_T_CON`
            ; above take names from the same word.
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (expTySeqNames (nodeAChild t) acc env)
              acc)))))
  })

(pub :: expTySeqNamesVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names MEnv Int Names))
(pub fn (expTySeqNamesVec tys acc env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    acc
    (expTySeqNamesVec tys (expTySeqNames (vecGet tys i) acc env) env (+ i 1))))

; A `...` leaf inside a constructor form: a repeat beneath a
; repeat. Only the spelling is checked here - no `...` can be
; sequence-bound (a repeating pattern cannot contain one), so any
; leaf spelling it is literal.
(pub :: expCtorHasEllipsis (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expCtorHasEllipsis c)
  (if (strEq (nodeAName c) "...")
    1
    (expTyHasEllipsisVec (expCtorFieldTys c) 0)))

(pub :: expTyHasEllipsis (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expTyHasEllipsis t)
  {
    (let tg (nodeTag t))
    (if (|| (== tg TAG_T_VAR) (== tg TAG_T_CON))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "...")
        1
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (expTyHasEllipsisVec (nodeBChildren t) 0)
          0))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
        (if (== (expTyHasEllipsis (nodeAChild t)) 1)
          1
          (expTyHasEllipsis (nodeBChild t)))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
          (expTyHasEllipsisVec (nodeAChildren t) 0)
          ; A pointer's referent, read as a node - see the reader
          ; above for why this arm does not share the tuple's.
          (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
            (expTyHasEllipsis (nodeAChild t))
            0))))
  })

(pub :: expTyHasEllipsisVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expTyHasEllipsisVec tys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    0
    (if (== (expTyHasEllipsis (vecGet tys i)) 1)
      1
      (expTyHasEllipsisVec tys (+ i 1)))))

; One float flag per field, from the SUBSTITUTED type - the same
; recomputation `expSigFlags` does per arrow, and for the same reason:
; a type rewritten without its flags prints 0.00 at exit 0.
(pub :: expCtorFlags (-> Nodes Ints))
(pub fn (expCtorFlags ftys)
  {
    (let acc vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen ftys)
        (vecPush acc (expTyIsFloatCon (vecGet ftys i))))
      acc
    }
  })

(pub :: expBuildStructFields (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildStructFields fs params args env isp)
  (expBuildStructFieldsIn fs params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: expBuildStructFieldsIn
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (expBuildStructFieldsIn fs params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fs))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let f (vecGet fs i))
      {
        (let fn0 (expSubstName (nodeAName f) params args env isp))
        (if (let (Some fn0) fn0)
          ; The parser's field layout (`parseOneFieldNamed`): the name
          ; in a, the FLOAT FLAG in b, the TYPE NODE in ty. This read b
          ; as the type. `expSubstTy` of the integer 1 a bare `Float`
          ; leaves there dereferenced it, and expansion died of SIGSEGV
          ; before `check` could say anything; an `Int` field's 0
          ; substituted to 0, so the field reached the checker with no
          ; type - a silent wildcard, `{unknown}` in AXSYM where its
          ; handwritten twin prints `Int`. The type substitutes from
          ; ty and the flag is recomputed from what came out, as
          ; `expCtorFlags` does for a constructor and for the same
          ; reason: the template's own flag is 0 for `(f : E)` however
          ; `E` substitutes. Pinned by
          ; tests/selfhost/396-macro-struct-field-types.ax and the
          ; zoo's `RecTwin`, whose AXSYM row carries `Rec`'s.
          ; Word c is the template field's `mut` marker, carried
          ; through unchanged: substitution cannot change whether a
          ; field was declared mutable, and a rebuilt field that
          ; dropped it made every `mut` field of a macro-generated
          ; struct AX3012 at its first write. Word 7, the field's
          ; `;@axiom:effect(...)` tags, is carried for the same reason:
          ; a rebuilt field that dropped them would hold any function
          ; at all where its template promised one row.
          {
            (let ty (expSubstTy (nodeTyChild f) params args env isp))
            {
              (let g
                (setNodeSpan (nodeBuildFieldFlagsPlain fn0 (expTyIsFloatCon ty) (nodeCWord f))
                  (expNameSpan (nodeAName f) params args)))
              {
                (setNodeTyChild g ty)
                (if (let (Some tags) (nodeAxtagsOpt f))
                  (writeNodeAxtags g tags))
                (expBuildStructFieldsIn fs params args env isp (vecPush acc g) (+ i 1))
              }
            }
          }
          None)
      }
    }))

(pub :: expSubstArgs (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSubstArgs v params args env isp)
  (expSubstArgsIn v params args env isp vecNew 0))

(pub :: expSubstArgsIn
  (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expSubstArgsIn v params args env isp acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc (substTpl (vecGet v i) params args env isp))
      (expSubstArgsIn v params args env isp acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; The in-place half: the first generated declaration takes over the
; invocation node - words 0-4, 6 and 9; module stays 0, vis is
; stamped 1, and the AXTAGs are re-homed by `expGiveTags`.
(pub :: expCopyDeclInto (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (expCopyDeclInto d g)
  {
    (nodeCopyWord d g 0)
    (nodeCopyWord d g 1)
    (nodeCopyWord d g 2)
    (nodeCopyWord d g 3)
    ; The span too: `g` carries the name argument's span
    ; (expNameSpan), and keeping the invocation head's here made the
    ; FIRST generated declaration claim its name at `(defPair` - the
    ; AXSYM position sweep caught exactly that.
    (nodeCopyWord d g 4)
    (writeNodeWord d 5 1)
    (nodeCopyWord d g 6)
    ; WORD 7 IS COPIED LIKE EVERY OTHER WORD, and it was a literal 0
    ; until 2026-08-31. That zero made the FIRST declaration a template
    ; generates the one place in this language where an AXTAG could be
    ; written and never read - not refused, not warned about, dropped.
    ;
    ; Measured on the merged tree before this line changed, with the
    ; same near-miss key moved one declaration down between runs:
    ;
    ;   (macro defF ((defF nm) ;@axiom:pur (fn (nm n) n) (:: nm ...)))
    ;       -> nothing
    ;   (macro defF ((defF nm) (:: nm ...) ;@axiom:pur (fn (nm n) n)))
    ;       -> W AX3039 axtag-key-typo
    ;
    ; and the same split for the claims that matter: `restrict(no-io)`
    ; over a template whose first declaration is the `::` checked `OK`
    ; on a body that calls `println`, and a `;@axiom:pre` there compiled
    ; to no check at all. `attachAxtags` had already been taught to walk
    ; a macro's rules for exactly this reason
    ; (`tests/diagnostics/361-macro-template-effect.ax` records the
    ; three bugs it took), and `expBuildDecls` copies word 7 onto every
    ; generated node - so the tag was parsed, attached, copied, and
    ; then zeroed here.
    ;
    ; The zero was not arbitrary: `expGiveTags` below parks the
    ; INVOCATION's tags on the first generated `fn`, and clearing the
    ; word first made that write unconditional. It does not need to be.
    ; `expGiveTagsIn` already reads `made[i]`'s own word 7 to decide
    ; whether a generated function is spoken for - "an invocation's
    ; tags are a claim about a different thing and must not overwrite
    ; them" - and `made[0]`'s word is intact, because only this copy
    ; was ever zeroed. So with the template's tags kept here, an
    ; invocation-level tag lands on the first UNTAGGED generated `fn`
    ; exactly as its own comment says it should, and a template-level
    ; tag survives on element 0 exactly as it does on element 1.
    ;
    ; ONE CASE STILL LOSES A TAG, named rather than left to be found: a
    ; template that generates NO `fn` at all, whose element 0 carries a
    ; tag, AND whose invocation carries one too. `expGiveTagsIn`'s
    ; no-fn fall-through writes the invocation's tags over this word,
    ; so the template's are lost. That case lost them before this
    ; change as well - every case did - so nothing regresses; it is
    ; simply the one this line does not reach, and an invocation tag on
    ; a template with no `fn` is inert by that function's own comment.
    (nodeCopyWord d g 7)
    (nodeCopyWord d g 9)
    0
  })

; Put every generated declaration into its module's namespace,
; exactly as `resolveDeclsPhase` put the module's written ones there:
; provenance in word 8, "may anything outside name it" in word 5, and
; the name rewritten to `Mod$name` with the bare slot claimed only
; when it is exported. Answers element 0's visibility, because
; `expCopyDeclInto` writes the invocation node's word 5 unconditionally
; and the module's answer is not always 1.
;
; The visibility rule is the written declaration's, read from the two
; halves that decide it there: the template's own `pub`, and the
; import filter that brought the module in - which phase D has to
; apply itself, since the generated NAME did not exist when import
; resolution applied it to everything else.
(pub :: expModuleize (-> Nodes MEnv Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expModuleize made env mod)
  {
    (let filter (modFilterFor (expModsVec env) (as mod String)))
    (expModuleizeIn made env mod filter 0 0)
  })

(pub :: expModuleizeIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int (Option Names) Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (expModuleizeIn made env mod filter i vis0)
  (if (>= i (vecLen made))
    vis0
    {
      (let g (vecGet made i))
      {
        (let exported (moduleDeclExport g (as mod String) (expModsVec env) filter))
        {
          (writeNodeText g 8 (as mod String))
          (writeNodeWord g 5 exported)
          ; After the stamps, because it rewrites the name the
          ; filter was asked about.
          (mangleDecl g (nodeModuleOpt g) (expMidx env) (expBaresVec env) (expFullsVec env)
            exported)
          (expModuleizeIn made env mod filter (+ i 1)
            (if (== i 0)
              exported
              vis0))
        }
      }
    }))

; Each attributed declaration owns its final spelling and the macro name.
; Record after module qualification so every reader asks about the same name.
(pub struct GeneratedOrigin
  (name : String)
  (macro : String))

(pub type GeneratedOrigins (Vec GeneratedOrigin))

(pub :: expRecordGens (-> MEnv Nodes String Int Int))
(pub fn (expRecordGens env made macroName i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen made))
    0
    {
      (let declaration (vecGet made i))
      (vecPush env.gens (GeneratedOrigin :name (nodeAText declaration) :macro macroName))
      (expRecordGens env made macroName (+ i 1))
    }))

; A present empty macro spelling is still an attribution; None is a miss.
(pub :: expGenOf (-> GeneratedOrigins String (Option String)))
(pub fn (expGenOf origins name)
  (expGenOfIn origins name 0))

(pub :: expGenOfIn (-> GeneratedOrigins String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expGenOfIn origins name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen origins))
    None
    {
      (let origin (vecGet origins i))
      (if (strEq origin.name name)
        (Some origin.macro)
        (expGenOfIn origins name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Every generated entry-file `fn` claims its bare name, overriding an
; import's claim exactly as an entry-file declaration written by hand
; does by being recorded first. Signatures are not recorded, mirroring
; `recordEntryFns`, which indexes definitions.
(pub :: expClaimEntryNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int Int))
(pub fn (expClaimEntryNames made env i)
  (if (vecIsNull env.bares)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen made))
      0
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag (vecGet made i)) TAG_D_FN)
          (mangleRecordEntry (expMidx env) (expBaresVec env) (expFullsVec env)
            (nodeAName (vecGet made i))))
        (expClaimEntryNames made env (+ i 1))
      })))

(pub :: expAppendRestMod (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expAppendRestMod decls made i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen made))
    0
    {
      (vecPush decls (vecGet made i))
      (expAppendRestMod decls made (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expAppendRest (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expAppendRest entryDecls decls made i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen made))
    0
    {
      (vecPush decls (vecGet made i))
      (vecPush entryDecls (vecGet made i))
      (expAppendRest entryDecls decls made (+ i 1))
    }))

; AXTAGs written above the invocation attach to the FIRST generated
; `fn` - `checkAxtags` validates fn declarations only, so parking a
; claim on a generated sig would leave it accepted and unenforced, the
; forged-claim shape MAC-SAFE-3 forbids. A template that generates no
; fn leaves the tags on the first declaration, where they are inert
; and visible.
(pub :: expGiveTags (-> ASTNode Nodes (Option Axtags) Int))
(pub fn (expGiveTags d made tags)
  (match tags
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some present) (expGiveTagsIn d made present 0))))

(pub :: expGiveTagsIn (-> ASTNode Nodes Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (expGiveTagsIn d made tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen made))
    ; no fn generated: first declaration keeps them
    {
      (writeNodeAxtags d tags)
      0
    }
    ; A generated `fn` that ALREADY carries tags got them from its own
    ; template, where an author wrote them against that function's
    ; body. An invocation's tags are a claim about a different thing
    ; and must not overwrite them - so the search skips it and looks
    ; for the next untagged function, which is the one an invocation
    ; -level tag can honestly describe.
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet made i)) TAG_D_FN) (isNone (nodeAxtagsOpt (vecGet made i))))
      (if (== i 0)
        {
          (writeNodeAxtags d tags)
          0
        }
        {
          (writeNodeAxtags (vecGet made i) tags)
          0
        })
      (expGiveTagsIn d made tags (+ i 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The join: a diagnostic anchored at a recorded invocation - same span
; HANDLE, same declaration module - gains that invocation's frames,
; outermost first. This is what MAC-DIAG-4 calls the expansion
; backtrace, and it is a post-pass rather than provenance on nodes
; because MAC-EXP-14 already makes the invocation span the one thing
; every generated node carries.
;
; `units` is the CUnit2 table (file, src, name); a frame stores the
; macro's MODULE and is resolved to that table here, where the table
; exists - the expander itself never sees units.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The expander's module cache is borrowed from live declaration nodes.
; Cross into an owning frame or optional lookup through this boundary.
(:: expModuleWordOpt (-> Int (Option String)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (expModuleWordOpt moduleWord)
  (if (== moduleWord 0)
    None
    (Some (as moduleWord String))))

; Entry modules may be absent or explicitly named by the empty string.
; Normalise both forms when comparing the source units of a frame.
(:: expModuleText (-> (Option String) String))
(fn (expModuleText name)
  (if (let (Some text) name)
    text
    ""))

(pub :: expModEq (-> (Option String) (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (expModEq a b)
  (if (strEq (expModuleText a) (expModuleText b))
    1
    0))

(pub :: expUnitMod (-> Units Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (expUnitMod units idx)
  (if (|| (== (as units Int) 0) (>= idx (vecLen units)))
    None
    (unitName (vecGet units idx))))

(pub :: expUnitOfMod (-> Units (Option String) (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expUnitOfMod units m)
  (if (vecIsNull units)
    None
    (expUnitOfModFrom units m 0)))

(pub :: expUnitOfModFrom (-> Units (Option String) Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (expUnitOfModFrom units m i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    None
    (if (== (expModEq (expUnitMod units i) m) 1)
      (Some i)
      (expUnitOfModFrom units m (+ i 1)))))

; Diagnostic renderers require a unit index. A missing module keeps the
; existing entry-file fallback at that boundary.
(pub :: expUnitForDiag (-> Units (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (expUnitForDiag units m)
  (match (expUnitOfMod units m)
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some u) u)))

(pub :: expAttachFrames (-> Diags Frames Units Int))
(pub fn (expAttachFrames diags table units)
  (if (== (as table Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for d in diags
        (if (! (== (diagSpan d) 0))
          {
            (expAttachTo d table units (expUnitMod units (diagUnit d)) 0)
            ; MAC-TOOL-5: a diagnostic that acquired a frame
            ; is a diagnostic about generated text, and its
            ; machine-applicable replacement was computed
            ; against text the author's file does not
            ; contain. The span it carries is the
            ; INVOCATION's - that is what makes the report
            ; useful and the FIX wrong, since applying it
            ; pastes the replacement over the macro call.
            (if (> (diagTraceCount d) 0)
              (diagDisarmFixes d))
          }))
      0
    }))

; How many expansion frames one diagnostic will carry.
;
; The frame table is appended to per instantiation and never popped, so
; the chain a diagnostic acquires records instantiations PERFORMED
; rather than nesting ENTERED. For an expression macro those are the
; same number and it is bounded by `expDepthLimit`; for a declaration
; macro that fans out they are not, and the refusal for one came out as
; a single AXDL line of 115 KB carrying 5,001 copies of one macro's
; name.
;
; Popping the frames is not the fix and was measured: they are attached
; post-hoc by span and module, so unwinding empties the table before
; anything renders and takes ten backtrace goldens with it. Bounding
; what one diagnostic CARRIES leaves the table alone and leaves the
; grammar alone - the overflow is reported as an ordinary note, which
; AXDL already has - and 128 is chosen because it is the depth limit,
; so the two cases that legitimately reach it (`991-macro-recursion`
; and `406-decl-macro-round-limit`, both exactly 128) are untouched and
; no golden moves.
(pub :: expMaxFrames Int)
(pub fn (expMaxFrames)
  128)

(pub :: expAttachTo (-> diag::Diag Frames Units (Option String) Int Int))
(pub fn (expAttachTo d table units dmod j)
  {
    (let n (expAttachFrom d table units dmod j 0))
    (if (> n expMaxFrames)
      (diagAddNote d
        (concat (concat "expansion reached this through " (expFmtInt n))
          " frames; the first 128 are shown")))
    0
  })

; Answers how many frames MATCHED, having added at most `expMaxFrames`
; of them. The count keeps rising after the cap so the note can say
; what it left out - a truncation nobody counts is how a reader stops
; being able to trust the ones that are shown.
(pub :: expAttachFrom (-> diag::Diag Frames Units (Option String) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (expAttachFrom d table units dmod j seen)
  (if (>= j (vecLen table))
    seen
    {
      (let e (vecGet table j))
      (if (&& (== (diagSpan d) e.span) (== (expModEq e.site dmod) 1))
        {
          (if (< seen expMaxFrames)
            (diagAddTrace d e.name e.defSpan (expUnitForDiag units e.defMod)))
          (expAttachFrom d table units dmod (+ j 1) (+ seen 1))
        }
        (expAttachFrom d table units dmod (+ j 1) seen))
    }))

; Push every diagnostic of `src` onto `dst`, answering `dst`. The
; expander runs before the checker, so its diagnostics are collected
; separately and merged; `orderDiags` is a stable partition, so
; merging this way round keeps expansion's refusals ahead of the
; checker's report about the code they poisoned.
(pub :: expMergeInto (-> Diags Diags Diags))
(pub fn (expMergeInto dst src)
  (expMergeIntoAt dst src 0))

(pub :: expMergeIntoAt (-> Diags Diags Int Diags))
(pub fn (expMergeIntoAt dst src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    dst
    {
      (vecPush dst (vecGet src i))
      (expMergeIntoAt dst src (+ i 1))
    }))

; ==================================================================
; CONTRACTS - `;@axiom:pre(...)` and `;@axiom:post(...)`, lowered.
;
; WHY THE LOWERING IS HERE AND NOT IN THE EMITTER. A contract is a
; claim this compiler cannot verify: there is no value analysis in this
; tree at all (measured: `grep -v '^ *;' FILE | grep -c
; 'constFold\|interval\|rangeOf'` over `typecheck.ax`, `codegen.ax`
; and this file answers 0, 0, 0 - the comment lines are excluded
; because this sentence matches the pattern it quotes), so
; `(> n 0)` is not a question the checker can answer about a caller it
; has not seen. What it CAN do is make the claim load-bearing at run
; time, which is Ada's answer, and this project's rule that a claim
; nothing checks is a comment says it has to do one or the other.
;
; So the check is compiled INTO the body, once, here - the pass that
; already "rewrites the declaration list" and that every consumer of a
; compiled program runs. Putting it in `codegen.ax` instead would
; require the same pass in `main.ax`, `repl.ax` and `lsp.ax`, and a
; caller that forgot it would be a contract that
; silently checked nothing, which is the failure this whole feature
; exists to prevent.
;
; THE TWO SHAPES.
;
;   ;@axiom:pre(P)    (fn (f a) BODY)  ->  (fn (f a) { (__contract P m) BODY })
;   ;@axiom:post(Q)   (fn (f a) BODY)  ->  (fn (f a)
;                                            (let ((result BODY))
;                                              { (__contract Q m) result }))
;
; A block's LAST expression is a tail position (`tailCallsSelf` in
; `codegen.ax`), so `pre` costs a self-recursive function nothing: the
; loop rewrite still fires. A `let`'s INITIALISER is not, so `post`
; costs it the rewrite - measured on `(fn (loopB n acc) (if (== n 0)
; acc (loopB (- n 1) (+ acc 1))))`, whose IR holds no call to itself
; and whose `let`-wrapped twin holds `call i64 @loopB`. That is not an
; artefact to fix: a `post` must observe the result, so the call it
; wraps cannot be a tail call, and Ada's `Post` has the same property.
; It is stated in `docs/reference.md` and in the reference
; rather than left for someone to measure again.
;
; `result` IS BOUND BY AN ORDINARY `let`, and a `let`'s initialiser is
; evaluated in the OUTER scope - probed: `(fn (f result) (let ((result
; (+ result 1))) result))` applied to 6 answers 7 - so wrapping the
; body cannot shadow anything the body itself reads. Parameters are
; immutable (`AX3012`), which is why there is no `'Old`: for a scalar
; parameter the name means in the `post` exactly what it meant in the
; `pre`.
; ==================================================================
(pub :: expLowerContracts (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expLowerContracts decls sigs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          (expLowerOne d (contractTagsFor sigs d)))
      }
      (expLowerContracts decls sigs (+ i 1))
    }))

; THERE IS NO "ALREADY LOWERED" GUARD, AND THE ONE THAT WAS HERE WAS A
; HOLE. It asked whether the body LOOKED lowered - a block whose first
; statement applies `__contract`, under an optional `result` binding -
; on the argument that "`__contract` is not a name a source program can
; reach". That argument was false. `__contract` is registered in `fns`
; and intercepted by `checkApp` like `__streq`, so a program CAN write
; it, and one that did silenced its own contract: measured, `(fn (f n)
; { (__contract true "...") n })` under `;@axiom:pre((> n 0))` called
; as `(f 0)` checked `OK` and exited **0**, where the same file without
; the first statement exits 77. The `post` arm was the same shape. A
; contract that a program can turn off by writing one expression is a
; check that cannot fail, which is the defect this whole feature
; exists to prevent.
;
; WHAT THE GUARD WAS FOR, AND WHY NOTHING REPLACES IT. `expandProgram`
; is called from `main.ax`, `lsp.ax`, `repl.ax` and `symbols`, and
; the guard existed so a second call over an already-lowered list
; would not wrap the body twice. No path in this tree does that -
; each of those parses first, and a diamond
; import splices one node once (measured: `(import B) (import C)` where
; both import a contract-carrying `D` emits exactly one
; `call i64 @__axiom_contract_fail`). And a doubled lowering would be
; semantically a no-op anyway: `AX3050` refuses a contract that
; performs anything, so evaluating it twice cannot differ from
; evaluating it once. The guard bought bytes in a case nothing
; exercises and cost soundness in one a program can write, so it is
; gone rather than made cleverer - any shape-based test of the body is
; forgeable by the body.
(pub :: expLowerOne (-> ASTNode Axtags Int))
(pub fn (expLowerOne d tags)
  (if (== (vecLen tags) 0)
    0
    {
      (let pres vecNew)
      (let posts vecNew)
      {
        (expSplitContracts d tags 0 pres posts)
        ; A tag whose value does not PARSE lowers nothing, and is
        ; `AX3050` from `checkContracts` instead. Compiling half a
        ; contract would be a run-time check of an expression the
        ; author did not write.
        (if (&& (== (vecLen pres) 0) (== (vecLen posts) 0))
          0
          (writeNodeChild d 3 (expContractBody d pres posts)))
      }
    }))

(pub :: expSplitContracts (-> ASTNode Axtags Int Nodes Nodes Int))
(pub fn (expSplitContracts d tags i pres posts)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet tags i))
      {
        (if (let (Some p) (contractExprOf t))
          (if (strEq (contractKeyOf t) "pre")
            (vecPush pres (expContractCheck d t p))
            (vecPush posts (expContractCheck d t p))))
        (expSplitContracts d tags (+ i 1) pres posts)
      }
    }))

; `{ pre... (let ((result BODY)) { post... result }) }`, with either
; half omitted when it is empty.
(pub :: expContractBody (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode))
(pub fn (expContractBody d pres posts)
  {
    (let sp
      (nodeSpan
        (vecGet
          (if (== (vecLen pres) 0)
            posts
            pres)
          0)))
    {
      (let inner
        (if (== (vecLen posts) 0)
          (nodeCChild d)
          (mkELetAt "result" (nodeCChild d)
            (nodeBuildEBegin (expBlockOf posts (mkEVarAt "result" sp)) 0 0 sp) sp)))
      (if (== (vecLen pres) 0)
        inner
        (nodeBuildEBegin (expBlockOf pres inner) 0 0 sp))
    }
  })

(pub :: expBlockOf (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (expBlockOf checks last)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (expPushAll v checks 0)
      (vecPush v last)
      v
    }
  })

(pub :: expPushAll (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (expPushAll v checks i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen checks))
    0
    {
      (vecPush v (vecGet checks i))
      (expPushAll v checks (+ i 1))
    }))

; `(__contract P "axiom: precondition failed in `f`: P")`. The span is
; the contract expression's own, which `contractExprOf` already made
; absolute, so a backtrace frame and any diagnostic land on the tag.
(pub :: expContractCheck (-> ASTNode AxtagEnt ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (expContractCheck d t p)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan p))
    (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEVarAt "__contract" sp) p) (nodeBuildEStr (expContractMsg d t) 0 0 sp))
  })

; The failure sentence, as a string-literal LEXEME - quotes included
; and escapes unprocessed, which is what `TAG_E_STR` holds and what
; `escBody` in the emitter reads (`\n` there is two source bytes).
(pub :: expContractMsg (-> ASTNode AxtagEnt String))
(pub fn (expContractMsg d t)
  (concat
    (concat
      (concat "\"axiom: "
        (if (strEq (contractKeyOf t) "pre")
          "pre"
          "post"))
      (concat "condition failed in `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))))
    (concat (concat "`: " (expContractEsc (contractTextOf t) 0 "")) "\\n\"")))

; The contract's own text, made safe to sit inside that lexeme. Only
; two bytes need it: `unescByte` in `core.ax` maps `\n`, `\t`, `\r` and
; `\0` and passes everything else through, so `\\` and `\"` are exactly
; the escapes that survive the round trip. A tag cannot contain a raw
; newline - `scanAxtags` ends it at one.
(pub :: expContractEsc (-> String Int String String))
(pub fn (expContractEsc s i acc)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    acc
    (expContractEsc s (+ i 1)
      {
        (let b (strByte s i))
        (if (== b 92)
          (concat acc "\\\\")
          (if (== b 34)
            (concat acc "\\\"")
            (concat acc (strSlice s i 1))))
      })))

; Pipelines are hygienic lets and ordinary calls. Every intermediate
; result is evaluated once, before the following stage's operands.
(pub :: expandPipe (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandPipe e env bs)
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (if (< (vecLen args) 2)
      (expPipeBad env (nodeSpan e) "a pipeline needs a value and at least one stage")
      (expandExpr (expPipeChain args 1 (vecGet args 0) env (nodeSpan e)) env bs))
  })

(pub :: expPipeChain (-> Nodes Int ASTNode MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expPipeChain args i value env sp)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    value
    {
      (let name (expFresh env "_pipe"))
      (mkELetAt name value
        (expPipeChain args (+ i 1)
          (expPipeStage (vecGet args i) (mkEVarAt name (nodeSpan value)) env) env sp)
        sp)
    }))

(pub :: expPipeStage (-> ASTNode ASTNode MEnv ASTNode))
(pub fn (expPipeStage stage value env)
  (if (!= (nodeTag stage) TAG_E_APP)
    (mkEApp stage value)
    {
      (let args (spineArgVec stage vecNew))
      (let out vecNew)
      {
        (let holes (expHoleCount args 0))
        (if (> holes 1)
          (expPipeBad env (nodeSpan stage)
            "a pipeline stage accepts at most one direct `_` argument")
          (if (&& (!= (nodeFieldNames stage) 0) (== holes 0))
            (expPipeBad env (nodeSpan stage)
              "a labelled pipeline stage needs one direct `_` field value")
            {
              (if (== holes 0)
                (vecPush out value))
              (expPipeArgs args out value 0)
              {
                (let rebuilt (expBuildSpine (spineHead stage) out 0))
                {
                  (setCtorFieldNames rebuilt (nodeFieldNamesNames stage))
                  (nodeCopyWord rebuilt stage 3)
                  (if (nodeGeneratedCall stage)
                    (markGeneratedCall rebuilt))
                  rebuilt
                }
              }
            }))
      }
    }))

(pub :: expPipeArgs (-> Nodes (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (expPipeArgs args out value i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (vecPush out
        (if (== (expIsHole (vecGet args i)) 1)
          value
          (vecGet args i)))
      (expPipeArgs args out value (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: expPipeBad (-> MEnv Int String ASTNode))
(pub fn (expPipeBad env sp message)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3127" "pipeline-stage" sp message None
          (Some
            "write `(|> value (f args...) (g args...))`; each stage receives the preceding value first, or at its single direct `_` argument"))
        env.declmod))
    (mkEInt 0)
  })

; Named struct construction lowers to the same checked application as
; positional construction. Reordered fields first bind their values in
; written order; the final call follows declaration order.
(pub :: expandNamedStruct (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expandNamedStruct e env bs)
  {
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let name (nodeAName head))
    (if (&& (== (expBound bs name) 1) (== (nodeDefScope head) 0))
      (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e)
        (concat "labelled construction requires a struct name; `"
          (concat name "` is a local binding")))
      (match (expNamedStructDecl env head)
        ((ExpStructAmbiguous)
          (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e)
            (concat "struct name `" (concat name "` is ambiguous; qualify it with its module")))
        )
        ((ExpStructMissing)
          (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e)
            (concat "labelled construction requires a visible struct; `"
              (concat name "` does not name one")))
        )
        ((ExpStructFound decl)
          {
            (let labels (nodeFieldNamesNames e))
            (let fields (nodeBChildren decl))
            (let names (expNamedFieldNames fields vecNew 0))
            (if (== (expCheckNamedLabels env e labels names) 0)
              (mkEInt 0)
              {
                (let params vecNew)
                (let values vecNew)
                (let callee (expNamedCallee head decl))
                {
                  (expHoleArgs (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 env bs params values)
                  {
                    (let body (expLowerNamedStruct callee values labels names env))
                    (if (== (vecLen params) 0)
                      body
                      (mkLamCurried params body (nodeSpan e)))
                  }
                }
              })
          }
        )))
  })

; Values are already expanded, including fresh parameters for direct
; holes. Reordering occurs inside the lambda when construction has holes.
(pub :: expLowerNamedStruct (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Strings Strings MEnv ASTNode))
(pub fn (expLowerNamedStruct callee values labels names env)
  (if (expNamedSameOrder labels names 0)
    (setCtorFieldNames (expBuildSpine callee values 0) names)
    {
      (let binds vecNew)
      (let refs vecNew)
      (let ordered vecNew)
      (let mut missing (:: None (Option String)))
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen values)
          {
            (let fresh (expFresh env "_field"))
            (let value (vecGet values i))
            {
              (vecPush binds (nodeBuildParallelBinding fresh value (nodeSpan value)))
              (vecPush refs (mkEVarAt fresh (nodeSpan value)))
            }
          })
        (for i in 0..(vecLen names)
          (match (strVecIdx labels (vecGetStr names i))
            ((Some k)
              (if (< k (vecLen refs))
                (vecPush ordered (vecGet refs k))
                (if (isNone missing)
                  (set missing (Some (vecGetStr names i)))))
            )
            ((None)
              (if (isNone missing)
                (set missing (Some (vecGetStr names i))))
            )))
        (match missing
          ((None)
            (foldLets binds (setCtorFieldNames (expBuildSpine callee ordered 0) names)
              (- (vecLen binds) 1))
          )
          ((Some name)
            (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan callee) (concat "missing struct field `" (concat name "`")))
          ))
      }
    }))

(pub :: expNamedFieldNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) Strings Int Strings))
(pub fn (expNamedFieldNames fields names i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    names
    (expNamedFieldNames fields (vecPush names (nodeAName (vecGet fields i))) (+ i 1))))

(pub :: expNamedSameOrder (-> Strings Strings Int Bool))
(pub fn (expNamedSameOrder labels names i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen labels))
    true
    (&& (strEq (vecGetStr labels i) (vecGetStr names i)) (expNamedSameOrder labels names (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: expCheckNamedLabels (-> MEnv ASTNode Strings Strings Int))
(pub fn (expCheckNamedLabels env e labels names)
  {
    (let seen vecNew)
    (let mut good 1)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen labels)
        {
          (let label (vecGetStr labels i))
          {
            (if (== (strVecHas names label) 0)
              {
                (expNamedBad env (expNamedLabelSpan e i)
                  (concat "unknown struct field `" (concat label "`")))
                (set good 0)
              }
              (if (== (strVecHas seen label) 1)
                {
                  (expNamedBad env (expNamedLabelSpan e i)
                    (concat "struct field `" (concat label "` is supplied more than once")))
                  (set good 0)
                }))
            (vecPush seen label)
          }
        })
      (for i in 0..(vecLen names)
        {
          (let name (vecGetStr names i))
          (if (== (strVecHas labels name) 0)
            {
              (expNamedBad env (nodeSpan e) (concat "missing struct field `" (concat name "`")))
              (set good 0)
            })
        })
      good
    }
  })

(pub :: expNamedLabelSpan (-> ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (expNamedLabelSpan e i)
  (if (== (nodeC e) 0)
    (nodeSpan e)
    (if (>= i (vecLen (nodeCWords e)))
      (nodeSpan e)
      (vecGet (nodeCWords e) i))))

(pub :: expStructBuckets (-> MEnv NodesRows))
(pub fn (expStructBuckets env)
  env.structBuckets)

; Declaration macros have finished before this index is built. Each
; name keeps its candidates in declaration order, including duplicates.
(pub :: expIndexStructDecls (-> MEnv ()))
(pub fn (expIndexStructDecls env)
  {
    (let names env.structNames)
    (let buckets (expStructBuckets env))
    (for d in (expDeclsVec env)
      (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT)
        {
          (let index (internIntern (internWordOf names) (nodeAName d)))
          (if (== index (vecLen buckets))
            {
              (let bucket (:: vecNew Nodes))
              (vecPush buckets bucket)
              ()
            })
          (vecPush (vecGet buckets index) d)
          ()
        }))
  })

; A supplied argument keeps its enclosing declaration’s module when a
; macro expands it again. A free template head resolves at its macro’s
; definition. Qualified names select a module and preserve privacy.
(pub data ExpStructResolution
  (ExpStructMissing)
  (ExpStructAmbiguous)
  (ExpStructFound ASTNode))

(pub :: expNamedStructDecl (-> MEnv ASTNode ExpStructResolution))
(pub fn (expNamedStructDecl env head)
  {
    (let name (nodeAName head))
    (let bare (bareOf name))
    (if (let (Some index) (internFind (internWordOf env.structNames) bare))
      (expChooseStructDecl (vecGet (expStructBuckets env) index) name bare
        (if (== (nodeDefScope head) 1)
          (nodeModule head)
          env.declmod))
      (ExpStructMissing))
  })

(pub :: expChooseStructDecl (-> Nodes String String Int ExpStructResolution))
(pub fn (expChooseStructDecl decls name bare site)
  {
    (let mut chosen (ExpStructMissing))
    (let mut priority 0)
    {
      (for d in decls
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT) (strEq (nodeAName d) bare))
          {
            (let own (== (expModEq (nodeModuleOpt d) (expModuleWordOpt (as site Int))) 1))
            (let qualified (! (strEq name bare)))
            (let full
              (if (== (nodeModule d) 0)
                bare
                (concat (nodeModuleName d) (concat "$" bare))))
            (if (&& (|| own (&& (== (nodeVis d) 1) (!= (nodeModule d) 0)))
                  (|| (! qualified) (strEq name full)))
              {
                (let rank
                  (if own
                    2
                    1))
                (if (> rank priority)
                  {
                    (set priority rank)
                    (set chosen (ExpStructFound d))
                  }
                  (if (== rank priority)
                    (set chosen (ExpStructAmbiguous))))
              })
          }))
      chosen
    }
  })

(pub :: expNamedBad (-> MEnv Int String ASTNode))
(pub fn (expNamedBad env sp message)
  {
    (expEmit env
      (expMarkUnit env
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3132" "named-construction" sp message None
          (Some
            "construct a visible struct with each declared field exactly once: `(Point :x 10 :y 20)`"))
        env.declmod))
    (mkEInt 0)
  })

; A struct head in a source value position cannot escape its labelled
; construction through an alias or a higher-order call. A zero-field
; type needs no labels and keeps its existing checking path.
(pub :: expStructNeedsLabels (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds Bool))
(pub fn (expStructNeedsLabels head env bs)
  (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
    false
    (if (|| (&& (== (expBound bs (nodeAName head)) 1) (== (nodeDefScope head) 0))
          (isSome (expFindMacro env (nodeAName head))))
      false
      (match (expNamedStructDecl env head)
        ((ExpStructFound decl) (> (vecLen (nodeBChildren decl)) 0))
        (_ false)))))

; Only a template head supplied by substitution can generate a
; declaration-order construction. An explicit constructor name written
; in a template is source and needs its labels there.
(pub :: expDynamicTemplateHead (-> ASTNode Names MEnv Bool))
(pub fn (expDynamicTemplateHead head params env)
  (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
    false
    {
      (let name (nodeAName head))
      (let template (expScResolveTpl env name))
      (let iteration (expForBindOf env name))
      (if (let (Some _) (expTemplateWinner template iteration))
        false
        (if (let (Some _) iteration)
          true
          (isSome (expParamIndex params name))))
    }))

(pub :: expSubstCall (-> ASTNode ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Names (Vec ASTNode) MEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (expSubstCall tpl templateHead values params args env isp)
  {
    (let head (substTpl templateHead params args env isp))
    (let call (expRebuildSpine head values isp 0))
    (if (!= (nodeFieldNames tpl) 0)
      (setCtorFieldNames call (nodeFieldNamesNames tpl))
      (if (expDynamicTemplateHead templateHead params env)
        (markGeneratedCall call)
        call))
  })

; Explicit conversion, type ascription and layout queries have a
; syntactic type slot. Its spine is for the checker, and never a value
; reference. Preserve it even at wrong arity so the checker diagnoses
; the missing or surplus operand at its normal location.
(pub :: expHasTypeOperand (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (expHasTypeOperand e)
  {
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (&& (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
      (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "__as_numeric")
        (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "__ascribe")
          (|| (strEq (nodeAName head) "sizeof") (strEq (nodeAName head) "alignof")))))
  })

(pub :: expExpandTypedApp (-> ASTNode MEnv ScopeBinds ASTNode))
(pub fn (expExpandTypedApp e env bs)
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (let values (vecPush vecNew (vecGet args 0)))
    (let params vecNew)
    (expHoleArgs args 1 env bs params values)
    (let call (expBuildSpine (spineHead e) values 0))
    (if (== (vecLen params) 0)
      call
      (mkLamCurried params call (nodeSpan e)))
  })

; Preserve the resolved nominal identity before another macro or the
; checker can interpret a bare constructor name in a different module.
(pub :: expNamedCallee (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (expNamedCallee head decl)
  {
    (let name
      (if (== (nodeModule decl) 0)
        (nodeAName decl)
        (concat (nodeModuleName decl) (concat "$" (nodeAName decl)))))
    (let callee (mkEVarAt name (nodeSpan head)))
    (nodeCopyWord callee head 8)
    (writeNodeWord callee 10 (nodeDefScope head))
    callee
  })
